ews-live-average-age-for-first-porn-viewing-reduces-by-two-years-nsw-nurses-to-begin-strike?page=with:block-6733323b8f083b2265325668#block-6733323b8f083b2265325668"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 raided multiple locations }}}\uc2  in Melbourne\u8217\'20\'19s western suburbs last night as part of an investigation into alleged fraud against the \uc2 National Disability Insurance\uc2  Scheme (NDIS).\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Tabcorp\uc2  was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/13/australia-news-live-average-age-for-first-porn-viewing-reduces-by-two-years-nsw-nurses-to-begin-strike?page=with:block-673327f68f08ef6f10472d83#block-673327f68f08ef6f10472d83"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fined over $260,000 }}}\uc2  for accepting bets on live tennis.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The Greens announced {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/13/australia-news-live-average-age-for-first-porn-viewing-reduces-by-two-years-nsw-nurses-to-begin-strike?page=with:block-673328358f088fa22a5be357#block-673328358f088fa22a5be357"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a $100m per year plan }}}\uc2  to ensure public hospitals can provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The treasurer, \uc2 Jim Chalmers\uc2  announced funding of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/13/australia-news-live-average-age-for-first-porn-viewing-reduces-by-two-years-nsw-nurses-to-begin-strike?page=with:block-6733288d8f08d0e6c0683aaf#block-6733288d8f08d0e6c0683aaf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $900m for states and territories }}}\uc2  through a new national productivity fund to boost competition and productivity across the economy.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2  says he supports the right of women to access legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but will {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/13/australia-news-live-average-age-for-first-porn-viewing-reduces-by-two-years-nsw-nurses-to-begin-strike?page=with:block-6733b9788f089359e632fb73#block-6733b9788f089359e632fb73"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 not require }}}\uc2  the Queensland Nationals senator \uc2 Matt Canavan\uc2  and the South Australian Liberal senator Alex Antic to withdraw a sponsored bill to change \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Australia\u8217\'20\'19s biggest lender, \uc2 Commonwealth Bank\uc2 , recorded {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/13/australia-news-live-average-age-for-first-porn-viewing-reduces-by-two-years-nsw-nurses-to-begin-strike?page=with:block-6733c52b8f084e2f44269674#block-6733c52b8f084e2f44269674"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a quarterly $2.5bn cash net profit }}}\uc2  backed by \u8220\'20\'1Cstrong momentum\u8221\'20\'1D in home lending.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 A senior Trump adviser has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/13/australia-news-live-average-age-for-first-porn-viewing-reduces-by-two-years-nsw-nurses-to-begin-strike?page=with:block-6733caa18f084e2f4426969e#block-6733caa18f084e2f4426969e"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 posted a gif }}}\uc2  of an hourglass in response to \uc2 Kevin Rudd\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s congratulation message to the incoming president, but former prime minister \uc2 Tony Abbott\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/13/australia-news-live-average-age-for-first-porn-viewing-reduces-by-two-years-nsw-nurses-to-begin-strike?page=with:block-6733dd928f084e2f4426971b#block-6733dd928f084e2f4426971b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 defended Rudd }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The Catholic church {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/13/australia-news-live-average-age-for-first-porn-viewing-reduces-by-two-years-nsw-nurses-to-begin-strike?page=with:block-6733e7508f084e2f44269746#block-6733e7508f084e2f44269746"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 won a landmark case }}}\uc2  limiting its liability for the abuse of children by priests not under its direct employment.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The wage price index for the September quarter has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/13/australia-news-live-average-age-for-first-porn-viewing-reduces-by-two-years-nsw-nurses-to-begin-strike?page=with:block-6733f3418f089359e632fcbb#block-6733f3418f089359e632fcbb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 come in at 3.5% compared with a year earlier }}}\uc2 , the ABS reported. That figure compared with the 3.6% pace expected by economists and the 4.1% clip reported by the ABS for the June quarter.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Independent senator David Pocock says {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/13/australia-news-live-average-age-for-first-porn-viewing-reduces-by-two-years-nsw-nurses-to-begin-strike?page=with:block-6733f2468f089359e632fcb6#block-6733f2468f089359e632fcb6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 he will be opposing the government\u8217\'20\'19s misinformation and disinformation bill }}}\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cunless major changes are made to the legislation\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Sydney trains will run 24 hours for the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/13/australia-news-live-average-age-for-first-porn-viewing-reduces-by-two-years-nsw-nurses-to-begin-strike?page=with:block-673432c08f082c1a66381d50#block-673432c08f082c1a66381d50"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 next three days }}}\uc2  amid an industrial dispute.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.39am GMT\par \par \uc2 Nacc releases fact sheet to dispel misinformation\par \par \uc2 Earlier today, the National Anti-Corruption Commission put out its \u8220\'20\'1Ccorrections to misinformation\u8221\'20\'1D factsheet.\par \par \uc2 In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nacc.gov.au/news-and-media/weekly-update-work-commission-13-november-2024"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 its weekly update }}}\uc2 , the federal integrity body said a \u8220\'20\'1Csignificant amount of misinformation\u8221\'20\'1D circulates about the Nacc.\par \par \uc2 While it could not \u8220\'20\'1Ccorrect everything\u8221\'20\'1D, it wanted to address some of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nacc.gov.au/news-and-media/corrections-misinformation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 primary issues it had }}}\uc2  by countering it with \u8220\'20\'1Cfacts and evidence\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Interestingly, one of the points related to a recent finding about the Nacc commissioner, Paul Brereton. The Nacc inspector, the watchdog\u8217\'20\'19s watchdog, found his involvement in a decision not to start a corruption investigation into robodebt referrals resulted in the process being \u8220\'20\'1Caffected by apprehended bias\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Read more:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/30/nacc-robodebt-investigation-misconduct-finding-commissioner-paul-brereton-ntwnfb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Nacc to reconsider whether to investigate robodebt after \u8216\'20\'18apprehended bias\u8217\'20\'19 finding against commissioner }}}\par \par \uc2 The Nacc says media references saying Brereton submitted a conflict of interest declaration because one of the persons referred to the Nacc was a \u8216\'20\'18close personal friend\u8217\'20\'19 are misinformation.\par \par \uc2 The Nacc helpfully supplied this correction:\par \par \uc2 The commissioner declared immediately and repeatedly that he had a perceived (not actual) conflict of interest, arising from a prior professional, not personal, relationship with one of the referred persons. The commissioner and that individual have never socialised other than at official functions, nor visited each other\u8217\'20\'19s homes.\par \par \uc2 So, what did the inspector\u8217\'20\'19s report say? In its executive summary, it referenced Brereton\u8217\'20\'19s own words about \u8220\'20\'1Cperson one\u8221\'20\'1D. That included that Brereton said he had a \u8220\'20\'1Cclose association\u8221\'20\'1D with person one, that they were someone he \u8220\'20\'1Cknows well\u8221\'20\'1D, and \u8220\'20\'1Cwho is well known to me\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The language changed when the Nacc provided a submission to the inspector in August 2024. Brereton said his relationship with person one was a \u8220\'20\'1Cprior professional association, and not a close personal relationship\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In a report prepared by Alan Robertson SC, the former federal court judge said the change in the Nacc\u8217\'20\'19s language was \u8220\'20\'1Cgloss\u8221\'20\'1D and the conflict existed in \u8220\'20\'1Cthe terms it was disclosed\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.44am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.24am GMT\par \par \uc2 Round-the-clock Sydney train services explained\par \par \uc2 If you\u8217\'20\'19re confused as to why Sydney trains is running 24 hours for the next three days, \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports it was one of the demands of the union.\par \par \uc2 The union wants a 32 % pay rise over four years and a 35-hour working week and has accused the government of \u8220\'20\'1Cdragging its heels\u8221\'20\'1D on an improved offer.\par \par \uc2 But RTBU NSW secretary Toby Warnes said commuters should not pay the price for that and would instead benefit from the \u8220\'20\'1Cnovel\u8221\'20\'1D industrial action.\par \par \uc2 Our protected industrial action is always about causing a headache for the government, so they actually listen to the needs of their workforce; it\u8217\'20\'19s never about disrupting the travelling public.\par \par \uc2 Our call for 24-hour transport has the benefit of being a pain for management and the government, while also resulting in improved services for commuters.\par \par \uc2 Previous industrial action was avoided when the government agreed to a union demand to have 50c fares for a weekend.\par \par \uc2 Planned industrial action on light rail services, which will see limited services run on Friday and Saturday, are still set to continue.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.41am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.09am GMT\par \par \uc2 Pandas bid farewell to Australia\par \par \uc2 Wang Wang and Fu Ni\u8217\'20\'19s love affair with Australia has ended with a final farewell to visitors at Adelaide zoo, \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports.\par \par \uc2 As a final treat, the pair \u8211\'20\'13 on loan from China since 2009 \u8211\'20\'13 were given a special meal on Wednesday enriched with letters, messages and drawings from more than 1000 adoring fans.\par \par \uc2 Zoo keepers provided {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.facebook.com/AdelaideZoo/videos/1090965729240806/?__cft__[0]=AZXGP9LD5uC5AHZriHUrN4mHVQvnA91TV_BBKrsIM8dKHRLzgHzHherI2Z8Nkwb8j1iobLPzxoEsyCo9WkEmVUCkoXkiW6X2ha7VCpn7m_QyVizpngr-RmySQSgpF8AZfxj4i9hp3eykr958uXs92P8cWBiGHM-8ul-41QSV9ZulBnGWuQZvpcslHnBe227ItQvNBX7WBwnDfMwOJTl2-a-S&__tn__=%2CO%2CP-R"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an emotional farewell }}}\uc2  to the animals in a video on social media.\par \par \uc2 Senior primate keeper Pij Olijnik said he loved greeting the pandas each morning.\par \par \uc2 I just love rocking up to work and opening the door and having pandas there that greet you.\par \par \uc2 They verbally greet you and seem happy to see you and welcome you into that environment.\par \par \uc2 There were long-held hopes that Wang Wang, 19, and Fu Ni, 18, would breed while in Australia, as part of a global conservation program for the vulnerable species.\par \par \uc2 But despite many attempts during their brief annual mating window, several \u8220\'20\'1Cfalse pregnancies\u8221\'20\'1D, and failed artificial insemination procedures for Fu Ni, parenthood remained out of reach for the popular pair.\par \par \uc2 Adelaide Zoo will welcome new giant pandas, male Xing Qiu (pronounced shing chee-y-ull) and female Yi Lan (ee-lun), later this year.\par \par \uc2 Both are from the Chinese city of Chengdu and are aged three, with Xing Qiu turning four just before his arrival.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.27am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.56am GMT\par \par \uc2 The Bureau of Metereology has issued a severe thunderstorm warning for parts of Queensland.\par \par \uc2 enltr?? UPDATED SEVERE THUNDERSTORM WARNING for people in parts of Scenic Rim, Southern Downs, Toowoomba, Lockyer Valley and Logan Council Areas.\par \par \uc2 DAMAGING WINDS, LARGE HAILSTONES and HEAVY RAINFALL possible.\par \par \uc2 See latest warning: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/vYLHCna3or"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/vYLHCna3or }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/fCmPfYamoh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/fCmPfYamoh }}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Bureau of Meteorology, Queensland (@BOM_Qld) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BOM_Qld/status/1856573006409031871?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 13, 2024 }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.06am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.30am GMT\par \par \uc2 Richard Marles\uc2  congratulates Trump\u8217\'20\'19s pick for defense secretary\par \par \uc2 The defence minister and deputy prime minister has congratulated Fox News host, Pete Hegseth for being picked by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to be defense secretary in the incoming Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 Marles told the ABC that he had not met Hegseth but extended his congratulations:\par \par \uc2 Representing the \uc2 United States government\uc2  as the secretary of defense is a huge responsibility. And this will be an amazing opportunity for Pete Hegseth and certainly from my point of view, I worked more closely with my American counterpart, \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  then I would have with any other defence minister around the world.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m sure that will be the case when Pete Hegseth becomes the secretary of defense. It is a critically important part of the way in which our bilateral relationship exists, so much of what happens between our two companies happens through the prism of defence. I very much look forward to meeting him in building a relationship which will help us take the alliance into the future.\par \par \uc2 On the future for US ambassador \uc2 Kevin Rudd\uc2 , Marles said Rudd had attended the Republican National Convention earlier this year, and had been important to introducing the government to a number of figures who may play a part in a future Trump administration. He said Rudd will build the best relationship Australia can with the future Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.33am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.11am GMT\par \par \uc2 Sydney trains to operate 24 hours from Thursday to Saturday amid industrial dispute\par \par \uc2 The NSW transport minister, Jo Haylen, has said rail services in suburban parts of the Sydney train network will need to run for 24 hours a day during planned industrial action from Thursday to Saturday otherwise \u8220\'20\'1Cthere will be no services at all\u8221\'20\'1D due to some of the work bans being put in place by the Rail, Tram and Bus Union.\par \par \uc2 She said:\par \par \uc2 We want confidence and certainty for passengers and for the transport workforce, we have made very good progress across some 28 bargaining meetings, all day long, meetings now for over six months.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the bargaining room is in operation today. I feel we are close to a resolution, but we need to round this out by Christmas.\par \par \uc2 The commitment of the government Sydney trains, New South Wales trains, and I understand the unions, is to resolve this as quickly as possible, but we also want passengers to be able to get where they need to go.\par \par \uc2 Haylen said there could be major delays, but the current plan was for a normal timetable to run across Sydney on Thursday, Friday and Saturday, and in the evening and in particular between midnight and 4am when there wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t normally be operating trains, there will be a train every 30 minutes.\par \par \uc2 Matt Longland, chief executive of Sydney trains said track work planned over the weekend had been cancelled, and urged customers to plan ahead in order to not be too put out by delays.\par \par \uc2 The period that we\u8217\'20\'19re entering into is one in which a number of new bans will take place from tomorrow \u8230\'20\'26 will make it increasingly difficult to operate a reliable network. Safety is always our number one priority, and none of our team will operate services where it\u8217\'20\'19s not safe to do so, but we do expect that there will be some delays.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.05am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.08am GMT\par \par \uc2 Senior TV executive leaves Nine\par \par \uc2 Another TV news executive has resigned suddenly from Nine Entertainment.\par \par \uc2 The resignation leaves the Sydney and the Brisbane TV newsrooms of Nine News without a leader.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNine can confirm further leadership changes were communicated to its TV News division today,\u8221\'20\'1D a spokesperson for Nine said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAn internal and external recruitment process is now under way for news directors in Sydney, Brisbane and Perth.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Perth news director position is vacant because of the internal promotion of Gareth Parker.\par \par \uc2 Brisbane news director Amanda Paterson left Nine a week ago after a 31-year career in TV news and current affairs including a stint as a reporter on A Current Affair.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday staff were told the Sydney news director, Simon Hobbs, will leave the business on Friday.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.55am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.55am GMT\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s the video of shadow foreign affairs minister, \uc2 Simon Birmingham\uc2 , speaking on whether US ambassador, \uc2 Kevin Rudd\uc2 , should stay in the job after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s election victory last week in the US.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.46am GMT\par \par \uc2 Police called in over allegations of vote tampering in Victorian council elections\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Victorian Electoral Commission\uc2  has referred allegations of suspected postal vote tampering in two Melbourne council elections to police.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 VEC\uc2  on Wednesday afternoon said it had detected a high number of multiple returned votes in Knox city council, in Melbourne\u8217\'20\'19s east, and Whittlesea city council \u8211\'20\'13 which was overseen by administrators between 2020 and 2024 after governance issues, in Melbourne\u8217\'20\'19s north, after voting closed in the state\u8217\'20\'19s council elections last month.\par \par \uc2 Both elections have been declared by the \uc2 VEC\uc2  and on Wednesday it requested the Victorian Civil and Administrative Tribunal (Vcat) review the results in Whittlesea\u8217\'20\'19s Lalor ward and Knox\u8217\'20\'19s Baird ward. Peter Lockwood won Baird ward on a margin of 1.87% while Stevan Kozmevski secured a victory in Lalor on a 0.16% margin. There is no suggestions of wrongdoing by either candidate.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/nov/13/melbourne-council-elections-postal-vote-tampering-investigation-knox-whittlesea-ntwnfb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Suspected postal vote tampering in two Melbourne council elections referred to police }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.51am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.35am GMT\par \par \uc2 Asio\uc2  using AI in data processing\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Australian Security Intelligence Organisation\uc2  (\uc2 Asio\uc2 ) has revealed it has been using artificial intelligence to process vast amounts of counter-terrorism data since April this year.\par \par \uc2 In its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.asio.gov.au/system/files/2024-10/ASIO%20Annual%20Report%202023-24.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2024 annual report }}}\uc2 , released at the end of October, the spy agency said it had deployed AI \u8220\'20\'1Cin support of a high-priority counter-terrorism investigation\u8221\'20\'1D to triage terabytes of data and \u8220\'20\'1Cidentify the intelligence-needle in a field of digital-haystacks.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Asio said:\par \par \uc2 The AI capabilities efficiently prioritised security-relevant information within hours, enabling \uc2 Asio\uc2  officers to go straight to the most security-relevant product that would otherwise take hundreds of hours to find.\par \par \uc2 The agency said an Asio officer reviews all results, and a human is responsible and accountable for \uc2 Asio\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s decision-making, but the use of AI significantly reduced the time between the collection and analysis of intelligence.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.43am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.27am GMT\par \par \uc2 Treasurer says \u8216\'20\'18direction of travel is pretty clear\u8217\'20\'19 in uptake of renewable energy\par \par \uc2 Jim Chalmers\uc2  also talked about the \u8220\'20\'1CGreat Fragmentation\u8221\'20\'1D that has unwound over the past fifteen years or so since the global financial crisis (followed by the Covid pandemic and global inflation spike after Russia\u8217\'20\'19s invasion of Ukraine).\par \par \uc2 We couldn\u8217\'20\'19t help wondering whether the incoming/returning Trump administration would create a particularly big fragmentation of global climate policy given Trump\u8217\'20\'19s promise to pull the US out of the Paris Climate agreement for a second time, and his \u8220\'20\'1Cdrill, baby, drill\u8221\'20\'1D mantra that echoes in parts of Australia too.\par \par \uc2 Chalmers said the change of administration \u8220\'20\'1Cdoes introduce another element of uncertainty in the global net zero transformation.\par \par \uc2 That said, \u8220\'20\'1Cthe weight of the world\u8217\'20\'19s direction, more broadly \u8230\'20\'26 encourages us and convinces us that we\u8217\'20\'19re on the right track\u8221\'20\'1D, Chalmers said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNo one, I think, can really argue against the role that cheaper and cleaner energy will play in our own industrial and economic development, or indeed, more broadly,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, adding that \u8220\'20\'1Cthe direction of travel is pretty clear in the world [and] in the global economy\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Overall, this will be the defining change and challenge in our economy over a longer time frame than governments in either country.\par \par \uc2 Physics, it should be said, also don\u8217\'20\'19t pay heed to electoral cycles \u8211\'20\'13 even though that might get lost in the coming political campaign. Guess we\u8217\'20\'19ll find out soon enough on this matter too.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.45am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.18am GMT\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18No whiff\u8217\'20\'19 of a wage-price spiral, and Australia must hold the net zero line, Chalmers says\par \par \uc2 Treasurer \uc2 Jim Chalmers\uc2  has been giving a speech to economists in Sydney this afternoon in the wake of some generally positive wages data from the September quarter. (See earlier posts here and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/nov/13/australians-wages-increase-faster-than-inflation-for-fourth-quarter-running"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 our article }}}\uc2  here.)\par \par \uc2 Guardian Australia managed to squeeze a couple of late questions in, including about how tough a message was it to convince Australians that a recovery in real wages would help households (when a similar message by incumbent US Democrats failed to cut through)\par \par \uc2 Chalmers, after all, had just shown this chart to the audience, and the red bits are some ways off making up for the black bits (which began prior to the Albanese government taking office in May 2022.\par \par \uc2 enltrReal wages are back in the positive - but have some ways to make up for the run of negative quarters. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/hzWRgfO20w"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/hzWRgfO20w }}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 @phannam.bsky.social (@p_hannam) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/p_hannam/status/1856541764779422083?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 13, 2024 }}}\par \par \uc2 Chalmers said the September quarter numbers were \u8220\'20\'1Ca great outcome\u8221\'20\'1D, and the four quarters in a row of gains in real terms \u8220\'20\'1Cvery, very encouraging\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Real wages growth is one of the things I focus most on. I read a lot of stuff over a period of time that said you couldn\u8217\'20\'19t have strong wages growth and have inflation moderating. We\u8217\'20\'19ve shown that\u8217\'20\'19s possible. We\u8217\'20\'19ve shown it\u8217\'20\'19s happening.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere is not a whiff of a wage-price spiral in our economy, and that\u8217\'20\'19s a good thing,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, adding that \u8220\'20\'1Cwe\u8217\'20\'19ve got a lot of ground to make up\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Guess we\u8217\'20\'19ll learn soon enough whether the electorate thinks the current government is more likely to deliver on-going real wage growth \u8211\'20\'13 or the coalition.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.52am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.09am GMT\par \par \uc2 Nurses reject pay offer\par \par \uc2 Striking nurses have rejected an offer to fund their requested pay rise in return for delaying the rollout of their \u8220\'20\'1Cbig ask\u8221\'20\'1D for more staff, NSW\u8217\'20\'19s health minister says.\par \par \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports, hundreds of surgeries are expected to be postponed as more than 50,000 nurses and midwives campaign for better pay.\par \par \uc2 The 24-hour strike across NSW public hospitals began at 7.30am on Wednesday, with nurses and midwives calling for a one-off pay bump of 15% after large wage increases for teachers, paramedics and, most recently, police officers.\par \par \uc2 The strike is estimated to force the postponement of 600 to 700 surgeries, according to \uc2 NSW Health\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Emergency department wait times are also expected to blow out and people with non-life threatening conditions are urged to contact Healthdirect before arriving at hospitals.\par \par \uc2 Minimal, life-preserving staffing will be maintained in all public hospitals and health services during the 24-hour strike.\par \par \uc2 The health minister, Ryan Park, said he was disappointed the union walked away from its commitment to negotiate before the Industrial Relations Commission:\par \par \uc2 We honoured our end of the bargain \u8230\'20\'26 there was an expectation, though, that this industrial action was not going to take place.\par \par \uc2 The government delivered the union\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cbig ask\u8221\'20\'1D to begin the rollout of staffing ratios despite the billion-dollar cost, but the sought pay rise was not possible to implement in a single year, Park said.\par \par \uc2 The union rejected an offer for the 15% increase to be provided over multiple years and for a slightly larger boost for early career nurses, the health minister said.\par \par \uc2 But the union said the government had not entered negotiations willing to use extra money to fund pay increases.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/nov/13/nsw-nurses-strike-pay-salary-minns-government-police-wage-increase"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Very clearly gendered\u8217\'20\'19: NSW nurses and midwives furious over pay offer disparity ahead of 24-hour strike }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.17am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.00am GMT\par \par \uc2 Many thanks for joining me on the blog today, Josh Taylor will be here to take you through the rest of our rolling coverage. Take care.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.50am GMT\par \par \uc2 The Bureau of Meteorology has provided an updated severe thunderstorm warning for eastern NSW.\par \par \uc2 It said severe thunderstorms are likely to produce damaging winds, large hailstones and heavy rainfall that may lead to flash flooding over the next several hours \u8211\'20\'13 affecting Grafton, Batemans Bay, Tenterfield, Moree, Parkes and Maclean.\par \par \uc2 3cm large hailstones were recorded at Parkes around 1pm, it said, while 24.4mm of rain was recorded at Jerangle in the 30 minutes to 12:16pm.\par \par \uc2 enltr??UPDATED Severe Thunderstorm Warning issued for many parts of central and eastern {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/NewSouthWales?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #NewSouthWales }}}\uc2 . Most recent update extends right to the NE NSW coast.\par \par \uc2 DAMAGING WINDS, LARGE HAILSTONES and HEAVY RAINFALL possible.\par \par \uc2 Details: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/tYiUXby2yr"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/tYiUXby2yr }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/AiYz6kCN5U"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/AiYz6kCN5U }}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Bureau of Meteorology, New South Wales (@BOM_NSW) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BOM_NSW/status/1856544205394284814?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 13, 2024 }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.28am GMT\par \par \uc2 Steggall says Labor and Coalition must disclose what 2035 target they will each take to next election\par \par \uc2 At Cop29, UK prime minister \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  will pledge to cut emissions by 81% compared with 1990 levels by 2035, a target in line with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/article/2024/jul/18/labour-must-ramp-up-renewable-energy-to-meet-2030-climate-vows-says-watchdog"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the recommendations of the Climate Change Committee }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2024/nov/11/keir-starmer-to-unveil-ambitious-new-uk-climate-goal-cop29-ndc"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Keir Starmer to unveil ambitious new UK climate goal at Cop29 }}}\par \par \uc2 Independent MP for Warringah, \uc2 Zali Steggall\uc2 , welcomed the move in a statement, and said:\par \par \uc2 The world is on track for 3.1 degrees of warming if nations do not increase their commitments to reduce emissions. The Albanese government and the Coalition must come clean with the Australian public and announce what 2035 target they will take to the next election. Australians deserve to know whether they are fair dinkum about acting on climate risk or pandering to the fossil fuel industry.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.54am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.24am GMT\par \par \uc2 Australian Fashion Week future uncertain as IMG abandons event\par \par \uc2 The Australian Fashion Council (AFC) has responded to news that IMG will abandon Australian Fashion Week after nearly 20 years, leaving its future uncertain.\par \par \uc2 IMG made the announcement after owning and operating AFW since 2005. The AFC said the event provided \u8220\'20\'1Ca critical platform for Australian designers to showcase their talent and innovation, and [positioned] Australia as a key player on the global fashion stage.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 AFC chair Marianne Perkovic and CEO Jaana Quaintance-James issued a joint statement on the decision, and said:\par \par \uc2 [AFC] has learnt today of IMG\u8217\'20\'19s decision to not proceed with Australian Fashion Week (AFW) in 2025 \u8230\'20\'26 Turning to the future, we know we need a platform to profile and celebrate Australian fashion.\par \par \uc2 This moment signals an opportunity for the industry to reimagine and reshape how Australian fashion is represented, and provides us all with an opportunity to explore new ways in which to showcase emerging and established Australian talent.\par \par \uc2 The AFC, as the peak body for fashion and textiles in Australia, will lead this conversation. We will work with members, government and other stakeholders to consider the future platform needed to position and elevate Australian fashion internationally.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.33am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.20am GMT\par \par \uc2 Master Builders welcomes new national productivity fund to boost productivity\par \par \uc2 Master Builders Australia\uc2  says it strongly supports the announcement by treasurer \uc2 Jim Chalmers\uc2  today of a new national productivity fund to boost competition and productivity across the economy.\par \par \uc2 Paul Karp had {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/13/australia-news-live-average-age-for-first-porn-viewing-reduces-by-two-years-nsw-nurses-to-begin-strike?page=with:block-6733288d8f08d0e6c0683aaf#block-6733288d8f08d0e6c0683aaf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 all the details on this earlier in the blog, here }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a statement, \uc2 Master Builders Australia\uc2  CEO \uc2 Denita Wawn\uc2  said the advocacy group had consistently called for incentives to support reform and lift productivity in the building and construction industry \u8211\'20\'13 with labour productivity down 18% over the last decade.\par \par \uc2 Productivity is more than a buzzword. When productivity is down, prices go up and our ability to build the homes and surrounding infrastructure communities need is slowed down.\par \par \uc2 We thank the treasurer for putting productivity back on the national agenda and listening to our concerns around barriers to building.\par \par \uc2 Master Builders analysis of build times found that 15 years ago, it took on average 9 months to build a stand-a-alone house from approval to completion, while today it now takes 12.7 months \u8211\'20\'13 an increase of over 40%.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.08am GMT\par \par \uc2 Severe thunderstorm warnings for parts of NSW, Queensland and Victoria\par \par \uc2 There are currently three severe weather warnings in place for NSW, Queensland and Victoria. The Bureau of Meteorology has alerted:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Severe thunderstorms are likely to cause heavy rainfall that may lead to flash flooding over the next several hours in parts of the NSW south coast, southern tablelands and Snowy Mountains regions.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 In Queensland, scattered thunderstorms are set to develop along the seat, with severe thunderstorms expected over the south-east into the afternoon and evening. This is likely to produce large hailstones, damaging winds and heavy rainfall that may lead to flash flooding.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 And in Victoria, severe thunderstorms are likely to produce heavy rainfall and could lead to flash flooding around Buchan, Gelantipy and Bonang.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the NSW SES has issued a \u8220\'20\'1Cmonitor conditions\u8221\'20\'1D alert for Parkes and Peak Hill amid the forecast heavy rain, damaging winds and large hailstones.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.10am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.02am GMT\par \par \uc2 Brumbies cull wins approval in NSW, but rift remains\par \par \uc2 A multi-party report backing the counting and shooting of feral horses in national parks should put to bed \u8220\'20\'1Cridiculous anti-science questioning\u8221\'20\'1D from critics, environmental advocates say.\par \par \uc2 As \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports, the NSW parliamentary inquiry report has found the state was using global best-practice methods to count brumbies and there was no clear evidence of any animal-welfare breaches under current culling methods.\par \par \uc2 At least 5,800 \u8211\'20\'13 or an estimated one in three \u8211\'20\'13 brumbies have been killed in NSW since aerial shooting resumed in November 2023. Ecologists estimate about that number will need to be culled annually to reach a 3000-horse cap by 2027 due to high birthrates.\par \par \uc2 The inquiry broadly dismissed concerns about distance sampling being used to measure the population annually, although it welcomed the trial of new methods in the most recent count.\par \par \uc2 The Invasive Species Council advocacy director, Jack Gough, said strong cross-party support from Liberals to the Greens and independents for effective brumby control reflected public backing for native wildlife to be protected from feral horses:\par \par \uc2 This report should once and for all end the ridiculous anti-science questioning of the feral horse count figures by a small minority that do not want to see a single feral horse removed from the national park.\par \par \uc2 Committee chair and Animal Justice MP Emma Hurst hit out at the Labor, Liberal and Shooters-backed report, saying the findings and recommendations did not reflect her views. Along with Nationals MLC Wes Fang, Hurst backed a finding that aerial shooting of brumbies could not be ethically justified.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2014/aug/20/-sp-a-time-to-cull-the-battle-over-australias-brumbies"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 A time to cull? The battle over Australia\u8217\'20\'19s brumbies }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.12am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.46am GMT\par \par \uc2 Labor to make student debt key election issue with announcement in coming weeks\par \par \uc2 The federal government will make an announcement on university funding in the coming weeks, the education minister has flagged, cementing student debt as a key election issue.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to a TEQSA conference in Melbourne on Wednesday, Jason Clare reiterated that, if Labor won next year\u8217\'20\'19s election, the first piece of legislation it would introduce would be to cut student debts by 20%. But he said there was \u8220\'20\'1Cmore to do\u8221\'20\'1D to make the system fairer.\par \par \uc2 When Hecs was first created students paid an average of about 24% of the cost of degree. This increased to about 36% in the late 1990s. And now, because of the changes the previous government made, it\u8217\'20\'19s about 45%.\par \par \uc2 Cutting student debt by 20% fixes that for a generation of Australians. But there is more to do. That includes changing the way we fund universities.\par \par \uc2 Part of that is uncapping the number of places at university for students from disadvantaged backgrounds \u8230\'20\'26 part of that is a new needs-based funding system \u8230\'20\'26 and part of that is a new Australian Tertiary Education Commission (\uc2 ATEC\uc2 ) \u8230\'20\'26 to drive reform over the long term. And I hope to provide you with more detail on all of that before the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 Clare has previously stated \uc2 ATEC\uc2  would be tasked with scrapping the Coalition\u8217\'20\'19s Job Ready Graduates Scheme, which has driven arts degrees above $50,000.\par \par \uc2 enltrLabor is the party of education. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/VLUDOXxeeh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/VLUDOXxeeh }}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Jason Clare MP (@JasonClareMP) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JasonClareMP/status/1856519621894865245?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 13, 2024 }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.50am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.41am GMT\par \par \uc2 Birmingham says Israeli settlements in Gaza \u8216\'20\'18should not be undertaken\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Simon Birmingham\uc2  was asked about comments from members of the Netanyahu government, advocating the establishment of Israeli settlements in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 He responded that \u8220\'20\'1CAustralian governments, Coalition and Labor, have long been clear that settlement activity that is counter-productive to achieving longstanding settlement of issues should not be undertaken\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 That position has not changed and shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t change.\par \par \uc2 He also said the Coalition has \u8220\'20\'1Cno plans\u8221\'20\'1D to change Australian laws in regard to supplying weapons or ammunition to Israel. But if the Israeli government was to ask for help? He responded:\par \par \uc2 The structure is indeed as to how those defence export laws operate and, you know, that is the consistency that we would apply.\par \par \uc2 And with that, Birmingham\u8217\'20\'19s appearance at the \uc2 National Press Club\uc2  has wrapped up.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.52am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.34am GMT\par \par \uc2 Birmingham says decision on further tax cuts yet to be made\par \par \uc2 Simon Birmingham\uc2  was asked about comments from \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2  on the radio this morning that the Coalition was rethinking stage-three tax cuts and getting inflation under control was more important. What is the Coalition\u8217\'20\'19s stance?\par \par \uc2 He criticised the Albanese government for breaking an election promise and added:\par \par \uc2 We accepted at the time we were not going to stand in the way of Australians receiving those funds. Spend the money, they have, and I think what Peter was rightly alluding to this morning is, as it comes into the next campaign, we will have to have a look, carefully, at the state of the books as we make those final budget decisions, and where we can go in terms of further tax cuts will be one of those big key points in terms of those final budget decisions.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.40am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.31am GMT\par \par \uc2 Birmingham says there could be \u8216\'20\'18glimmers of lessons\u8217\'20\'19 in Trump\u8217\'20\'19s approach to government\par \par \uc2 Asked if he is underestimating how difficult and challenging the next four years under Trump may be, \uc2 Simon Birmingham\uc2  said he is optimistic about working with the administration and hopes to make success of it.\par \par \uc2 And yes, there are disrupters like president [Donald] Trump who is clearly bringing in to disrupt things like government efficiency in the \uc2 US government\uc2  through the role that he has given to \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t mind having a bit of disruption of efficiency in the Australian government at times either. I\u8217\'20\'19m not saying it will be the right pathway, but who knows? Maybe there will be glimmers of lessons that could be learned along the way.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.43am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.26am GMT\par \par \uc2 Birmingham says Coalition \u8216\'20\'18clear in principles\u8217\'20\'19 on Israel-Gaza war despite civilian death toll\par \par \uc2 Our own Sarah Basford Canales has asked \uc2 Simon Birmingham\uc2  a series of questions on the Coalition\u8217\'20\'19s response to the conflict in Gaza:\par \par \uc2 Is there a red line for the opposition in terms of that death toll continuing to rise by the thousands in the coming months and potentially years? Does the opposition still think this is the best way to peace and a two-state solution in the situation? And what would a Dutton government do differently if it were to be elected next election?\par \par \uc2 Birmingham answered the last question first and said the Coalition\u8217\'20\'19s view is that any prospect of a two-state solution should be \u8220\'20\'1Cfully negotiated, and negotiated by settling questions of borders, of security guarantees and rights of return, but also that we should be engaging in what other regional processes and pathways might be possible \u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He said the Coalition is \u8220\'20\'1Cresolute in terms of our position around Israel\u8217\'20\'19s right to self-defence\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 We equally want to see the tragic loss of life come to an end and for a breakthrough point. But you can see by statements out of Qatar just in the last week the frustration that exists in relation to \uc2 Hamas\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 unwillingness to release hostages, to lay down arms and to stop using Palestinian civilians as human shields in this conflict.\par \par \uc2 Is the civilian death toll a consideration that might affect the Coalition\u8217\'20\'19s resoluteness on that position? Birmingham replied:\par \par \uc2 The civilian death toll is something that pains absolutely all of us and, of course, it is a tragedy in any and all conflict and a very significant tragedy in this one, but we\u8217\'20\'19re clear in terms of the principles we stand by and the outcomes we ultimately seek.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.49am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.20am GMT\par \par \uc2 Birmingham says Coalition aiming to deter invasion of Taiwan and be \u8216\'20\'18principled and predictable with China\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 One reporter asked if the Coalition is elected at the next election and faces a \u8220\'20\'1Chorror scenario\u8221\'20\'1D of an invasion of Taiwan, would it move to defend Taiwan if attacked by China?\par \par \uc2 Simon Birmingham\uc2  responded that \u8220\'20\'1Cyou\u8217\'20\'19re taking us forward quite a few steps in that regard\u8221\'20\'1D, but added:\par \par \uc2 Principled and predictable is the framework that I\u8217\'20\'19ve outlined for how our engagement with China should be and should operate \u8230\'20\'26 [and] part of that is that we would not wish to see any unilateral change to Taiwan and its circumstances and not wish to see an invasion of the sort that you\u8217\'20\'19ve characterised. And clearly it \u8230\'20\'26 should be entirely predictable to Beijing that Australia would condemn such action, oppose such action and, indeed, do everything we can to deter such action from occurring in the first place.\par \par \uc2 Birmingham said that if an invasion occurred \u8220\'20\'1Cwhat the nature of conflict is, what role the US or others undertake, are all unknowns before we get to the question of how Australia would respond\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Our prime job is to deter it in the first place and to work with our partners to try to deter that from happening in the first place.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.27am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.14am GMT\par \par \uc2 Birmingham says he has tried to keep an open mind over whether \uc2 Kevin Rudd\uc2  stays as ambassador\par \par \uc2 Q: If the president-elect in the last year has referred to our ambassador in the US as \u8220\'20\'1Cnot the brightest bulb\u8221\'20\'1D, is it really tenable for that person to be at the forefront of our engagement in Washington?\par \par \uc2 Simon Birmingham\uc2  responded:\par \par \uc2 The president-elect has just named a secretary of state who had some pretty sharp things to say about him, but who ultimately campaigned alongside him. He\u8217\'20\'19s been elected alongside a vice-president who had very sharp and pointed things to say about him, but who he chose to make his running mate. So I think we\u8217\'20\'19ve got to be conscious of keeping an open mind, which is what we have tried to do in relation to the question around Kevin.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.18am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.13am GMT\par \par \uc2 Birmingham says Coalition does not want to politicise \uc2 Kevin Rudd\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s role\par \par \uc2 Simon Birmingham\uc2  was asked to respond to the tweet from senior Trump adviser Dan Scavino overnight, suggesting time was running out for \uc2 Kevin Rudd\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s tenure as ambassador.\par \par \uc2 You can {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/13/australia-news-live-average-age-for-first-porn-viewing-reduces-by-two-years-nsw-nurses-to-begin-strike?page=with:block-6733caa18f084e2f4426969e#block-6733caa18f084e2f4426969e"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 read more on this earlier in the blog, here }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Birmingham was asked for the Coalition\u8217\'20\'19s position on Rudd\u8217\'20\'19s tenure, and whether he should stand aside amid his now-deleted commentary on Trump?\par \par \uc2 He responded that \u8220\'20\'1Cwhat we have here is a need for Australia not to be debating the who, but the what, and the outcomes and the effectiveness of getting those outcomes\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2  and I have been very clear that we have wanted to see \uc2 Kevin Rudd\uc2  succeed all along. We have acknowledged and praised some of his successes, acknowledged his work in getting the export trade restrictions eased between Australia and the US and big Aukus breakthroughs that have been really important in that regard. And we want to see that success continue and that\u8217\'20\'19s what matters in Australia\u8217\'20\'19s national interest.\par \par \uc2 Now, ambassador Rudd and the prime minister are the ones who are in the box seat to best make the assessment in coming weeks or months about how effectively he is going to be able to continue to have the influence and get the outcomes that Australia needs.\par \par \uc2 Birmingham said the Coalition was not seeking to politicise the role. In terms of the tweet from Scavino specifically, he said:\par \par \uc2 A significant member of the incoming president\u8217\'20\'19s campaign team posted that. What we are to make of the image, well, I think I\u8217\'20\'19ll leave it for others to comment on the image. I\u8217\'20\'19ve spoken to the substance.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.20am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.08am GMT\par \par \uc2 Birmingham says climate ambition must not end in broken promises\par \par \uc2 On the climate, \uc2 Simon Birmingham\uc2  sought to reinforce that the upcoming election would be about how Australia gets to net zero \u8211\'20\'13 not if.\par \par \uc2 That that is a really important message to drive home to those Pacific nations, that the construct of debate in this country is focused on actually achieving that \u8230\'20\'26 That\u8217\'20\'19s how we need to really make sure we take a careful and thoughtful approach to framing that election contest.\par \par \uc2 Speaking about Paris agreements, Birmingham said \u8220\'20\'1Cbeing upfront about what is achievable in the time horizons that are there\u8221\'20\'1D is important.\par \par \uc2 It is no more helpful \u8211\'20\'13 in fact, arguably it\u8217\'20\'19s more counter-productive \u8211\'20\'13 to be in a situation where targets are made but not realised and you\u8217\'20\'19ve broken your promise to nations, than it is to have been realistic in the first place.\par \par \uc2 And of course we want to stretch to maximum ambition, and I\u8217\'20\'19ve been clear about my desire to see maximum ambition at all times, but it\u8217\'20\'19s not going to go down well if maximum ambition simply translates into broken promises.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.17am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.05am GMT\par \par \uc2 Birmingham says Australia still \u8216\'20\'18a few steps away\u8217\'20\'19 from being interlocutor between US and China\par \par \uc2 Back at the \uc2 National Press Club\uc2 , \uc2 Simon Birmingham\uc2  has been taking questions from reporters.\par \par \uc2 He was asked about comments from the prime minister today that Australia is a trusted middle power that could help manage the strategic competition between the US and China. Does he agree with this, or is it a naive view and great powers will just do what great powers do?\par \par \uc2 Birmingham replied that \u8220\'20\'1CAustralia should always seek to play as constructive a role as we possibly can\u8221\'20\'1D:\par \par \uc2 But I think at this point of change and contest we also need to be realistic about the challenges that we face.\par \par \uc2 He said when it comes to the Trump administration in the US there are immediate priorities to address, including Aukus and tariffs.\par \par \uc2 Beyond those bilateral points of engagement, there will be ways in which we want to seek to encourage the US as to how they engage in our region. Active engagement remains critical and important across particularly the Asean nations, and no doubt there will be times for discussion about the nature of their engagement with China and the implications of that with other nations and on the global economy.\par \par \uc2 But are we going to step up and be able to fulfil some situation that is foreseeable as a significant interlocutor between the two? I think we\u8217\'20\'19re a few steps away from that at this point in time.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.59am GMT\par \par \uc2 Tasmanian independent labels funding for Maugean skate as \u8216\'20\'18self-serving handout\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Breaking away from the \uc2 National Press Club\uc2 : independent MP \uc2 Andrew Wilkie\uc2  has labelled government funding aimed at saving the endangered Maugean skate as an \u8220\'20\'1Cexpensive and self-serving handout\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, Anthony Albanese announced a $28m boost for new measures in Macquarie harbour, including scaling up oxygenation to offset the effects of human activities on the Maugean skate.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/12/australia-news-live-qantas-faces-inquiry-over-use-of-airport-slots-chalmers-warns-nobody-wins-from-a-trade-war?page=with:block-6732a4558f088fa22a5be045#block-6732a4558f088fa22a5be045"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 During his second press conference of the day }}}\uc2 , protesters were heard chanting various slogans including what sounded like \u8220\'20\'1Csave the Maugean skate\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In a post to X today, Wilkie \u8211\'20\'13 the federal member for Clark in Tasmania \u8211\'20\'13 said:\par \par \uc2 $28m to Macquarie harbour is just an expensive and self-serving handout to try and win Braddon, while at the same time kowtowing to Labor\u8217\'20\'19s mates in the salmon industry. Again, the loser is the imperilled Maugean skate.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/article/2024/aug/15/tasmania-salmon-industry-macquarie-harbour-maugean-skate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Salmon industry in key Tasmanian location should be cut to save Maugean skate, scientists advise government }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.37am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.53am GMT\par \par \uc2 Simon Birmingham\uc2  argued that the United Nations is \u8220\'20\'1Cfailing to meet its charter to maintain international peace and security\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 With the \uc2 UN\uc2  paralysed, effective peace-keeping initiatives no longer enjoy international momentum. To avoid a repeat of the world wars of last century, deterrence and diplomacy must be more effective today than appeasement was then.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.51am GMT\par \par \uc2 Birmingham says its more important than ever China act with \u8216\'20\'18great responsibility\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 On China, \uc2 Simon Birmingham\uc2  said Australia\u8217\'20\'19s approach should be \u8220\'20\'1Cboth principled and predictable\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Ideally, China would respect international rulings on maritime boundaries and its forces would not engage in acts of aggression or actions that risk miscalculation.\par \par \uc2 Ideally, China would use its influence and relations with countries like Russia, Iran and North Korea to stem the rise in conflict, not enable it.\par \par \uc2 Ideally, China\u8217\'20\'19s systems would operate with integrity and transparency, whether in matters of cyber security, or market competitiveness.\par \par \uc2 Australia\u8217\'20\'19s position on issues like these should be of no surprise.\par \par \uc2 As China continues its growth, including the rapid growth of its nuclear armed military capabilities, it becomes more important than ever that this great power act with great responsibility.\par \par \uc2 Birmingham welcomed China\u8217\'20\'19s decision to restore ministerial level dialogue with Australia and said it was \u8220\'20\'1Ccounterproductive of them to have chosen to cease it in the first place.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He said this dialogue should continue, but later noted \u8220\'20\'1Csuch ambitions for cooperation may be thwarted if the current trajectory of global conflict continues.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.46am GMT\par \par \uc2 Birmingham said no reason to \u8216\'20\'18catastrophise\u8217\'20\'19 over second Trump presidency\par \par \uc2 Moving to a second \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  presidency, \uc2 Simon Birmingham\uc2  labelled him a \u8220\'20\'1Cdisrupter\u8221\'20\'1D who is \u8220\'20\'1Cprone to pursuing less conventional policies and making bolder pronouncements\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 but said this is not a reason to \u8220\'20\'1Ccatastrophise\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The Turnbull and Morrison governments demonstrated how to work with a Trump administration. There are opportunities in the second Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 He said Aukus must play a key role into the future, and for the case to be made to Trump as follows:\par \par \uc2 No other US ally in this region is investing to expand their defence capability like Australia is. No other US ally is investing directly in US defence industrial capabilities like Australia is. And no other US ally is investing to build defence industrial capabilities that complement those of the US like Australia is \u8230\'20\'26 Prime Minister [Anthony] Albanese should be making [these] points to President Trump in person, at the earliest opportunity.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.23am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.44am GMT\par \par \uc2 Birmingham wants formal trilateral cooperation between India, Indonesia and Australia\par \par \uc2 Simon Birmingham\uc2  says the contest for influence in the Pacific is symbolic of a greater global contest, and that the world we live in today \u8220\'20\'1Cis profoundly different from the established order that I, or most of us, grew up with\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He said trilateral cooperation between India, Indonesia and Australia should be sought \u8220\'20\'1Cto secure the open maritime domain that we all rely upon, the security we desire, and the growth we all seek\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The significant roles India, Indonesia and Australia play within our region, coupled with our different alliances and partnerships, can aid our ability to navigate and shape the contested world we face.\par \par \uc2 Such a minilateral partnership can complement our work in the Quad, and other partnerships, especially our relations across our fellow Asean nations.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.46am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.40am GMT\par \par \uc2 Simon Birmingham\uc2  addresses \uc2 National Press Club\par \par \uc2 The shadow foreign affairs minister, \uc2 Simon Birmingham\uc2 , is addressing the \uc2 National Press Club\uc2  in Canberra today, about preserving Australia\u8217\'20\'19s interests in a contested world.\par \par \uc2 He has been outlining work done by previous leaders throughout the decades to secure Australia\u8217\'20\'19s standing, and said that \u8220\'20\'1Cnowhere is the creation of a partisan distinction in foreign policy \u8230\'20\'26 more counterproductive than the Pacific\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Birmingham said the contest for Pacific influence and engagement was \u8220\'20\'1Centrenched\u8221\'20\'1D:\par \par \uc2 China\u8217\'20\'19s model of elite capture and less regulated funding is enticing to some. In contrast, Australia\u8217\'20\'19s ethical, values-based approach is better policy but harder politics.\par \par \uc2 To advance our interests, we must be at one in promoting the bipartisanship of Australia\u8217\'20\'19s investment in \u8211\'20\'13 and respect for \u8211\'20\'13 Pacific Island nations, their sovereignty and their people.\par \par \uc2 He said the singular message of Australian leaders to Pacific leaders \u8220\'20\'1Cmust be one of enduring and reliable partnership, above all politics\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.18am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.38am GMT\par \par \uc2 Faith leaders express concern about Queensland LNP move to abolish truth-telling inquiry\par \par \uc2 Chrisian and Muslim faith leaders in Queensland say they have \u8220\'20\'1Cprofound concerns\u8221\'20\'1D about the new LNP government\u8217\'20\'19s call to abolish the state\u8217\'20\'19s First Nations Truth-telling and Healing Inquiry.\par \par \uc2 A statement from 13 religious leaders \u8211\'20\'13 including from the Catholic and Anglican churches, and the Council of Imams \u8211\'20\'13 acknowledges that faith-based organisations \u8220\'20\'1Care historically complicit\u8221\'20\'1D in the suffering of First Nations people \u8220\'20\'1Cincluding in the way that churches have collaborated with the state\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The premier, David Crisafulli, had promised before the state election to end the inquiry. The new government has since directed it to end its hearings, including sessions planned in remote communities in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 The church leaders\u8217\'20\'19 statement says the inquiry would be vital for listening and recording stories and \u8220\'20\'1Ccollating evidence to shine a light on our past\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Before abolishing this independent inquiry, we believe the new state government needs to clarify to all Queenslanders what will be implemented in its place \u8230\'20\'26 noting the advanced age and physical frailty of many Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander inquiry contributors. How all Queenslanders will be able to heal and move forward together \u8211\'20\'13 with dignity and respect \u8211\'20\'13 and understand our shared history in all its challenges and richness.\par \par \uc2 In the midst of the tragic impacts of displacement, dispossession, the forcible removal of children and policies of assimilation \u8230\'20\'26 there are also stories of appreciation, reconciliation and triumph in adversity. All these stories need to continue to be heard.\par \par \uc2 We believe Queensland remains ready to hear the experiences of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and that in the sharing of stories that a more compassionate and resilient Queensland will emerge.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.41am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.33am GMT\par \par \uc2 Public schools will \u8216\'20\'18never reach\u8217\'20\'19 full funding under proposed forms, senate inquiry hears\par \par \uc2 Public schools will \u8220\'20\'1Cnever reach\u8221\'20\'1D full funding under the federal government\u8217\'20\'19s proposed reforms, a senate inquiry has heard.\par \par \uc2 Labor\u8217\'20\'19s bill allows the commonwealth to provide extra funding to public schools in excess of 20%, making the figure a floor, not a ceiling. States and territories front 75%, leaving a 5% gap.\par \par \uc2 All jurisdictions except Victoria, NSW and Queensland have agreed to Labor\u8217\'20\'19s proposal to bring its contribution 2.5% above the floor to 22.5%, leaving the remaining 2.5% gap to be filled by the states by 2029.\par \par \uc2 But peak bodies including Save Our Schools, the Isolated Children\u8217\'20\'19s Parents\u8217\'20\'19 Association and the Australian Secondary Principals\u8217\'20\'19 Association told the enquiry the federal government should lift its funding floor to 25% and remove \u8220\'20\'1Caccounting tricks\u8221\'20\'1D in the bill.\par \par \uc2 National Convenor of Save Our Schools, Trevor Cobbold, said even with a 5% increase in funding public schools wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t reach the dream of Gonski, citing a 4% loophole retained in the agreement which allows jurisdictions to claim funding on non-school expenditures like capital depreciation.\par \par \uc2 18 years after Gonski public schools still won\u8217\'20\'19t be funded according to need. It\u8217\'20\'19s a devastating outcome.\par \par \uc2 President of the Australian Secondary Principals\u8217\'20\'19 Association, Andy Mison said the body was also concerned about the 4%, arguing schools would \u8220\'20\'1Cnever reach\u8221\'20\'1D 100% funding if it were retained.\par \par \uc2 I think it\u8217\'20\'19s appalling. Public schools \u8230\'20\'26 bear the brunt of social inequities and are increasingly being asked to do more with less \u8230\'20\'26 the concentration of disadvantage is increasing. We urge greater ambition... I don\u8217\'20\'19t think it passes the pub test to include capital depreciation\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.49am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.25am GMT\par \par \uc2 More on the wages figures\par \par \uc2 As always, some industries reported higher wage increases than others. Perhaps surprisingly, the highest quarterly increase was 2.5% for those in accommodation and food services \u8211\'20\'13 a sector that might be considered \u8220\'20\'1Cdiscretionary\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Financial and insurance services posted a 0.5% quarterly rise, or the least of all industries.\par \par \uc2 At an annual rate, those in electricity, gas and water posted the largest increase at 5%, while (sadly for some close to this blog) arts and recreation lagged other industries with a 2.9% rise.\par \par \uc2 And, as for the different regions of this great browned-off land, Queensland posted the largest quarterly wage rise at 1.6% (though it seemed to do the Labour government there no favours). The ACT, with all its public servants, managed a 1% rise \u8211\'20\'13 or the least among the jurisdictions.\par \par \uc2 For annual increases, Tassie was the place to be, with wages up 4%. At the other end of the country, the Northern Territory posted the smallest annual rises at 3%.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ll return to the workers\u8217\'20\'19 tale roughly this time tomorrow, when we get October labour market numbers. \uc2 CBA\uc2  reckons the economy added 20,000 jobs last month, or not quite enough to stop the jobless rate ticking higher to 4.2% (from 4.1% in September).\par \par \uc2 Watch out for those numbers then on your favourite blog (yes, this one).\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.38am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.11am GMT\par \par \uc2 Coles\uc2  to launch \u8216\'20\'18AI-powered\u8217\'20\'19 trolley\par \par \uc2 Coles\uc2  is launching a trial of an \u8220\'20\'1CAI-powered\u8221\'20\'1D smart trolley that will allow customers to scan and pay all from their trolley beginning in 2025.\par \par \uc2 The tech will be first trialled at Coles\u8217\'20\'19 Richmond Traders store in Melbourne from January, with customers able to learn how to use the trolleys from today until the trial commences.\par \par \uc2 The trolley has sensors, an in-built scale to scan groceries including fresh produce, and a digital screen, which shows aisle locations and specials.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.17am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.57am GMT\par \par \uc2 Severe storms in parts of Queensland and NSW\par \par \uc2 The Bureau of Meteorology has published a severe weather update, as thunderstorms are forecast across the east today:\par \par \uc2 enltrSevere Weather Update: Severe thunderstorms for south-east Qld and north-east NSW today.\par \par \uc2 Video current as of 11:00 am AEDT 13 November 2024.\par \par \uc2 Latest forecasts and warnings: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/4W35o8iFmh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/4W35o8iFmh }}}\uc2  or the BOM Weather app. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/zr6r7DF1G0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/zr6r7DF1G0 }}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Bureau of Meteorology, Australia (@BOM_au) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BOM_au/status/1856501392770511332?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 13, 2024 }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.01am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.40am GMT\par \par \uc2 More on the latest wage numbers\par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank should be content with these wage numbers as they have come in slightly on the lower side of expectations for both the annual and quarterly figures.\par \par \uc2 Stocks have pared their losses for the day a bit (a sign that investors were a bit more hopeful of a near-term interest rate cut), while the Australian dollar is hovering a bit above 65.2 US cents.\par \par \uc2 The easing in wage price increases were also matched across both the public and private sectors. Wages for the former were up a seasonally adjusted 3.7%, the least since the September quarter of 2022.\par \par \uc2 For the private sector (which is about four in five jobs), wages were up 3.5%, or matching the increase in the September quarter of 2023.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.39am GMT\par \par \uc2 Pocock to oppose misinformation and disinformation bill unless there are \u8216\'20\'18major changes\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Independent senator David Pocock says he will be opposing the government\u8217\'20\'19s misinformation and disinformation bill \u8220\'20\'1Cunless major changes are made to the legislation\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In the video {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NbPVb8qn7D8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 posted to YouTube }}}\uc2 , Pocock said that as it stands, \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t believe it takes the right approach to dealing with misinformation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Freedom of speech and the freedom to protest are critical parts of our democracy. And in Australia, we don\u8217\'20\'19t have an overarching Human Rights Act that entrenches these rights. So we need to tread very carefully whenever we make changes that may impact on them.\par \par \uc2 He said that Labor and Liberal governments have cracked down on the right to protest at a state level and while misinformation and disinformation causes harm, \u8220\'20\'1Cpeople are also entitled to their opinion, whatever it may be.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Pocock argued the issue is not so much with individuals spreading misinformation but the social media algorithms that boost this content \u8211\'20\'13 so \u8220\'20\'1CI find it very strange that the government wants to trust these giant corporations who have at best very questionable reputations with deciding what is or isn\u8217\'20\'19t mis and disinformation\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He also criticised the government for delaying changes to the Privacy Act, which he argued would go further to protecting Australians online.\par \par \uc2 The truth is really hard to define and often changes over time, so there\u8217\'20\'19s a risk platforms could just end up unintentionally censoring people.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.36am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.31am GMT\par \par \uc2 Australian wages rose faster than inflation for fourth quarter in a row\par \par \uc2 The wage price index for the September quarter has come in at 3.5% compared with a year earlier, the ABS has just reported. That figure compared with the 3.6% pace expected by economists and the 4.1% clip reported by the ABS for the June quarter.\par \par \uc2 At that rate, wages were increasing more than the headline inflation rate as they have since the final three months of 2023. The September quarter consumer price index rose at an annual pace of 2.8%.\par \par \uc2 A similar story was true for the quarter alone, with the WPI up 0.8%, or well ahead of the 0.2% pace of CPI. Economists had tipped that to come in at 0.9% or similar to the 0.8% rate recorded by the ABS for the June quarter.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.32am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.23am GMT\par \par \uc2 Only 13% of health and medical research workforce in regional areas\par \par \uc2 An audit of the Australian health and medical research workforce shows only 13% of the workforce is based in regional, rural or remote areas.\par \par \uc2 The National Rural Health Alliance says this is insufficient to serve Australia\u8217\'20\'19s 30% rural population, with the audit also pointing to additional barriers that exist for regional researchers.\par \par \uc2 Conducted by the Department of Health and Aged Care, the audit found the health and medical research workforce is highly mobile, moving jobs every 1.6 years \u8211\'20\'13 suggesting researchers may be willing to move to rural areas.\par \par \uc2 The National Rural Health Alliance chief executive, Susi Tegen, said:\par \par \uc2 The underrepresentation of rural researchers is worrying given that rural communities face distinct health challenges that require tailored and place-based solutions \u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Funding for research does not adequately flow to the regions. Rural researchers become a secondary consideration for funding. They have difficulty accessing the same opportunities available to researchers in metropolitan locations.\par \par \uc2 The limited access to training, funding and collaborative networks in regional areas means that researchers may be left behind, and the needs of these communities may remain unmet.\par \par \uc2 Tegen called on the government to increase funding access, provide targeted training and development, and opportunities for collaborative networks to the regional workforce and researchers.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.25am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.12am GMT\par \par \uc2 Multilateral trade liberalisation \u8216\'20\'18right strategy for geopolitical environment\u8217\'20\'19: Leigh\par \par \uc2 Andrew Leigh\uc2  was also asked about trade with the US \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/13/australia-news-live-average-age-for-first-porn-viewing-reduces-by-two-years-nsw-nurses-to-begin-strike?page=with:block-6733d68d8f084e2f442696f3#block-6733d68d8f084e2f442696f3"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 as has been discussed much this morning }}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 following \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s threat to impose tariffs on imports. How can Australia stop that?\par \par \uc2 Leigh said \u8220\'20\'1Cthe periods of open markets have been the periods of greatest prosperity\u8221\'20\'1D in history, which was achieved \u8220\'20\'1Cnot just by doing cosy bilateral deals, but by engaging in broad multilateral reform\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 You see us supporting the entry of the UK into the Trans-Pacific Partnership, broadening out the networks through the \uc2 World Trade Organisation\uc2 . Multilateral trade liberalisation doesn\u8217\'20\'19t just work better, it\u8217\'20\'19s also the right strategy for the current geopolitical environment.\par \par \uc2 So, engaging in that with open markets is going to be what we\u8217\'20\'19ll do. You\u8217\'20\'19ve seen us broadening out the trading relationships through the Asia Pacific and dealing with some of the issues that we faced with around $20bn of trade blockages to China when we came to office now being dealt with steadily, methodically, by Don Farrell as trade minister.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.19am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.07am GMT\par \par \uc2 Andrew Leigh\uc2  says he \u8216\'20\'18absolutely\u8217\'20\'19 expected domestic airfares to increase after collapse of \uc2 Rex\par \par \uc2 The assistant competition minister, \uc2 Andrew Leigh\uc2 , was on ABC RN earlier this morning where he discussed the increase in capital city route flights.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 ACCC\uc2  has found the average domestic airfare increased by 13% after the collapse of Rex flights between capital cities. Adelaide-Melbourne jumped 95% to $296, Melbourne-Gold Coast was up 70% to $432, and Canberra-Melbourne rose 54% to $298.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/nov/12/domestic-airfares-surge-by-13-following-collapse-of-rex-flights-between-capital-cities"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Cheapest economy airfares more than doubled on some routes after collapse of Rex flights }}}\par \par \uc2 Leigh said he was \u8220\'20\'1Cabsolutely\u8221\'20\'1D expecting things to get this bad this quickly:\par \par \uc2 \u8230\'20\'26 when you move from a monopoly carrier on a route to three carriers on the route, then the price per kilometre is halved, goes down from 40 cents a kilometre to 19 cents a kilometre. So, it\u8217\'20\'19s expected and disappointing that when you had that collapse that you immediately saw prices for flyers increase.\par \par \uc2 Asked what the government would do about the growing cost of airfares until a new airline emerges as a serious third competitor, he said it would be a \u8220\'20\'1Cmatter for the commercial market\u8221\'20\'1D as to who fills that gap \u8211\'20\'13 and \u8220\'20\'1Cclearly the government is looking at creating settings that make it attractive for competitors\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re talking now about airlines, but we\u8217\'20\'19ve been talking previously about supermarkets or banks. And if you go to baby food and beer, the same challenge occurs. And that\u8217\'20\'19s why the government\u8217\'20\'19s focus on competition isn\u8217\'20\'19t just to go sector by sector, but to revitalise national competition policy in order to see the gains right across the economy.\par \par \uc2 We had more on this from treasurer \uc2 Jim Chalmers{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/13/australia-news-live-average-age-for-first-porn-viewing-reduces-by-two-years-nsw-nurses-to-begin-strike?page=with:block-6733288d8f08d0e6c0683aaf#block-6733288d8f08d0e6c0683aaf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 earlier in the blog, here }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.14am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.56pm GMT\par \par \uc2 South Australian CFA testing warning systems\par \par \uc2 The South Australian Country Fire Service says it is testing its warning system today from 10.30am to 1pm:\par \par \uc2 Please disregard any warnings issued during this time.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.50pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Melbourne police address media after alleged armed stand-off\par \par \uc2 Earlier, Melbourne police spoke to the media after an alleged armed stand-off with officers in Weir Views.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, police were monitoring a stolen vehicle when a man allegedly got out of the car and fired shots at police, who were not injured. The man then allegedly entered a house where two occupants, a man and a toddler not known to him, were inside. Police negotiated with him as the occupants left uninjured.\par \par \uc2 The man allegedly fired shots at police again, with police returning fire (which did not hit him) and deploying a police dog to assist with the arrest just after 6pm.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to the media, the officer said the man \u8211\'20\'13 a 26-year-old man from Snake Valley \u8211\'20\'13 is known to police but couldn\u8217\'20\'19t provide further details. He said the occupant was in his 30s or early 40s and is the father of the one-year-old. He added it is \u8220\'20\'1Cextremely lucky\u8221\'20\'1D that no police were injured:\par \par \uc2 It is lucky, and it is completely unacceptable that members were exposed to such dangers.\par \par \uc2 In terms of the negotiations police had with the man, police alleged:\par \par \uc2 We attempted for him to surrender and put the firearm down. We provided safe passage for that to occur. That did not occur prior to the incident unfolding.\par \par \uc2 It was two and a half hours from when the alleged offender entered the home to when he was arrested. He said the man was jumping on a trampoline at one point and \u8220\'20\'1Cthere was a period of time where he was behaving erratically in the back yard of the premises\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The officer said it is expected the alleged offender will be charged today with a range of offences including aggravated carjacking, aggravated burglary and conduct endangering life and firearms offences.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.55pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.44pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Catholic church wins case limiting liability for abuse by priests\par \par \uc2 The Catholic church has won a landmark case limiting its liability for the abuse of children by priests not under its direct employment.\par \par \uc2 Last year, the highest Victorian court delivered an unprecedented ruling that the church was vicariously responsible for the abuse of a five-year-old child, known as DP, by assistant priest Father Bryan Coffey.\par \par \uc2 The ruling was significant because Coffey, as an assistant parish priest, was not directly employed by the church.\par \par \uc2 Vicarious liability is typically used to hold employers responsible for the wrongful or negligent actions of their employees during the course of their employment, regardless of whether the employer is at fault.\par \par \uc2 The Victorian courts held that Coffey was a \u8220\'20\'1Cservant of the church\u8221\'20\'1D, despite the lack of a formal employer-employee relationship, and that his church position gave him \u8220\'20\'1Cpower and intimacy\u8221\'20\'1D to access and abuse children.\par \par \uc2 The high court rejected that ruling on Wednesday morning, finding in the church\u8217\'20\'19s favour, and declining to expand the concept of vicarious liability beyond employer-employee relationships. It said doing so would create uncertainty and indeterminacy.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.48pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.39pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Sydney Water\uc2  working to address mosquito outbreak in south-west Sydney\par \par \uc2 Sydney Water\uc2  says it has a dedicated team working to address a mosquito outbreak in south-west Sydney.\par \par \uc2 The Daily Telegraph {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dailytelegraph.com.au/news/nsw/southwest-sydney-residents-turn-to-google-to-vent-their-anger-over-growing-mosquito-plague-concerns/news-story/79dc91ceadb5494292e753949fbdac2e"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported }}}\uc2  that the infestation has been linked to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dailytelegraph.com.au/news/nsw/mosquito-plague-liverpool-council-to-clean-up-backyard-pools-sydney-water-spraying-pond/news-story/e9cdc866119b573433876c9827aba511"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Warwick Farm settling pond  }}}\uc2  owned by \uc2 Sydney Water\uc2 , and neglected pools in the area. Social media users have reported an outbreak of mozzies about the south-west, and Liverpool mayor Ned Mannoun has been providing daily updates to social media on the situation.\par \par \uc2 Sydney Water\uc2  said in a statement that it has taken immediate action, including herbicide spraying, larvicide deployment, helicopter spraying, and aquatic vegetation removal.\par \par \uc2 Operational staff and licenced contractors have deployed boats, helicopters, excavators, and other specialised machinery to clear around half of the aquatic material contributing to mosquito breeding.\par \par \uc2 These measures are starting to reduce mosquito populations around the Liverpool Water Resource Recovery Facility. \uc2 Sydney Water\uc2  staff are on the ground talking directly with customers in the impacted areas.\par \par \uc2 It said the mosquito impact is \u8220\'20\'1Cexpected to improve significantly over the next two weeks\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 enltrthey aren\u8217\'20\'19t exaggerating about the mosquito plague in SW sydney {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/FHHkIlUcrA"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/FHHkIlUcrA }}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Evan (@fictillius) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/fictillius/status/1852871453512781911?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 3, 2024 }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.44pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.11pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Abbott defends Rudd after deleted tweets about Trump\par \par \uc2 The former prime minister \uc2 Tony Abbott\uc2  has defended Australia\u8217\'20\'19s ambassador to the US \uc2 Kevin Rudd\uc2  for his {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/nov/07/australia-us-ambassador-kevin-rudd-deletes-posts-criticising-trump-after-election-win"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 now-deleted tweets about  }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/nov/07/australia-us-ambassador-kevin-rudd-deletes-posts-criticising-trump-after-election-win"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump }}}\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Among the deleted comments, Rudd had in 2020 described Trump as \u8220\'20\'1Cthe most destructive president in history\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 On {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://australiasfuture.podbean.com/e/s3e19-australia-s-future-with-tony-abbott-%e2%80%93-the-return-of-donald-trump/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an episode of his podcast Australia\u8217\'20\'19s Future with Tony Abbott }}}\uc2 , the former PM defended Rudd and said it would be \u8220\'20\'1Cunusual for our closest ally to start being prescriptive about who can and can\u8217\'20\'19t be our ambassador\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Sure, Rudd has said some injudicious things about the incoming president, but a lot of people have. A lot of people, including a lot of people on my side of politics here in Australia, have said I think injudicious and ill-advised [things] about president Trump.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19ve always taken the view that the American president is the leader of the free world. In effect he\u8217\'20\'19s our president as well as the American president. And we should be careful, very careful, about public criticism of an American president \u8211\'20\'13 or indeed an American ex-president. Certainly, if we have to disagree let\u8217\'20\'19s disagree on politics rather than the personalities.\par \par \uc2 Abbott also said that whatever you think of Rudd, \u8220\'20\'1Cyou couldn\u8217\'20\'19t say that the guy is not hyper active\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 I am confident that Kevin has been hyper active on our behalf as he sees it in Washington. So I would be surprised if there is any pressure from the Americans to change our ambassador. I have no reason to think that Kevin is not doing a good job at present. He will do whatever he humanly can to win over senior people in the incoming administration. And he\u8217\'20\'19s already done everything he humanly can to row back his previous ill-advised remarks about the incoming president.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.36pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.51pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Wages data today likely to mark one year of pay rising faster than headline inflation\par \par \uc2 Australia\u8217\'20\'19s workers should receive some good news later today with ABS data likely to show wages have risen faster than the annual headline inflation rate for four consecutive quarters.\par \par \uc2 Economists are expecting the wage price index will have risen 0.9% for the September quarter alone and 3.6% from the same quarter a year ago.\par \par \uc2 Compare that increase with the 0.2% gain in the headline consumer price index numbers for the quarter and 2.8% annual rise \u8211\'20\'13 as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/oct/30/australia-annual-inflation-rate-september-quarter-cpi-interest-rates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 we saw a couple of weeks ago }}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 and it would be a big surprise if wages were advancing at a faster clip.\par \par \uc2 The treasurer, \uc2 Jim Chalmers\uc2 , as it happens has a speech in Sydney starting about 90 minutes after the wage price index figures land at 11.30am.\par \par \uc2 In the US it has been difficult for the Biden administration (and losing Democratic presidential candidate \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  ) to get voter \u8220\'20\'1Ccut-through\u8221\'20\'1D with real income gains. So it\u8217\'20\'19ll be understandable for Chalmers to talk up any positive signs of the cost of living crunch easin, with an Australian federal election looming over the next six months.\par \par \uc2 (As we {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/27/australia-inflation-figures-cpi-rba-interest-rates-abs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 noted here recently }}}\uc2 , there are some indications of things improving.)\par \par \uc2 Anyway, stay tuned for the numbers to land here later this morning.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.03pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.36pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Wrap of commentary on US-Australia relationship this morning\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s been much talk this morning about the Australia-US relationship under a second Trump presidency, and the future of \uc2 Kevin Rudd\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s role as ambassador after his now-deleted tweets.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/nov/07/australia-us-ambassador-kevin-rudd-deletes-posts-criticising-trump-after-election-win"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Australia\u8217\'20\'19s US ambassador Kevin Rudd deletes posts criticising \u8216\'20\'18destructive\u8217\'20\'19 Trump after election win }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/13/australia-news-live-average-age-for-first-porn-viewing-reduces-by-two-years-nsw-nurses-to-begin-strike?page=with:block-6733b8698f089359e632fb6d#block-6733b8698f089359e632fb6d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 In case you missed it }}}\uc2  : opposition leader \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2  said Rudd had \u8220\'20\'1Can incredible work ethic, but he\u8217\'20\'19s made disparaging comments, and that\u8217\'20\'19s an issue for for the government to deal with\u8221\'20\'1D. Dutton said:\par \par \uc2 Following [Rudd\u8217\'20\'19s] term, that\u8217\'20\'19s an issue for the government of the day to decide who would be in that role. I think \uc2 Joe Hockey\uc2 , for example, did a great job when he was ambassador.\par \par \uc2 Australia\u8217\'20\'19s former ambassador to the US \uc2 Arthur Sinodinos\uc2  described Trump as a \u8220\'20\'1Ctransactional figure\u8221\'20\'1D and suggested he would be focused on the relationship going forward and \u8220\'20\'1Cit would not be a good look to just suddenly yank the ambassador out.\u8221\'20\'1D You can {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/13/australia-news-live-average-age-for-first-porn-viewing-reduces-by-two-years-nsw-nurses-to-begin-strike?page=with:block-6733bfc68f084e2f4426964e#block-6733bfc68f084e2f4426964e"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 read more earlier in the blog, here. }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/13/australia-news-live-average-age-for-first-porn-viewing-reduces-by-two-years-nsw-nurses-to-begin-strike?page=with:block-6733caa18f084e2f4426969e#block-6733caa18f084e2f4426969e"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 As we flagged just earlier }}}\uc2 , a senior Trump adviser has posted a gif of an hourglass in response to Rudd\u8217\'20\'19s congratulation message to the incoming president.\par \par \uc2 And as \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports, Anthony Albanese said he remained optimistic about the trading relationship under a Trump presidency, telling ABC Radio Sydney that he and the president-elect discussed trade in their first phone call.\par \par \uc2 I pointed out the United States has a trade surplus with Australia so it\u8217\'20\'19s in the United States\u8217\'20\'19 interest that they trade fairly with Australia. The United States is a major investor here in Australia [and] that investment creates economic activity and creates jobs.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.51pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.24pm GMT\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Nurses and midwives are more than disappointed with this government\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NSW Nurses and Midwives Association\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s secretary, Shaye Candish, has spoken with ABC News Breakfast amid the strike action, which began this morning.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/nov/13/nsw-nurses-strike-pay-salary-minns-government-police-wage-increase"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Very clearly gendered\u8217\'20\'19: NSW nurses and midwives furious over pay offer disparity ahead of 24-hour strike }}}\par \par \uc2 The union has been negotiating with the state government since April, with the government not budging on a blanket 10.5% pay rise offered over three years for all NSW public-sector workers. Candish said nurses and midwives were asking for 15% and \u8220\'20\'1Cit comes at a time when Queensland and Victoria are paid at least 18% above nurses here in NSW\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Nurses and midwives are more than disappointed with this government. This government committed to sit down in good faith and negotiate on pay and conditions for nurses and midwives, but has not happened \u8230\'20\'26 We have been bleeding nurses and midwives and that has been part of the problem.\par \par \uc2 After the strike ends tomorrow morning, Candish said she hopes \u8220\'20\'1Cthe government comes to the table and finds a way to resolve this issue and put new money on the table\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 That is ultimately where we hope this goes but should that not be the case, our members are determined to continue undertaking action. It will not stop until we are in a situation where members can actually have parity with other states.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.33pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.16pm GMT\par \par \uc2 School anti-vaping program will teach \u8216\'20\'18how to say no\u8217\'20\'19 and to recognise addiction in friends\par \par \uc2 Continuing from our last post, \uc2 Mark Butler\uc2  said kids often vaped thinking it wasn\u8217\'20\'19t harmful:\par \par \uc2 We know from our research is that teenagers think vaping is harmless. They think smoking\u8217\'20\'19s bad, and they think vaping is a relatively harmless product. \u8230\'20\'26 The real facts about vaping [is there are] 200 very harmful chemicals in them.\par \par \uc2 But also it\u8217\'20\'19s a pathway to smoking \u8230\'20\'26 that\u8217\'20\'19s important.\par \par \uc2 The program goes on to teach them how to say no effectively, how to recognise addiction in friends who might be addicted to vapes and to talk to them about it. It really is an important sort of socialisation about not just how to recognise risky behaviour or risky products like vapes or alcohol or drugs for that matter, but also how to go through the process of resisting peer pressure, resisting temptation, and taking the healthy choice.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/oct/25/young-people-think-vapes-arent-harmful-because-they-are-so-easy-to-buy-study-finds"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Young people think vapes aren\u8217\'20\'19t harmful because they are so easy to buy, study finds }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.38pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.12pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Anti-vaping program for schools to begin in 2025\par \par \uc2 A new anti-vaping program for teenagers will be run in high schools across Australia next year.\par \par \uc2 The health minister, \uc2 Mark Butler\uc2 , gave more details about the program the government in launching today on ABC Brisbane Breakfast:\par \par \uc2 This is a program targeted at year seven and eight students, a really formative age as you\u8217\'20\'19re starting to be exposed to some of these risky behaviours, but also take decisions about whether you go into them or whether you say no.\par \par \uc2 The program is a partnership with the \uc2 University of Sydney\uc2  who have been working with kids for years in areas of alcohol and illicit drug use and have now reshaped their program to around vaping.\par \par \uc2 The launch of the program nationally comes after trialling it with about 5,000 high school students over the past year Butler said was \u8220\'20\'1Cextraordinarily positive\u8221\'20\'1D with very high ratings from students.\par \par \uc2 But also, importantly, teachers who\u8217\'20\'19ve been crying out for some supports, given that they were saying right across the country vaping is now the number one behavioural issue in schools, not just high schools, too in primary schools as well, increasingly, which is just terrifying.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re rolling out the programme now to all high schools in 2025. When the new academic year begins, all high schools will be able to do that. And I know they\u8217\'20\'19re really keen to do that because when we opened up the trial, we were just overwhelmed with high schools reaching out to these providers and saying, we want something, we want to be a part of the trial.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/article/2024/sep/08/vaping-damages-young-peoples-lungs-as-much-as-smoking-study-suggests"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Vaping damages young people\u8217\'20\'19s lungs as much as smoking, study suggests }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.27pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.02pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Severe thunderstorms forecast to continue across eastern Australia\par \par \uc2 Severe thunderstorms are expected to continue throughout eastern parts of the country today.\par \par \uc2 Senior meteorologist Jonathan How from the Bureau of Meteorology said thunderstorms are forecast from northern Queensland through central parts of the state, through much of eastern and inland New South Wales, eastern Victoria and potentially parts of Tasmania.\par \par \uc2 In Queensland there is the possibility of severe thunderstorms around the southeast, including the border with NSW. How said \u8220\'20\'1Cisolated very dangerous thunderstorms\u8221\'20\'1D could occur in Brisbane and the Gold Coast.\par \par \uc2 In NSW, severe thunderstorms are likely across the north-east \u8211\'20\'13 including the Northern Rivers and north of Lismore.\par \par \uc2 enltr??? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/Thunderstorms?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #Thunderstorms }}}\uc2  are again possible today across much of eastern Australia.\par \par \uc2 These storms may become severe in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/SEQLD?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #SEQLD }}}\uc2  & {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/NENSW?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #NENSW }}}\uc2  from mid-afternoon & into the evening, with heavy rain, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/FlashFlooding?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #FlashFlooding }}}\uc2 , damaging winds & large {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/hail?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #hail }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Latest: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/jlOoTZL1iF"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/jlOoTZL1iF }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/zpla2tHAXm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/zpla2tHAXm }}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Bureau of Meteorology, Australia (@BOM_au) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BOM_au/status/1856446445907226959?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 12, 2024 }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.12pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.48pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Senior Trump adviser replies to Rudd's congratulations tweet with hourglass gif\par \par \uc2 A senior Trump adviser has posted a gif of an hourglass in response to \uc2 Kevin Rudd\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s congratulation message to the incoming president.\par \par \uc2 In a post to X on 7 November, Rudd \u8211\'20\'13 Australia\u8217\'20\'19s ambassador to the US \u8211\'20\'13 shared a statement congratulation \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on his election win. This came as he deleted comments he previously made on X about Trump, saying they did not reflect the view of the Australian government.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/nov/07/australia-us-ambassador-kevin-rudd-deletes-posts-criticising-trump-after-election-win"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Australia\u8217\'20\'19s US ambassador Kevin Rudd deletes posts criticising \u8216\'20\'18destructive\u8217\'20\'19 Trump after election win }}}\par \par \uc2 Dan Scavino, a senior advisor to Trump, quoted the post with this gif overnight:\par \par \uc2 zxxltr {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/gZgn50AWYi"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/gZgn50AWYi }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/ieUTiYW6bG"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/ieUTiYW6bG }}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Dan Scavino Jr.?????? (@DanScavino) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/DanScavino/status/1856245824675545479?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 12, 2024 }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.07am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.43pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Muswellbrook mayor says region has experienced more than 50 earthquakes in three months\par \par \uc2 The mayor of Muswellbrook Shire council, Jeffrey Drayton, spoke with ABC News Breakfast earlier after the town experienced a 4.1 magnitude earthquake yesterday.\par \par \uc2 As Kate Lyons {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/12/australia-news-live-qantas-faces-inquiry-over-use-of-airport-slots-chalmers-warns-nobody-wins-from-a-trade-war?page=with:block-6732edd38f08ef6f10472bf9#block-6732edd38f08ef6f10472bf9"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported in yesterday\u8217\'20\'19s blog }}}\uc2 , the earthquake was the fourth event over magnitude four in three months in the NSW Hunter region \u8211\'20\'13 and was likely part of an \u8220\'20\'1Cearthquake swarm\u8221\'20\'1D that has been affecting the region for several months.\par \par \uc2 Drayton said the region has experienced more than 50 earthquakes in three months:\par \par \uc2 There doesn\u8217\'20\'19t appear to be as much damage as there was with the first two earthquakes, it was a little bit smaller. It was 4.1 versus 4.9 earlier on. But certainly people felt it and it probably was felt \u8230\'20\'26 in a much wider area this time \u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 The mayor also said residents are reporting to council that their insurance claims are not being accepted:\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s been a number of stories, you know, stories like \u8230\'20\'26 we think the damage is from movement of the earth, but it\u8217\'20\'19s more likely blasting in the mines, so there was obviously an attempt to try and push away that liability \u8230\'20\'26 We\u8217\'20\'19ve had some examples of people in the community who are in their 70s and 80s, been paying insurance all their life and tell us they\u8217\'20\'19ve never, ever made a claim.\par \par \uc2 You can only imagine that people expect the comfort of what insurance brings and certainly when they do need to claim, the insurance companies should be doing the right thing and making sure they accept these claims when they are reasonable and genuine.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.03pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.33pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Ministers write to principals and parents associations about social media age limit\par \par \uc2 The education minister, Jason Clare, and communication minister, \uc2 Michelle Rowland\uc2 , have written to peak principals and parents associations to quell concerns about Labor\u8217\'20\'19s proposed ban of social media to under-16s.\par \par \uc2 The move to restrict access to social media follows the decision by education ministers last year to ban access to mobile phones in public schools, in line with \uc2 OECD\uc2  data indicating boosted academic performance.\par \par \uc2 Clare said schools were \u8220\'20\'1Cseeing the difference\u8221\'20\'1D from the phone ban.\par \par \uc2 Ask any teacher, any principal, ask most students and they will tell you this was the right decision. And that it\u8217\'20\'19s making a difference. But when school finishes, students get their phone back and they\u8217\'20\'19re back in that social media cesspit. That\u8217\'20\'19s why setting a minimum age limit for social media is so important.\par \par \uc2 Pat Murphy, president of the Australian Government Primary Principals Association, said more than half of his body\u8217\'20\'19s students in years five and six were regularly using social media sites.\par \par \uc2 Our students are facing unprecedented challenges with social media influencing their self-esteem, relationships, and concentration in the classroom. With social media\u8217\'20\'19s constant comparisons and pressures, many primary school students feel overwhelmed, and they are not mature enough to deal effectively with these pressures.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.09pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.26pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Man charged with murder of 75-year-old man\par \par \uc2 A man has been charged with murder after the body of a 75-year-old man was found at Parkes at the weekend.\par \par \uc2 The man\u8217\'20\'19s body was discovered inside a home on Alluvial Street about 1.40pm last Sunday. Officers established a crime scene and following inquiries, a 28-year-old man was arrested at a home in Tullamore \u8211\'20\'13 about 100km north-west of Parkes \u8211\'20\'13 yesterday.\par \par \uc2 He was taken to Parkes Police Station where he was charged with murder. He has been refused bail to appear at Parkes Local court today.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.25pm GMT\par \par \uc2 NSW nurses begin strike action\par \par \uc2 The NSW Nurses and Midwives strike has officially begun, kicking off about 45 minutes ago. You can read more about this below:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/nov/13/nsw-nurses-strike-pay-salary-minns-government-police-wage-increase"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Very clearly gendered\u8217\'20\'19: NSW nurses and midwives furious over pay offer disparity ahead of 24-hour strike }}}\par \par \uc2 enltrPublic sector nurses and midwives: ON STRIKE NOW! ??? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/valueus?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #valueus }}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 NSW Nurses & Midwives (@nswnma) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/nswnma/status/1856438681814679984?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 12, 2024 }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.04pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.21pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Sammy J leaves ABC Melbourne Breakfast\par \par \uc2 ABC Melbourne Breakfast presenter Sammy J has announced he is leaving his radio show after five years.\par \par \uc2 Hosting Melbourne Breakfast was an unexpected opportunity that became an unexpected joy. But radio demands all of you and deserves nothing less, and after five years I\u8217\'20\'19m ready to trade the 4:15 am alarm for more regular hours.\par \par \uc2 Sammy farewelled listeners in his usual fashion, with a song. ABC Radio Melbourne acting manager Shelley Hadfield said Sammy had connected with the audience on 774 in a unique way:\par \par \uc2 Sammy has asked the questions we never knew we wanted answered. He\u8217\'20\'19s grilled the Prime Minister and the Premier, he broke the news to Melbourne of the Queen\u8217\'20\'19s death, and he interviewed musicians, magicians, meat workers and mathematicians.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.27pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.18pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Commonwealth Bank\uc2  posts $2.5bn quarterly profit\par \par \uc2 Australia\u8217\'20\'19s biggest lender, \uc2 Commonwealth Bank\uc2 , has recorded a quarterly $2.5bn cash net profit backed by \u8220\'20\'1Cstrong momentum\u8221\'20\'1D in home lending.\par \par \uc2 Credit card and personal loan arrears fell slightly, despite enduring cost-of-living pressures, as consumers benefited from income tax rate changes. Home loan arrears were stable over the three-month period.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 CBA\uc2  chief executive, \uc2 Matt Comyn\uc2 , said:\par \par \uc2 Growth in the Australian economy remains slow, as higher rates continue to weigh on consumer demand and bring inflation back to the target range. We remain optimistic on the overall outlook and the Australian economy remains fundamentally sound.\par \par \uc2 The bank recorded growth in home loans and deposits during the quarter, helping its earnings. The September quarter result was in line with last year\u8217\'20\'19s corresponding period.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.28pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.13pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Former US ambassador Sinodinos on second Trump presidency\par \par \uc2 Australia\u8217\'20\'19s former ambassador to the US, \uc2 Arthur Sinodinos\uc2 , was on ABC RN earlier this morning to discuss the second Trump presidency.\par \par \uc2 Sinodinos said Trump \u8220\'20\'1Csays what he means and means what he says\u8221\'20\'1D and policies around tariffs and immigration are core promises of his \u8211\'20\'13 but Trump thinks other countries pay tariffs when in fact they\u8217\'20\'19re passed on to US consumers:\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ll have a bit of a challenge to explain some of this to him but he\u8217\'20\'19s determined to do this \u8230\'20\'26 The big focus will be on what he tries to do with China \u8230\'20\'26 Complicated is one way to describe it.\par \par \uc2 On the Australia-US relationship and \uc2 Kevin Rudd\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s deleted tweets, Sinodinos said the president was a \u8220\'20\'1Ctransactional figure\u8221\'20\'1D and he would be focused on the relationship going forward.\par \par \uc2 In relation to Australia I think he has a benign view of Australia \u8230\'20\'26 we\u8217\'20\'19re a strong partner in defence, we more than pull our weight, so we\u8217\'20\'19re in reasonably good shape compared to other countries \u8230\'20\'26 It would not be a good look to just suddenly yank the ambassador out.\par \par \uc2 He said Australia would have to tout its benefits to the US, rather than relying on them:\par \par \uc2 I think we should always talk about the value we provide [and] our value as a middle power \u8230\'20\'26 the important regional groupings. We have a lot of influence, we shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be afraid to use it \u8230\'20\'26 and we should have the ambition that goes with that.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.32pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.00pm GMT\par \par \uc2 One-third of referred telco complaints unresolved\par \par \uc2 A third of complaints made by telco consumers to the sector\u8217\'20\'19s ombudsman remained unresolved at the end of the latest reporting period, \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports.\par \par \uc2 The figures, between July and September of 2024, found 30%, or 3,389, of complaints returned unresolved after a referral to a telco in relation to issues including poor customer service, problems with bills or equipment, and service drop outs.\par \par \uc2 This followed the Telecommunications Industry Ombudsman beginning a new follow-up process with consumers to get a better picture of resolutions at the end of the referral period.\par \par \uc2 A total of 13,541 complaints about phone and internet services were lodged between July and September of 2024. This figure constitutes a 4.6% increase on complaints in the same period in 2023, or a total of 599 additional complaints.\par \par \uc2 More than half the complaints made by customers related to no or delayed action from a telco, followed by complaints about service and equipment fees and complaints about no phone or internet service.\par \par \uc2 Complaints raised in relation to failure to cancel a service, where a customer claims a telco has not terminated an agreement properly, jumped by 7% since the previous quarter.\par \par \uc2 The top five local government areas with the highest number of complaints were Brisbane (426), Gold Coast (279), Moreton Bay (251), Sunshine Coast (199) and Wyndham (198). These five areas alone contributed 10% of all the complaints received in this reporting period.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.50pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Albanese on social media ban: \u8216\'20\'18We don\u8217\'20\'19t pretend this is easy [or] you can just flick a switch\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Anthony Albanese was also asked about moves to ban under-16s from social media, and the privacy concerns around this \u8211\'20\'13 will everyone need to prove they are over 18? How would this policy actually be implemented and regulated?\par \par \uc2 He responded that there will be one year to develop the details, and that there would be a trial of age verification:\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19ll be detail in the legislation but we don\u8217\'20\'19t pretend this is easy [or] you can just flick a switch \u8230\'20\'26 Parents won\u8217\'20\'19t be penalised, people won\u8217\'20\'19t be penalised, the onus will be on these social media companies.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.04pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.47pm GMT\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Increasingly we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing \u8230\'20\'26 opinion reported as fact\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Moving to the misinformation bill, Anthony Albanese said what is important is that \u8220\'20\'1Cthere is a distinction between fact and opinion\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Increasingly we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing in the media things that are opinion reported as fact and that is a concern for people. The changing media landscape where everyone can be a producer as well with their device means society has to respond to that. It\u8217\'20\'19s not easy, the whole world is grappling with this.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.59pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.44pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Albanese says Australia can play a role in US and China \u8216\'20\'18competition\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Anthony Albanese is speaking with ABC Sydney before he leaves for South America to attend \uc2 APEC\uc2  and the G20 leaders\u8217\'20\'19 summit.\par \par \uc2 Asked if he would be meeting with Chinese president Xi Jinping, Albanese said the \u8220\'20\'1Cstrategic competition\u8221\'20\'1D between the US and China is \u8220\'20\'1Csomething we\u8217\'20\'19re dealing with\u8221\'20\'1D and that Australia can play a role as a middle power, because \u8220\'20\'1Cwe\u8217\'20\'19re trusted and our word matters\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The prime minister said that during his recent call with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  after his US election win, he told him it was in the US\u8217\'20\'19s interest to trade with Australia and that Australia was in one of the fastest growing regions in human history.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.53pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.36pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Dutton on Rudd as US ambassador: \u8216\'20\'18He\u8217\'20\'19s got an incredible work ethic, but he\u8217\'20\'19s made disparaging comments\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2  was also asked if ambassador \uc2 Kevin Rudd\uc2  would remain in his role as US ambassador under a Coalition government.\par \par \uc2 This comes after Rudd deleted comments he previously made about Trump after the Republican\u8217\'20\'19s election win, saying they did not reflect the view of the Australian government.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/nov/07/australia-us-ambassador-kevin-rudd-deletes-posts-criticising-trump-after-election-win"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Australia\u8217\'20\'19s US ambassador Kevin Rudd deletes posts criticising \u8216\'20\'18destructive\u8217\'20\'19 Trump after election win }}}\par \par \uc2 Dutton told ABC RN the Coalition has supported Rudd in his role as ambassador and \u8220\'20\'1Cit\u8217\'20\'19s important that he does work in our country\u8217\'20\'19s name\u8221\'20\'1D:\par \par \uc2 He\u8217\'20\'19s been a very effective contributor to public debate, particularly as a former prime minister is well respected, and I hope that he\u8217\'20\'19s able to form a relationship with the new administration, as he\u8217\'20\'19s done with with the current one.\par \par \uc2 He\u8217\'20\'19s got an incredible work ethic, but he\u8217\'20\'19s made disparaging comments, and that\u8217\'20\'19s an issue for for the government to deal with.\par \par \uc2 Asked if the opposition would like to see Scott Morrison in the role, Dutton said Rudd has a term to finish:\par \par \uc2 Following that term, that\u8217\'20\'19s an issue for the government of the day to decide who would be in that role. I think \uc2 Joe Hockey\uc2 , for example, did a great job when he was ambassador.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.48pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.31pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Dutton will not require Canavan and Antic to withdraw bill to change \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law\par \par \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2  says he supports the right of women to access legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but will not (NOT) require the Queensland Nationals senator \uc2 Matt Canavan\uc2  and the South Australian Liberal senator Alex Antic to withdraw a sponsored bill to change \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law.\par \par \uc2 The opposition leader old ABC RN earlier that Canavan and Antic were entitled to sponsor the bill, which has been on the Senate notice paper for two years, and he would not be issuing instructions either for or against presenting it:\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m not advocating that they withdraw their bill or put it forward. They as senators \u8211\'20\'13 as the Labor senators, the Green senators, David Pocock, the independents \u8211\'20\'13 have the right to put forward a private member\u8217\'20\'19s bill. In most cases \u8211\'20\'13 99% of the cases \u8211\'20\'13 the bill won\u8217\'20\'19t get forward unless it\u8217\'20\'19s got the support of the government. So if the government\u8217\'20\'19s playing games, then that\u8217\'20\'19s an issue for them, but for us, our senators have a right to put their own bills forward and it can be voted on on that basis.\par \par \uc2 Last week, Dutton used his weekly private address to coalition MPs to urge them to show more \u8220\'20\'1Cdiscipline\u8221\'20\'1D and stop speaking publicly about the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue because it was potentially politically damaging. This morning, he offered his personal view on the issue:\par \par \uc2 I support a women\u8217\'20\'19s right - a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to choose. And I\u8217\'20\'19ve been in very difficult circumstances, where, as a detective working in the sex offender squad, I\u8217\'20\'19ve dealt with women who have been raped. I\u8217\'20\'19ve dealt with women in domestic relationships who have been raped. It\u8217\'20\'19s a very, very difficult situation, and ultimately, that\u8217\'20\'19s a choice and a decision for that individual to make, and that\u8217\'20\'19s the position I support.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.44pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.23pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Treasurer to announce $900m productivity and competition boost for states\par \par \uc2 The treasurer, \uc2 Jim Chalmers\uc2  will today announce funding of $900m for states and territories through a new national productivity fund to boost competition and productivity across the economy.\par \par \uc2 Launching the plan in a speech to the Australian Business Economists in Sydney, Chalmers is hoping the plan will will incentivise states to achieve productivity gains through pro-competitive policies such as streamlining commercial planning and zoning, and removing barriers to the uptake of modern construction methods.\par \par \uc2 This is noteworthy, recalling Australia\u8217\'20\'19s competition policy in the late 90s under \uc2 John Howard\uc2 , paying states for microeconomic reform. The Albanese government already has payments to incentivise states to build houses \u8211\'20\'13 could this now extend to zoning too?\par \par \uc2 Chalmers will say that the government has been \u8220\'20\'1Crevitalising national competition policy with states and territories since last December\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In March this year, on behalf of the Council on Federal Financial Relations, I asked the Productivity Commission to model the potential impacts of a revitalised NCP.\par \par \uc2 Its final report landed this month and the benefits on offer are substantial, if not staggering. The PC found a revitalised NCP could boost GDP by up to $45bn a year and reduce prices by 1.45 percentage points. That GDP boost represents about $5,000 per household, per year.\par \par \uc2 But it reflects tough reforms and takes commitment from the Commonwealth, states and territories. On 29 November I will meet with my counterparts to kick off this work. While not every option modelled by the PC will become policy, we want to make meaningful progress where we can.\par \par \uc2 I expect we\u8217\'20\'19ll start by fast-tracking the adoption of trusted international product safety standards and developing a general right to repair \u8211\'20\'13 both Commonwealth-led reforms.\par \par \uc2 Both involve small implementation costs but provide significant benefits: in the order of $5bn over the next 10 years for product safety; and over $400m per annum for right to repair.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.19pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Full Story podcast: the high cost of Australia\u8217\'20\'19s dental care divide\par \par \uc2 In today\u8217\'20\'19s Full Story podcast, we look at why dental care in Australia is largely privatised and nearly always expensive.\par \par \uc2 According to the \uc2 Australian Institute of Health and Welfare\uc2 , almost one in five adults delay or don\u8217\'20\'19t see a dentist due to cost. For those who can access the public system, staff are often overstretched and the long wait times mean crucial appointments come far too late.\par \par \uc2 Guardian Australia health reporter Natasha May speaks to Reged Ahmed about how the perfect smile is a privilege increasingly available only to the wealthy:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/audio/2024/nov/13/the-high-cost-of-australias-dental-care-divide-full-story-podcast"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The high cost of Australia\u8217\'20\'19s dental care divide - podcast }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.15pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Greens to announce plan to ensure public hospitals can provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services\par \par \uc2 The Greens will announce a $100m a year plan to ensure public hospitals across the country will be able to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services.\par \par \uc2 The party\u8217\'20\'19s spokesperson for women, \uc2 Larissa Waters\uc2 , said access to affordable \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services should not be a \u8220\'20\'1Cpostcode lottery\u8221\'20\'1D or \u8220\'20\'1Cculture war\u8221\'20\'1D debate after it was raised as an issue by some in the Coalition.\par \par \uc2 The Greens expect the announcement to fund hospitals who don\u8217\'20\'19t yet offer the services would cost around $307m to 2027-28 if it was adopted from July 2025.\par \par \uc2 While the minor party cannot form government in its own right, it could influence the next federal government if it were to pick up a number of seats to form a minority government with one of the major parties.\par \par \uc2 Waters said those living in rural and regional areas often had to travel far or pay for expensive private services in order to access an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 Reproductive healthcare must not be a culture war or a postcode lottery. No one should fall through the cracks when it comes to accessing vital healthcare... We\u8217\'20\'19ve seen conservatives in Queensland, South Australia and the USA open the culture war again and it\u8217\'20\'19s critical Labor take this opportunity to make sure every person in the country can access the reproductive healthcare they need.\par \par \uc2 The NT Country Liberal party senator Jacinta Nampijinpa Price recently reopened debate at the federal level after she condemned later-term \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which she described as anything after the first trimester.\par \par \uc2 The opposition leader, \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2 , told Coalition MPs earlier this month not to fuel a federal debate on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, declaring there would be no change to the opposition\u8217\'20\'19s policy if he wins government.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/nov/05/peter-dutton-liberal-coalition-abortion-policy-ntwnfb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Peter Dutton rules out Coalition \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  policy changes and blasts MPs for stirring debate }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.57pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.12pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Good morning\par \par \uc2 Emily Wind here, signing on for blogging duties \u8211\'20\'13 thanks to Martin for kicking things off. I\u8217\'20\'19ll be with you through most of our rolling coverage today.\par \par \uc2 See something that needs attention? You can always get in touch via X, @emilywindwrites, or you can send me an email: emily.wind@theguardian.com.\par \par \uc2 Let\u8217\'20\'19s get started.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.01pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Tabcorp\uc2  fined $262,000 for accepting bets on live tennis\par \par \uc2 The gambling comapny \uc2 Tabcorp\uc2  has been fined more than $260,000 for accepting bets during 69 tennis matches over a six-month period.\par \par \uc2 Australian law prohibits bets being placed during live sport matches to limit gambling harm and the risk of match fixing.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Australian Communications and Media Authority\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 s Carolyn Lidgerwood said she was concerned the breach \u8211\'20\'13 which was due to a technical error in \uc2 Tabcorp\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s systems \u8211\'20\'13 was not fixed for six months.\par \par \uc2 Tabcorp\uc2  is a major wagering operator and it is concerning that it took some 6 months for the system error to be identified and fixed.\par \par \uc2 There has been significant growth in online sports betting in recent years and it\u8217\'20\'19s important all online wagering services have systems in place so that illegal in-play bets are not accepted.\par \par \uc2 According to the regulator, \uc2 Tabcorp\uc2  voided all bets, which meant consumers did not suffer losses and the company did not profit from its error.\par \par \uc2 In 2021, \uc2 Tabcorp\uc2  escaped punishment for accepting in-play bets on a US college basketball game as it promised to fix an error that allowed them to be accepted.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.28pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.57pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Police raid properties in Melbourne as part of NDIS fraud investigation\par \par \uc2 Australian Federal Police\uc2  raided multiple locations in Melbourne\u8217\'20\'19s western suburbs last night as part of an investigation into alleged fraud against the \uc2 National Disability Insurance\uc2  Scheme (NDIS).\par \par \uc2 The searches were led by the \uc2 National Disability Insurance Agency\uc2  \u8216\'20\'18s (\uc2 NDIA\uc2 ) anti-fraud taskforce and supported by the NDIS Quality and Safeguards Commission and other agencies.\par \par \uc2 Government agencies worked to ensure the safety of NDIS participants during the operation.\par \par \uc2 The NDIS minister, \uc2 Bill Shorten\uc2 , told Guardian Australia the raids were \u8220\'20\'1Cpart of an investigation into alleged fraudulent activity against the NDIS\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 I established the fraud fusion taskforce (FFT) two years ago to combat any attempts by serious criminals attempting to infiltrate the NDIS and other government support systems. The FFT has wide-reaching powers, with the taskforce now comprising 21 government agencies working together and sharing information.\par \par \uc2 I have complete confidence in the FFT as they undertake this important work to tackle any serious criminal activity that comes at the expense of Australians who rely on such supports.\par \par \uc2 No arrests were made during the operation and the investigation is ongoing.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.27pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.50pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Two-year-old boy dies after falling into dam in regional NSW\par \par \uc2 A two-year-old boy has died after being pulled from a dam in regional New South Wales, police have said.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, NSW police said emergency services were called to a property on Pipers Flat Road, Portland in the state\u8217\'20\'19s central west, about 3pm on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 They were responding to reports of a concern for welfare, police said, adding that they found the child had been pulled from a dam on the property when they arrived.\par \par \uc2 Police said the boy was treated at the scene by NSW Ambulance paramedics before being taken to Bathurst Hospital in a critical condition, where he later died.\par \par \uc2 Police have said their inquiries into the incident continue, with a report to be prepared for the coroner.\par \par \uc2 Guardian Australia understands police are not treating the boy\u8217\'20\'19s death as suspicious.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.27pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.44pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Average age of girls first viewing porn falls by two years\par \par \uc2 Girls are now seeing porn for the first time at 13.6 years, on average \u8211\'20\'13 two years younger than they were in 2018.\par \par \uc2 And more than 70% of young people say porn often shows aggression and violence towards women, a new report from Our Watch has found.\par \par \uc2 Patty Kinnersly, the chief executive of Our Watch, said young people are using porn to learn about sex, and that it has been normalised with greater online access. She said:\par \par \uc2 Our Watch\u8217\'20\'19s concern is with the representation of certain people, particularly women, rather than porn itself.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s the frequent viewing of violent porn, that is sexist and derogatory, [and] associated with a greater likelihood of men and boys holding attitudes that can lead to violence against women.\par \par \uc2 Kinnersly said porn overwhelmingly shows women as submissive, and men as controlling, and that it has made the dangerous practice of choking mainstream.\par \par \uc2 Research published earlier this year found almost 60% of people under 35 had been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/article/2024/jul/02/risk-of-serious-injury-as-strangling-during-sex-becomes-normalised-among-young-australians"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 choked at least once }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Parents might not realise the extent of porn use, and the extent to which the internet and porn is \u8220\'20\'1Cflooded with misogyny and hate for women, for LGBTI+\u8221\'20\'1D and others, Kinnersly said:\par \par \uc2 The line between IRL [in real life] and online is very blurry, so we can\u8217\'20\'19t just say certain types of porn are bad\u8230\'20\'26 we\u8217\'20\'19ve actually got to take proactive actions across the rest of a young person\u8217\'20\'19s life so they can critique it.\par \par \uc2 Our Watch wants to see pornography literacy integrated into nation-wide education about respectful relationships, and for parents to understand the new world their children are in.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.54pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.43pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Longer emergency wait times expected amid NSW nurses' strike, health minister says\par \par \uc2 The New South Wales health minister, Ryan Park, said last night he was preparing the state\u8217\'20\'19s hospital system to cope with today\u8217\'20\'19s nurses\u8217\'20\'19 strike. He said the industrial action \u8211\'20\'13 due to start at 7.30am \u8211\'20\'13 would mean planned surgeries being postponed and longer wait times in emergency departments.\par \par \uc2 I want to assure the community that we are doing everything we can to minimise the impact of this on their care.\par \par \uc2 NSW Health is working hard to ensure postponed surgeries are rescheduled as quickly as reasonably possible.\par \par \uc2 I also remind the community of pathways to care for non-life-threatening conditions outside of the emergency department \u8211\'20\'13 by first phoning Healthdirect on 1800 022 222, where you can speak to a registered nurse about your care options \u8211\'20\'13 potentially avoiding an unnecessary wait in the hospital.\par \par \uc2 He added that over the course of four weeks of negotiations the state government had reached agreement on all of the nurses\u8217\'20\'19 non-wage claims, and put forward options to fund and deliver a new increased wage offer.\par \par \uc2 He claimed that the Nurses & Midwives Association had previously agreed to cease industrial action contingent on the government paying nurses and midwives an interim increase while work towards a final settlement remained ongoing.\par \par \uc2 But feeling among nurses has been inflamed by news that the police force have been given a 40% pay rise.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.01pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.31pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Welcome\par \par \uc2 Good morning and welcome to our live news blog. I\u8217\'20\'19m Martin Farrer bringing you some of the top overnight stories and then it\u8217\'20\'19ll be Emily Wind to take over.\par \par \uc2 Nurses and midwives in New South Wales will stage a 24-hour strike from 7.30am this morning amid anger over the state government\u8217\'20\'19s decision to grant {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/nov/11/nsw-police-pay-rise-minns-government-details"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 NSW police a 40% pay deal }}}\uc2 . Members of the NSW Nurses and Midwives\u8217\'20\'19 Association are calling on the Minns government to address the wage disparity that has left the state\u8217\'20\'19s nurses and midwives the lowest paid in the country. The NSW health minister, Ryan Park, said he was \u8220\'20\'1Cdisappointed\u8221\'20\'1D about the strike. More coming up.\par \par \uc2 The treasurer, Jim Chalmers, will today announce funding of $900m for states and territories through a new national productivity fund to boost competition and productivity across the economy. He hopes the plan will encourage states to achieve productivity gains through pro-competitive policies such as streamlining planning laws. More coming up.\par \par \uc2 Federal police raided a number of locations in Melbourne\u8217\'20\'19s western suburbs last night as part of an investigation into alleged fraud against the National Disability Insurance Scheme. More shortly.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.00pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241112ekbc0038q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
