\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Wisconsin supreme court seems hostile to 1849  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ban in oral arguments\b0\par\par\uc2 Alice Herman \par \uc2 705 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 November 2024\par 19:53 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Status of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in state has been contested since Roe was overturned, triggering the 175-year-old ban into effect\par \par \uc2 During heated oral arguments on Monday morning, the Wisconsin supreme court appeared poised to find an 1849 law banning most abortions cannot be enforced.\par \par \uc2 The legal status of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Wisconsin has been contested since the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade and ended the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide, triggering bans across the country \u8211\'20\'13 including in Wisconsin, where a 175-year-old ban immediately went into effect.\par \par \uc2 Democrats in Wisconsin have seized on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a campaign issue, with Justice Janet Protasiewicz expressing her support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and winning a seat on the court in spring 2023. Protasiewicz\u8217\'20\'19s election to the court helped flip the ideological balance on the bench, which is now controlled by a narrow liberal majority.\par \par \uc2 It is highly unlikely the liberal-controlled court will uphold the ban.\par \par \uc2 The 1849 statute, which was nullified by Roe v Wade and then reanimated when the landmark decision was overturned, declares that ending \u8220\'20\'1Cthe life of an unborn child\u8221\'20\'1D is a felony, except when required to save the life of the mother. In July 2023, a Dane county judge {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/abortion-wisconsin-ban-challenge-lawsuit-866eed85d2918113bfe644459e62171a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruled }}}\uc2  that the 1849 ban applies only to feticide and not \u8220\'20\'1Cconsensual\u8221\'20\'1D \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , citing a previous ruling that interpreted the statute as an anti-feticide law, and in September, providers including \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  resumed offering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.\par \par \uc2 The Sheboygan county district attorney Joel Urmanski appealed the ruling, which is now before the Wisconsin supreme court.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe position of the circuit court below \u8230\'20\'26 is ultimately indefensible,\u8221\'20\'1D said Matthew Thome, an attorney representing Urmanski, during his opening argument. Thome argued that the 1849 law should be interpreted to \u8220\'20\'1Cprohibit consensual abortions from conception until birth, subject to an exception when it is necessary to save the life of the mother\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Justice Jill Karofsky interrogated Urmanski\u8217\'20\'19s interpretation of the law, asking if it would provide exceptions for rape, incest, the health of the mother, or fetal abnormalities.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CJust to be clear, a 12-year-old girl who was sexually assaulted by her father and as a result, became pregnant, under your interpretation \u8230\'20\'26 she would be forced to carry her pregnancy to term?\u8221\'20\'1D asked Karofsky. She noted that the \u8220\'20\'1Cpenalty for aborting after a sexual assault would be more severe than the penalty for the sexual assault\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In response to a question about the medical consequences of a ban, Thome responded that he was unsure, given that he is \u8220\'20\'1Cnot a doctor\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI fear that what you are asking this court to do is to sign the death warrants of women and children and pregnant people in this state, because under your interpretation, they could all be denied life saving medical care, while the medical professionals who are charged with taking care of them are forced to sit idly by,\u8221\'20\'1D said Karofsky.\par \par \uc2 The court weighed the question of whether laws that were passed regulating \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  while Roe was in effect \u8220\'20\'1Cimpliedly repealed\u8221\'20\'1D the 1849 ban and rendered it unenforceable.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAll of those statues\u8221\'20\'1D passed after 1973 and before it was overturned, \u8220\'20\'1Cjust go to the dust pile?\u8221\'20\'1D asked Karofsky.\par \par \uc2 Justice Brian Hagedorn, a conservative-leaning judge, argued, of the 1849 ban, that \u8220\'20\'1Cthe law is still there\u8221\'20\'1D, adding that \u8220\'20\'1Cthe judiciary doesn\u8217\'20\'19t get to edit laws, the judiciary doesn\u8217\'20\'19t get to rewrite them, we didn\u8217\'20\'19t delete it, we prevented its enforcement\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The assistant attorney general Hannah Jurss disagreed, arguing that \u8220\'20\'1Cthere is nothing in the text of these statutes that says in the event that Roe is overturned we somehow go back to the old law and throw out all of the new ones,\u8221\'20\'1D drawing a distinction between Wisconsin\u8217\'20\'19s more than a century old law and \u8220\'20\'1Ctrigger\u8221\'20\'1D laws passed in certain states that were specifically designed to go into effect after Roe v Wade was overturned.\par \par \uc2 A separate case, which the Wisconsin supreme court has also agreed to hear, would decide whether the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is protected under the state\u8217\'20\'19s constitution \u8211\'20\'13 possibly opening the door to challenge other laws regulating \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241111ekbb00335\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
