\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18A big cratering\u8217\'20\'19: an expert on gen Z\u8217\'20\'19s surprise votes \u8211\'20\'13 and young women\u8217\'20\'19s growing support for Trump\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,743 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 November 2024\par 19:55 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The gender gap among young people was predictably vast. But the numbers still defied expectations, as the political scientist Melissa Deckman explainsA masculinity researcher on the Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 \u8216\'20\'18fatal miscalculation\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Long before voting closed in the 2024 elections, pundits predicted that young Americans would be riven by a canyon-wide gender gap. Those predictions turned out to be correct.\par \par \uc2 As a whole, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  won voters between the ages of 18 and 29 by six points. But {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://circle.tufts.edu/2024-election#economy-was-the-top-youth-issue,-drove-youth-vote-for-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 preliminary exit polling }}}\uc2  indicates that Donald Trump opened up a 16-point gender gap between young men and young women: 56% of men between the ages of 18 and 29 voted for Trump while just 40% of their female peers did so.\par \par \uc2 Even more surprisingly, Trump managed to improve on his 2020 performance among young women, despite that gap. In 2020, 33% of young women voted for him.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the campaign, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kff.org/womens-health-policy/poll-finding/women-voters-revisited-inflation-abortion-and-increased-motivation-in-the-2024-election-countdown/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 polling indicated that \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  was the top issue }}}\uc2  for women under 30. Other surveys also found that young women {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/649826/exploring-young-women-leftward-expansion.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have veered to the left }}}\uc2 , becoming, by some measures, the most {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2024/aug/07/gen-z-voters-political-ideology-gender-gap"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 progressive cohort ever measured }}}\uc2  in US history \u8211\'20\'13 but many did not vote like it. In fact, many appeared not to vote at all. Early estimates show that only 42% of young people {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://circle.tufts.edu/latest-research/overall-youth-turnout-down-2020-strong-battleground-states"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 turned out to vote }}}\uc2 . That\u8217\'20\'19s less than in the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 The political scientist Melissa Deckman runs the Public Religion Research Institute (PRRI) and recently published The Politics of Gen Z: How the Youngest Voters Will Shape Our Democracy. Although she\u8217\'20\'19s an expert on the youth vote, and in particular on how young women vote, even she was taken aback by Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s results \u8211\'20\'13 and especially by the diminished turnout, as her research has found that young women are more politically engaged than ever.\par \par \uc2 We discussed what we can glean from the youth vote, what it indicates about young people\u8217\'20\'19s lives and what it means for the future of the United States.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re still waiting for more detailed data on how young men and women prioritized issues in this campaign, but what do we know so far about the issues that were most important to gen Z?\par \par \uc2 By and large, it was the economy. For gen Z voters who care about the economy, they really broke for Donald Trump.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  really dropped as being the most salient issue for younger people. I think that was the most surprising to me.\par \par \uc2 If you look at the youth vote in 2022 \u8211\'20\'13 and this is all young voters, not just men or women \u8211\'20\'13 44% said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the issue they put at their top priority. Whereas this fall, the issue was only 13% [exit polling shows]. That\u8217\'20\'19s a pretty big cratering.\par \par \uc2 Typically, why do we see gender gaps like this?\par \par \uc2 The gender gap among gen Z voters reflects the larger gender gap we\u8217\'20\'19ve often seen historically in this country. Women have tended to vote for Democrats while men have tended to vote for Republicans, and we saw that same pattern among women more generally and men more generally this election cycle. Historically, that\u8217\'20\'19s been because women have tended to want a larger size and scope of government. They tend to be more supportive of government programs. Men have tended to vote pocketbook issues and want less government.\par \par \uc2 Why do you think we saw such a gender gap between gen Z men and women?\par \par \uc2 A lot of young women came of age politically during the Trump presidency. We often in political science talk about these being \u8220\'20\'1Cthe impressionable years\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 that a lot of people often develop their orientations toward government as late teens, early adults. They\u8217\'20\'19re witnessing the election of Trump, who has said openly misogynistic things, who many women have [spoken out] about how they\u8217\'20\'19ve been harassed and even assaulted by him. He bragged about sexual assault on that infamous Access Hollywood tape.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m really struck that gen Z stayed home in ways they didn\u8217\'20\'19t in 2020\par \par \uc2 You combine that with the #MeToo movement a couple years later, which was a larger, broader conversation about sexual harassment and its prevalence in society. That made a cognitive dissonance for these young women: America\u8217\'20\'19s elected Trump in an era where we\u8217\'20\'19re recognizing that sexual harassment is a problem. It made them far less likely to embrace the GOP.\par \par \uc2 This generation of young women is strongly supportive of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legality, and they\u8217\'20\'19re having fewer rights than their mothers and grandmothers. All of those things together have fomented for them a gender consciousness in ways that we don\u8217\'20\'19t see with older generations of American women.\par \par \uc2 What\u8217\'20\'19s notable about gen Z, however, is that unlike perhaps the last several election cycles \u8211\'20\'13 where you had a majority of young men voting for Democrats, either for Congress or for Biden in 2020 \u8211\'20\'13 we saw a more rightward turn in voting behavior among young men, and that\u8217\'20\'19s probably driven by two things. One: the Democratic party didn\u8217\'20\'19t have a convincing message for a lot of young men, especially on the economy. Secondly: Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s decision to meet young men where they are \u8211\'20\'13 going on Joe Rogan \u8211\'20\'13 it sent the message that he cared about their votes. When you don\u8217\'20\'19t have someone willing to fight for your votes and talk about your interests, you\u8217\'20\'19re less interested in voting for that party.\par \par \uc2 The 2022 midterms took place only months after the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade in the decision Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization. Since then, we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen more than a dozen states ban almost all abortions and heard reports of at least {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/nov/01/teen-dies-abortion-ban-texas-neveah-crain"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 four women dying }}}\uc2  as a result of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. After all that, why has \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  become less important to young women?\par \par \uc2 Dobbs \u8211\'20\'13 it was such a political earthquake. It really, really motivated young women to vote at much higher levels in that midterm election. But you also have to remember the electorate in a midterm is different than the electorate in a general election, and midterm elections tend to draw more motivated voters to begin with. To think that that was going to carry over in 2024 maybe was not the most accurate prediction.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m really struck that gen Z stayed home in ways they didn\u8217\'20\'19t in 2020. It was one of the biggest surprises for me \u8211\'20\'13 mainly because we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen, in the last three federal election cycles, gen Z outperforming younger voters in earlier cycles.\par \par \uc2 Gen Z is really mistrustful of institutions \u8211\'20\'13 at higher rates than an older Americans. Perhaps they felt like they\u8217\'20\'19ve gone to the ballot box, they\u8217\'20\'19ve tried to make these changes and they haven\u8217\'20\'19t really seen enough action. Maybe this is a reflection of the fact that increasingly younger voters are are less in tune to government and don\u8217\'20\'19t think government can provide them solutions to their problems.\par \par \uc2 So why was the economy so important to gen Z?\par \par \uc2 Economic anxiety is really palpable among this generation. They\u8217\'20\'19re disproportionately more likely to feel the pain of the economy because they want to move out of their parents\u8217\'20\'19 basement. They can\u8217\'20\'19t afford rent or to buy a house. They have massive student loan debt. There\u8217\'20\'19s a sense among younger people that the American dream isn\u8217\'20\'19t really available for them.\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s a sense among younger people that the American dream isn\u8217\'20\'19t really available for them\par \par \uc2 Even though you have, on a macro level, some indicators of the economy doing quite well \u8211\'20\'13 low unemployment, some growth, there\u8217\'20\'19s even actually been a reduction in inflation \u8211\'20\'13 that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t matter. Because you have younger Americans really feeling the pinch of higher prices.\par \par \uc2 In many ways, maybe young voters were just like older Americans, in voting their pocketbook and being unhappy with the status quo politically.\par \par \uc2 Do you think the Harris campaign then erred in centering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  so much?\par \par \uc2 Public opinion polls show that most Americans are broadly supportive of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legality \u8211\'20\'13 like more than two-thirds. It\u8217\'20\'19s even higher for young women. We find about seven in 10 say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases. So I don\u8217\'20\'19t think it was necessarily a bad strategy.\par \par \uc2 I do think, though, that it\u8217\'20\'19s a strategy that assumes that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the top issue that voters cared about. Perhaps focusing more on the economy and how her policies would help young people \u8211\'20\'13 maybe more attention should have been focused there.\par \par \uc2 We expected \u8211\'20\'13 and my data has shown \u8211\'20\'13 that when gen Z women have been able to vote, they tend to have voted for Democrats, for House or Senate or president. They broke really wide for Biden in 2020.\par \par \uc2 It was still a pretty big gap [in 2024]. Most young women really preferred Harris over Trump, by far.\par \par \uc2 What do you think this portends for the future? Are these younger women a little bit more amenable to Republicans \u8211\'20\'13 or are they just amenable to Trump?\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s the million-dollar question.\par \par \uc2 [On the issues] young women are really to the left, and I don\u8217\'20\'19t see any evidence that any of those things will change. They\u8217\'20\'19re far more likely to prioritize climate change than gen Z men are. They want to do more to mitigate gun violence. They want to have more spending on mental health. They are very, very supportive of LGBTQ+ rights and racial justice.\par \par \uc2 If young people find that their economic situation hasn\u8217\'20\'19t improved in four years, I could totally see them going in the other direction. I don\u8217\'20\'19t see a massive switch or any kind of realignment happening necessarily.\par \par \uc2 Notably, young men are more liberal [than conservative] on these same policies. But I think that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2024/aug/05/young-men-voters-us-election-trump-harris"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 young men who are disaffected }}}\uc2 , who feel like women\u8217\'20\'19s gains have come at their expense \u8211\'20\'13 this is a common theme you hear on the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/nov/07/joe-rogan-elon-musk-heterodoxy-trump-win-reaction"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 manosphere }}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 they were receptive to a change.\par \par \uc2 This interview reflects\par \par \uc2 two conversations and has been edited for length and clarity.\par \par \uc2 Read more of the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s 2024 US election coverage\par \par \uc2 *\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/nov/07/election-deniers-trump-victory-senate-races"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Election deniers use Trump victory to sow more doubt over 2020 result }}}\par \par \uc2 *\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2024/nov/08/what-a-second-trump-presidency-means-for-big-us-tech-firms"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 What a second Trump presidency means for big US tech firms }}}\par \par \uc2 *\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/nov/06/pompeo-kennedy-and-musk-who-could-be-in-trumps-new-administration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Who could be in Trump\u8217\'20\'19s new administration }}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241108ekb8002s7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
