\par\par \uc2 Australia news\par \b \uc2 Shrinkflation a \u8216\'20\'18difficult decision\u8217\'20\'19 for manufacturers, inquiry told \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Natasha May and Tory Shepherd (earlier) \par \uc2 18,800 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 November 2024\par 07:05 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 This blog is now closedAlbanese says Trump call \u8216\'20\'18very constructive\u8217\'20\'19 as Dutton calls US president-elect \u8216\'20\'18not somebody to be scared of\u8217\'20\'19Get our breaking news email, free app or daily news podcast\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.34am GMT\par \par \uc2 What we learned; Thursday 7 November\par \par \uc2 This is where we\u8217\'20\'19ll leave the blog for today, but first a quick summary:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Questions about the central bank\u8217\'20\'19s decision to keep interest rates on hold {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/07/australia-politics-live-anthony-albanese-donald-trump-peter-dutton-labor-coalition-reserve-bank-interest-rates-question-time?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-672b9be38f08bc33caae1179#block-672b9be38f08bc33caae1179"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 were levelled at Reserve Bank governor }}}\uc2 Michele Bullock\uc2  at her regular parliamentary grilling.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Penny Wong\uc2  said \uc2 Kevin Rudd\uc2  will \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/07/australia-politics-live-anthony-albanese-donald-trump-peter-dutton-labor-coalition-reserve-bank-interest-rates-question-time?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-672bcee38f082be82baf0ec2#block-672bcee38f082be82baf0ec2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 continue to do an excellent job }}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D as Australian ambassador to the US.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Malcolm Turnbull\uc2  warned Anthony Albanese {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/07/australia-politics-live-anthony-albanese-donald-trump-peter-dutton-labor-coalition-reserve-bank-interest-rates-question-time?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-672bd62c8f08040dded39711#block-672bd62c8f08040dded39711"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 not to \u8220\'20\'1Csuck up\u8221\'20\'1D to the new US president }}}\uc2  and says Aukus is \u8220\'20\'1Ca dud deal\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The government {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/07/australia-politics-live-anthony-albanese-donald-trump-peter-dutton-labor-coalition-reserve-bank-interest-rates-question-time?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-672bef128f08040dded397f2#block-672bef128f08040dded397f2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced a new minimum age limit }}}\uc2  will be 16 for social media platforms.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Ray Hadley {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/07/australia-politics-live-anthony-albanese-donald-trump-peter-dutton-labor-coalition-reserve-bank-interest-rates-question-time?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-672bf8428f082be82baf104a#block-672bf8428f082be82baf104a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced he would retire }}}\uc2  from 2GB.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Kevin Rudd\uc2  deleted social media posts critical of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to avoid the comments \u8220\'20\'1Cbeing misconstrued\u8221\'20\'1D, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/07/australia-politics-live-anthony-albanese-donald-trump-peter-dutton-labor-coalition-reserve-bank-interest-rates-question-time?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-672bf9498f082be82baf104f#block-672bf9498f082be82baf104f"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 foreign affairs department has confirmed }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Anthony Albanese confirmed he had a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/07/australia-politics-live-anthony-albanese-donald-trump-peter-dutton-labor-coalition-reserve-bank-interest-rates-question-time?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-672c2d0e8f08040dded39a0f#block-672c2d0e8f08040dded39a0f"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cvery constructive\u8221\'20\'1D chat }}}\uc2  with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  this morning. \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2  said the president-elect is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/07/australia-politics-live-anthony-albanese-donald-trump-peter-dutton-labor-coalition-reserve-bank-interest-rates-question-time?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-672c2e088f082be82baf120e#block-672c2e088f082be82baf120e"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cnot somebody to be scared of\u8221\'20\'1D }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Nine Entertainment\u8217\'20\'19s acting CEO \uc2 Matt Stanton{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/07/australia-politics-live-anthony-albanese-donald-trump-peter-dutton-labor-coalition-reserve-bank-interest-rates-question-time?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-672c129f8f081ed4eda7b571#block-672c129f8f081ed4eda7b571"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced another $50m in cost cuts }}}\uc2  just months after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/article/2024/jun/28/nine-job-cuts-200-publishing-smh-the-age-afr"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cutting 200 jobs }}}\uc2  from the media business.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Labor revealed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/07/australia-politics-live-anthony-albanese-donald-trump-peter-dutton-labor-coalition-reserve-bank-interest-rates-question-time?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-672c13868f08040dded39942#block-672c13868f08040dded39942"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 plans to electronically monitor }}}\uc2  some people freed from immigration detention.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The responsible minister {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/07/australia-politics-live-anthony-albanese-donald-trump-peter-dutton-labor-coalition-reserve-bank-interest-rates-question-time?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-672c1acc8f08040dded3998b#block-672c1acc8f08040dded3998b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 called off meeting with the chair }}}\uc2  of Queensland\u8217\'20\'19s truth-telling inquiry.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Tony Burke {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/07/australia-politics-live-anthony-albanese-donald-trump-peter-dutton-labor-coalition-reserve-bank-interest-rates-question-time?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-672c1ffa8f082be82baf11d2#block-672c1ffa8f082be82baf11d2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 introduced a bill to increase power }}}\uc2  to send non-citizens to other countries.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The Greens said Labor was \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/07/australia-politics-live-anthony-albanese-donald-trump-peter-dutton-labor-coalition-reserve-bank-interest-rates-question-time?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-672c468c8f081ed4eda7b6de#block-672c468c8f081ed4eda7b6de"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 trying to out-Dutton Peter Dutton }}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D on deportation.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Labor will seek to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/07/australia-politics-live-anthony-albanese-donald-trump-peter-dutton-labor-coalition-reserve-bank-interest-rates-question-time?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-672c47158f08040dded39aae#block-672c47158f08040dded39aae"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 publish the sealed robodebt findings }}}\uc2  at the \u8220\'20\'1Cfirst appropriate opportunity\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 And public sector employees have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/nov/07/australia-politics-live-anthony-albanese-donald-trump-peter-dutton-labor-coalition-reserve-bank-interest-rates-question-time?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-672c49728f082be82baf12ad#block-672c49728f082be82baf12ad"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reached 2.5 million people }}}\uc2  with wages of $232bn a year.\par \par \uc2 Enjoy the rest of your Thursday evening.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.02am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.19am GMT\par \par \uc2 Australia\u8217\'20\'19s big investors have been told to keep the faith on climate finance, with the United States expected to - again - dismantle climate policies under a Trump administration, \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports.\par \par \uc2 Chair of the Superpower Institute economist Rod Sims told an investor summit today the world must press ahead with climate action because president-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will remove America from serious climate action.\par \par \uc2 He told the Investor Group on Climate Change\u8217\'20\'19s annual conference in Melbourne:\par \par \uc2 The US can rejoin the ranks of like-minded countries when it is ready to do so.\par \par \uc2 Professor Sims, a former head of the competition commission, also urged the group\u8217\'20\'19s members, who have $5 trillion under management in Australia, to get behind federal Labor\u8217\'20\'19s Future Made in Australia program.\par \par \uc2 But be clear you are supporting its economic logic of addressing market failures, and particularly green energy intensive exports, and not supporting government funding to make things in Australia for its own sake.\par \par \uc2 If you think the world will not decarbonise, you think there will not be a sufficient market for our green energy intensive products.\par \par \uc2 But Australia can show the world what\u8217\'20\'19s possible if it starts producing green iron, aluminium, fertiliser and green transport fuel, Prof Sims added.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.09am GMT\par \par \uc2 Barden, the \uc2 Australian Food and Grocery Council\uc2  chief, also refused to answer a question about the major supermarkets\u8217\'20\'19 profit margins while the \uc2 ACCC\uc2  supermarkets inquiry was being heard publicly.\par \par \uc2 During the hearing this afternoon, which is being live-streamed, the counsel assisting the inquiry, Naomi Sharp SC, told Barden that \uc2 Coles\uc2  and \uc2 Woolworths\uc2  had told the \uc2 ACCC\uc2  their supermarket businesses were \u8220\'20\'1Chigh volume, low margin\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Sharp asked Barden what feedback the \uc2 Australian Food and Grocery Council\uc2  (\uc2 AFGC\uc2 ) members had provided about the net price of the goods they supplied to the supermarkets compared with the retail prices of the same goods.\par \par \uc2 Barden replied:\par \par \uc2 I would prefer questions related to two major players, profit margins and pricing to be taken in private hearing.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s just quite a narrow question focused around two major players whom the \uc2 AFGC\uc2  does need to collaborate closely with for the benefit of suppliers and consumers on a number of different matters.\par \par \uc2 And I think in those interests, it would be better for us to answer those questions in a private hearing to maintain those constructive relationships.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.47am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.04am GMT\par \par \uc2 Shrinkflation of food products a \u8216\'20\'18difficult decision\u8217\'20\'19 for companies, peak body says\par \par \uc2 The head of Australia\u8217\'20\'19s peak body for grocery suppliers has defended so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cshrinkflation\u8221\'20\'1D, saying it was a \u8220\'20\'1Cdifficult decision\u8221\'20\'1D for manufacturers and not one they \u8220\'20\'1Ctake lightly\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Australian Food and Grocery Council\uc2  chief executive officer, Tanya Barden, has been giving evidence before the \uc2 Australian Competition and Consumer Commission\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s supermarket inquiry this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 As Guardian Australia reported last year, food companies have been regularly reducing the size of their products but not the price under a practice known as shrinkflation.\par \par \uc2 A growing list of companies were doing so to pass on costs and increase profits by avoiding a sensitive price hike that was more likely to attract the ire of customers.\par \par \uc2 Asked about shrinkflation at the hearing, Barden said:\par \par \uc2 It is often portrayed as being misleading or sneaky, but the reality is, it reflects a very difficult decision that a manufacturer has to make in light of significant cost increases.\par \par \uc2 They can put the price up for a consumer, or they can reduce the pack size, or they can cease to manufacture the product, or move the manufacturing of that product offshore.\par \par \uc2 Now, a manufacturer knows that a consumer is highly sensitive to price.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes, when faced with significant cost pressures [and] limited ability to absorb those, a manufacturer may decide that the consumer can\u8217\'20\'19t bear any further price increase without ceasing to purchase a product.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.41am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.56am GMT\par \par \uc2 Head of CDC will be separate role to chief medical officer, Senate estimates told\par \par \uc2 The head of the government\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Centre for Disease Control\uc2  will be a separate role from the chief medical officer, the health department has told Senate estimates.\par \par \uc2 Blair Comley, the department secretary, is providing more details about the CDC, which is still in development and intended to be an expert advisory body:\par \par \uc2 Going forward, when the CDC is established that we will have the chief medical officer, and really the head of the CDC will be more akin to a Chief Health Officer looking at the surveillance, preventative health, etc, and information in a crisis response. But they\u8217\'20\'19ll work very closely together.\par \par \uc2 So, for example, the chief medical officer would still be the chair of the AHPC [Australian Health Protection Committee], so have that direct link to the states and territories.\par \par \uc2 And the CDC head, we\u8217\'20\'19d intend to be also a member of the AHPC, which is that key Coordination Group in the in the time of a crisis.\par \par \uc2 Comley said there is no suggestion the CDC will override state and territory public health decisions.\par \par \uc2 He elaborated it will be a progressive implementation of the CDC\u8211\'20\'13 the starting point will be communicable diseases, \u8220\'20\'1Csetting ourselves up for the next pandemic or other shocks,\u8221\'20\'1D but over time it will have a focus on non-communicable diseases, including chronic diseases, as well.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.39am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.38am GMT\par \par \uc2 Four men arrested in Victoria in \u8216\'20\'18day of action\u8217\'20\'19 against neo-Nazi group\par \par \uc2 Four men have been arrested today after what Victoria police called \u8220\'20\'1Ca day of action\u8221\'20\'1D against the Australian neo-Nazi group the National Socialist Network (NSN).\par \par \uc2 Police sources have confirmed those arrested include neo-nazi leader Thomas Sewell, 31.\par \par \uc2 A 31-year-old man was charged with two counts of intimidating a police officer/family member.\par \par \uc2 The charges follow incidents alleged to have occurred in late October, police said in a statement, including \u8220\'20\'1Conline commentary\u8221\'20\'1D linked to a rally on Bourke Street. He was also interviewed in relation to an incident outside an embassy where a flag was burned.\par \par \uc2 NSN members documented their attempts to disrupt a refugee rights rally in Docklands on October 22 on their social media channels, as well as a stunt outside the Chinese consulate in Toorak four days later.\par \par \uc2 The Wantirna South man was bailed to appear at Ringwood magistrates court on 18 February, and was released \u8220\'20\'1Cpending further enquiries in relation to offences against the Racial and Religious Tolerance Act\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 A North Melbourne man, 25, along with a Baxter man, 23, and an Ashwood man, 21, were also interviewed by detectives in relation to the offences of serious racial vilification and grossly offensive public conduct. All were released and are expected to be charged on summons, police said.\par \par \uc2 The police interviews relate to an incident the evening of October 31, Halloween, when a number of men in offensive clothing are alleged to have verbally harassed two women in a Port Melbourne car park.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.05am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.31am GMT\par \par \uc2 Man accused of stealing Eftpos machines and processing $670,000 in fraudulent reimbursements in Brisbane\par \par \uc2 A 38-year-old man has allegedly committed more than $670,000 in fraudulent reimbursement transactions using stolen Eftpos terminals.\par \par \uc2 Police say they have charged the man with 41 offences after the alleged Eftpos scam which targeted medical businesses.\par \par \uc2 It will be alleged between October 1 and November 2, the man was involved in a series of break ins, mostly on Brisbane\u8217\'20\'19s southside, where he stole EFTPOS terminals.\par \par \uc2 He then committed the fraudulent reimbursement transactions using the machines, police allege.\par \par \uc2 They will further allege just before 6pm last night officers attempted to intercept a stolen motorbike travelling dangerously in Wishart.\par \par \uc2 Police took the man into custody at a fuel station.\par \par \uc2 He was subsequently charged with 17 counts of enter premises and commit by break, 13 counts of fraud, four counts of receiving tainted property, two counts of possess dangerous drugs, and one count each of unlawful use of motor vehicle, stealing, dealing identification, possess tainted property, and possess utensil.\par \par \uc2 He is due to appear at Brisbane magistrates court today.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.05am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.21am GMT\par \par \uc2 Coalition wants government to pass social media ban for children by end of the month\par \par \uc2 The Coalition is challenging the government to rush through its proposed ban on children using social media as quickly as possible, with shadow communications minister \uc2 David Coleman\uc2  saying they want it passed by the end of this month.\par \par \uc2 Coleman, who has long called for age restrictions on social media, told a press conference that the legislation must be \u8220\'20\'1Cstrong\u8221\'20\'1D and without \u8220\'20\'1Cloopholes\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 but also said the opposition wanted it drafted and passed through both houses of parliament this month. He said:\par \par \uc2 This is an issue which we believe is urgent.\par \par \uc2 One kink in that plan \u8211\'20\'13 the government\u8217\'20\'19s $6.5m trial of age assurance technology hasn\u8217\'20\'19t even begun. So we don\u8217\'20\'19t yet know whether the government\u8217\'20\'19s plan is feasible, or if it is, what type of technologies or information \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , TikTok, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  or \uc2 Instagram\uc2  would need to block their platforms to those under 16.\par \par \uc2 Anthony Albanese and communications minister \uc2 Michelle Rowland\uc2  this morning didn\u8217\'20\'19t rule out biometric scanning or having personal documents made available to the platforms to meet that age assurance barrier.\par \par \uc2 Coleman said the Coalition wanted the plan to be bipartisan, but he didn\u8217\'20\'19t say what trade-offs in terms of privacy would be acceptable for the opposition. He claimed companies like \uc2 Facebook\uc2  already did age verification in some circumstances, but said the government should legislate \u8220\'20\'1Ca standard which is sufficiently strong to be meaningful\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Coleman said:\par \par \uc2 Let\u8217\'20\'19s be very clear to \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , let\u8217\'20\'19s be very clear to Tiktok, let\u8217\'20\'19s be very clear to \uc2 Snapchat\uc2 , you are captured by this. Your products we believe, as Australians, are not safe for younger children, and we\u8217\'20\'19re going to do everything we sensibly can to require you to protect those Australian children.\par \par \uc2 It won\u8217\'20\'19t be perfect. Will people be able to point to examples of imperfections, sure. Will it be infinitely better than what we have today? Absolutely. And that\u8217\'20\'19s why it\u8217\'20\'19s so important that we get this done.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.59am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.11am GMT\par \par \uc2 Public sector employees reach 2.5 million people with wages of $232bn a year\par \par \uc2 Federal and state governments employed 2,517,900 public sector staff as of June, the ABS reported today, up 3.6% for the fiscal year.\par \par \uc2 According to the ABS \u8216\'20\'18s June employment figures, jobs in the economy totalled 14.406 million, up 2.8% for the year. The private sector provides the bulk of jobs but such hires obviously grew more slowly than for the public sector \u8211\'20\'13 perhaps at a pace of about 2.5%, give or take.\par \par \uc2 Public sector wages, meanwhile, rose 8%, to $232bn for the 2023-24 fiscal year. Overall wages were up 4.1%, according to the separate Wage Price Index, so government employees enjoyed twice that increase. (We\u8217\'20\'19ll get September quarter WPI next Wednesday, which will be closely watched by employees, among others.)\par \par \uc2 Other interesting stats include the fact state governments employ about four in five public sector workers (1.94 million), with about 15% hired by the commonwealth and the rest working for local governments. The federal wage bill rose 10% for the year.\par \par \uc2 Some of the wage increase will reflect an end to some of the Covid-era wage caps (for example in New South Wales). The higher spending, though, did add to demand in the economy (and hence, make it a bit harder for the Reserve Bank to cut interest rates).\par \par \uc2 According to \uc2 UBS\uc2 , an investment bank, the bigger staff bill added about 2% to GDP. (Overall GDP rose 1.5%, so subtracting that contribution, the economy would have shrunk.)\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn Australia, growth in nominal government spending will soon average about 8% year on year for about a decade,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 UBS\uc2  said, adding the \uc2 RBA\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cwill keep interest rates higher-for-longer\u8221\'20\'1D as a result.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.31am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.56am GMT\par \par \uc2 Labor will seek to publish sealed robodebt findings at \u8216\'20\'18first appropriate opportunity\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 The government will seek to publish the sealed section of the robodebt royal commission \u8220\'20\'1Cat the first appropriate opportunity\u8221\'20\'1D once they have appointed and then consulted a National Anti-Corruption Commission (NACC) decision maker.\par \par \uc2 A statement released Thursday afternoon by attorney general Mark Dreyfus explained the process by which the government intends to still go ahead publishing the confidential additional chapter of the robodebt royal commission report:\par \par \uc2 In her 7 July 2023 letter to the Governor-General presenting the final report of the Robodebt Royal Commission, the Hon Catherine Holmes AC SC recommended an additional chapter \u8220\'20\'1Cremain sealed and not be tabled with the rest of the report so as not to prejudice the conduct of any future civil action or criminal prosecution\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 With civil and criminal matters largely complete, the Government had planned to table the additional chapter in the House of Representatives in the current sitting week.\par \par \uc2 Last week the Inspector of the National Anti-Corruption Commission (NACC) recommended that the NACC appoint an appropriate person to remake the decision on whether to investigate six people named in the confidential additional chapter, and the NACC accepted the recommendation.\par \par \uc2 The Government will now seek advice from the NACC decision maker, once appointed, on whether the tabling of the confidential additional chapter would prejudice any current or future decision of the NACC.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.55am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.48am GMT\par \par \uc2 Labor \u8216\'20\'18trying to out-Dutton \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 on deportation, Greens say\par \par \uc2 The Greens\u8217\'20\'19 immigration spokesperson, \uc2 David Shoebridge\uc2 , has commented on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/nov/07/labor-immigration-detention-bill-tony-burke-pay-countries-unlawful-citizens"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Labor\u8217\'20\'19s new deportation bill }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He said:\par \par \uc2 This bill gives the government fresh powers to pay countries like Nauru and PNG to take people against their will if the Minister orders them to be removed from Australia.\par \par \uc2 We already know that people who have been forcibly removed from Australia to Nauru and PNG live with untreated trauma, entrenched poverty and few if any rights. If this law passes then people who have come to Australia having fled persecution from Iran, Afghanistan or Russia will face either indefinite detention or removal to countries that have been bribed to take them.\par \par \uc2 Just 24 hours after we saw a politician take power in the US on a platform of punching down against migrants we have the Albanese government doing the same with this bill. We invite Labor to withdraw this Bill, stop trying to out-Dutton \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2  on migration and respect the decision of the High Court.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.28am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.37am GMT\par \par \uc2 Penny Wong\uc2 : \u8216\'20\'18We don\u8217\'20\'19t want innocent Palestinians to starve\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Returning back to Senate estimates and just after lunch the topic of \uc2 Unrwa\uc2  funding reared again.\par \par \uc2 In case you need a quick refresher, the \uc2 UN Relief and Works Agency\uc2 , or \uc2 Unrwa\uc2 , has been providing food, water and medicine for the more than 1.9 million displaced Palestinians in the Gaza Strip since the recent conflict began in October last year.\par \par \uc2 The Israeli parliament voted to ban the aid agency and designate it a terror group last month after a report found nine \uc2 Unrwa\uc2  staff in Gaza \u8220\'20\'1Cmay have been\u8221\'20\'1D involved in the 7 October \uc2 Hamas\uc2  attacks that led to the deaths of more than 1,200 Israelis and the kidnapping of hundreds more.\par \par \uc2 Responding to the announcement, the foreign affairs minister, \uc2 Penny Wong\uc2 , said \uc2 Unrwa\uc2  did \u8220\'20\'1Clife-saving work\u8221\'20\'1D while urging Israel to \u8220\'20\'1Ccomply with the binding orders of the ICJ to enable the provision of basic services and humanitarian assistance at scale in Gaza\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The Liberal senator, Clare Chandler, asked what the government\u8217\'20\'19s rationale behind the statement was.\par \par \uc2 Wong replied:\par \par \uc2 Well, we don\u8217\'20\'19t want children to starve... we don\u8217\'20\'19t want innocent Palestinians to starve. And let me just take you through some of what we know. We know that there is not enough humanitarian assistance entering Gaza. We know that the humanitarian situation in Gaza is unacceptable. We know that the ICJ [international court of justice] and international law requires Israel to facilitate aid into Gaza, but that has been getting worse. Insufficient aid has been allowed through.\par \par \uc2 Chandler said Australia\u8217\'20\'19s statement was urging Israel to keep \uc2 Unrwa\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Creserve privileges and immunities\u8221\'20\'1D untouched despite its alleged ties to \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Wong said:\par \par \uc2 One thing I never hear from you, Senator, I never hear you talk about the experience of civilians in Gaza. I never hear you talking about the children. I never hear you talking about polio, or the number of trucks entering, or the number of people displaced, or the number of people who face food insecurity. Those are the realities.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.44am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.34am GMT\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18It does reflect data\u8217\'20\'19: Ahpra chief on push for GP check ups for older doctors\par \par \uc2 The medical board\u8217\'20\'19s preferred option to ensure older doctors are practicing safely would be for them to get a regular GP check up after they turn 70, the board has told Senate estimates.\par \par \uc2 The Australian Health Practitioner Regulation agency (Ahpra) has been consulting on the issue. The CEO of Ahpra Martin Fletcher said:\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s really important to say this is not a mandatory retirement age, but it does reflect data.\par \par \uc2 Our data that shows an increase in complaints about the performance of doctors over 70, and also that those complaints are often more likely to result in regulatory action than for doctors under 70.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s fair to say there\u8217\'20\'19s been a lot of interest in this consultation. We\u8217\'20\'19ve had over 200 submissions, a lot of debate about this, and there is quite a lot of work to do, I think, obviously, to consider all those submissions.\par \par \uc2 One in 20 doctors practicing in Australia are over 70, according to Fletcher who said they make up 5.27% of registered practitioners.\par \par \uc2 Ranjana Srivastava has written for us on this topic, noting the absolute difference in complaints is small: in 2023, for every 1,000 doctors there were 69 complaints in the over-70 group and 38 in the under-70. Regulatory action occurred in 23% of complaints in the first group and 14% in the second. You can read more here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2024/oct/09/when-is-it-time-for-an-older-doctor-to-hang-up-their-stethoscope-we-owe-it-to-their-patients-to-get-it-right"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 When is it time for an older doctor to hang up their stethoscope? We owe it to our patients to get it right | Ranjana Srivastava }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.47am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.29am GMT\par \par \uc2 Thank you Tory and go well! Still plenty more news coming your way this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.27am GMT\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m running away to join the circus, Natasha May will be your sensible guide this afternoon. Thanks for being here!\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.27am GMT\par \par \uc2 Melbourne lord mayor says \u8216\'20\'18the best days for our city are ahead of us\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 More on that news about Melbourne lord mayor Nicholas Reece being re-elected:\par \par \uc2 Reece is linked to the Labor party and his deputy Roshena Campbell with the Liberals, formerly running for the party at the Aston byelection. But neither was running on their party\u8217\'20\'19s ticket.\par \par \uc2 Liberal Owen Guest has been elected to council, alongside \uc2 Gladys Liu\uc2 , who was on Anthony Koutoufides\u8217\'20\'19 ticket but was formerly the Liberal MP for the federal seat of Chisholm.\par \par \uc2 Greens councillor Olivia Ball has also been re-elected, along with Kevin Louey, who has served four terms and is considered a bit of a kingmaker.\par \par \uc2 Reece is holding a press conference outside Queen Victoria Market. He says:\par \par \uc2 I am so proud and humbled to have been elected the lord mayor of Melbourne. It is truly the honour of my life to be serving our city in this role I\u8217\'20\'19m looking forward to the next four years knowing that the best days for our city are ahead of us. It is truly the honour of my life to be serving our city in this role.\par \par \uc2 He thanks his deputy, Roshena Campbell, saying:\par \par \uc2 She\u8217\'20\'19s been by my side through this hard fought contest, and she\u8217\'20\'19s been nothing short of amazing. It is also no small thing that Roshena Campbell is the first person of Indian heritage to serve in the position of deputy lord mayor in the 182 year history of Melbourne. It\u8217\'20\'19s very much a sign of modern Melbourne that we are a diverse and inclusive city and a city of opportunity, where anything is possible.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.54am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.14am GMT\par \par \uc2 And that was that, the last question time until 18 November. Then only two weeks left this year!\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.41am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.12am GMT\par \par \uc2 Australia urges Israel to \u8216\'20\'18allow free, unfettered humanitarian access to Gaza\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Independent MP Sophie Scamps wants to know about Israel\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/nov/04/israel-formally-tells-un-of-intent-to-sever-all-ties-with-unrwa-relief-agency"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 banning of Unrwa }}}\uc2  and what the government can do to \u8220\'20\'1Censure that the people of Gaza do not die of starvation and preventable diseases such as polio\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Pat Conroy\uc2 , the defence industry minister, says the government supports \uc2 Unrwa\uc2  because they don\u8217\'20\'19t want civilians to starve to death:\par \par \uc2 Let me begin by condemning the attacks of October 7 and the resulting war and civilian deaths. We call for an immediate release of all hostages for an immediate ceasefire and for the free and unfettered flow of humanitarian aid. \uc2 Unrwa\uc2  does life-saving work, and that\u8217\'20\'19s why successive Australian governments have funded it since 1951.\par \par \uc2 The humanitarian situation in Gaza is catastrophic. Only \uc2 Unrwa\uc2  has the mandate and the infrastructure to receive and distribute aid on the scale that\u8217\'20\'19s needed right now.\par \par \uc2 The government will continue to urge a reversal of the decision, he says:\par \par \uc2 We call on Israel to allow free, unfettered humanitarian access to Gaza in line with the \uc2 International Court of Justice\uc2  ruling.\par \par \uc2 He says Australia has made its opposition clearly known, and continues to work through diplomatic channels to urge access for aid workers and supplies. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve provided a total of $94.55m in humanitarian funding to the region since October 23,\u8221\'20\'1D he says.\par \par \uc2 This includes $6m to \uc2 Unrwa\uc2 , which continues to flow, along with the other partner agencies we\u8217\'20\'19ve funded in Gaza, including \uc2 Unicef\uc2 , the \uc2 World Food Programme\uc2 , the Red Cross, the \uc2 UN\uc2  operations services and the \uc2 UN Population Fund\uc2 . We\u8217\'20\'19ve also provided our regular annual contribution of $20 million to \uc2 Unrwa\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.38am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.02am GMT\par \par \uc2 Attorney general says \u8216\'20\'18very high threshold\u8217\'20\'19 must be met before someone is release on parole\par \par \uc2 The Liberal MP \uc2 Melissa Price\uc2  is asking about a convicted terrorist released on parole into the community. She asks what their offence was, which state or territory they were released into, and whether they pose a threat.\par \par \uc2 Attorney general Mark Dreyfus says there is a \u8220\'20\'1Cvery high threshold\u8221\'20\'1D to be met before a person is released and they\u8217\'20\'19re put under strict supervision:\par \par \uc2 The decision is informed by advice from relevant agencies.\par \par \uc2 And the safety of the community is the most important consideration in these decisions. Parole keeps the community safer. It means mandatory controls and mandatory supervision are in place, which would otherwise not be available at the conclusion of someone\u8217\'20\'19s sentence.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are not going to talk about the details of individual offenders,\u8221\'20\'1D he says, when pushed for specifics, then talks about people released under the former Liberal government.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.28am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.54am GMT\par \par \uc2 Aukus is about advanced manufacturing as well as defence, Albanese says, because of the new submarines being developed.\par \par \uc2 He says:\par \par \uc2 It has a massive spin-offs as well that we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing through education with many Australians engaging already in the United States and in the UK. So this is about Australia\u8217\'20\'19s national interest. I\u8217\'20\'19ll always stand up for Australia\u8217\'20\'19s national interest.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.24am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.53am GMT\par \par \uc2 Bandt says Trump a \u8216\'20\'18dangerous demagogue\u8217\'20\'19 and asks Albanese about Aukus\par \par \uc2 Greens leader \uc2 Adam Bandt\uc2  asks about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s election win. He says the win is \u8220\'20\'1Cbad for women, bad for people of colour, bad for the climate, and bad for democracy\u8221\'20\'1D:\par \par \uc2 Many people are terrified about what having this dangerous demagogue in charge will mean for the world, including here in Australia. To help keep Australia safe, will you now cancel the Aukus nuclear submarine agreement so that we\u8217\'20\'19re not tied at the hip to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 Prime minister Anthony Albanese says the submarines are in Australia\u8217\'20\'19s national interest, because there\u8217\'20\'19s no more effective way for a country to defend itself than with submarines:\par \par \uc2 Because of stealth, because of the amount of time that they can stay under the water, because of undetectability, because of the depths they can dive and how long they can stay under for, in terms of Australia\u8217\'20\'19s national interest, I was convinced \u8211\'20\'13 and I was convinced through proper briefings with the defence department and with defence officials \u8211\'20\'13 that this is the correct way to go.\par \par \uc2 He says \u8220\'20\'1Coh, seriously\u8221\'20\'1D and deputy opposition leader \uc2 Sussan Ley\uc2  is booted from the chamber for interjecting.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.23am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.47am GMT\par \par \uc2 The Liberal National party MP \uc2 Llew O\u8217\'20\'19Brien\uc2  asks if any of those released into the community have been charged with sexual assault. Burke says that would mostly be up to state and territory authorities. He says:\par \par \uc2 The concept of people on visas who have committed crimes previously being in the community with no restrictions is what used to happen.\par \par \uc2 And that\u8217\'20\'19s why I give the example \u8211\'20\'13 you refer to those particular crimes \u8211\'20\'13 102 convicted sex offenders were released with no conditions into the community from detention by the leader of the opposition.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.19am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.42am GMT\par \par \uc2 Tony Burke: \u8216\'20\'18The cheapest option is to get them out of the country\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 The Nationals MP Pat Conaghan is asking the prime minister to confirm whether those released into the community are getting welfare and housing benefits. Home affairs minister Tony Burke says:\par \par \uc2 The most expensive option is when they are in detention. The next most expensive is when they are in the community, and the cheapest option is to get them out of the country.\par \par \uc2 The arrangements when they are in the community and the expenses involved there are using procedures that were put in place and that existed for the entirety of the previous government.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.18am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.39am GMT\par \par \uc2 Nicholas Reece re-elected as Melbourne lord mayor\par \par \uc2 Incumbent Melbourne lord mayor Nicholas Reece has been re-elected, with councillor Roshena Campbell to become his deputy.\par \par \uc2 Reece became lord mayor in July after \uc2 Sally Capp\uc2  stepped down.\par \par \uc2 His ticket received the most number of votes followed by the Greens, the Liberals and the former \uc2 AFL\uc2  footballer Anthony Koutoufides.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.23am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.38am GMT\par \par \uc2 Burke explains new immigration regulations introduced today\par \par \uc2 Treasurer \uc2 Jim Chalmers\uc2  was up answering a Dorothy dixer on inflation, when shadow treasurer \uc2 Angus Taylor\uc2  interjected, so Chalmers started talking about headline inflation \u8211\'20\'13 please see Peter Hannam\u8217\'20\'19s post earlier for an explanation.\par \par \uc2 Then there\u8217\'20\'19s a barney about emotion as a motive for questions, and health minister \uc2 Mark Butler\uc2  gets involved and\u8230\'20\'26 now we\u8217\'20\'19re back to inflation.\par \par \uc2 Nup, we\u8217\'20\'19ve moved on, shadow immigration spokesman \uc2 Dan Tehan\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s up on the high court\u8217\'20\'19s decision on ankle bracelets and curfews for former immigration detainees \u8211\'20\'13 see Paul Karp \u8217\'20\'19s extensive work below for catch ups.\par \par \uc2 Home affairs minister Tony Burke explains the new regulations he brought in this morning and says they\u8217\'20\'19re now being applied by the department. Legislation will also be introduced. He says:\par \par \uc2 First of all the decision from the high court that has been referred to is not the decision the government wanted, but it is a decision we prepared for so this morning I was with the governor general, signing in new regulations and those \u8230\'20\'26 allowed what was cut out by the high court yesterday to now go through reconsideration on a new test which is viewed as being legal, and for that new test to be applied to the full caseload.\par \par \uc2 The government\u8217\'20\'19s preference is for the people not to be here at all, he says:\par \par \uc2 And that is why we introduced powers today in the legislation to improve the government\u8217\'20\'19s capacity to remove people from this country in that situation.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.13am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.26am GMT\par \par \uc2 Government pressed on scam protections after announcing new laws\par \par \uc2 The independent MP Allegra Spender is asking about scam losses. She says:\par \par \uc2 94% of scam losses are borne by individuals and only 6% by banks. This is despite banks having far greater tools at their disposal to stop scams. Does the minister think that consumers are responsible for and have the ability to prevent 94% of scam losses? And if not, will you take action to make banks and other companies pay for losses in proportion to their ability to prevent them?\par \par \uc2 Stephen Jones, the assistant treasurer, says he introduced anti-scam legislation today. You can read all about that here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/money/2024/nov/07/banks-and-social-media-companies-to-be-fined-over-scams-under-new-australian-laws-touted-as-strongest-in-world"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Banks and social media companies to be fined over scams under new Australian laws touted as \u8216\'20\'18strongest in world\u8217\'20\'19 }}}\par \par \uc2 Spender says she specifically asked about who bears the losses. Jones says:\par \par \uc2 The focus of our legislation and the focus of the question is around how we prevent scams occurring in the first place. And when they do occur, how we ensure the victims are compensated.\par \par \uc2 And he says most scams originate on social media, so they need to be kept to account as well.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.01am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.22am GMT\par \par \uc2 Choice CEO stares down criticism from retailers\par \par \uc2 Returning to the first day of public hearings in the \uc2 ACCC\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s supermarkets inquiry, the head of \uc2 consumer advocacy group Choice\uc2  has stared down criticism from retailers.\par \par \uc2 In September, Choice released the results of the second quarterly, government-funded report on supermarket prices across Australia.\par \par \uc2 It found that the cost of both \uc2 Aldi\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s and \uc2 Coles\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 baskets - including specials - had decreased since Choice\u8217\'20\'19s first survey earlier this year, while the price of the \uc2 Woolworths\uc2  basket with specials had increased.\par \par \uc2 Choice prepared the report by visiting 104 supermarkets in June, including \uc2 Aldi\uc2 , \uc2 Coles\uc2 , \uc2 Woolworths\uc2 , and IGA, and recording the the prices of 14 common grocery items.\par \par \uc2 At the \uc2 ACCC\uc2  hearing today, Choice chief executive officer Ashley de Silva was asked about criticism by the \uc2 Australian Retailers Association\uc2  that Choice was not \u8220\'20\'1Ccomparing like for like\u8221\'20\'1D across brands and different stores.\par \par \uc2 De Silva disputed this, saying Choice made an effort to look at comparable items rather than the \u8220\'20\'1Cexact same product\u8221\'20\'1D in different stores.\par \par \uc2 He said:\par \par \uc2 There seems to be sometimes an underlying sentiment that we\u8217\'20\'19re not showing the best possible value or the cheapest possible products in any store.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re not looking to find the cheapest possible price. We\u8217\'20\'19re looking to understand general pricing practices at the stores and what value in a comparable basket looks like across stores.\par \par \uc2 So it\u8217\'20\'19s true that we may be buying a national brand from, say, a \uc2 Coles\uc2  or \uc2 Woolworths\uc2 , but because of \uc2 Aldi\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s model, be buying one of their house brands there.\par \par \uc2 We look at country of origin, we look at ingredients, nutritional information.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.52am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.17am GMT\par \par \uc2 Giles \u8216\'20\'18baffled\u8217\'20\'19 that opposition wants to criticise fee-free Tafe program\par \par \uc2 Deputy opposition leader \uc2 Sussan Ley\uc2  tries to ask skills and training minister Andrew Giles a question but also mentions his previous portfolio \u8211\'20\'13 immigration.\par \par \uc2 She can\u8217\'20\'19t refer to a former portfolio. So she restarts, addressing it to him just as the member for skills and training \u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 And it\u8217\'20\'19s about fee-free Tafe places anyway. She wants to know how many people have dropped out of courses.\par \par \uc2 (Yes, we have been through this many times this week.)\par \par \uc2 Giles says 580,000 people have enrolled, then repeats his explanation that the data isn\u8217\'20\'19t there yet for graduations.\par \par \uc2 Ley says:\par \par \uc2 The question addressed the number of dropouts of completions, the only thing the minister is addressing is commencements.\par \par \uc2 Giles says he\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cfrankly baffled\u8221\'20\'1D the opposition wants to talk the program down.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.51am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.11am GMT\par \par \uc2 Australian ambassador to China visited Tibet recently to raise human rights concerns, Senate estimates told\par \par \uc2 The Australian ambassador to China, Scott Dewar, made a hushed visit to Tibet last month to raise concerns over human rights violations, Senate estimates has revealed.\par \par \uc2 Daniel Sloper, a senior official in the Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade, told senators the ambassador visited the autonomous region from 20 to 24 October and met with executive vice chairman, Chen Yongqi, the region\u8217\'20\'19s third-most senior official.\par \par \uc2 Sloper said Dewar\u8217\'20\'19s visit was to \u8220\'20\'1Cexamine the local situation and also to raise Australia\u8217\'20\'19s concerns over the human rights there directly with senior officials\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The Liberal senator, \uc2 Simon Birmingham\uc2 , asked whether it was officially announced by the ambassador.\par \par \uc2 Sloper said he\u8217\'20\'19d have to check but \u8220\'20\'1Cit was certainly public knowledge in China. It was reported in domestic media\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Last month, James Larsen, Australia\u8217\'20\'19s ambassador to the United Nations, delivered a joint statement from 15 countries, including Australia, the US and the UK, on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/24/albanese-dismisses-beijings-claim-australia-plagued-by-systemic-racism-and-hate-crimes-ntwnfb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the \u8220\'20\'1Congoing concerns about serious human rights violations\u8221\'20\'1D in Xinjiang and Tibet }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 China\u8217\'20\'19s foreign ministry\uc2  shot back, claiming Australia is plagued by \u8220\'20\'1Csystemic racism\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Chate crimes\u8221\'20\'1D and that the statement was \u8220\'20\'1Cpolitical manipulation under the pretext of human rights\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.50am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.10am GMT\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18We\u8217\'20\'19re determined to get this right\u8217\'20\'19: Rowland on gambling ads\par \par \uc2 Rowland says they\u8217\'20\'19ll satisfy three key areas: Protecting children, breaking the nexus between sport and online wagering, and addressing \u8220\'20\'1Cthat particular cohort that is at risk which is young men who are being targeted with that advertising to a saturation level\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re determined to get this right,\u8221\'20\'1D she says, adding it will add to previous efforts including {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.betstop.gov.au/?gad_source=1&gclid=CjwKCAiAxKy5BhBbEiwAYiW--_4heE45n3AuFNVQZy2mAn2CsIZGsOxh4rHO-z8l-bHusAjrQi-oCBoCHUQQAvD_BwE&gclsrc=aw.ds"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 BetStop }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.49am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.07am GMT\par \par \uc2 Communications minister says \u8216\'20\'18we are making good progress\u8217\'20\'19 on gambling ad reforms\par \par \uc2 Communications minister \uc2 Michelle Rowland\uc2  is responding to a question about when the government plans to bring in gambling advertising reforms. She says they\u8217\'20\'19re carefully considering the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/article/2024/jun/20/labor-under-pressure-from-within-to-act-on-gambling-reform-as-one-year-anniversary-of-landmark-report-nears"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 late Peta Murphy\u8217\'20\'19s report }}}\uc2 . She says:\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m sure everyone in this place has constituents, everyone in this place knows someone who has been personally impacted by the scourge of what happens when people have addictive problems with gambling and the fact that this is costing our economy some $25bn a year.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s important to note that there are issues in this report that go not only to issues of federal responsibility, but also to states and territories, that is why we are doing this in a coordinated way and we are making good progress.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.44am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.05am GMT\par \par \uc2 Dutton says Trump is \u8216\'20\'18not somebody to be scared of\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2  is congratulating president Trump and the Republican party. The opposition leader says his party will ensure \u8220\'20\'1Cpresident Trump is not somebody to be scared of, but somebody that we can work very closely with\u8221\'20\'1D. He says it\u8217\'20\'19s a \u8220\'20\'1Chistoric outcome:\par \par \uc2 We want the United States to continue to be the great democracy that it is.\par \par \uc2 We have worked together closely with our friends through the \uc2 Anzus alliance\uc2 , through the Aukus arrangement, which the Coalition signed with the Americans and the Brits during our term in government.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.17am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.03am GMT\par \par \uc2 Albanese says he will ensure Australia always has a say in decisions that \u8216\'20\'18shape our world\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Albanese goes on to say:\par \par \uc2 As an ally, as an economic partner, as a stable democracy, as a leader in the region, and a power in our own right, my government will always make sure that Australia has a seat at the table and a say in the decisions that shape our region and that shape the world.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re investing in our defence capability, we are promoting security across our region, we are seizing economic opportunities so we reap the benefits for the Australian people. In all of these endeavours over decades, we have worked together with the United States.\par \par \uc2 Both sides of politics here in Australia, but both sides of politics in the United States as well. This has been to the enduring and mutual benefit of both of our nations and I look forward to working with president Trump in the future in the interests of both of our nations.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.28am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.02am GMT\par \par \uc2 Albanese speaks of 'very constructive' chat with Trump as question time begins\par \par \uc2 It was a rowdy question time yesterday, leading speaker Milton Dick to look like he wanted to roll his eyes, walk away, and shout at someone all at once.\par \par \uc2 So we\u8217\'20\'19ll see how today goes.\par \par \uc2 Prime minister Anthony Albanese is leading with his conversation with president-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 Just after 10am this morning, I spoke with president Trump and I congratulated him on his election victory. It was a very constructive discussion.\par \par \uc2 We affirmed the strong relationship between our two nations and committed to working together for the benefit of our people, including through Aukus. Our government\u8217\'20\'19s approach has always been about investing in our capabilities and investing in our relationships.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s true at a national level and it\u8217\'20\'19s also true at a personal one. As prime minister, I have made it a priority to invest in relationship with world leaders to build trust and respect in Australia\u8217\'20\'19s best interests. I built that with president Biden and I will do so with president Trump.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.42am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.53am GMT\par \par \uc2 Indigenous communities often struggle to access fresh food or eat three meals a day, hearing told\par \par \uc2 Consumer advocates have been giving evidence this morning at the first of a series of public hearings in the consumer watchdog\u8217\'20\'19s inquiry into the supermarket sector.\par \par \uc2 Martina Kingi, from the Indigenous Consumer Assistance Network, told the \uc2 Australian Competition and Consumer Commission\uc2  (\uc2 ACCC\uc2 ) that people in remote communities often struggled to access fresh food or eat three meals a day.\par \par \uc2 Kingi said:\par \par \uc2 If you can get two meals a day you\u8217\'20\'19re doing pretty well.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s very rare to eat a salad when you\u8217\'20\'19re in community, or even have any kind of fresh salad available.\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s been a few times where I\u8217\'20\'19ve... been in community for work and forgotten a tomato for my ham and cheese tomato sandwich or something, and gone to this store... and there\u8217\'20\'19s no tomatoes. So, it\u8217\'20\'19s a lot of packaged food.\par \par \uc2 Kingi said remote Indigenous communities had to deal with significantly inflated grocery prices and that:\par \par \uc2 There are some stores that don\u8217\'20\'19t even advertise the prices on their goods.\par \par \uc2 I was recently back home in Derby [in the Kimberly region of Western Australia] and there were plenty of products that did not have prices on them.\par \par \uc2 Kingi said privately-owned stores operating in remote communities needed to be more transparent about why they charged higher prices, beyond simply blaming freight costs, as some of them were just \u8220\'20\'1Cthere to make a buck\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Guardian Australia recently reported on an investigation by consumer advocacy body Choice that found remote communities were paying more than double the capital city prices for everyday groceries.\par \par \uc2 Choice representatives have also been giving evidence at the \uc2 ACCC\uc2  hearing today.\par \par \uc2 Read more:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/31/remote-indigenous-australians-paying-more-than-double-capital-city-prices-for-everyday-groceries"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Remote Indigenous Australians paying more than double capital city prices for everyday groceries }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.20am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.46am GMT\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18A ban isn\u8217\'20\'19t the answer\u8217\'20\'19: Tammy Tyrrell says social media ban won\u8217\'20\'19t address problems young people face\par \par \uc2 Independent senator Tammy Tyrrell has raised major questions over the government\u8217\'20\'19s proposal to ban kids under 16 from using social media, saying Australians have \u8220\'20\'1Cno idea\u8221\'20\'1D how the idea will even be enforced.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe policy behind the proposed social media ban is as deep as a kiddy pool. We have no idea what technology the government wants to use to enforce this. They want to rush this bill through before the tech trial has even begun and hope that it still checks all the boxes in 12 months time,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 The Tasmanian senator said she had kids of her own, and that she had \u8220\'20\'1Cset them up with social media accounts when they were younger.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/nov/07/australian-government-to-legislate-social-media-age-limit-of-16-but-cant-say-how-platforms-will-enforce-it"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Australia to legislate social media age limit of 16 \u8211\'20\'13 but can\u8217\'20\'19t say how platforms will enforce it }}}\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI had the same concerns about online content that parents have today,\u8221\'20\'1D Tyrrell said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA ban isn\u8217\'20\'19t the answer. It\u8217\'20\'19s not going to stop kids accessing social platforms and it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t actually address the problems young people are facing online. It\u8217\'20\'19s a feel-good moment for the older generations without asking the people affected what\u8217\'20\'19s best for them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.10am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.36am GMT\par \par \uc2 Private hospital sector in focus in Senate estimates\par \par \uc2 Shadow health minister \uc2 Anne Ruston\uc2  is asking the Senate estimates about health minister \uc2 Mark Butler\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 s comments about releasing the government\u8217\'20\'19s Private Hospital Sector Financial Health Check, a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/nov/01/australian-private-hospitals-say-government-review-shows-sector-isnt-profitable-as-it-struggles-to-keep-surgery-doors-open"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 review of the sector\u8217\'20\'19s viability, on Friday }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ruston raises concern about Butler\u8217\'20\'19s statement that \u8220\'20\'1Cthere will be no silver bullet from Canberra or funding solution from taxpayers to deal with what are essentially private pressures in this system\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Blair Comley, the department secretary, says, the financial health check led to a better fact base and the government is actively considering its issues:\par \par \uc2 One of the things that came out of that private financial health check is that this is by no means a uniform sector. There\u8217\'20\'19s a lot of variation, which was reported in the public document that was put out.\par \par \uc2 It also indicated that variation includes variation across different types of services, particularly called out obstetrics and mental health as being particular areas of challenge and a high level value.\par \par \uc2 So the government said there\u8217\'20\'19s no magic bullet. The minister made it clear that we would continue - we established in that process a CEO forum, which I chair, that has both private hospitals, private health insurers, a representative of the AMA and a representative of the states.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Read more about the Health Check here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/nov/01/australian-private-hospitals-say-government-review-shows-sector-isnt-profitable-as-it-struggles-to-keep-surgery-doors-open"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Australian private hospitals say government review shows sector isn\u8217\'20\'19t profitable as it struggles to \u8216\'20\'18keep surgery doors open\u8217\'20\'19 }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.05am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.26am GMT\par \par \uc2 Mining company pushes for judicial review of Plibersek\u8217\'20\'19s order to protect sacred site from Blayney gold project\par \par \uc2 The mining company behind the proposed McPhillamys gold project near Blayney in central western New South Wales has begun proceedings for a judicial review of the Aboriginal heritage protection order made by environment minister \uc2 Tanya Plibersek\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a statement to the \uc2 ASX\uc2  on Thursday, Perth-based \uc2 Regis Resources\uc2  said it had commenced formal legal proceedings in the federal court in respect of the decision, which was made under section 10 of the federal Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Heritage Protection Act 1984.\par \par \uc2 It is seeking a declaration that the s.10 is \u8220\'20\'1Clegally invalid\u8221\'20\'1D and that it be redetermined by a different minister. It\u8217\'20\'19s also seeking costs.\par \par \uc2 Plibersek {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/article/2024/aug/16/rare-order-given-to-protect-wiradjuri-sacred-site-from-goldmine-tailings-dam"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 made the partial s.10 declaration in August }}}\uc2  in response to an application by Wiradyuri elder Aunty Nyree Reynolds, a member of the Wiradyuri Traditional Owners Central West Aboriginal Corporation (WTOCWAC). The order blocked the construction of a proposed tailings dam, which \uc2 Regis\uc2  said \u8220\'20\'1Cmade the project in its current form unviable\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/12/tanya-plibersek-defends-aboriginal-heritage-order-blocking-irreversible-damage-of-goldmine-tailings-dam"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 statement of reasons released last month }}}\uc2 , Plibersek said the proposed tailings dam, which would have been built in the headwaters of the Belubula River and involved concreting over natural springs, would cause \u8220\'20\'1Cirreversible damage and permanent loss\u8221\'20\'1D to Aboriginal cultural heritage sites, particularly related to the Blue Banded Bee dreaming.\par \par \uc2 The Orange Local Aboriginal Land Council, supported by NSW Aboriginal Land Councils, said it was neutral on the mine but opposed the s.10 application, arguing that the responsibility for heritage protection should fall with Local Aboriginal Land Councils and branding WTOCWAC a \u8220\'20\'1Crebel\u8221\'20\'1D group. The federal laws allow for any traditional owner to make an application.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.54am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.20am GMT\par \par \uc2 More modern contraceptives and menopause therapies under consideration for \uc2 PBS\par \par \uc2 Advice on including more modern contraceptives and menopause hormone therapy on the Pharmaceutical Benefits Scheme (\uc2 PBS\uc2 ) is being prepared for the health minister, \uc2 Mark Butler\uc2 , who is \u8220\'20\'1Cvery concerned\u8221\'20\'1D about the issue, the health department says.\par \par \uc2 In the health department\u8217\'20\'19s Senate budget estimates, the Greens senator \uc2 Larissa Waters\uc2  about the lowest cost comparative test she says has come up as a barrier towards more modern contraceptives and menopause hormone therapy (also known as HRT) being listed on the PBS:\par \par \uc2 What policy work, if any, is being done in considering whether that rule should in fact be chucked out the window and a better rule that factors in people\u8217\'20\'19s quality of life be either added or replace that other rule?\par \par \uc2 Waters said the response from first assistant secretary Duncan McIntyre, that there was action in the area, was welcome news.\par \par \uc2 McIntyre said:\par \par \uc2 Certainly PBAC [Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee] has indicated this is an area of concern for them.\par \par \uc2 The minister has indicated he is very concerned about this issue as well so he has directed PBAC to provide him with advice about how that might be managed in a way that ensures that patient outcomes are considered in the broadest way appropriate.\par \par \uc2 One of the challenges for the PBAC process is that PBAC can only consider medicines for the listings on the \uc2 PBS\uc2  that sponsors choose to bring forward.\par \par \uc2 As a result of the minister\u8217\'20\'19s direction, PBAC has had a number of discussions including holding meetings with stakeholders about oral contraceptives and HRT medications.\par \par \uc2 PBAC is considering that information and is on notice to provide advice to the minister as a result.\par \par \uc2 The department told Waters the contraception medication Slinda is being considered for listing on the \uc2 PBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.50am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.10am GMT\par \par \uc2 Burke introduces bill to increase power to send non-citizens to other countries\par \par \uc2 The home affairs minister, Tony Burke, has introduced a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/search/display/display.w3p;page=0;query=BillId%3Ar7276%20Recstruct%3Abillhome"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bill }}}\uc2  to \u8220\'20\'1Cstrengthen the government\u8217\'20\'19s power to remove people who have had their visas cancelled to third countries\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 This bill is far more extensive than the removals bill that was stalled in the Senate earlier this year, and does far more more than supporting the government\u8217\'20\'19s power to reimpose electronic monitoring and curfews.\par \par \uc2 According to the explanatory memorandum, the bill:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Authorises the government to pay a third country to accept unlawful non-citizens from Australia; and\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Ensures that bridging visas granted to people released from immigration detention because they can\u8217\'20\'19t be removed are terminated when a mandatory notice is given to them that a country is willing to accept them.\par \par \uc2 It says:\par \par \uc2 If a person becomes an unlawful non-citizen due to the cessation of a [bridging visa R]... the person may be taken into immigration detention under section 189 of the Migration Act, and may be liable to be removed under section 198 of the Act. In certain circumstances, if a foreign country that is a party to a third country reception arrangement has granted permission for the person to enter and remain in that country, there would then be a real prospect that the person may be removed to that country under section 198 in the reasonably foreseeable future.\par \par \uc2 This is extremely significant. It responds to the NZYQ high court decision of November 2023 that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/nov/08/australia-high-court-indefinite-detention-ruling-government"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 indefinite detention is unlawful  }}}\uc2  where it is not practical to remove someone, which has resulted in the release of 224 people from immigration detention.\par \par \uc2 If this bill were passed, it appears Australia could pay Nauru or another country to offer a visa to these 224 people, and then use that offer to re-detain the person in immigration detention.\par \par \uc2 As the high court found in ASF17 that refusal to cooperate with removal means detention is lawful, this could see people detained indefinitely if they refuse to go to the third country (which they may have no links to).\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.19am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.02am GMT\par \par \uc2 Faruqi and Wong trade blows over Zomi Frankcom investigation\par \par \uc2 The Greens senator, Mehreen Faruqi, and foreign affairs minister, \uc2 Penny Wong\uc2 , traded blows in Senate estimates this morning over Australia\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/article/2024/aug/02/zomi-frankcom-aid-worker-killed-gaza-israel-compensation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 investigation into the killing of aid worker  }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/article/2024/aug/02/zomi-frankcom-aid-worker-killed-gaza-israel-compensation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Zomi Frankcom }}}\uc2  by \uc2 Israeli defence forces\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Faruqi asked for a progress update on efforts by Australian officials to retrieve audio evidence from Israel taken from an Israeli drone the day Frankcom was killed.\par \par \uc2 The Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade deputy secretary, Craig Maclachlan, said the department had sought the information but had not been given an audio transcript, nor the audio, of the incident. He said:\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s up to [Israel] to determine whether or not they wish to release it. It has not yet been released to us.\par \par \uc2 Wong added she had \u8220\'20\'1Cpersonally raised\u8221\'20\'1D the matter with her Israeli counterpart.\par \par \uc2 Faruqi responded it had been eight months since Frankcom\u8217\'20\'19s death and Wong had not called for an independent investigation into the incident. She said:\par \par \uc2 Don\u8217\'20\'19t shake your head disparagingly... at me. I repeat, minister, what is stopping you from doing anything real to hold Israel to account? Is it the pressure from the US? Is it the pressure from the pro-Israeli lobby? Is it because your government doesn\u8217\'20\'19t think brown lives are valuable?... \u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Wong began responding but was briefly interrupted by Faruqi. The minister asked for Faruqi to listen respectfully just as she had during the \u8220\'20\'1Cvery offensive tirade\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Wong continued:\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19ve spent my whole life advocating for equality, for an end to prejudice, in a world where every human being is valued, regardless of their cultural heritage, their faith, who they are, where they come from.\par \par \uc2 And I have the experience also of migration, and I\u8217\'20\'19ve spoken about this in the Senate, including in support of you. I regard the assertion that you made as deeply offensive and false, and it is an example of the way in which you are seeking to make really awful assertions in an attempt to make a political point. One of the things that has been lost in this debate is the capacity for people to disagree about issues without denigrating one another, but your default is to denigrate.\par \par \uc2 The two began speaking over each other until the committee chair, Raff Ciccone, ended Faruqi\u8217\'20\'19s questioning and handed the call to One Nation senator Malcolm Roberts.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.13am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.48am GMT\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  says age-assurance technology not reliable\par \par \uc2 The lack of grandfathering for existing users in age assurance, as flagged by the prime minister today, also suggests every existing user will need to prove their age, and \uc2 Meta\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Antigone Davis said that it will impact the whole population.\par \par \uc2 Davis said the company would comply with whatever final result the government lands on, but said that age-assurance technology had not been settled as a reliable technology yet:\par \par \uc2 The idea that somehow you can sort of force the industry to be in a technological place that it isn\u8217\'20\'19t is probably a bit misunderstood in terms of where the industry is.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.50am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.46am GMT\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  says government\u8217\'20\'19s social media ban poses a privacy risk\par \par \uc2 The parent company of \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  has warned that forcing every social media site to assure the age of users presents a privacy risk, given the need to assure age on every platform.\par \par \uc2 The prime minister, Anthony Albanese, announced on Thursday that social media sites will be required to ban users under 16, with no grandfathering for existing users. It will come into effect 12 months after legislation passes and the onus will be on platforms to implement age-assurance technology to check the ages of users.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/nov/07/australian-government-to-legislate-social-media-age-limit-of-16-but-cant-say-how-platforms-will-enforce-it"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Australia to legislate social media age limit of 16 \u8211\'20\'13 but can\u8217\'20\'19t say how platforms will enforce it }}}\par \par \uc2 Albanese has said there will be \u8220\'20\'1Cprivacy protections\u8221\'20\'1D in place to protect identifying information, but did not outline what those protections would be.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2 , which is arguing for app stores to conduct age assurance rather than the social media sites, released an \uc2 Ipsos\uc2  survey of Australian parents on Thursday showing while parents are overwhelmingly in favour of teens under 16 needing parental approval (82%), 70% would prefer this approval process to be done on the app store rather than for each individual app.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s global head of safety, Antigone Davis, told reporters that teens download as many as 40 apps, and requiring age assurance for each app would be complicated, time-consuming and present a privacy risk:\par \par \uc2 The current state of age-assurance technology... requires a level of personally identified information to be shared. It\u8217\'20\'19s usually in the form of an ID or document ID, documentation or biometric type data, facial feature data for young people, and if it\u8217\'20\'19s parental consent, the data that will be involved there to verify the parent is just another additional layer of data to establish.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.49am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.42am GMT\par \par \uc2 Minister calls off meeting with chair of Queensland\u8217\'20\'19s truth-telling inquiry\par \par \uc2 Queensland\u8217\'20\'19s truth-telling and healing inquiry says its minister has called off their first meeting.\par \par \uc2 Chair Joshua Creamer was scheduled to meet with minister for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander partnerships, Fiona Simpson, this afternoon. The new Liberal National party government has ordered a halt to the inquiry and plans to repeal its legislation in a few weeks. A spokesperson for the inquiry said:\par \par \uc2 The inquiry has today been advised by minister Simpson of the need to postpone the meeting with the truth telling and healing inquiry that was scheduled for this afternoon. There is no alternate date currently advised.\par \par \uc2 Creamer said yesterday he\u8217\'20\'19d been yet to speak to Simpson or premier David Crisafulli.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople have said to me, this is like the chief protector days, a single person deciding what\u8217\'20\'19s best for every Aboriginal and Torres Strait Island person in the state,\u8221\'20\'1D Creamer said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.47am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.24am GMT\par \par \uc2 Households face more bill stress as energy prices rise\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Australian Energy Regulator\uc2  (\uc2 AER\uc2 ) is warning that without systemic change, some Australians will continue to struggle to pay their power bills, \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports:\par \par \uc2 Releasing an annual {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aer.gov.au/system/files/2024-11/State%20of%20the%20energy%20market%202024%20-%20Full%20report_0.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 electricity and gas markets report }}}\uc2 , \uc2 AER\uc2  said affordability would likely be an \u8220\'20\'1Congoing challenge for the foreseeable future\u8221\'20\'1D. Chair Clare Savage said:\par \par \uc2 We recognise that more must be done to drive systemic change for energy consumers who struggle to pay their energy bills.\par \par \uc2 Debt levels and other indicators of financial difficulties are expected to escalate from late 2024 to early 2025, even more than anticipated.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are reviewing the payment difficulty protections that currently exist and considering how these protections could be strengthened,\u8221\'20\'1D Savage said.\par \par \uc2 Economist Nicki Hutley said no one should have to choose between cooling their home or putting food on the table in a predicted period of sweltering temperatures.\par \par \uc2 So-called \u8220\'20\'1Cgame changer\u8221\'20\'1D reforms were submitted to the nation\u8217\'20\'19s energy ministers almost a year ago, setting out a suite of proposals to break the cycle of energy debt, with some changes endorsed in July.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe know Aussie households and small businesses are doing it tough,\u8221\'20\'1D federal energy minister, Chris Bowen, said:\par \par \uc2 Our government is providing energy bill relief now while working to deliver an overdue reliable, modern energy grid that won\u8217\'20\'19t be exposed to international price spikes.\par \par \uc2 The report found retail energy prices were typically higher in regional and remote areas than in urban areas, with estimated annual customer electricity bills in 2023/24 ranging from $1,413 for a customer in urban Victoria to $3,102 in rural NSW.\par \par \uc2 The average amount of debt per customer increased most in NSW (17%), South Australia (10%) and Queensland (6%).\par \par \uc2 In gas markets, the regulator predicted supply risks in the short to medium term with gas to remain a critical fuel for electricity reliability and large industries for some time.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, consumers who can afford rooftop solar, batteries and electric vehicles have become an even more integral part of the energy system.\par \par \uc2 Residential solar in the national electricity market now exceeds 20 gigawatts \u8211\'20\'13 2.9GW more than last year \u8211\'20\'13 equivalent to 25% of generation capacity.\par \par \uc2 New wind, solar and storage installations are expected to triple in the next 12 months as people look for more ways to take power into their own hands.\par \par \uc2 But current consumer protections were designed for a one-way supply of electricity directly to a home or business and not a system where a customer consumes, trades and produces energy.\par \par \uc2 Savage said a report is due by the end of the year on next steps for additional help for consumers in energy stress.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.29am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.12am GMT\par \par \uc2 Labor reveals plans to electronically monitor some people freed from immigration detention\par \par \uc2 The government wants to reimpose ankle bracelets and curfews on some people released from immigration detention by legislating that the conditions are no longer the default, reversing amendments {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/nov/16/labor-emergency-immigration-detention-bill-strict-visa-conditions-electronic-monitors-curfews"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pushed by the Coalition and accepted by Labor }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On Thursday the home affairs minister, Tony Burke, made new {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.legislation.gov.au/F2024L01410/asmade/text/explanatory-statement"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 regulations }}}\uc2  to regain the power to impose the conditions \u8220\'20\'1Conly if the minister is satisfied\u8221\'20\'1D that the bridging visa holder \u8220\'20\'1Cposes a substantial risk of seriously harming any part of the Australian community by committing a serious offence\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 That is in response to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/nov/06/high-court-quashes-albanese-governments-ankle-bracelet-and-curfew-regime-for-former-immigration-detainees-ntwnfb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Wednesday\u8217\'20\'19s high court decision }}}\uc2  that the existing regulations breached the separation of powers and amounted to punishment.\par \par \uc2 The YBFZ decision is set to temporarily result in 150 non-citizens no longer being electronically monitored and 130 having their curfew lifted. A further {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/nov/07/up-to-29-people-freed-from-australian-immigration-detention-to-have-visa-breach-charges-dropped"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 27 will have charges dropped for alleged offences }}}\uc2  of breaching visa conditions, and two who were convicted may now have those overturned.\par \par \uc2 According to its explanatory memorandum, the new regulation introduces a \u8220\'20\'1Cconfined and specific test \u8230\'20\'26 related to protecting any part of the Australian community from serious harm\u8221\'20\'1D. It says:\par \par \uc2 The new test requires consideration of the risk of particular criminal conduct occurring and the nature, degree and extent of harm the [bridging visa R (BVR)] holder may pose to any part of the Australian community.\par \par \uc2 Four visa conditions \u8211\'20\'13 financial and debt reporting, ankle bracelets and curfews \u8211\'20\'13 can now only be imposed \u8220\'20\'1Cif the minister is satisfied on the balance of probabilities that the BVR holder poses a substantial risk of seriously harming any part of the Australian community by committing a serious offence\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Serious offences include those punishable by imprisonment of at least five years and involving loss of life, serious personal injury, sexual assault, child abuse material, child sex offences, family or domestic violence, inciting violence, or people smuggling or human trafficking.\par \par \uc2 The minister must also be \u8220\'20\'1Csatisfied that the imposition of the condition(s) is, on the balance of probabilities, reasonably necessary, and reasonably appropriate and adapted for the purpose of protecting any part of the Australian community by addressing that substantial risk\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The explanatory memorandum said the new regulation clarifies the \u8220\'20\'1Cprotective purpose\u8221\'20\'1D of the visa conditions \u8220\'20\'1Cby referring to an objective way of demonstrating whether the offences that the minister is concerned with are serious or not\u8221\'20\'1D:\par \par \uc2 This is in contrast to the way the invalid provision had purported to operate previously, which was that the minister was required to impose the conditions unless satisfied that the imposition of the conditions were not reasonably necessary to protect any part of the Australian community.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.30am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.10am GMT\par \par \uc2 Record number of medicines submitted for approval\par \par \uc2 The health department has received a record number of submissions for new medicine approvals from pharmaceutical companies for its upcoming meeting, more than double the previous record for a single meeting.\par \par \uc2 The \u8220\'20\'1Cunprecedented\u8221\'20\'1D number of submissions will not be able to be dealt with in the meeting and will require an additional meeting \u8211\'20\'13 the health department is unsure whether the situation is a \u8220\'20\'1Cone-off spike\u8221\'20\'1D or \u8220\'20\'1Cthe new normal\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The March 2025 Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee (PBAC) meeting received a total of 63 submissions, of which 51 will require economic evaluations which need to be undertaken by contracted external evaluators, the department has told Senate health estimates.\par \par \uc2 Caroline Turnour, the assistant secretary from the department\u8217\'20\'19s office of health technology assessment (HTA) said:\par \par \uc2 That is by far a record number for one meeting, what we have described as unprecedented. The largest number we\u8217\'20\'19ve ever had before is 25.\par \par \uc2 PBAC \u8211\'20\'13 a group of clinicians, health economists, consumer representatives and industry representatives \u8211\'20\'13 considered 32 major submissions would fully take up the capacity of the March meeting.\par \par \uc2 The department said the minister yesterday announced an additional meeting of PBAC to be called in May to allow the submissions that couldn\u8217\'20\'19t be considered in March to be considered.\par \par \uc2 The deputy secretary, Penny Shakespeare, said it was an issue of external health technology assessment (HTA) experts, not the department\u8217\'20\'19s capacity.\par \par \uc2 Blair Comley, the secretary of the entire department, said it was highly specialised work with a limited labour pool of people who are qualified to ensure the quality of the assessment is not compromised. He said:\par \par \uc2 We don\u8217\'20\'19t know if this is a one-off spike or this is the new normal, we don\u8217\'20\'19t know that.\par \par \uc2 However, the conversation I\u8217\'20\'19ve had with the team is we don\u8217\'20\'19t want to be in a position in the future if we get a spike that we\u8217\'20\'19re not able to manage it.\par \par \uc2 So the team is actively looking at how we expand our HTA capacity in Australia. I\u8217\'20\'19ve also asked the question about do we have to have contingency arrangements for international evaluators as a safety net for a future spike.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.19am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.07am GMT\par \par \uc2 Nine Entertainment to cut $50m more in costs\par \par \uc2 Nine Entertainment\u8217\'20\'19s acting CEO \uc2 Matt Stanton\uc2  has announced another $50m cut to costs just months after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/article/2024/jun/28/nine-job-cuts-200-publishing-smh-the-age-afr"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cutting 200 jobs }}}\uc2  from the media business.\par \par \uc2 Speaking at the annual general meeting, Stanton said a program dubbed Nine2028 would be established to make changes to the company\u8217\'20\'19s operation, as the share price has almost halved this year.\par \par \uc2 The broadcasting division is down 9% on the 22/23 financial year and publishing is down 3%. Stanton told investors:\par \par \uc2 Like you, we are disappointed by the market valuation of Nine, which seems too heavily focused on our traditional television business while largely ignoring the digital growth opportunity of our key assets across publishing, streaming, audio and marketplaces.\par \par \uc2 We have committed to take a further $50m of underlying costs out in FY25 \u8211\'20\'13 equating to a two-year total of around $100m.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.28am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.51am GMT\par \par \uc2 Josh Butler is across the (existing) detail of the government\u8217\'20\'19s plans to stop children under 16 accessing social media:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/nov/07/australian-government-to-legislate-social-media-age-limit-of-16-but-cant-say-how-platforms-will-enforce-it"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Australian government to legislate social media age limit of 16 but can\u8217\'20\'19t say how platforms will enforce it }}}\par \par \uc2 And here\u8217\'20\'19s the video of Albanese making the announcement:\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.40am GMT\par \par \uc2 Albanese confirms conversation with Trump this morning\par \par \uc2 The prime minister, Anthony Albanese, has confirmed in a post on X that he spoke with US president-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  this morning:\par \par \uc2 Good to speak this morning with president Trump to personally congratulate him on his election victory. We talked about the importance of the alliance and the strength of the Australia-US relationship in security, trade and investment. I look forward to working together in the interests of both of our countries.\par \par \uc2 enltrGood to speak this morning with President Trump to personally congratulate him on his election victory.\par \par \uc2 We talked about the importance of the Alliance, and the strength of the Australia-US relationship in security, AUKUS, trade and investment.\par \par \uc2 I look forward to working\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Anthony Albanese (@AlboMP) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AlboMP/status/1854320237656609027?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 7, 2024 }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.46am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.32am GMT\par \par \uc2 Dfat \u8216\'20\'18reluctant\u8217\'20\'19 to outline how many people being investigated within passport office\par \par \uc2 The Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade (Dfat) is \u8220\'20\'1Creluctant\u8221\'20\'1D to outline the number of people under investigation within its passport office, noting the numbers \u8220\'20\'1Cmay change\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Last week, the \uc2 Australian National Audit Office\uc2  revealed at least 18 passport office officials were facing investigation after an audit report found conflicts of interest were not declared and preferred suppliers had wrongly been identified ahead of time across $1.6bn in contracts.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/nov/01/australia-passport-officials-apo-investigation-audit-report-ntwnfb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 At least 18 federal passport office officials under investigation after scathing audit report }}}\par \par \uc2 The department\u8217\'20\'19s chief operating officer, Clare Walsh, said the number of officials being looked at \u8220\'20\'1Cmay change\u8221\'20\'1D as Dfat was \u8220\'20\'1Cstill going through the detailed investigation that is warranted\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m very reluctant to say it\u8217\'20\'19s 18 at this stage of the investigation,\u8221\'20\'1D Walsh said.\par \par \uc2 The deputy secretary, Craig Maclachlan, said Dfat had a \u8220\'20\'1Cpreference\u8221\'20\'1D for \uc2 ANAO\uc2  not to reveal the number but the audit report went ahead and published it anyway.\par \par \uc2 Walsh said:\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m reluctant to say whether the number\u8217\'20\'19s bigger or smaller, because we just haven\u8217\'20\'19t... aren\u8217\'20\'19t at that stage in the investigation where I could be confident in providing an accurate answer to.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.38am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.26am GMT\par \par \uc2 Australian shares flat as traders digest Wall Street surge\par \par \uc2 Australian shares were flat early on Thursday, as traders digested the US election-fuelled surge on Wall Street overnight.\par \par \uc2 The S&P/ASX 200 was trading at the 8,200 mark shortly after trading commenced, in line with opening levels. Some of the exuberance seen on US equity markets was already replicated on the \uc2 ASX\uc2  late yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Shares in \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  were down more than 2% after the release of its annual results, while shares in \uc2 Sigma Healthcare\uc2  surged 30% after the competition regulator said it would not oppose its planned merger with \uc2 Chemist Warehouse\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While equities and crypto assets soared in the US after it became clear \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would win the US presidential race, bond prices fell as investors fretted that the incoming president\u8217\'20\'19s spending policies and promised tariffs could fuel another bout of inflation.\par \par \uc2 The US dollar has been strengthening against its global counterparts, including the Australian dollar.\par \par \uc2 A trade war between the US and China would also have a significant impact on Australian companies, especially in the resources sector. Australian iron ore miners including \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2 , \uc2 BHP\uc2  and \uc2 Fortescue\uc2  were all trading either flat or slightly positive early on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.32am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.18am GMT\par \par \uc2 Penny Wong\uc2  says Greens have \u8216\'20\'18no plan\u8217\'20\'19 to keep Australia safe when questioned about US alliance\par \par \uc2 The foreign affairs minister, \uc2 Penny Wong\uc2 , has dismissed questions by the Greens in Senate estimates this morning about Australia\u8217\'20\'19s ties with the US.\par \par \uc2 After the election of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for a second term in the US yesterday, Greens senator Jordon Steele-John asked Wong whether the Albanese government was considering, or re-thinking, Australia\u8217\'20\'19s military relationship with the US.\par \par \uc2 Wong was initially curt but went on to say Labor and the Greens \u8220\'20\'1Cfundamentally don\u8217\'20\'19t share the same views\u8221\'20\'1D about Australia\u8217\'20\'19s strategic interests and the minor party is \u8220\'20\'1Cout of step with any rational assessment of Australia\u8217\'20\'19s interests in relation to Aukus\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Wong continued:\par \par \uc2 We don\u8217\'20\'19t think Australia is safer in the world by walking away from partnerships and alliances. We think fundamentally, a middle power such as Australia, we work within our alliance with the US, and we work in our partnerships, which we are seeking to strengthen and deepen with many other countries. That is what assures Australia\u8217\'20\'19s safety and security, not domestic yelling and going it alone.\par \par \uc2 The shadow foreign affairs minister, \uc2 Simon Birmingham\uc2 , then asked Wong what she thought Australians should know about the Greens\u8217\'20\'19 policies.\par \par \uc2 Wong responded:\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19ve said publicly previously \u8230\'20\'26 the Greens have no plan, no practical plan, to keep our country safe or strong or in difficult times.\par \par \uc2 Birmingham responded: \u8220\'20\'1CIndeed.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.37am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.11am GMT\par \par \uc2 Here is the prime minister, Anthony Albanese, congratulating president-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 you can see some more responses from political leaders as his win started looking likely in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/nov/06/us-election-2024-abbott-morrison-australian-mps-praise-trump-likely-victory"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 this piece from  }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/nov/06/us-election-2024-abbott-morrison-australian-mps-praise-trump-likely-victory"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Paul Karp }}}\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.14am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.58pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Health department says concession card holders will benefit from \uc2 PBS\uc2  freeze\par \par \uc2 Concession card holders will benefit from the freeze on their \uc2 PBS\uc2  co-payments for five years, the health department has told Senate estimates this morning.\par \par \uc2 Nikolai Tsyganov, the assistant secretary for pricing and Pharmaceutical Benefits Scheme (PBS) policy, affirmed the current \uc2 PBS\uc2  general co-payment for Medicare card holders is $31.60 and for concession card holders is $7.70.\par \par \uc2 Labor senator Louise Pratt asked how long they are frozen for, to which Tsyganov replied:\par \par \uc2 The current 2024 level of \uc2 PBS\uc2  level of co-payments is frozen for all patients for 2025 \u8211\'20\'13 so both general and concessional patients. But concessional patients will benefit from the freeze for five years. So the $7.70 will be frozen for five years.\par \par \uc2 Australians have saved an estimated $484.4m in 2023 due to the reduction in \uc2 PBS\uc2  general co-payment \u8211\'20\'13 based on 5.36 million people who have benefited across 38.7m prescriptions, he also said.\par \par \uc2 From the move to 60-day prescriptions for 300 medicines, patients have saved an additional $86m, he said.\par \par \uc2 From July 2022 through to November 2024 there have been 265 new medicines listed on the \uc2 PBS\uc2  where new budget funding has been approved by government, and an additional 476 new and amended medicines which fit within the existing PBS budget, Tsyganov said.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.08am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.43pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Nine chair thanks Peter Costello and \uc2 Mike Sneesby\par \par \uc2 The Nine Entertainment chair, Catherine West, has thanked former chair Peter Costello and former chief executive \uc2 Mike Sneesby\uc2  for their contribution to the media company at the first annual general meeting since the departure of the two men.\par \par \uc2 West said Costello played a key role in securing the news media bargaining code and Nine\u8217\'20\'19s merger with \uc2 Fairfax Media\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/article/2024/jun/09/peter-costello-resigns-as-chairman-of-nine-entertainment-effective-immediately"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Peter Costello resigns as chairman of Nine Entertainment \u8216\'20\'18effective immediately\u8217\'20\'19 }}}\par \par \uc2 She said:\par \par \uc2 Mike started Stan nearly 10 years ago and progressed to CEO, during which time Nine strengthened its place in digital media in Australia, secured the rights of the Olympic Games through to Brisbane 2032 while overseeing the company\u8217\'20\'19s increased focus on values and purpose.\par \par \uc2 West said Nine was undergoing a recruitment process for a new CEO.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.26am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.33pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Headline or underlying: which is the \uc2 RBA\uc2  targeting?\par \par \uc2 Among the many faux fights coming out of Canberra is whether the Reserve Bank is \u8220\'20\'1Cat war\u8221\'20\'1D with the treasurer, \uc2 Jim Chalmers\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Given there are real wars in Europe and the Middle East, use of \u8220\'20\'1Cwar\u8221\'20\'1D was always hyperbole. Anyway, it is fair to say there is a trade-off between more government spending (eg election promises) and interest rates falling as fast as many would like. (See {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/nov/05/bullock-is-sending-a-message-to-politicians-rate-cuts-or-big-spending-promises-you-can-only-have-one"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  more on this issue here }}}\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 Anyway, some media (eg {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/nov/05/bullock-is-sending-a-message-to-politicians-rate-cuts-or-big-spending-promises-you-can-only-have-one"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Australian }}}\uc2  ) jumped on a bit of nerdy issue \u8211\'20\'13 whether Chalmers had contradicted the \uc2 RBA\uc2  over whether the central bank was targeting headline or underlying inflation.\par \par \uc2 Asked about this matter, Bullock said the \uc2 RBA\uc2  was actually talking about headline inflation when the bank talks about getting inflation back to between 2% and 3%. That said, the \uc2 RBA\uc2  uses the underlying rate \u8220\'20\'1Cto give us an indication where headline inflation is trending\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 As we have seen, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/oct/30/australia-annual-inflation-rate-september-quarter-cpi-interest-rates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 headline inflation is back within that band }}}\uc2 , as of the September quarter. So why isn\u8217\'20\'19t the \uc2 RBA\uc2  considering an interest rate cut?\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe key word is \u8216\'20\'18sustainably\u8217\'20\'19,\u8221\'20\'1D Bullock said. Yes, CPI is down but that\u8217\'20\'19s mostly because of energy rebates (state and federal), and unless they are extended headline inflation will start to rise from the middle of 2025.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 RBA\uc2  can\u8217\'20\'19t model what hasn\u8217\'20\'19t been announced. Presumably there will be some \u8220\'20\'1Ccost of living relief\u8221\'20\'1D promised by major parties going into next year\u8217\'20\'19s federal election.\par \par \uc2 When they get announced, we\u8217\'20\'19ll be back asking about how \u8220\'20\'1Csustainable\u8221\'20\'1D those rebates are, and hence where headline inflation is heading.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.27am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.27pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Kevin Rudd\uc2  deletes tweets critical of \uc2 Donald Trump\par \par \uc2 The former prime minister \uc2 Kevin Rudd\uc2 , who is now Australian ambassador to the US, has deleted social media posts critical of president-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to avoid the comments \u8220\'20\'1Cbeing misconstrued\u8221\'20\'1D, the foreign affairs department has confirmed.\par \par \uc2 In Senate estimates on Thursday, a Dfat official said a statement from his private office account confirmed the \u8220\'20\'1Cpast commentaries\u8221\'20\'1D had been removed from his personal website and social media channels \u8220\'20\'1Cout of respect\u8221\'20\'1D for the office of president of the United States and following the election of Trump.\par \par \uc2 The statement, which was read out in estimates, said:\par \par \uc2 This has been done to eliminate the possibility of such comments being misconstrued as reflecting his positions as ambassador and, by extension, the views of the Australian government. Ambassador Rudd looks forward to working with President Trump and his team to continue strengthening the US-Australia alliance.\par \par \uc2 This morning, the prime minister, Anthony Albanese, said Rudd was doing a \u8220\'20\'1Cterrific job\u8221\'20\'1D as ambassador.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt says a lot about how important we regard the relationship with the United States that we appointed a former prime minister,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 In a now-deleted tweet, Rudd called Trump \u8220\'20\'1Cthe most destructive president in history\u8221\'20\'1D and that he \u8220\'20\'1Cdrags America and democracy through the mud\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/mar/20/donald-trump-calls-kevin-rudd-nasty-and-says-he-wont-be-there-long-as-australias-ambassador-to-us"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an interview in March }}}\uc2 , Trump called Rudd \u8220\'20\'1Cnasty\u8221\'20\'1D and claimed he \u8220\'20\'1Cwon\u8217\'20\'19t be there long\u8221\'20\'1D as ambassador.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.37pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.15pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Ray Hadley to retire from 2GB\par \par \uc2 Former cab driver and rugby league caller Ray Hadley has announced he will step down from his 2GB mornings program next month after clocking up a record-breaking 160 consecutive ratings wins. He told his listeners:\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s been a hell of a ride from a young bloke who wanted to call the races.\par \par \uc2 But the time has come for someone else to do the job.\par \par \uc2 My own four children made many sacrifices for me over the years, allowing me to realise my ambitions, and I want to spend more time with them and my seven grandchildren.\par \par \uc2 Nine Radio\u8217\'20\'19s content manager, Luke Davis, said Hadley\u8217\'20\'19s contribution to Australian radio and millions of listeners over the years can be marked by volume, impact and tenure, telling staff:\par \par \uc2 Ray was the founding member of the Continuous Call Team \u8211\'20\'13 which has been our country\u8217\'20\'19s leading rugby league radio coverage for the past 38 seasons \u8211\'20\'13 inventing the \u8216\'20\'18fun & football\u8217\'20\'19 format that has proven so successful on weekends.\par \par \uc2 Ray has amassed 99 State of Origin and 35 grand final game calls.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.22pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.08pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Penny Wong\uc2  says Australian government still 'seeking a call' from \uc2 Donald Trump\par \par \uc2 In the foreign affairs and trade committee in Senate estimates this morning, the foreign minister, \uc2 Penny Wong\uc2 , said the Australian government is still \u8220\'20\'1Cseeking a call\u8221\'20\'1D from president-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  after his win on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The shadow foreign affairs minister, \uc2 Simon Birmingham\uc2 , said the UK prime minister, \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2 , the Indian prime minister, \uc2 Narendra Modi\uc2 , the Israeli prime minister, \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 , and Saudi Arabian crown prince \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2  had all noted they had received calls from Trump.\par \par \uc2 Wong said she would update once the call occurred with the prime minister, Anthony Albanese. A Dfat official said Albanese had not met with Trump previously.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.29am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.06pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Greens senator \uc2 Nick McKim\uc2  says Trump an economic \u8216\'20\'18wrecking ball\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 So WA Liberal senator \uc2 Dean Smith\uc2  kicked off the questions about Trump, and Greens senator for Tasmania \uc2 Nick McKim\uc2  has gone in even harder at Senate estimates.\par \par \uc2 McKim warns \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will be an \u8220\'20\'1Cunstable wrecking ball in perhaps the most influential position in the entire global economy\u8221\'20\'1D, and isn\u8217\'20\'19t happy the \uc2 RBA\uc2  doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have precise answers about what\u8217\'20\'19s going to happen next.\par \par \uc2 Isn\u8217\'20\'19t that why the \uc2 RBA\uc2  chiefs get paid \u8220\'20\'1Cthe big bucks\u8221\'20\'1D, he wonders. The key question for Australian borrowers, though, is this one:\par \par \uc2 Do you think that a Trump presidency increases the risk that the cash rate in Australia will need to be held higher for longer?\par \par \uc2 Bullock replies bluntly: \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t have a view on that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Her assistant governor, \uc2 Christopher Kent\uc2 , does have a stab at explaining why the \uc2 RBA\uc2  can\u8217\'20\'19t tell what a Trump presidency will mean. For one thing, should he carry out his threat to slap tariffs of 60% on Chinese imports, \u8220\'20\'1Cyou can\u8217\'20\'19t imagine \u8230\'20\'26that China will do nothing\u8221\'20\'1D, he says.\par \par \uc2 That \u8220\'20\'1Csomething\u8221\'20\'1D might be retaliation (which Kent doesn\u8217\'20\'19t go into), or it could be extra stimulus measures than have already been announced. It\u8217\'20\'19s no accident that the Chinese government is holding a special meeting of its parliamentary standing committee from 4-8 November.\par \par \uc2 Kent notes that while the Australian dollar has lately slid against the US dollar, its wider trade-weighted measure (tracking currencies in their proportion of our trade) hadn\u8217\'20\'19t changed \u8220\'20\'1Cvery much\u8221\'20\'1D. In fact, it even strengthened overnight.\par \par \uc2 But in any case, who can tell what Trump will do when he takes office?\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.19pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.03pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Penny Wong\uc2  says Australia has \u8216\'20\'18few ways to move the dial in the Middle East\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 In \uc2 Penny Wong\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s opening statement to Senate estimates, the foreign affairs minister noted the conflict in Gaza, where the death toll has officially surpassed 43,000 according to the Gaza health ministry.\par \par \uc2 Wong acknowledged Australians were \u8220\'20\'1Cunderstandably traumatised\u8221\'20\'1D by the past year but said Australia had \u8220\'20\'1Cfew ways to move the dial\u8221\'20\'1D:\par \par \uc2 I understand people want their government to make this war end, but this isn\u8217\'20\'19t Vietnam. It isn\u8217\'20\'19t Iraq. Australia is not contributing to the war, nor are we supplying weapons for it on our own, Australia has few ways to move the dial in the Middle East. That is why we are so focused on partnering with other countries, which also want to end this war, deliver life-saving aid and uphold international law.\par \par \uc2 Wong said the federal government had committed $94.5m in humanitarian assistance to support civilians affected by conflicts in Gaza and Lebanon in the period.\par \par \uc2 The Labor senator said \u8220\'20\'1CPalestinian civilians cannot be made to pay the price of defeating \uc2 Hamas\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D, noting the sanctions Australia has applied against Israeli extremist settlers in the West Bank.\par \par \uc2 She said:\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, I hope all of us want the Middle East cycle of violence to end. That can only happen when the promise of two states is fulfilled, a Palestinian state and an Israeli state, the state of Israel living side by side in peace and security within internationally recognised borders.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.18pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.58pm GMT\par \par \uc2 A reporter asks Albanese if he is rethinking his approach to the next election after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s election. He says he ran the last campaign saying no one would be held back or left behind:\par \par \uc2 We needed to make sure that gaps in equality in terms of income did not continue to grow, and that\u8217\'20\'19s why we\u8217\'20\'19ve been very focused on our industrial relations legislation. Our IR legislation is about giving people a fair crack, giving people a fair wage. Same job, same pay.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.16pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.56pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Albanese says he won\u8217\'20\'19t apologise for comments about Trump\par \par \uc2 Anthony Albanese is asked if he\u8217\'20\'19ll apologise for unflattering things he has said about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the past. He says \u8220\'20\'1Cno, I look forward to working with President Trump\u8221\'20\'1D and that he\u8217\'20\'19ll speak with him soon.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve demonstrated, I think, my ability to work with world leaders and to develop relationships with them, which are positive,\u8221\'20\'1D he says. He goes on to say that former prime minister \uc2 Kevin Rudd\uc2  is doing a \u8220\'20\'1Cterrific job\u8221\'20\'1D, and that Australia is prepared if Trump goes ahead with tariffs:\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ve had considerable briefings, whether it be across security, economic and other issues, but Australia is a supporter of trade. We\u8217\'20\'19re a trading nation and we will continue to be advocates for free and fair trade.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.16pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.52pm GMT\par \par \uc2 National cabinet to discuss social media ban on Friday\par \par \uc2 National cabinet will meet to discuss the proposal tomorrow, but Albanese is confident of support, he says. Asked why not teach children to safely navigate social media rather than banning them, Albanese says that \u8220\'20\'1Cassumes an equal power relationship\u8221\'20\'1D:\par \par \uc2 The fact is that young women see images of particular body shapes that \u8230\'20\'26 have a real impact, in the real world, and young men, through some of the misogynistic material that they get sent to them \u8211\'20\'13 not because they asked for it \u8211\'20\'13 but if you\u8217\'20\'19re a 14-year-old kid getting this stuff, at a time where you\u8217\'20\'19re going through life\u8217\'20\'19s changes and maturing, it can be a really difficult time and what we\u8217\'20\'19re doing is listening and then acting.\par \par \uc2 Rowland says she recognises those harms don\u8217\'20\'19t end at a specific age and it is also important for children to be equipped to deal with the potential harms of social media. She says the common understanding of what constitutes social media includes \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , TikTok, \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and X:\par \par \uc2 YouTube\uc2  would likely fall within that definition as well. That goes to some of the issues also of exemptions \u8211\'20\'13 like whether it is done in a logged-out state and there will be the potential for regulations and also for the eSafety commissioner to make determinations of what could constitute low-risk for the purposes of that exemption regime.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.28am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.47pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Rowland is asked whether all adults will have to prove their age, whether that will put off social media users, and what sort of pushback she expects from the likes of billionaire \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (X) owner (and mate of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ) \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 She says the details will be worked out during the age assurance trial, and that any business operating here will be covered:\par \par \uc2 Every company that operates in Australia, whether domiciled here or otherwise, is expected and must comply with Australian law or face the consequences.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.02pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.44pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Rowland says a trial of age-assurance technology is looking at how users will identify themselves and prove their age. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a complex area but it is one that we are determined to get right,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 Albanese says it won\u8217\'20\'19t be perfect:\par \par \uc2 What we are wanting to state upfront, from the very beginning, is we don\u8217\'20\'19t pretend that you can get a 100% outcome here. [On underage people buying alcohol] people have to produce ID, but I bet this weekend I wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be surprised if somewhere in Australia, someone who is under 18, it is possible that they might get access to some alcohol.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.41pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Rowland says Labor has taken \u8216\'20\'18pragmatic approach\u8217\'20\'19 on social media ban\par \par \uc2 The communications minister, \uc2 Michelle Rowland\uc2 , says Labor will seek agreement from the national cabinet, and that the government had taken a \u8220\'20\'1Cpragmatic approach\u8221\'20\'1D to choosing 16 as the minimum age for social media access:\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ve consulted widely with experts, with parents, with youth organisations, with advocacy groups, with academics and, of course, our state and territory colleagues.\par \par \uc2 I want to particularly thank New South Wales and South Australia for jointly hosting the social media summit, which was most instructive in this record \u8230\'20\'26 what our approach does is helps to achieve a balance between minimising harms caused to young people accessing social media, while still enabling connection and inclusion.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.02pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.38pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Government announces new minimum age limit of 16 for social media platforms\par \par \uc2 Albanese says the there will be \u8220\'20\'1Cno penalties for users\u8221\'20\'1D, for parents or young people:\par \par \uc2 The eSafety commissioner will provide oversight and enforcement. The legislation will come into force 12 months after passage. There will be a review of the legislation following its commencement to make sure. This is world-leading legislation.\par \par \uc2 There will be exemptions, and a flexible definition of social media service to avoid unintended consequences, he says. There will be privacy protections on the information collected for age-assurance purposes, and it won\u8217\'20\'19t feature grandfathering arrangements. He says:\par \par \uc2 We don\u8217\'20\'19t argue that the changes that we will be legislating will fix everything immediately. We have laws such as people can\u8217\'20\'19t buy alcohol if they\u8217\'20\'19re under 18 and, from time to time, that can be broken. But those laws set what the parameters are for our society and they assist in ensuring the right outcomes.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.51pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.34pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Albanese \u8216\'20\'18calling time\u8217\'20\'19 on the harm social media doing to Australian children\par \par \uc2 A ban on social media for under 16s will put the onus on social media platforms to keep kids off, the prime minister, Anthony Albanese, says. He\u8217\'20\'19s holding a press conference, and says:\par \par \uc2 This one\u8217\'20\'19s for the mums and dads.\par \par \uc2 Social media is doing harm to our kids and I\u8217\'20\'19m calling time on it. I\u8217\'20\'19ve spoken to thousands of parents, grandparents, aunties and uncles. They, like me, are worried sick about the safety of our kids online. And I want Australian parents and families to know that government has your back.\par \par \uc2 I want parents to be able to say, \u8216\'20\'18Sorry, mate, that\u8217\'20\'19s against the law for me to get to you do this\u8217\'20\'19.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.58pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.28pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Trump victory points to higher global interest rates, \uc2 RBA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Kent says.\par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank is up before Senate estimates this morning and not surprisingly the first question is how a second Trump administration will affect Australia.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAt this stage we would be sticking to our inflation outlook\u8221\'20\'1D, the \uc2 RBA\uc2  governor, \uc2 Michele Bullock\uc2 , says after noting it was \u8220\'20\'1Ctoo early\u8221\'20\'1D to tell what the return of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to the presidency (from 20 January) would mean for interest rates and the wider economy.\par \par \uc2 Assistant governor \uc2 Christopher Kent\uc2 , though, explains what the market movements are telling us. US stocks, in particular, surged to record highs overnight as the US dollar had its best day since 2022, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2024-11-05/stock-market-today-dow-s-p-live-updates?srnd=homepage-asia"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to Bloomberg }}}\uc2 . Higher yields on US debt was one driver for the US currency, as investors bet US interest rates won\u8217\'20\'19t fall as fast as previously expected.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHigher US deficits through tax cuts in the US are in prospect, and one of the things that means is probably higher long-term interest rates in the US, higher inflation in the US, and quite possibly higher growth for a time,\u8221\'20\'1D Kent said. And since the US debt market is huge, the move would \u8220\'20\'1Chave upward effects on global interest rates\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 And that\u8217\'20\'19s before Trump tariffs get slapped on imports, particularly those from China \u8211\'20\'13 Australia\u8217\'20\'19s biggest trading partner by far. (Most of Australia\u8217\'20\'19s other big export markets, eg Japan and South Korea, also depend a lot on Chinese growth.)\par \par \uc2 Kent said:\par \par \uc2 Because US customers will be buying less [fewer] goods from the rest of the world, and they\u8217\'20\'19ll need less foreign exchange, but it means less demand by the US for global goods.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSo that\u8217\'20\'19s sort of a negative for growth elsewhere\u8221\'20\'1D, which is why European stocks were \u8220\'20\'1Cdown a little bit overnight\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s perhaps notable that Bullock let Kent pass on those views, when she could have made them herself.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 This post has been edited from an earlier version to correct a quote from the \uc2 RBA\uc2  governor\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.03pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.14pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Amid everything else going on today, the government will introduce legislation to make social media companies responsible for the first time for scams that occur on their platforms. Catie McLeod reports:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/money/2024/nov/07/banks-and-social-media-companies-to-be-fined-over-scams-under-new-australian-laws-touted-as-strongest-in-world"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Banks and social media companies to be fined over scams under new Australian laws touted as \u8216\'20\'18strongest in world\u8217\'20\'19 }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.47pm GMT\par \par \uc2 NAB\uc2  posts $7bn profit as mortgage arrears jump\par \par \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  has recorded a $6.96bn full-year net profit, down 6.1% from last year\u8217\'20\'19s strong result, as more home and business owners fell behind on their loan repayments.\par \par \uc2 While its net profit for the 12 months to September was down from a year ago, it is the second-highest result recorded during the pandemic years.\par \par \uc2 The major bank warned there was a risk of an \u8220\'20\'1Cincrease in bankruptcies, business failures and higher unemployment\u8221\'20\'1D if interest rates remain elevated for a protracted period of time:\par \par \uc2 Elevated interest rates to combat inflationary pressures have resulted in increases in arrears, primarily in the home loan and small and medium business portfolios.\par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/nov/05/rba-interest-rates-november-meeting-inflation-data"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 left its key interest rate unchanged }}}\uc2  earlier this week after underlying inflation remained above the central bank\u8217\'20\'19s target range.\par \par \uc2 NAB\uc2  reported a jump in the number of 90-plus-days mortgage delinquency rate, representing those who have fallen behind by more than three months, from 0.9% of its housing book to 1.08% over the past 12 months.\par \par \uc2 NAB\uc2  declared a total dividend of $1.69 for the year, up 2c from a year ago.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.58pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.33pm GMT\par \par \uc2 ACCC\uc2  to begin public hearings for inquiry into supermarkets\par \par \uc2 Customers and food suppliers will air their grievances over supermarket prices as the consumer watchdog digs into the grocery giants, \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports :\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Australian Competition and Consumer Commission\uc2  (\uc2 ACCC\uc2 ) will begin a series of public hearings for its inquiry into the supermarket sector.\par \par \uc2 Consumer advocacy groups and supplier representatives are first up, with Choice and the \uc2 Australian Food and Grocery Council\uc2  set to attend on Thursday. The commission\u8217\'20\'19s deputy chair, \uc2 Mick Keogh\uc2 , said:\par \par \uc2 We have received a large volume of information \u8230\'20\'26 and these hearings will provide an opportunity for us to seek clarification on a range of the very complex matters.\par \par \uc2 Aldi\uc2 , \uc2 Metcash\uc2 , \uc2 Woolworths\uc2  and \uc2 Coles\uc2  will all appear at the hearing in coming days.\par \par \uc2 Coles\uc2  and \uc2 Woolworths\uc2  have come under fire as Australians accuse them of benefiting from the cost-of-living crisis.\par \par \uc2 The federal government has vowed to take action, announcing in January it would set the consumer watchdog for the supermarkets and directing the \uc2 ACCC\uc2  to conduct an inquiry into the sector.\par \par \uc2 Many consumers have lost trust in supermarket pricing while suppliers have said they are forced to agree to unfavourable terms, according to the commission\u8217\'20\'19s interim report.\par \par \uc2 A final report on the inquiry is due to be delivered to the government by February.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 ACCC\uc2  in September launched a court case against \uc2 Coles\uc2  and \uc2 Woolworths\uc2 , claiming the retailers had misled customers with illusory discount schemes.\par \par \uc2 Both grocers deny the allegations and say the legal cases against them are misconceived.\par \par \uc2 The federal government will provide $30m in extra funding for the \uc2 ACCC\uc2  to continue investigations and enforcements in the supermarket and retail sector.\par \par \uc2 It has also proposed changes to merger laws and a mandatory food and grocery code of conduct that proposes multimillion-dollar penalties for serious breaches.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.42pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.24pm GMT\par \par \uc2 While I am very reluctant to direct you away from this Australian blog, US vice-president \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has just taken to the stage to make her concession speech. So if you just can\u8217\'20\'19t get enough of United States politics, head to our US blog \u8230\'20\'26 then come straight back here after, ta.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2024/nov/06/us-presidential-election-2024-donald-trump-kamala-harris-latest-news-updates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US election live: Kamala Harris to give concession speech after congratulating Donald Trump on the phone }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.27pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.13pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Labor to introduce fee-free Tafe legislation\par \par \uc2 The Albanese government will today introduce new legislation to offer at least another 100,000 fee-free Tafe places a year from 2027.\par \par \uc2 The bill\u8217\'20\'19s introduction intends to add further free spots in vocational education after a partnership with states and territories offered 180,000 places in 2023 and 300,000 a year from 2024.\par \par \uc2 Another 20,000 places for construction and housing courses is now being finalised, the government said.\par \par \uc2 A national advertising campaign will be launched this weekend to increase awareness about the places aimed at addressing key workforce shortages.\par \par \uc2 The skills and training minister, Andrew Giles, said:\par \par \uc2 More than half a million Australians are gaining the skills they want and which we need to build Australia\u8217\'20\'19s future. We want more Australians to take advantage of the opportunities on offer, which is why we\u8217\'20\'19re launching this advertising campaign.\par \par \uc2 The opposition is yet to announce whether it would wind back the policy if elected at the next federal election but has been critical of completion rates.\par \par \uc2 The deputy opposition leader, \uc2 Sussan Ley\uc2 , said 13% of enrolments had resulted in a qualification as of 31 March 2024, with the program costing $1.5bn.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFee-free Tafe has not made a meaningful impact in alleviating skills shortages,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.22pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.03pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Nationals senator \uc2 Bridget McKenzie\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s attacks on prime minister Anthony Albanese \u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/31/anthony-albanese-qantas-flight-upgrades"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 flight upgrades }}}\uc2  hit some turbulence yesterday. The shadow transport minister apologised and updated her official register of interests after revealing she had not declared 16 flight upgrades.\par \par \uc2 Two were in July and August, not so long before she claimed she had never been offered a single upgrade on \uc2 Qantas\uc2 . We\u8217\'20\'19ll see how this lands:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/nov/06/bridget-mckenzie-to-update-register-after-failing-to-declare-more-than-a-dozen-flight-upgrades"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bridget McKenzie updates register and apologises after failing to declare 16 flight upgrades }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.59pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s election \u8216\'20\'18not good for those who believe in climate action\u8217\'20\'19, Turnbull says\par \par \uc2 And on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as a threat to the fight against climate change, \uc2 Malcolm Turnbull\uc2  says his election was \u8220\'20\'1Cnot good for those who believe in climate action\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHe\u8217\'20\'19s said the quiet bit out loud,\u8221\'20\'1D Turnbull, says, referring to Trump\u8217\'20\'19s slogan \u8220\'20\'1Cdrill, baby, drill\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Turnbull says both Labor and the Liberal party will be influenced by Trump\u8217\'20\'19s victory:\par \par \uc2 I think Dutton\u8217\'20\'19s approach will be to project himself as a Trump-like strong man and present Albanese \u8211\'20\'13 as he\u8217\'20\'19s seeking to do \u8211\'20\'13 as weak.\par \par \uc2 Please refer to yesterday\u8217\'20\'19s question time, when the opposition leader, \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2 , and others in his team repeatedly referred to Albanese as \u8220\'20\'1Cweak\u8221\'20\'1D, despite protestations from speaker Milton Dick. But Dutton should think \u8220\'20\'1Cvery carefully\u8221\'20\'1D about that approach, Turnbull says, because Australia is different, \u8220\'20\'1Cmore sober\u8221\'20\'1D and rational, and has compulsory voting, so \u8220\'20\'1Crushing off to the Trumpiest extreme\u8221\'20\'1D could just \u8220\'20\'1Cget more teals elected\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.22pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.53pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Turnbull warns Albanese not to \u8216\'20\'18suck up\u8217\'20\'19 to new president and says Aukus 'a dud deal'\par \par \uc2 Former prime minister \uc2 Malcolm Turnbull\uc2 , who had some colourful moments in his relationship with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in his first presidency, is up now being colourful on the ABC. He says the Australian prime minister \u8220\'20\'1Chas to stand up for Australia and not follow the conventional approach, which is being urged on everyone, which is to suck up to Trump and be utterly deferential:\par \par \uc2 I had a very successful relationship with Trump because I stood up to him, stood my ground, incurred his wrath, won his respect, and then got very good outcomes for Australia.\par \par \uc2 Karvelas asks Turnbull about his comments that Trump is a \u8220\'20\'1Cthreat to democracy\u8221\'20\'1D. Turnbull demurs over the exact wording, but says Trump isn\u8217\'20\'19t committed to democracy or the rule of law in the way previous presidents have been:\par \par \uc2 He did sort of encourage a mob to try to overthrow the peaceful transfer of power in 2021, on January 6.\par \par \uc2 And he says he doesn\u8217\'20\'19t think Australia will \u8220\'20\'1Cever\u8221\'20\'1D get any Virginia-class submarines from the US because the US is not producing enough even for themselves. We signed up to a dud deal, he says:\par \par \uc2 The deal that Morrison instigated and Albanese signed up to is a very, very asymmetrical deal. All of the risk was on Australia. We have no agency and no leverage over this, and that\u8217\'20\'19s why I think the most likely outcome is we\u8217\'20\'19ll end up with no submarines, and that will be entirely our own fault because we signed up to a dud deal.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.12pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.48pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Birmingham says Liberals have \u8216\'20\'18no plans\u8217\'20\'19 to change \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws\par \par \uc2 Still on Birmingham on the ABC, host Patricia Karvelas asks whether he thinks cost of living, immigration, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and character of the candidate will shape Australia\u8217\'20\'19s next election, as it did the US\u8217\'20\'19s. Birmingham says cost of living will be the No 1 issue, and Karvelas presses him on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and whether he would vote to remove an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bill brought in by his colleagues Matt Canavan and Alex Antic. He says he would personally vote in favour of removing it, and:\par \par \uc2 We have been clear, in terms of the Liberal party leadership, that Peter Dutton and the Liberal party have no plans, no intentions, to see any changes to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, that we respect the states\u8217\'20\'19 rights in that regard, and I am very clear in terms of my respect for women\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.10pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.45pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Simon Birmingham says Australia needs to make sure Aukus delivered speedily\par \par \uc2 Simon Birmingham, the shadow foreign affairs minister, says Australia\u8217\'20\'19s priorities relate to the security environment and \u8220\'20\'1Cthe need to ensure that Aukus is delivered upon as quickly and effectively as possible\u8221\'20\'1D:\par \par \uc2 That is clearly mutually beneficial to both of our nations and, when it comes indeed to the strength of our two economies, it is mutually beneficial for us each to be as strong as one another, to be able to underpin those security considerations.\par \par \uc2 And they are some of the key arguments you\u8217\'20\'19d advance when it comes to dealing with trade and tariff agendas, or with ensuring that we are actually delivering upon those Aukus commitments that have been made and secured.\par \par \uc2 He says Australia has a \u8220\'20\'1Cvery good deal\u8221\'20\'1D, that it has been secured, and that it is unique to Australia because of the shared capability. Asked about whether Australia should pitch in more money for production in the US, he says it would be \u8220\'20\'1Cirresponsible to foreshadow that we wanted to change things\u8221\'20\'1D:\par \par \uc2 This is something that enjoys strong bipartisan support in both of our countries. It was launched and, of course, initially negotiated by the Morrison government with the Biden administration, but has enjoyed support across the parliament in Australia, across the Congress in the United States, and it has done so because of those mutually beneficial aspects.\par \par \uc2 He says the prime minister, Anthony Albanese, took a \u8220\'20\'1Crisk\u8221\'20\'1D appointing former prime minister Kevin Rudd as ambassador. Rudd is \u8220\'20\'1Cnot bigger than Australia\u8217\'20\'19s interests\u8221\'20\'1D, he says, and he hopes he is able to continue to \u8220\'20\'1Cbe as effective as he has been to date\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 On tariffs, Birmingham says Australia has successfully carved out exemptions (on steel and aluminium) before. And he describes Trump thus:\par \par \uc2 He takes an approach with policies that are sometimes less conventional than people are customarily used to, and he makes a strong and bold pronouncements that are less normal and more surprising than people are used to.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ll have to look carefully at the \u8220\'20\'1Ctotality\u8221\'20\'1D of measures, Birmingham says, when asked about Trump pulling out of climate change agreements.\par \par \uc2 I want to see Australia stay committed to net zero by 2050 \u8230\'20\'26 I don\u8217\'20\'19t anticipate any change in those commitments.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.55pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.30pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Penny Wong says Rudd will \u8216\'20\'18continue to do an excellent job\u8217\'20\'19 as ambassador\par \par \uc2 The foreign affairs minister, Penny Wong, has congratulated Donald Trump on his election on ABC Radio this morning.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe will obviously continue to work very closely with America as an old ally and a true friend,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 She is talking about a recent meeting with Mike Pompeo, who is set to work in Trump\u8217\'20\'19s administration. It was a \u8220\'20\'1Cvery important engagement,\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 Asked about Trump\u8217\'20\'19s foreshadowing of tariffs on all imports, she says he \u8220\'20\'1Chas run a campaign based on change\u8221\'20\'1D, so it won\u8217\'20\'19t come as a surprise.\par \par \uc2 On former prime minister Kevin Rudd as Australia\u8217\'20\'19s ambassador to the US, she says he has been \u8220\'20\'1Chighly effective with both Republicans and Democrats\u8221\'20\'1D:\par \par \uc2 So we\u8217\'20\'19re confident that Mr Rudd will continue to do an excellent job in engaging with the new administration.\par \par \uc2 On Aukus, she says Australia is buying submarines as a deterrence \u8220\'20\'1Cto enable peace\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat is the objective and that is the intention,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CIt is an important part of making sure we maintain a peaceful, stable and prosperous region. That\u8217\'20\'19s why we want this, this capability.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 And she says the \u8220\'20\'1Csovereign capability\u8221\'20\'1D is supported by legislation and public comments in the US.\par \par \uc2 On Ukraine, she says the alliance is \u8220\'20\'1Cone on which we share so many strategic objectives\u8221\'20\'1D, and democratic values. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd it\u8217\'20\'19s an alliance which has, through its history \u8230\'20\'26 there have been times where leaders and governments have disagreed\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 And on working-class white men deserting the Democrats and lessons for Labor, Wong says \u8220\'20\'1Cwe don\u8217\'20\'19t need the US election to understand that cost of living is the first priority of Australian voters right now\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople are doing it tough,\u8221\'20\'1D she says, and points to Labor\u8217\'20\'19s tax cuts, Medicare measures, cheaper measures and energy bill relief as examples of what they\u8217\'20\'19re doing.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.05pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.12pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The high court\u8217\'20\'19s ruling of ankle bracelets and curfews for former immigration detainees {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/nov/06/high-court-quashes-albanese-governments-ankle-bracelet-and-curfew-regime-for-former-immigration-detainees-ntwnfb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 as unconstitutional }}}\uc2  unfolded rapidly yesterday, and it will keep moving today. The Albanese government {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/nov/06/tony-burke-high-court-immigration-detention-ruling-ntwnfb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 will scramble }}}\uc2  to bring in rules to \u8220\'20\'1Callow for an adjusted process for electronic monitoring devices and curfews\u8221\'20\'1D. See Paul Karp \u8217\'20\'19s report for all the details:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/nov/07/up-to-29-people-freed-from-australian-immigration-detention-to-have-visa-breach-charges-dropped"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Up to 29 people freed from Australian immigration detention to have visa breach charges dropped }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.13pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.09pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Good morning, all \u8211\'20\'13 and thanks, Martin Farrer, for your overnight work. Tory Shepherd with you, trying not to sound like I stayed up too late watching telly with a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2022/nov/21/teal-spicy-cough-and-bachelors-handbag-the-contenders-for-macquarie-dictionarys-2022-word-of-the-year"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bachelor\u8217\'20\'19s handbag }}}\uc2  and bottle of shiraz.\par \par \uc2 The reactions to Donald Trump 2.0 will be coming thick and fast today. It\u8217\'20\'19s also the last sitting day of the week, estimates will keep bringing the goods, and there\u8217\'20\'19ll be legislation, interest rates and student debt on the agenda.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.48pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s return to the White House presents a series of disruptive challenges to Anthony Albanese, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/global/2024/nov/06/as-the-us-opts-for-more-of-trumps-unpredictability-albanese-will-be-focusing-on-what-he-can-control"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 writes our political editor }}}\uc2 , Karen Middleton, this morning.\par \par \uc2 But, she writes \u8220\'20\'1Cthe Australian government is focused on what it can control\u8221\'20\'1D rather than what it can\u8217\'20\'19t as it contemplates a second Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 Read her full piece here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/global/2024/nov/06/as-the-us-opts-for-more-of-trumps-unpredictability-albanese-will-be-focusing-on-what-he-can-control"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 As the US opts for more of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s unpredictability, Albanese will be focusing on what he can control }}}\par \par \uc2 And there\u8217\'20\'19s more on that reaction from politicians here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/nov/06/us-election-2024-abbott-morrison-australian-mps-praise-trump-likely-victory"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Australia\u8217\'20\'19s conservatives praise Trump\u8217\'20\'19s \u8216\'20\'18self-belief\u8217\'20\'19 as PM sends congratulations }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.19pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.41pm GMT\par \par \uc2 RBA chief to face grilling in parliament\par \par \uc2 Questions about the central bank\u8217\'20\'19s decision to keep interest rates on hold will be levelled at the Reserve Bank governor, Michele Bullock, at her regular parliamentary grilling, writes Australian Associated Press.\par \par \uc2 The RBA board has yet to declare victory in Australia\u8217\'20\'19s war on inflation and on Tuesday kept the interest rate on hold, bringing up a calendar year since the last rate rise.\par \par \uc2 Bullock and assistant governor Chris Kent may get a hostile reception on Thursday, with the Greens senator Nick McKim likely to press them on why they have still not dropped the cash rate from 4.35%.\par \par \uc2 McKim called on the government to override the central bank and use its powers to cut rates back in September, when the quarterly inflation rate still read 3.8%.\par \par \uc2 That figure is now back within the RBA\u8217\'20\'19s target band at 2.8%.\par \par \uc2 But the board in its post-meeting statement on Tuesday said it would be \u8220\'20\'1Csome time yet before inflation is sustainably in the target range and approaching the midpoint\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The bank\u8217\'20\'19s preferred measure of underlying inflation, the trimmed mean, is still at 3.5% and proving much stickier, given elevated services price growth.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile headline inflation has declined substantially and will remain lower for a time, underlying inflation is more indicative of inflation momentum, and it remains too high,\u8221\'20\'1D the board said.\par \par \uc2 The jobs market was still running too hot for Bullock\u8217\'20\'19s liking, even though the RBA increased its peak unemployment prediction to 4.5% from 4.4%.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.25pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.35pm GMT\par \par \uc2 PM says Australia \u8216\'20\'18will strive to strengthen\u8217\'20\'19 co-operation with US\par \par \uc2 Anthony Albanese says the election of the US president marked an important moment for Australia and the Asia Pacific region, and vowed to work closely with Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s administration. This was his full statement as the election result became clear:\par \par \uc2 I congratulate President Trump on his election victory.\par \par \uc2 The election of the president of the United States is always an important moment for the world, for our region and for Australia.\par \par \uc2 Our government will work closely with the new Trump administration to realise the benefits of our strong economic partnership.\par \par \uc2 The United States has long played a leadership role in the stability and security of the Indo-Pacific. Australia will strive to strengthen the co-operation between our two nations in the region.\par \par \uc2 Australia and America are old allies and we are true friends. Our nations are bound by a history of shared sacrifice, a commitment to common values and \u8211\'20\'13 above all \u8211\'20\'13 enduring respect and affection between our peoples.\par \par \uc2 I look forward to talking with President Trump and working with him in the best interests of both our nations.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.29pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.30pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Welcome\par \par \uc2 Good morning and welcome to our live politics blog. I\u8217\'20\'19m Martin Farrer with the best of the overnight stories and then Tory Shepherd will take the controls.\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s really only one place to start this morning and that is Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s election win. Anthony Albanese sent his congratulations and said he looked forward to talking with Trump and working with him \u8220\'20\'1Cin the best interests of both our nations\u8221\'20\'1D. Peter Dutton, Scott Morrison and Tony Abbott all delivered messages of congratulation to Trump, with the latter saying Trump had the self-belief the \u8220\'20\'1Cwest needs \u8230\'20\'26 in spades\u8221\'20\'1D. More reaction coming up.\par \par \uc2 We also caught up with the expat and Australian Trump supporters {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/nov/06/we-want-to-bring-this-to-australia-contrasting-emotions-at-sydney-watch-parties-for-trump-and-harris"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 who celebrated at watch parties }}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 \u8220\'20\'1CWe want to bring this to Australia\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 and with the Kamala Harris backers drowning their sorrows.\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s win had an immediate effect on the financial markets overnight, sending the US dollar soaring at the expense of the Aussie dollar. But it also saw Wall Street stocks hit another all-time high as investors moved into more risky assets amid the prospect of a new government united across the presidency and Congress. The ASX200 futures market looks becalmed this morning although that might change later as the Reserve Bank governor, Michele Bullock, is quizzed in parliament. More details on that soon.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241106ekb600364\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
