\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 People around the world are appalled by Trump\u8217\'20\'19s win, but women have been gripped by a visceral horror\b0\par\par\uc2 Rhiannon Lucy Cosslett \par \uc2 1,194 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 November 2024\par 12:22 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 This misogynist administration\u8217\'20\'19s hatred has been written on women\u8217\'20\'19s bodies. You don\u8217\'20\'19t have to be American, pregnant or a mother to feel itUS election 2024 \u8211\'20\'13 latest updates\par \par \uc2 If \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s previous administration taught us anything, it\u8217\'20\'19s that women\u8217\'20\'19s bodies do not matter. The autonomy of those bodies, the frequent injustices and abuses inflicted on them, are of little consequence to a far-right misogynist administration that is \u8211\'20\'13 unbelievably to some \u8211\'20\'13 back in power after an all too brief period of reprieve, if not change. Women\u8217\'20\'19s bodies are also of little consequence to the people who voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Shortly after Trump was elected in 2016, I saw Regina Spektor playing at the Royal Festival Hall. She sat down at a piano and sang her song {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=15rOTVe-icE"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ballad of a Politician }}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CA man inside a room is shaking hands with other men,\u8221\'20\'1D it opens: \u8220\'20\'1CThis is how it happens / Our carefully laid plans.\u8221\'20\'1D The almost physical weight of sadness in Spektor\u8217\'20\'19s performance, and that of the women in the audience, is something I have never forgotten.\par \par \uc2 Women across the world are today experiencing the visceral, secondhand body horror of another Trump administration, and that is nothing compared with the all-too-real feeling of peril that many American women will be having as I write. We hold fear, tension and trauma in our bodies, and though that is the kind of idea that strongmen such as Trump and their acolytes would snigger at, it\u8217\'20\'19s well documented that, to quote the psychiatrist and author {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2021/sep/20/trauma-trust-and-triumph-psychiatrist-bessel-van-der-kolk-on-how-to-recover-from-our-deepest-pain"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bessel van der Kolk }}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://guardianbookshop.com/the-body-keeps-the-score-9780141978611/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the body keeps the score }}}\uc2 . It is a complete tragedy that yet more American women are facing the prospect of their \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  being curtailed even further.\par \par \uc2 This body horror will be felt by the many others on Trump\u8217\'20\'19s hitlist of undesirables: immigrants, gay and transgender people, disabled people, protesters. Anyone capable of empathy. Yet in the context of the assault on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/reproductive-rights"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22reproductive rights\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 , and being a woman myself, it is women I am writing about today.\par \par \uc2 I am thinking particularly of three women. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.propublica.org/article/candi-miller-abortion-ban-death-georgia"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Candi Miller died at home in bed }}}\uc2  with her three-year-old daughter next to her after being too afraid to seek medical care because of Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. She had a son as well as a daughter. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.propublica.org/article/georgia-abortion-ban-amber-thurman-death"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Amber Nicole Thurman }}}\uc2 , also in Georgia, died after taking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills at home and being made to wait 20 hours in agony for the dilation and curettage procedure that would have saved her life. She had a six-year-old son. And {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.propublica.org/article/josseli-barnica-death-miscarriage-texas-abortion-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Josseli Barnica, who died }}}\uc2  when she was 17 weeks pregnant after doctors in Texas delayed treating her miscarriage for 40 hours. She had a young daughter. There are many more women, children and their families who have suffered profoundly as a result of the Republican-led rollback of women\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The outcome of this election is not surprising. After last time, I didn\u8217\'20\'19t believe America\u8217\'20\'19s hatred of women could be overestimated; why should it elect one? The hatred all too evidently runs deep. Increasingly, its currents run separately to the mainstream: on podcasts, in the \u8220\'20\'1Cmanosphere\u8221\'20\'1D and on our online news feeds.\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  announcing that she was running for president coincided with my social media algorithms bombarding me with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/ng-interactive/2024/jul/24/tradwives-tiktok-women-gender-roles"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Christian \u8220\'20\'1Ctradwife\u8221\'20\'1D content }}}\uc2  about the importance of serving and obeying one\u8217\'20\'19s husband, and not working outside the home. This alternative reality isn\u8217\'20\'19t a side of America that many Europeans had seen. You might be dimly aware that historically the country was founded by religious fundamentalists deemed too extreme for European sensibilities, but there\u8217\'20\'19s something about seeing quite how unhinged and retrograde the content is that really brings the ideological disconnect home. Naturally, many people living in the US are just as horrified by the rise of these sorts of ideas, not to mention how they intersect with online misogyny and white supremacy, but their prevalence is less of a surprise because they have been exposed to them to some degree their entire lives.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/aug/06/jd-vance-childless-cat-ladies-tim-walz"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cchildless cat lady\u8221\'20\'1D discourse }}}\uc2  was dominating the political debate, as if things weren\u8217\'20\'19t already feeling medieval enough. Having researched and written a book, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://guardianbookshop.com/the-year-of-the-cat-9781472290748/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Year of the Cat }}}\uc2 , that is partly about childless cat ladies \u8211\'20\'13 and where the sexist myths about them come from \u8211\'20\'13 I found it as predictable as the \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://slate.com/news-and-politics/2016/07/chris-christies-malicious-attack-on-hillary-clinton-was-full-of-lies.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 burn the witch }}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D rhetoric deployed against \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 . It\u8217\'20\'19s easy to laugh at the ridiculous notion that women who have not given birth are bitter, rageful harpies without a stake in the country they live in, and even easier to spin it into a clever \u8220\'20\'1CCat Ladies for Kamala\u8221\'20\'1D campaign, but this fear of childless women is an old, old form of misogyny. That it was being deployed, especially in the context of global panic about the birthrate, did not bode well.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, there were yet more rape and sexual assault allegations to add to the pile \u8211\'20\'13 which barely seemed to make a dent in the election debate, despite the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/oct/23/donald-trump-accuser-stacey-williams-jeffrey-epstein"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 alleged involvement of the convicted paedophile }}}\uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2 . Many of us have been left wondering what exactly Trump would have to do to a woman that would be deemed sufficiently beyond the pale to prevent his return to power. If women didn\u8217\'20\'19t matter in 2016, we seem to matter even less in 2024.\par \par \uc2 There is good news for American women to cling to, with Missouri and Arizona {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2024/nov/05/abortion-ballot-results-tracker-ban-states"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voting to expand \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights }}}\uc2 , and Colorado, New York, Maryland, Montana and Nevada all passing measures to protect them. Yet misogyny can be catching: in Europe, governments must take steps to protect our own laws (Labour must urgently decriminalise \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  here in the UK after the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2024/apr/08/decriminalise-abortion-in-england-and-wales-parliament-women-stella-creasy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 legislation fell by the wayside }}}\uc2  earlier this year).\par \par \uc2 I know I am not alone in saying that I had never experienced such profound physical revulsion as I did during the Trump years, whenever that man tweeted or went on television. When Trump was last in office, I had not ever been pregnant or given birth. I was, though, a survivor of assault, and found the \u8220\'20\'1Cgrab them by the pussy\u8221\'20\'1D rhetoric and the treatment of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2018/oct/01/christine-blasey-ford-testimony-sexual-assault-kavanaugh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Christine Blasey Ford }}}\uc2  particularly sickening. You don\u8217\'20\'19t need to have been assaulted, or raped, to feel that revulsion, just as you didn\u8217\'20\'19t need to have had an IUD fitted to empathise with all the women who were suddenly rushing to get theirs. You don\u8217\'20\'19t need to have had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to have been pregnant or given birth to understand the trauma of being forced to carry an unwanted pregnancy to term.\par \par \uc2 The body keeps the score, and that feeling of horror is back.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Rhiannon Lucy Cosslett is a Guardian columnist\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Do you have an opinion on the issues raised in this article? If you would like to submit a response of up to 300 words by email to be considered for publication in our {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/letters"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 letters }}}\uc2  section, please {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:mailto:guardian.letters@theguardian.com?body=Please%20include%20your%20name,%20full%20postal%20address%20and%20phone%20number%20underneath%20your%20letter.%20Letters%20are%20usually%20published%20with%20the%20author’s%20name%20and%20city/town/village.%20The%20rest%20of%20the%20information%20is%20for%20verification%20only%20and%20to%20contact%20you%20if%20your%20letter%20is%20used."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 click here }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241107ekb7000xe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
