\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Fur and loathing: do America\u8217\'20\'19s \u8216\'20\'18childless cat ladies\u8217\'20\'19 hold the key to the US election?\b0\par\par\uc2 Gaby Hinsliff \par \uc2 2,858 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 November 2024\par 12:47 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 When JD Vance dismissed \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  as a \u8216\'20\'18childless cat lady\u8217\'20\'19, it sparked controversy, brought Taylor Swift into the presidential debate and focused attention on right-wing political pressure placed on women to have children\par \par \uc2 When writer and artist {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.alicemaddicott.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Alice Maddicott}}}\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s beloved rescue cat died, she was understandably bereft. Dylan was a proper character, she says, the kind of gregarious cat that follows you to the pub or for a walk. But mourning him made Alice, who was then in her late 30s and single, feel faintly self-conscious. What if people thought she was a mad cat lady, weeping spinsterish tears for her pet?\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf I\u8217\'20\'19d had a dog, there would be no stereotype. But as a single woman approaching 40, it could be seen very differently,\u8221\'20\'1D says Maddicott. Curious about the origins of such a kneejerk prejudice, she started digging into its history. The research became a book, Cat Women, reclaiming an insult long used to belittle older women (especially non-compliant ones) or frighten younger women into settling down, lest they end up like the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2018/apr/16/claws-pop-culture-crazy-cat-lady-lonely-sexless-eccentric-stereotype-millennial-ailurophiles"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 crazy cat lady}}}\uc2  from The Simpsons : once a high achiever, now a burnt-out drunk.\par \par \uc2 But as Maddicott points out, it\u8217\'20\'19s an objectively ridiculous insult. There\u8217\'20\'19s no such thing as a \u8220\'20\'1Ccrazy cat man\u8221\'20\'1D and single hamster owners aren\u8217\'20\'19t considered a threat to the patriarchal order. Only cat ladies touch a nerve, because only cat ladies immediately conjure up the idea of witches.\par \par \uc2 A Childless Cat Lady for Kamala T-shirt is now a gleeful statement of defiance\par \par \uc2 In medieval times, the devil was believed to give cats as gifts to women inducted into witchcraft, Maddicott explains: so strong was the association that at the Bideford witch trials in 17th-century Devon, one woman was accused after a cat was seen slipping in through her window. \u8220\'20\'1CProbably an eccentric older woman feeding a stray, but that was used to condemn her.\u8221\'20\'1D Single women, unrestrained and also undefended by any man, made dangerously easy scapegoats if sickness came or the crops failed. Thankfully, people no longer believe in witches ruining lives out of spite. Or do they?\par \par \uc2 Three years ago, the then aspiring Republican senator (and now vice-presidential candidate) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/jd-vance"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 JD Vance}}}\uc2  complained on Fox News that America was being run by \u8220\'20\'1Ca bunch of childless cat ladies who are miserable at their own lives and the choices they\u8217\'20\'19ve made and so they want to make the rest of the country miserable, too.\u8221\'20\'1D He named vice-president {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/kamala-harris"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Kamala Harris}}}\uc2 , secretary of transportation {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/pete-buttigieg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Pete Buttigieg}}}\uc2  (who is gay) and the young New York representative {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/alexandria-ocasio-cortez"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez}}}\uc2  as politicians who supposedly had no stake in the future they were legislating for.\par \par \uc2 The idea that if you haven\u8217\'20\'19t given birth, you don\u8217\'20\'19t count, was hurtful to many doting stepmothers (as Harris is) or adoptive parents (like Buttigieg). But most incendiary of all was the idea that not having children renders a person a bitter, vengeful husk, desperate to drag everyone else down with them. Polling this summer, after those comments were dug up and recirculated, found two-thirds of Americans disagreed that not having biological children was a hindrance in a president; even among Republicans, only 15% agreed.\par \par \uc2 Though Vance now insists he was merely being sarcastic, for many women on both sides of the Atlantic, those words summed up a strand of politics that seemingly values them mainly for their ability to procreate and views their right to choose with suspicion. Though powerful women have long been shamed for not being mothers \u8211\'20\'13 \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/2020/feb/07/it-took-on-a-life-of-its-own-the-story-behind-julia-gillards-misogyny-speech"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 deliberately barren}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D, the former Australian prime minister \uc2 Julia Gillard\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s opponents called her \u8211\'20\'13 in the current climate, attacks on childless cat ladies have a sharper edge.\par \par \uc2 In both the US and Europe, rightwing parties are weaponising female fertility as a political issue, tapping into a potent mix of misogyny, economic anxiety and overtly racist angst about being \u8220\'20\'1Creplaced\u8221\'20\'1D as the dominant culture if white women don\u8217\'20\'19t start breeding more enthusiastically. Though falling birth rates across the developed world are an undeniable economic headache \u8211\'20\'13 shrinking populations mean fewer young workers to fund pensions for the old and, typically, lower growth \u8211\'20\'13 populists focus on the way a dwindling workforce leads to more imported labour. Hungary\u8217\'20\'19s populist leader {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/viktor-orban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Viktor Orb\u225\'00\'E1n}}}\uc2  has claimed the west is \u8220\'20\'1Ccommitting suicide\u8221\'20\'1D by not making babies; in Germany, the far right AfD called in its manifesto for \u8220\'20\'1Clarger families instead of mass immigration\u8221\'20\'1D. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , the X owner and Donald Trump donor, tweets in apocalyptic terms about impending \u8220\'20\'1Cpopulation collapse\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Yet still, the baby drought persists. Around one in five British women don\u8217\'20\'19t have children by the end of their fertile years \u8211\'20\'13 the \uc2 Office for National Statistics\uc2  doesn\u8217\'20\'19t keep equivalent statistics for men \u8211\'20\'13 and this year deaths overtook births for the first time (outside a pandemic) since the 1970s. Almost half of Americans under 50, meanwhile, are childless. Or should that be childfree? For this year of shaming women has also been a year of women refusing to be shamed: older women reclaiming witchy insults like \u8220\'20\'1Chag\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Ccrone\u8221\'20\'1D, while younger ones resist what they see as a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/the-handmaids-tale"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Handmaid\u8217\'20\'19s Tale}}}\uc2  future of curtailed rights over their own bodies.\par \par \uc2 Like the cat-eared pink hats that anti-Trump marchers wore in 2017, referencing his boasts about grabbing women \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2017/nov/29/denying-accuracy-of-access-hollywood-tape-would-be-trumps-biggest-lie"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 by the pussy}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D, in 2024 a \u8220\'20\'1CChildless Cat Lady for Kamala\u8221\'20\'1D T-shirt or sticker is now a gleeful statement of defiance. When Taylor Swift {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/guardian/status/1833719916618195203"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 identified as one}}}\uc2  in her \uc2 Instagram\uc2  post formally endorsing Harris, it felt like something of a Spartacus moment \u8211\'20\'13 arguably even more so when \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  responded with a creepy tweet offering to get her pregnant.\par \par \uc2 What\u8217\'20\'19s different about Swift isn\u8217\'20\'19t merely that she, too, is unmarried, childfree and a cat owner at 34; it\u8217\'20\'19s that she makes it look like a blast. Rich, powerful and taking her pick of trophy boyfriends, she lives closer to what would once have been called a rakish bachelor\u8217\'20\'19s life than a spinster\u8217\'20\'19s \u8211\'20\'13 though these days spinsterdom is a story told very differently.\par \par \uc2 In Kate Winslet\u8217\'20\'19s recent feminist biopic {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/article/2024/sep/08/kate-winslet-insisted-her-film-about-lee-miller-must-have-female-director#:~:text=%E2%80%9CLee%20is%20a%20great%20role,into%20the%20heart%20of%20darkness%E2%80%9D."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Lee}}}\uc2 , about the pioneering Second World War photographer Lee Miller, there is a scene in which Winslet\u8217\'20\'19s Miller and Vogue magazine editor Audrey Withers ask each other if they\u8217\'20\'19re planning to have children. \u8220\'20\'1CGod no,\u8221\'20\'1D says Withers, cheerfully. \u8220\'20\'1COh God no,\u8221\'20\'1D echoes Winslet\u8217\'20\'19s Miller. It\u8217\'20\'19s a bonding moment in the film which implies neither could have done what they did \u8211\'20\'13 in Miller\u8217\'20\'19s case following the US army\u8217\'20\'19s advance across Europe and documenting newly liberated concentration camps; and in Withers\u8217\'20\'19s case battling the censors to publish her images \u8211\'20\'13 if they\u8217\'20\'19d had families at home. Though Miller did eventually have a son after the war, the film doesn\u8217\'20\'19t duck the fact their relationship was difficult and ultimately lets her work take centre stage. It\u8217\'20\'19s how great men\u8217\'20\'19s stories are often told, drawing a veil over domestic shortcomings, but more rarely women\u8217\'20\'19s.\par \par \uc2 Once upon a time in Hollywood, childless women could be mad, murderous bunny boilers \u8211\'20\'13 in films like Single White Female and Fatal Attraction \u8211\'20\'13 or at a push Bridget Jones, terrified of dying \u8220\'20\'1Cfat and alone\u8221\'20\'1D. Now they can be Carrie Bradshaw, reimagined in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/and-just-like-that"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 And Just Like That\u8230\'20\'26}}}\uc2  as a sexy fiftysomething widow with a fluffy rescue cat, or even Greta Gerwig\u8217\'20\'19s Barbie, the eternally childless doll who (according to her creator Ruth Handler) \u8220\'20\'1Calways represented the fact that a woman has choices\u8221\'20\'1D. Though the film has a darker side, with the Kens\u8217\'20\'19 angry revolt against the Barbie matriarchy echoing a backlash many young women say they\u8217\'20\'19re experiencing in real life, the Barbieland both sides seek to control is a hedonistic land of parties and beach days where nobody is spending their Saturdays bleary-eyed at soft play. The toymaker \uc2 Mattel\uc2  has always resisted pleas to give Barbie and Ken children; instead they stayed forever aspirational Dinks, an 80s acronym for Dual Income No Kids now gleefully revived by millennials.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re DINKs. Of course we are already planning our European vacation next year,\u8221\'20\'1D says TikToker {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.tiktok.com/@johnefinance?lang=en"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @johnefinance}}}\uc2 , in a chirpy post last December that racked up over 290,000 likes. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re DINKs; we spend our discretionary income on $8 lattes,\u8221\'20\'1D his partner giggles. Social media is still awash with #dinkcouple posts bragging about how much more money, sex and sleep they get compared to haggard new parents, recasting childless life as the opposite of sad and empty. Unusually, the trend brings young men\u8217\'20\'19s perspectives into a debate normally (and exhaustingly) focused on women. But it also reflects polling released by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2021/11/19/growing-share-of-childless-adults-in-u-s-dont-expect-to-ever-have-children/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Pew Center}}}\uc2  thinktank earlier this year showing the most common reason childless Americans under 50 give for not being parents is that they just don\u8217\'20\'19t want to.\par \par \uc2 TikTok\u8217\'20\'19s Dinks are young, and maybe not ruling parenthood out forever. But for now their lives arguably look a lot more fun than being a tradwife, those sourdough-baking, God-fearing, home-schooling mothers of multiple children whose retro social media content is \u8211\'20\'13 depending on your view \u8211\'20\'13 either a bit of guilty escapist fantasy for tired working women or a sinister attempt to drag women back into the kitchen. This summer a British newspaper interview with uber-tradwife Hannah Neeleman (aka {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.instagram.com/ballerinafarm/?hl=en"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @ballerinafarm}}}\uc2  ), in which the writer wondered how exactly she\u8217\'20\'19d ended up sacrificing a ballet career to have eight children and why her husband kept answering questions for her, triggered days of debate about whether she was being secretly oppressed or whether (as Neeleman\u8217\'20\'19s fans argued) this was a hit piece by a writer who doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have kids.\par \par \uc2 Cut through all the noise, however, and there is one clear signal: neither the tradwife nor the Dink camp seem to think having it all is an option any more. You can\u8217\'20\'19t have kids and a career in ballet; you can\u8217\'20\'19t have a thrilling social life and children. You have to choose, with the implication that either way, something is lost. If there is a sadness to Neeleman talking about giving up on her professional ambitions, there is a faint whiff of financial distress around some of those Dink reels, too; a sense they\u8217\'20\'19re blowing their cash on bucket-list travel destinations because the idea of buying a family home (while paying nursery bills that cost more than a mortgage) seems wildly out of reach.\par \par \uc2 The second most common reason childless Britons aged 35-44 gave for not having children (after thinking they were too old) was a split between \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t want the impact on my lifestyle\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cthe cost is too high\u8221\'20\'1D, according to \uc2 YouGov\uc2 . But for those on tight budgets, the two are related. It may be no accident that Britain\u8217\'20\'19s last mini-baby boom was in the early Noughties \u8211\'20\'13 fuelled by cheaper childcare, longer maternity leave and rising prosperity \u8211\'20\'13 or that it fizzled out with the 2009 recession.\par \par \uc2 On both sides of the Atlantic, women who do want kids some day are tired of being blamed for their childlessness by politicians who seemingly won\u8217\'20\'19t meet them halfway: who instead of building cheap houses for first-time buyers or slashing their nursery bills, suggest (in Vance\u8217\'20\'19s case) that grandparents could help out for free. But for some women, there\u8217\'20\'19s an added worry that politicians cajoling or shaming women into having children may eventually turn to forcing them.\par \par \uc2 For American women\par \par \uc2 under 30, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now the number one election issue, according to an October poll by the health policy researchers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kff.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 KFF}}}\uc2 . Two years ago, the overturning of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/24/roe-v-wade-overturned-abortion-summary-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Roe v Wade}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 the landmark ruling underpinning legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in America \u8211\'20\'13 left the way open for an all-out assault on \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . At campaign rally after rally, Harris has hammered away at fears of what a Republican administration might do. She often quotes {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/project-2025"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Project 2025}}}\uc2 , the infamous leaked wishlist for a second Trump term drawn up by a rightwing thinktank, which argues for giving foetuses legal rights and scrapping funding for contraceptive services.\par \par \uc2 In Europe, too, rightwing governments in Hungary, Italy and Poland have tightened \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, though furious female voters helped propel a pro-liberalisation Polish government into power last year.\par \par \uc2 For now, Britain seems to be on a much more progressive path. The prospect of curbs on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in this parliament looks at first glance remote, though the Labour MP {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/stella-creasy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Stella Creasy}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 who has been aggressively targeted in her constituency by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists \u8211\'20\'13 argues complacency is dangerous. \u8220\'20\'1CIf the election hadn\u8217\'20\'19t been called, it\u8217\'20\'19s not clear that we wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have seen the first rollback on rights in 50 years,\u8221\'20\'1D she says, pointing to a backbench attempt to reduce the legal time limit from 24 to 22 weeks in the dying months of the last Conservative government.\par \par \uc2 But the Tory MP {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2023/dec/18/tory-mp-miriam-cates-investigated-standards-watchdog"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Miriam Cates}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 who once argued that dwindling fertility rates are \u8220\'20\'1Cthe one overarching threat\u8221\'20\'1D to western society \u8211\'20\'13 lost her seat in July, while the Tory leadership favourite \uc2 Kemi Badenoch\uc2  says she isn\u8217\'20\'19t convinced governments can make women have babies.\par \par \uc2 America\u8217\'20\'19s fate, however, still hangs by a thread, with a stark divide emerging particularly among the young: women for Harris, angry young men for Trump.\par \par \uc2 Whether she wins or whether she loses in the coming election, it almost certainly won\u8217\'20\'19t be because (along with every other occupant of the Oval Office in history) \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  hasn\u8217\'20\'19t physically given birth. Most Americans say this election is still about the economy, stupid. But what is clear from months of arguing about childless cat ladies is that being one is just another American story now.\par \par \uc2 When the pollsters \uc2 Ipsos\uc2  went looking this summer for female voters with no kids at home, but with a feline companion, the surprise was all the ways in which they were pretty much like everyone else: mostly suburban, disproportionately white, mostly with some college education, no richer or poorer than average \u8211\'20\'13 and in almost a third of cases, Republicans. They\u8217\'20\'19re living behind the same white-picket fences as everyone else, shopping at Target, working nine to five, just getting on with life. And the clearer that becomes, the harder it is to pretend that every childless cat lady is a witch.\par \par \uc2 Feline friendship\par \par \uc2 Cat lover and author Britt Collins on her furry companions\par \par \uc2 I never imagined that childless cat ladies would become a political force and a whole new demographic. Over the years, I\u8217\'20\'19ve heard all the snarks: mad, lonely, obsessive \u8211\'20\'13 the old witch-hunting slurs that have dogged outlier women forever. Am I miserable by the choices I made? Hell, no. Here\u8217\'20\'19s the thing, cats rock and I don\u8217\'20\'19t have to defend my choices. If I wanted kids, I\u8217\'20\'19d have adopted one.\par \par \uc2 The only time I felt lonely was in the last year of my marriage when I lost my sense of self. I\u8217\'20\'19ve had boyfriends since I was 15, always cat men, and I still adore my two long-term exes with whom I spent my 20s and 30s. I\u8217\'20\'19ve raised dozens of strays with them and had the best of times. However, these days, my ideal man is a gay vet. I\u8217\'20\'19m happy with my mogs, enjoying the deep, uncomplicated love and pure joy of feline friendship.\par \par \uc2 Still, I\u8217\'20\'19ve had strangers tell me that giving up on relationships, as I moved into my 50s, is no way to live. Women and the cats they love have long been maligned, somehow seen as lesser-than or unfulfilled. Carving out my own little universe, I\u8217\'20\'19ve filled it with cats and creativity. I\u8217\'20\'19ve travelled to more than 35 countries, looked after big cats, baboons, bats and various wild orphans, written two books, ghostwritten and edited others, edited and created magazines, run a sell-out cat festival, directed pop videos and completed the screenplay adaption for my book, Strays, that has sparked interest from Hollywood producers. I do what I want, when I want \u8211\'20\'13 enjoying the privileges historically afforded to men.\par \par \uc2 Journalist and activist Gloria Steinem, who\u8217\'20\'19s had many felines over the decades, credits them as her teachers \u8216\'20\'18when it comes to a strong will and self-authority\u8217\'20\'19. When Steinem was asked how to raise the next generation of feminists, she said: \u8216\'20\'18Like cats. They tell you what they\u8217\'20\'19re going to do, and that\u8217\'20\'19s that.\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Cats, with their punky attitudes, have taught me defiance and everything else worth knowing. They are sensualists, things of beauty, who\u8217\'20\'19ve inspired artists for centuries. Lennon, McCartney, Bowie, Dylan and many of the greatest rock stars were all crazy-cat ladies. Us cat people of all political stripes may not agree on everything, but we don\u8217\'20\'19t let it get in the way of what matters most.\par \par \uc2 Strays: A Lost Cat, A Homeless Man and Their Journey Across America by Britt Collins is published by \uc2 Simon & Schuster\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241106ekb40030q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
