\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 History in the making: is the US finally about to elect its first female president?\b0\par\par\uc2 Milli Legrain \par \uc2 1,392 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 November 2024\par 20:37 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nail-biter of an election could result in Harris being the first female and first Asian American president\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is monumental,\u8221\'20\'1D said 19-year-old Kai Carter as she stood in line behind the White House where \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was about to take the stage a week before the 5 November election.\par \par \uc2 Carter was ecstatic at the prospect of Harris making history as the first Black female president of the United States. She attended the event with a group of fellow students from \uc2 Howard University\uc2 , the historically Black college in Washington DC, which is also the vice-president\u8217\'20\'19s alma mater.\par \par \uc2 Born in the United States of an Indian mother and Jamaican father, Harris, the first female vice-president, is also potentially on the verge of becoming the first Asian American president, as well the country\u8217\'20\'19s first female president. Yet she is not making a big deal about it.\par \par \uc2 In her closing argument in Washington DC before one of the most consequential elections in the country\u8217\'20\'19s history, Harris did not refer to her gender or her race or how she may be breaking a glass ceiling. It\u8217\'20\'19s not something she brings up often on the campaign trail, choosing instead to focus on her middle-class upbringing and how she hopes to be a president for \u8220\'20\'1Call Americans\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Her central message that night was about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as a threat to democracy. \u8220\'20\'1CThis election is more than a choice between two parties and two different candidates. It is a choice about whether we have a country rooted in freedom for every American. Or one ruled by chaos and division.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Unlike \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , who made gender a central part of her 2016 run for office, at a time of historic polarization Harris chose to focus on issues over identity. That is also how she chose to run her unusually short campaign of 13 weeks after an ageing Biden finally passed her the mantle on 21 July.\par \par \uc2 Laurie Pohutsky, a Democratic state representative in Michigan, decided to run in 2018 after witnessing Trump\u8217\'20\'19s misogynistic campaign against \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  in 2016. Since then, she has introduced two key pieces of state legislation that lifted restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In a phone interview from the swing state governed by the Democrat \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , she said: \u8220\'20\'1CYou know, we weren\u8217\'20\'19t elected because we were women. And I think that when we frame it that way, we do a disservice to ourselves.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She said she agreed with Harris\u8217\'20\'19s choice not to focus on gender: \u8220\'20\'1CWhile it\u8217\'20\'19s historic, it\u8217\'20\'19s not what would make her a good president.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re long overdue for a female president,\u8221\'20\'1D she added. \u8220\'20\'1CBut that\u8217\'20\'19s not why I think people are voting for her. They\u8217\'20\'19re voting for her because of her record and the work that she\u8217\'20\'19s done and the things that she believes, versus what we know \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  believes.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Identity politics\par \par \uc2 In the face of misogyny and racism, it is Harris\u8217\'20\'19s detractors who have attempted to use her identity against her. Republicans regularly mispronounce her name or call her a \u8220\'20\'1CDEI hire\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 At the beginning of her campaign, Trump sought to steer the conversation towards race in an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/video/2024/aug/01/trump-repeats-lies-and-attacks-kamala-harriss-racial-identity-at-black-journalists-panel-video"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 interview}}}\uc2  with the \uc2 National Association of Black Journalists\uc2 , questioning whether Harris is indeed Black. Many recognize these {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2018/aug/10/trump-attacks-twitter-black-people-women"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 personal attacks}}}\uc2  as Trump\u8217\'20\'19s hallmark. Their purpose is to undermine debate, take his opponent off script, stoke division and ultimately attract media attention.\par \par \uc2 Christina Reynolds, senior vice-president for communications for Emily\u8217\'20\'19s List, a political action committee that backs pro-choice Democratic female candidates, including Harris, explains that women are often the butt of personal attacks whereas men are attacked for their policies. Reynolds has witnessed this first-hand after working on five presidential campaigns, including \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s.\par \par \uc2 This is just one example of the double standards women and particularly women of color face to get to the top. Another is the pressure on women to be both likable and competent, whereas a man can be one or the other. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.berkeley.edu/2024/08/15/kamala-harris-hidden-foe-pervasive-bias-against-powerful-middle-aged-women/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Research}}}\uc2  by UC Berkeley\u8217\'20\'19s Hass School of Business also shows that women in positions of power lose likability. This is particularly true of successful middle-aged women.\par \par \uc2 In 2016, Trump accused Clinton of being a \u8220\'20\'1Cnasty woman\u8221\'20\'1D while male pundits {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theatlantic.com/politics/archive/2016/10/the-sexism-of-telling-women-to-smile/623090/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told}}}\uc2  her to \u8220\'20\'1Csmile\u8221\'20\'1D more. When Harris, a former prosecutor, successfully grilled \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  in his confirmation hearing for the supreme court, Trump accused her too of being \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/trump-kamala-harris-joe-biden-vice-president-pick/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 nasty}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 A champion of women\u8217\'20\'19s rights\par \par \uc2 Despite Harris\u8217\'20\'19s attempts to detract attention from her gender and race, she has campaigned heavily on the issue of women\u8217\'20\'19s rights. \u8220\'20\'1CShe may not frame things in terms of her gender, but the first president or vice-president to invite \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers to the White House and to visit an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider \u8211\'20\'13 both of those firsts were \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D Reynolds said.\par \par \uc2 The overturning of Roe v Wade by three Trump-appointed supreme court justices in 2022 placed women\u8217\'20\'19s rights at the forefront of voters\u8217\'20\'19 concerns. The right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a hard-fought battle that was won in 1973. A poll from May 2024 from the nonpartisan \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2  suggested that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/politics/2024/05/13/broad-public-support-for-legal-abortion-persists-2-years-after-dobbs/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 63% of Americans believed \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  should be legal}}}\uc2  in all or most cases.\par \par \uc2 In perhaps one of the most moving moments of the Democratic national convention, three women told their harrowing personal stories of being denied medical care in states where abortions are restricted.\par \par \uc2 At the closing rally in Washington DC, Harris suggested Trump could take things even further: \u8220\'20\'1CHe would ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide, restrict access to birth control and put IVF at risk and force states to monitor women\u8217\'20\'19s pregnancies,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Harris has also proposed policies to appeal to people \u8211\'20\'13 especially women \u8211\'20\'13 who need to care for parents and young children at the same time, known as the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2024/oct/21/at-home-medicare-senior-spend-down-harris-plan"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sandwich generation}}}\uc2 . She talks about how she had to care for her mother before she died of cancer in 2009, and she has talked about her plan to have Medicare pay for home healthcare.\par \par \uc2 Read more of the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s 2024 US election coverage:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/nov/04/when-do-polls-close-election-day"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 When do polls close}}}\uc2  ?\par \par \uc2 *\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/nov/03/election-trump-harris-policies-issues"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump v Harris on key issues}}}\par \par \uc2 *\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2024/nov/03/if-trump-wins-the-election-these-11-issues-will-be-under-threat"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 What\u8217\'20\'19s at stake}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2024/nov/03/us-presidential-election-trump-harris"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 what else to know}}}\par \par \uc2 Signs of progress\par \par \uc2 Harris is running for office in a divided country, with Trump threatening {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2024/11/01/politics/donald-trump-liz-cheney-war-hawk-battle/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 violence}}}\uc2  against his political opponents. \u8220\'20\'1COn day one, if elected, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would walk into that office with an enemies list. When elected, I will walk in with a to-do list,\u8221\'20\'1D she said in DC last week to a crowd of more than 75,000 people.\par \par \uc2 And while in her closing argument the Democratic nominee made clear that she pledged to be a \u8220\'20\'1Cpresident for all Americans\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cto always put country above party and above self\u8221\'20\'1D, at the same time Reynolds noted that \u8220\'20\'1Cshe has taken the communities that she has been a part of\u8221\'20\'1D and ensured that they \u8220\'20\'1Chave a voice\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cthat they are included in conversations\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 As Americans watched \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  as speaker of the House and Harris as vice-president sitting behind Biden as he gave his {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.house.gov/feature-stories/2021-4-29-president-biden-addresses-congress"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 first address}}}\uc2  to Congress in April 2021, they were reminded of how women are increasingly occupying positions of power. The numbers tell a similar story. According to data provided by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://cawp.rutgers.edu/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Center for American Women and Politics}}}\uc2 , in 2017 the US had 105 female members of Congress out of 535. Today the number has reached 150, including rising stars such as \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  and Jasmine Crockett.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe still have a long way to go,\u8221\'20\'1D said Reynolds. But people no longer hear the word \u8220\'20\'1Ccandidate\u8221\'20\'1D \u8220\'20\'1Cwith the assumption that a candidate is a man\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAnd that\u8217\'20\'19s progress,\u8221\'20\'1D she added.\par \par \uc2 At Harris\u8217\'20\'19s closing address in Washington DC, Elaine Callahan, a self-described independent voter, felt compelled to back Harris in 2024: \u8220\'20\'1CIt is historic. Yes!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But as polls show Harris and Trump neck and neck in many swing states, she remembers what happened to Clinton back in 2016 and is prompted to \u8220\'20\'1Cpray to God there will be a shift\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 This article was amended on 4 November 2024. Laurie Pohutsky first ran for Michigan\u8217\'20\'19s house of representatives in 2018, not 2022.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241104ekb4001s1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
