\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 As I vote for president, I\u8217\'20\'19ll be thinking of what Amanda Zurawski told me\b0\par\par\uc2 Sophie Brickman \par \uc2 1,387 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 November 2024\par 20:15 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m casting my ballot on behalf of women who have suffered or even died because of Republican \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions\par \par \uc2 Shortly before America\u8217\'20\'19s first presidential election since the fall of Roe v Wade, I want to tell you the story of Amanda Zurawski, a bright light in the center of a perfect, horrendous storm.\par \par \uc2 A little over two years ago, Zurawski was 18 weeks pregnant with her first child, a child she and her husband had conceived after a year and a half of fertility treatments. When she started leaking fluid and sought medical help, her doctors told her there was no chance the fetus would survive. But Zurawski lives in Texas, a state with some of the most restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws in the country: in May of the previous year, the governor, Greg Abbott, had signed into law the Texas Heartbeat Act, also known as SB8, which makes performing abortions after detection of embryonic or fetal cardiac activity, usually at six weeks, illegal. That was on top of several existing statutes. Then, in June 2022, Roe fell.\par \par \uc2 And so Zurawski\u8217\'20\'19s doctors told her that by the letter of the law \u8211\'20\'13 as far as they understood it; more on that later \u8211\'20\'13 in order to get the medical care she so desperately needed, either her daughter\u8217\'20\'19s heart would have to stop, or her health would have to devolve into a \u8220\'20\'1Clife-threatening situation\u8221\'20\'1D, something Zurawski has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://wearethemeteor.com/texas-abortion-ban-stopped-doctors-helping-woman-miscarrying/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 previous called}}}\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cthe most horrific version of a staring contest: whose life would end first? Mine, or my daughter\u8217\'20\'19s?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Her doctors advised her not to leave a 15-minute radius of the hospital lest her situation spiral, nixing the already unfathomable idea of getting into a car or on to a plane to seek help from a less restrictive state, and risking going into septic shock in the middle of the Texas desert, or 30,000ft up in the air. So she went home to grieve her impending loss and brace for what might come \u8211\'20\'13 during which time, Texas\u8217\'20\'19s total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  trigger ban went into effect, which made performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  punishable by life in prison. And there Zurawski sat, waiting.\par \par \uc2 The next day, she developed sepsis \u8211\'20\'13 a condition her doctors felt was extreme enough to protect them from unintentionally violating the new law, allowing them to induce labor \u8211\'20\'13 and after three days in the ICU, she emerged from the experience having almost died, with her own future fertility compromised, and galvanized to make a change about the inhumane laws.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI admittedly didn\u8217\'20\'19t realize the ways in which an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  truly is just healthcare,\u8221\'20\'1D Zurawski told me this week when I reached her by phone during her early morning walk with her sheepadoodle, Millie, in Austin, where she lives with her husband, Josh. \u8220\'20\'1CI couldn\u8217\'20\'19t imagine that I would ever need or want one, since I was desperately trying to have a baby.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The first moment \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws and her own fertility journey intersected was early on in the IVF process. The likelihood of a multiples pregnancy increases when using IVF, but as she is not able to carry multiples, her doctor had discussed the possibility of needing to perform selective reduction surgery if more than one embryo implanted, something that is currently illegal in Texas.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSo I was aware that these laws could affect us, but not from the perspective that I would need it to save my life, and be denied healthcare,\u8221\'20\'1D she told me. When she found herself in the unimaginable situation of being turned away from the hospital by doctors who wanted to help her, but weren\u8217\'20\'19t sure they could, her eyes opened, and she and Josh vowed to fight.\par \par \uc2 Zurawski became the lead plaintiff in the landmark case, Zurawski v Texas, which sued the state of Texas to clarify the \u8220\'20\'1Cmedical emergency\u8221\'20\'1D exception in the law \u8211\'20\'13 a riveting and harrowing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.zurawskivtexas.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 new documentary}}}\uc2  about the case follows Zurawski and two fellow plaintiffs through the legal fight \u8211\'20\'13 and soon found herself catapulted on to the national stage. Her natural charisma, straight talk, and tragic story calcified into a perfect trifecta with the power \u8211\'20\'13 so hopes \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , who made her a campaign surrogate \u8211\'20\'13 of firing up the electorate.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHumanizing it is what\u8217\'20\'19s really getting people to sit up and pay attention,\u8221\'20\'1D Zurawski told me. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen you see a face and a real human who\u8217\'20\'19s been impacted by this, it\u8217\'20\'19s impossible to say, \u8216\'20\'18This is reasonable, this is exactly what we want for our country.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D She paused to take a breath. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s barbaric.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Where else in healthcare do we put a blanket rule over where you can and cannot receive treatment?\par \par \uc2 Amanda Zurawski\par \par \uc2 One of the most powerful scenes in the documentary shows Zurawski at home with her parents, her mother saying that she\u8217\'20\'19s always voted Republican, but won\u8217\'20\'19t after seeing her daughter almost die.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWill I say they\u8217\'20\'19re converted Democrats? No!\u8221\'20\'1D Zurawski told me, laughing, as she huffed her way up a hill. \u8220\'20\'1CBut I do think they are single-issue voters, at least in this election. It opened up their eyes a little bit to the legislature, and how laws are written, and how bans go into effect, and the real implications.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The real implications of, say, \u8220\'20\'1Cmedical exceptions\u8221\'20\'1D to a near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban?\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey don\u8217\'20\'19t work! Categorically!\u8221\'20\'1D she scoffed, citing the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://reproductiverights.org/case/zurawski-v-texas-abortion-emergency-exceptions/zurawski-v-texas/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 multiple patient plaintiffs}}}\uc2  in her case, alongside {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.propublica.org/article/georgia-abortion-ban-amber-thurman-death"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 other}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.propublica.org/article/josseli-barnica-death-miscarriage-texas-abortion-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 women}}}\uc2  who have died in our country awaiting care their doctors are prohibited, by law, from providing. \u8220\'20\'1CEvery pregnancy is inherently unique. Where else in healthcare do we put a blanket rule over where you can and cannot receive treatment?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In her work over the years since she lost her pregnancy, she\u8217\'20\'19s found that one key to changing minds lies in reframing the conversation from \u8220\'20\'1Cpro-life\u8221\'20\'1D v \u8220\'20\'1Cpro-choice\u8221\'20\'1D to one about healthcare access.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFor 50 years, the right worked really hard to politicize and weaponize and stigmatize the word \u8216\'20\'18\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. You say pro-choice or pro-life, and people are already on a side. But some of the time, she pointed out, people simply don\u8217\'20\'19t understand what it means to be on one side or the other.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ll be at a rally, and someone will come up to me and say, \u8216\'20\'18I didn\u8217\'20\'19t realize that in 1985, when I had a D&C\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 a dilation-and-curettage surgical procedure that removes tissue from the uterus after miscarriage \u8211\'20\'13 \u8220\'20\'1C\u8216\'20\'18that\u8217\'20\'19s an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\u8217\'20\'19 That\u8217\'20\'19s the same as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As Zurawski has crisscrossed the country, campaigning for the Harris-Walz ticket, another part of her family has also moved: her embryos. In February, the Alabama supreme court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.asrm.org/news-and-events/asrm-news/legally-speaking/frozen-embryo-destruction-and--potential-travel-restrictions-for-surrogacy-arrangements2/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruled}}}\uc2  that frozen embryos are \u8220\'20\'1Cunborn children\u8221\'20\'1D. Zurawski, living in a state that has a similar political climate \u8211\'20\'13 one in which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.everythinglubbock.com/news/local-news/city-of-slaton-city-council-unanimously-passes-abortion-ban-ordinance/amp/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 city councils}}}\uc2  have enacted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  travel bans, in effect criminalizing the use of cities\u8217\'20\'19 roads and highways to seek \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care \u8211\'20\'13 panicked, and rushed to move them to a safer place.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe implications of the ruling are just staggering,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. But, by some estimates, she admits that moving the embryos is itself a stopgap measure. \u8220\'20\'1CIf Trump is elected, it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t matter where the embryos are, or where we are. He will unleash chaos.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She cited {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://static.project2025.org/2025_MandateForLeadership_FULL.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Project 2025}}}\uc2 , a rightwing policy manifesto for Trump\u8217\'20\'19s second term that indicates plans not only to restrict birth control access and block access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills and medical equipment, but also potentially ban IVF and surrogacy in certain states.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWell, Josh and I have to use a surrogate now because of what my body went through. It\u8217\'20\'19s like they\u8217\'20\'19re saying, you\u8217\'20\'19re out of luck!\u8221\'20\'1D She paused, catching her breath on the other end of the phone, perhaps reaching the top of a hill. \u8220\'20\'1CIt could theoretically prevent us from having children.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 So, what\u8217\'20\'19s to be done? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://watch.eventive.org/togetherfilms/play/670db1d13a247100e803df13"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Watch}}}\uc2  the documentary. Share her story. Vote. Fight.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Sophie Brickman is a contributor to the New Yorker, the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  and other publications, and the author of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.amazon.com/Baby-Unplugged-Mothers-Balance-Digital/dp/0062966480/ref=tmm_hrd_swatch_0?_encoding=UTF8&qid=&sr="}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Baby, Unplugged: One Mother\u8217\'20\'19s Search for Balance, Reason, and Sanity in the Digital Age}}}\uc2  and the novel {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.harpercollins.com/products/plays-well-with-others-sophie-brickman?variant=41223635697698"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Plays Well With Others}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241104ekb4001bb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
