\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 How the Christian right is twisting the legacy of an anti-Nazi hero\b0\par\par\uc2 Alice Herman, the Guardian, and Karen Goll, Documented \par \uc2 2,504 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 November 2024\par 19:48 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 As the election approaches, experts say a rightwing author is using the theologian Dietrich Bonhoeffer \u8211\'20\'13 executed for resisting Hitler \u8211\'20\'13 as a figurehead against the left, raising fears of political violence\par \par \uc2 This article is co-published with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://documented.net/about-documented"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Documented}}}\par \par \uc2 Leading figures on the Christian right have seized on an unlikely hero in their campaign against secular government: Dietrich Bonhoeffer, an antifascist theologian and pastor who resisted the Nazi regime before he was executed in 1945.\par \par \uc2 Driving the trend is Eric Metaxas, a prolific author, speaker and celebrity on the Christian right, whose writings on Bonhoeffer and American politics provide the intellectual fodder for a movement that seeks to turn evangelicals against liberal policies on women\u8217\'20\'19s rights, LGBTQ+ inclusion and racial justice.\par \par \uc2 In the short term, this push has taken the form of a well-funded voter mobilization campaign ahead of the 5 November presidential election, with conservative organizations coordinating to screen Metaxas\u8217\'20\'19s 2024 film \u8211\'20\'13 which lays out an argument equating liberal policies with Nazism, and urges believers to emulate Bonhoeffer \u8211\'20\'13 in churches across the country.\par \par \uc2 In the long run, experts worry that the push to liken American liberal democracy to Nazi Germany could spur political violence, citing past examples of Christian extremists who invoked Bonhoeffer to justify bombing and shooting up \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re worried about post-election political violence, and this is a way of inspiring that,\u8221\'20\'1D said Victoria Barnett, a theologian and eminent scholar of Bonhoeffer and the Holocaust who has advocated for a nuanced understanding of Bonhoeffer and has cautioned against depicting Bonhoeffer as a kind of evangelical \u8220\'20\'1CLone Ranger\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 According to documents obtained by the Guardian and Documented, the production and distribution of the movie, called Letter to the American Church, was coordinated by the rightwing group Turning Point USA and American Letter Productions \u8211\'20\'13 the film division of Metaxas Media, an entertainment business founded by Eric Metaxas.\par \par \uc2 Through the Letter to the American Church tour, an initiative launched in mid-2024, these groups and others have screened the film at churches, community organizations, and small groups for donations of any amount; churches willing to screen the film received an \u8220\'20\'1Cextensive marketing kit\u8221\'20\'1D to promote it.\par \par \uc2 Funding for this film and tour was pledged, in part, by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.propublica.org/article/inside-ziklag-secret-christian-charity-2024-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the secretive Christian donor network Ziklag}}}\uc2 , a non-profit that embraces the aims of a growing movement of Christian nationalists who strive to rule over \uc2 US government\uc2  and society. As a piece of Ziklag\u8217\'20\'19s larger, coordinated effort to get out the vote, the group committed to funding movie screenings in churches across the country \u8220\'20\'1Cwith a focus on oversaturation in the battleground states\u8221\'20\'1D to galvanize congregations and increase evangelical voter turnout.\par \par \uc2 Since its launch, the film has been screened at least 170 times across the country, including more than 40 times in key swing states. Local GOP chapters and numerous outside organizations on the right have also held screenings, some in conjunction with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://shastascout.org/bethel-church-considers-launching-election-groups-partners-with-activist-laura-hobbs-to-sign-up-poll-workers/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 poll worker sign-up}}}\uc2  initiatives and alongside {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/schaykg/status/1814738778088743079"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Turning Point Action}}}\uc2 , a group the Trump campaign has relied on for its voter registration and turnout efforts.\par \par \uc2 Internal videos produced by Ziklag, obtained by the Guardian and Documented, detail Ziklag\u8217\'20\'19s 2024 election strategy, pledging $800,000 to \u8220\'20\'1Cfocus on rallying the church behind biblically based voting using Eric Metaxas\u8217\'20\'19 new documentary, Letter to the American Church\u8221\'20\'1D. Organizations that partnered with Metaxas, including Turning Point Action and TPUSA Faith, were promised donations from Ziklag in this effort to engage evangelical voters.\par \par \uc2 Ziklag and Turning Point USA did not return requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 In an email, Metaxas denied having \u8220\'20\'1Canything to do with the making of the LETTER film\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 although he stars in the movie and founded one of the companies that produced it. Metaxas rejected the term \u8220\'20\'1CChristian nationalism\u8221\'20\'1D, saying it was used to \u8220\'20\'1Cdemonize people who believe that we Christians are obliged to live our faith in every sphere, including the political\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 And he suggested that Bonhoeffer scholars and his critics were in fact the ones inciting political violence, not him.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Co-opted by extremists\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Born in 1906 and raised in a family of intellectuals and academics, Bonhoeffer dedicated himself as a young man to theology and ministry. At 21, he wrote a dissertation exploring the idea of Christians\u8217\'20\'19 ethical and moral obligations to one another and society.\par \par \uc2 But Bonhoeffer\u8217\'20\'19s prodigious academic career was cut short by the rise of Hitler\u8217\'20\'19s Nazi party.\par \par \uc2 An early dissident, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bonhoeffersociety.org/about/bonhoeffer/biography/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bonhoeffer wrote}}}\uc2  in 1933 that the Hitler government\u8217\'20\'19s increasingly discriminatory and violent oppression of Jews was a \u8220\'20\'1Cproblem for the church\u8221\'20\'1D, which he viewed as responsible for opposing such policies, even if they were not directed at Christians.\par \par \uc2 His work in the following decade, with other dissenting clergy and networks of resisters, would eventually lead the regime to accuse him of aiding in a plot to assassinate Hitler. He was arrested in 1943 and hanged in 1945 in the Flossenb\u252\'00\'FCrg concentration camp, leaving behind his letters from prison and numerous writings on ethics, morality and the role of Christians in a secular, modern society.\par \par \uc2 Before he was executed, Bonhoeffer warned of the dangers of zealotry and groupthink \u8211\'20\'13 perils he believed societies face during times of political upheaval.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C[The] upsurge of power is so terrific that it deprives men of an independent judgement,\u8221\'20\'1D wrote Bonhoeffer, \u8220\'20\'1Cand they give up trying \u8211\'20\'13 more or less unconsciously \u8211\'20\'13 to assess the new state of affairs for themselves.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Scholars of Bonhoeffer, and Bonhoeffer\u8217\'20\'19s living relatives, have argued that Bonhoeffer teaches Christians to reject nationalisms of all kinds.\par \par \uc2 To their dismay, Christian nationalists have embraced Bonhoeffer, frequently invoking his participation in the 20 July 1944 plot to assassinate Hitler as an example of moral courage. In this interpretation, Bonhoeffer is cast not as the contemplative theologian who agonized over his role in the antifascist resistance, but as a Christian warrior with the political leanings of a 21st-century American evangelical.\par \par \uc2 Tobias Korenke, Bonhoeffer\u8217\'20\'19s great-nephew, has expressed frustration about the use of Bonhoeffer by the religious right, saying in a recent interview with Germany\u8217\'20\'19s Die Zeit newspaper that Bonhoeffer\u8217\'20\'19s name had been \u8220\'20\'1Cco-opted by extremists\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 At its worst, this interpretation of Bonhoeffer has led to violence. Michael Bray, a pastor who was convicted for his role in bombing numerous \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in 1984 and 1985, cited Bonhoeffer as an inspiration. Paul Jennings Hill, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  zealot who shot and killed a physician at an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in 1994, too, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.christiancentury.org/article/2015-05/claims-bonhoeffer"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 invoked Bonhoeffer}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Metaxas\u8217\'20\'19s political evolution\par \par \uc2 One evangelical celebrity who has consistently and effectively worked to popularize Bonhoeffer on the right is Eric Metaxas, a Yale-educated talkshow host whose popular biography of Bonhoeffer helped introduce the historical figure to a broader audience in the US.\par \par \uc2 Metaxas\u8217\'20\'19s 2009 book, Bonhoeffer: Pastor, Martyr, Prophet, Spy, was marketed to Christians but achieved popular acclaim \u8211\'20\'13 serving as a biography and an inspirational history for readers familiar and unfamiliar with Bonhoeffer.\par \par \uc2 To explain Bonhoeffer\u8217\'20\'19s participation in the resistance, Metaxas writes that God had called him to \u8220\'20\'1Cget his hands dirty\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In turn the New York-based Metaxas, already an unusual east coast ambassador for conservative evangelicalism, achieved a new level of fame.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHe was the rare figure in the evangelical world who was mixing it up with the culture shapers and the intellectuals in New York City,\u8221\'20\'1D said John Fea, a historian who has documented the rise of contemporary Christian nationalism. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd then the Bonhoeffer book came out, and that skyrocketed him.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 At the 2012 National Prayer Breakfast, an annual gala in Washington convening lawmakers and Christian faith leaders, Metaxas spoke about the genesis of his Bonhoeffer biography in a speech delivered with the cadence and occasional vulnerability of a stand-up routine.\par \par \uc2 Fifteen minutes into the 30-minute talk, Metaxas reflected on the book\u8217\'20\'19s widespread popularity, joking that \u8220\'20\'1Cit was read even by president \uc2 George W Bush\uc2 , who is intellectually incurious, as we\u8217\'20\'19ve all read. He read the book.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Metaxas paused, turning to Barack and \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2 , who were seated to his right. \u8220\'20\'1CNo pressure,\u8221\'20\'1D he added, and thrust a copy of the book into the hands of the president, who played along, smiling for a photo with the book.\par \par \uc2 During the remainder of his speech, Metaxas became sharply political, railing in particular against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a Bonhoeffer moment,\u8221\'20\'1D Metaxas declared, implicitly comparing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the Holocaust and calling on Christians to intervene in the manner that many Germans did not.\par \par \uc2 In the decade that followed, Metaxas\u8217\'20\'19s political evolution has turned even more dramatically to the right. In a 2016 column in the Wall Street Journal, he endorsed \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , acknowledging his reservations about the real estate mogul but writing that if Christians voted for \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , \u8220\'20\'1CGod will not hold us guiltless,\u8221\'20\'1D citing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a top issue.\par \par \uc2 Metaxas eventually embraced Maga politics fully, vowing to support Trump as the former president falsely claimed the 2020 presidential election had been stolen and attempted to overturn the results.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is the most horrible thing that has happened in the history of our nation,\u8221\'20\'1D Metaxas told Trump on 30 November, in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://web.archive.org/web/20210102002101/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=c3MAz7Pvp_o"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 since deleted }}}\uc2  recording of a phone call between Metaxas and the former president that ran on Metaxas\u8217\'20\'19s show. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19d be happy to die in this fight,\u8221\'20\'1D he told Trump later in the call.\par \par \uc2 Experts question the underpinnings of Metaxas\u8217\'20\'19s work on Bonhoeffer. A recent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.change.org/p/stop-misusing-dietrich-bonhoeffer-to-support-political-violence-and-christian-nationalism"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 petition}}}\uc2  circulated by eight Bonhoeffer scholars, and signed by dozens of clergy and scholars of religion, argues that Metaxas \u8220\'20\'1Chas manipulated the Bonhoeffer story to support Christian Nationalism\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 It warns that in his social media posts and public appearances, Metaxas \u8220\'20\'1Cglorifies violence and draws inappropriate analogies between our political system and that of Nazi Germany\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Barnett argues that Metaxas\u8217\'20\'19s book overstated Bonhoeffer\u8217\'20\'19s role in the plot to assassinate Hitler and that Metaxas \u8220\'20\'1Ctapped right into\u8221\'20\'1D a \u8220\'20\'1Cmythology that Bonhoeffer was like the Lone Ranger, the Christian hero who fought the Nazis\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the full extent of Bonhoeffer\u8217\'20\'19s role in the conspiracy has been disputed \u8211\'20\'13 and however closely involved he might have been in the plot, Bonhoeffer did not legitimize political violence in religious terms.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHe did not justify his knowledge of the conspiracy on his being Christian \u8211\'20\'13 he just refused to do that, because he understood the dangers of that,\u8221\'20\'1D said Barnett.\par \par \uc2 Blurred lines between religion and politics\par \par \uc2 In Letter to the American Church, Metaxas, who narrates much of the documentary-style film, and a roster of rightwing pastors and activists take the Bonhoeffer narrative a step further, casting liberals and Democrats as being as destructive as Nazis and calling on evangelicals to take action and oppose evil.\par \par \uc2 They insist liberal teachings are destroying the family and religion in an effort to strip away freedoms from the American people. The speakers warn that if evangelicals do not rise up against ideas that they portray as evil, such as LGBTQ+ rights and women\u8217\'20\'19s rights, the country is headed for destruction.\par \par \uc2 At the heart of their argument is Bonhoeffer.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C Bonhoeffer effectively told the church that if we\u8217\'20\'19re going to see any effective change for the better, they needed to start taking action and getting political,\u8221\'20\'1D Metaxas tells his audience in the film. \u8220\'20\'1CHe said those who call themselves Christians have an obligation to God to get political if necessary, and to take a bold and likely dangerous stance against their own government.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Letter to the American Church has partnered with influential rightwing organizations, including the pro-Trump Moms for America, the anti-LGBTQ+ Her Voice Movement, and Patriot Academy \u8211\'20\'13 a Christian nationalist group that seeks to rewrite the constitution \u8211\'20\'13 to promote the film and spread its message. The organization also partnered with Million Voices, an evangelical get-out-the-vote initiative, to launch a \u8220\'20\'1CPledge to Vote\u8221\'20\'1D campaign, aiming to see \u8220\'20\'1C250,000 pledge to vote\u8221\'20\'1D after seeing the movie.\par \par \uc2 The effort highlights how some tax-exempt religious organizations push the boundaries of legal restrictions on electioneering.\par \par \uc2 Churches are banned from issuing endorsements or campaigning on behalf of a candidate, but they may be able to participate in the screenings without fear of incurring legal penalties, said Andrew Seidel, a constitutional attorney who specializes in first amendment and religious freedoms cases.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COne of the ways that this Christian nationalist movement has started operating in the political space, is to create these kinds of movies and then push them out through churches,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Despite the timing of the screenings \u8211\'20\'13 which end on election day \u8211\'20\'13 and the film\u8217\'20\'19s ultra-political content, \u8220\'20\'1Cthe churches would all have, probably, some pretty credible deniability, if they said: \u8216\'20\'18Hey, we were just [given] a chance to run a movie we thought our folks would be interested in.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 The Letter to the American Church tour officially ends on 5 November \u8211\'20\'13 but don\u8217\'20\'19t expect Bonhoeffer to go away anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 A splashy feature film, Bonhoeffer: Pastor. Spy. Assassin, is to debut in theaters across the US on 22 November. Bonhoeffer, the movie, features a star-studded cast of German actors and promises to be a captivating second world war drama. (Americans might recognize August Diehl, who plays the resistance theologian Martin Niem\u246\'00\'F6ller, from his role in Quentin Tarantino\u8217\'20\'19s Inglourious Basterds, or the 2010 thriller Salt.)\par \par \uc2 Posters for the movie show Bonhoeffer carrying a gun. \u8220\'20\'1CWith world-shattering stakes,\u8221\'20\'1D the Christian streaming company \uc2 Angel Studios{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://shop.angel.com/pages/show-bonhoeffer?srsltid=AfmBOorLuRi0Re6swyCD-rPKXK_sRk2Zusr_YrasvcgGFfxUCtn8-zQz"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 writes}}}\uc2  in its promotional materials for the film, Bonhoeffer \u8220\'20\'1Cbegs the question, how far will you go to stand up for what\u8217\'20\'19s right?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Bonhoeffer scholars reject this gun-toting version of the theologian \u8211\'20\'13 and the film\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Chow-far-would-you-go\u8221\'20\'1D framing. \u8220\'20\'1C[In] the current, highly-polarized climate in the United States, these are dangerous words,\u8221\'20\'1D wrote the leaders of the English and German-language International Bonhoeffer Society last month in Die Zeit.\par \par \uc2 Even the actors starring in the feature film have distanced themselves from the Christian far right, writing in a 4 November statement that figures on the far right \u8220\'20\'1Cdivide us, spread fear, lies and threaten their political opponents by framing their selves as victims and marginalized \u8211\'20\'13 a common ploy by far-right groups worldwide. These kind of people, this demagoguery and violent system was what Bonhoeffer spoke up against.\u8221\'20\'1D They added that they stand by the film, \u8220\'20\'1Cwhich to our belief tells a completely different story to what a radical few want to make out of it\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Still, in their petition, the scholars warn more broadly of a possible uptick in violence after the election linked to the Christian far right.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDietrich Bonhoeffer\u8217\'20\'19s words and witness will be used to pit one side against the other, to fight \u8216\'20\'18evil\u8217\'20\'19, to put \u8216\'20\'18America First\u8217\'20\'19, and to justify violence,\u8221\'20\'1D they write. \u8220\'20\'1CThe misalignment between these views and actions and Bonhoeffer\u8217\'20\'19s own cannot be overstated. When you hear these grievous misuses, and you will, do not be fooled.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241103ekb300107\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
