\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 In Florida, the future of  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  might come down to men\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman in Orlando \par \uc2 977 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 November 2024\par 15:23 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Women\u8217\'20\'19s access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a top issue, as activists rush to convince men they also have stake in the fight\par \par \uc2 When Maxwell Frost bounded on stage at a Saturday morning rally in support of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/florida"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Florida}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  rights, the 27-year-old congressman was quick to explain why he had shown up.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m so proud to be here as an ally and partner in this fight!\u8221\'20\'1D he yelled to the roughly hundred-strong crowd who had gathered in an Orlando church courtyard, clutching handwritten sings with messages like \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans are killing us\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cwomb-tang clan ain\u8217\'20\'19t nothing to fuck with\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 His biological mother had given him up for adoption, said Frost, who wore a black T-shirt that read \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is Health Care\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CThe thing that made it sacred was the fact that she had a choice,\u8221\'20\'1D Frost said. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve had enough of people trying to use parts of all of our identities to take away freedoms from other people.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The crowd \u8211\'20\'13 mostly women \u8211\'20\'13 roared in response.\par \par \uc2 In an election where women\u8217\'20\'19s access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has become a top issue, activists are now rushing to convince men that they also have a stake in the fight \u8211\'20\'13 and that, come Tuesday, they should vote accordingly.\par \par \uc2 Although men support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights at similar rates as women, they seem to be far less driven by the issue. Less than half of men identify as \u8220\'20\'1Cpro-choice\u8221\'20\'1D, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/506759/broader-support-abortion-rights-continues-post-dobbs.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Gallup}}}\uc2 , and are far more likely to see the economy or immigration as their top issue. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thehill.com/policy/healthcare/4694925-majority-men-of-color-support-abortion/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 One poll}}}\uc2  of men of color found that, although more than 80% believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal, less than half prioritized candidates who supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s that common misconception that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a woman\u8217\'20\'19s issue,\u8221\'20\'1D said Zach Rivera, a 24-year-old activist with Men4Choice, a national group dedicated to energizing men who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Over the last several weeks, Rivera has spent countless hours knocking on doors in Florida neighborhoods in support of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/nov/02/florida-amendment-4-abortion-rights"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Amendment 4}}}\uc2 , a ballot measure that would enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights into the Florida constitution and overturn the state\u8217\'20\'19s ban on the procedure past six weeks of pregnancy. Nine other states are also set to vote on similar ballot measures on election day, but Amendment 4 may face the steepest odds. In order to pass, the measure must secure 60% of the vote in a state that has veered sharply to the right in recent years and whose state government has repeatedly tried to kneecap the campaign behind the amendment.\par \par \uc2 Recent polling has found that support for measure hovers somewhere in the 50% range: while {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fau.edu/newsdesk/articles/oct24nationalandflapoll"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one poll found}}}\uc2  that 58% of Florida voters support it, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://floridapolitics.com/archives/703828-poll-shows-abortion-rights-measure-still-falling-short-of-passage-in-florida/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 another}}}\uc2  put it closer to 54%. In the latter poll, 55% of women supported the measure, compared with 53% of men. In an election as tight as Florida\u8217\'20\'19s, nudging more men to vote yes could mean the difference between victory and defeat.\par \par \uc2 As Saturday morning gave way to a humid afternoon punctuated by bursts of rain, Rivera trudged from house to house in a wealthy, blue area of Orlando, dropping off Men4Choice stickers and attempting to talk to voters about Amendment 4. Numerous houses had blue \u8220\'20\'1CHarris/Walz\u8221\'20\'1D signs in their front lawns \u8211\'20\'13 but not a single one had a purple \u8220\'20\'1CYes on 4\u8221\'20\'1D sign. Voters were reluctant to talk about it. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m friends with everyone,\u8221\'20\'1D one woman said.\par \par \uc2 Rivera has had better luck, he said, with phone banking. In one recent conversation, Rivera described urging one reluctant man to think about his future wife and children: what if, 10 years down the line, his wife died because an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban blocked her from accessing medical care? How would he reveal to his kids that he didn\u8217\'20\'19t vote?\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe whole point of this movement is to think about future you,\u8221\'20\'1D Rivera recalled telling him. The man, Rivera said, decided to vote in favor of Amendment 4.\par \par \uc2 At an early voting site in Tampa, 24-year-old Brandon McCray cited women\u8217\'20\'19s rights as one of his greatest concerns in the 2024 elections. It helped convince him to vote for Harris. \u8220\'20\'1CAmendment 4 would just protect a lot of women,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. Banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said, \u8220\'20\'1Cis the biggest violation to a human right\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 McCray may be a relative anomaly among his peers. Appalled by the triple-punch of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s victory in the 2016 election, the sexual violence exposed by the #MeToo movement in late 2017 and the 2022 overturning of Roe v Wade, young women have become the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2024/aug/07/gen-z-voters-political-ideology-gender-gap"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 most progressive cohort ever measured}}}\uc2  in US history \u8211\'20\'13 but young men have inched towards the right. While 62% of young women now support Harris, 55% of young men back \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , according to recent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2024/10/18/upshot/polls-trump-harris-young-men.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New York Times}}}\uc2  polling. Moreover, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2024/aug/07/gen-z-voters-political-ideology-gender-gap"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 young men\u8217\'20\'19s participation in politics}}}\uc2  is falling, with young women now on track to vote, rally and donate more frequently.\par \par \uc2 For many young women, the trend is so obvious that its unremarkable. \u8220\'20\'1CThe right-leaning has more traditional values and more traditional values tends to benefit men more than it benefits women,\u8221\'20\'1D said Briana Valle, 22. \u8220\'20\'1CFor obvious reasons, people are always gonna go for what benefits them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Leila Wotruba, 22, added: \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s a lot more at stake for women.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As a gay man, Rivera knows that he may appear to not have much at stake in the fight over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. But Rivera sees the future of the issue, especially in Florida, as a \u8220\'20\'1Clitmus test\u8221\'20\'1D for other rights.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s what I tell people: Even if this might not be a personal issue to you overall \u8230\'20\'26 you are definitely next,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey are just waiting until there\u8217\'20\'19s nobody left to defend you.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241103ekb3001xh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
