\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Sounding alarm for 10 years\u8217\'20\'19: Mississippi residents warn of Project 2025 ramifications\b0\par\par\uc2 Adria R Walker \par \uc2 1,382 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 November 2024\par 12:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 In the state with a conservative majority in power, many of the project\u8217\'20\'19s proposed policies are making life difficult, with \u8216\'20\'18cruelty the point\u8217\'20\'19, say activists\par \par \uc2 Project 2025, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/may/26/what-is-project-2025-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 conservative blueprint}}}\uc2  for a second Trump presidency, has been used as a warning by Democrats to highlight what would be in store for the country if he were to win the upcoming election. But for some Americans, much of Project 2025 isn\u8217\'20\'19t a distant possible future \u8211\'20\'13 it is a current-day reality.\par \par \uc2 In several states across the country, there are already {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/sep/25/abortion-bans-healthcare-maternal-mortality"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 extreme \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  bans}}}\uc2  that have led to the deaths of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.propublica.org/article/georgia-abortion-ban-amber-thurman-death"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 multiple pregnant women}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2024/11/01/nevaeh-crain-death-texas-abortion-ban-emtala/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 at least one teen}}}\uc2  ; restrictive voting policies that make it difficult for citizens to cast their ballots; defunding of education and censorship of books; and other such policies that have also been proposed by the authors of Project 2025. If the plan is successfully implemented, many policies that are already reshaping some states would become federal laws.\par \par \uc2 Project 2025 is \u8220\'20\'1Ca fascist blueprint for governance\u8221\'20\'1D, said Lea Campbell, the founding president of the Mississippi Rising Coalition, a grassroots organization that supports lower-income communities. But Mississippi, she said, which has an entrenched conservative majority, is already dealing with many of the proposed policies, specifically the policing and surveilling of marginalized people.\par \par \uc2 Families across Mississippi are still rebuilding after the largest immigration raid in the country, which happened {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wapt.com/article/mississippi-ice-raid-5-years-later/61825647"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 five years ago.}}}\uc2  In 2019, on the first day of school, scores of children returned home to find that their parents were part of 680 people who were taken into custody, some of whom were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://immigrantjustice.org/research-items/explainer-ongoing-harms-trump-era-mississippi-raids-and-immigration-prosecutions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 subsequently deported,}}}\uc2  after \uc2 US Immigration and Customs Enforcement\uc2  agents raided seven poultry plants. Under Project 2025, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vera.org/news/project-2025-is-a-pathway-to-mass-deportations-and-family-separation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 mass deportations}}}\uc2  would be expedited, further tearing families apart.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have been sounding the alarm for more than 10 years, just around the policies in this state, enacted by conservatives that target the most vulnerable among us,\u8221\'20\'1D Campbell said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve been saying about policies under this ultra-conservative legislature that we have here in Mississippi [that] the cruelty is the point, it seems, with a lot of this legislation that targets poor people and people of color, and women, and the queer and trans community.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Even when voters have made it clear that they disagree with proposed conservative policies, lawmakers have found ways to maneuver around their wishes.\par \par \uc2 In 2011, 58% of Mississippians rejected a \u8220\'20\'1Cpersonhood amendment\u8221\'20\'1D, which, had it passed, would have defined fertilized eggs as people. Opponents warned that because of the way {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/sections/itsallpolitics/2011/11/08/142159280/mississippi-voters-reject-personhood-amendment"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the amendment defined life}}}\uc2 , it would ban all abortions with no exceptions for rape or incest, and it would have complicated in vitro fertilization.\par \par \uc2 Still, in 2013, the state, along with Kansas, Kentucky, Wyoming, Ohio and North Dakota attempted to pass so-called {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2019/us/abortion-laws-states.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cfetal heartbeat\u8221\'20\'1D bills,}}}\uc2  in which \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned after as early as six weeks once cardiac activity is detected. For several years, multiple states tried to pass similar bills and other restrictions. By 2019, 15 states introduced \u8220\'20\'1Cfetal heartbeat\u8221\'20\'1D bills; six were successful in passing them.\par \par \uc2 Project 2025 aims to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/aug/05/project-2025-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 enforce the Comstock Act}}}\uc2 , a 151-year-old anti-obscenity law that prohibits the mailing of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related materials. Doing so could lead to a de facto nationwide ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics and advocates rely on the mail to send and receive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. The plan also indicates a goal of legally recognizing fetuses as people.\par \par \uc2 Currently in Mississippi, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://theintercept.com/2024/10/16/abortion-pills-mail-usps/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 drug-sniffing dogs}}}\uc2  have been used to intercept \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. And in nearby Louisiana, two common \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills that are also often used for miscarriage management, softening the cervix during labor and other procedures {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/oct/01/louisiana-abortion-pills"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have been reclassified}}}\uc2  as \u8220\'20\'1Ccontrolled substances\u8221\'20\'1D, despite doctors warning that doing so will harm women.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Don\u8217\'20\'19t miss important US election coverage. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Get our free app}}}\uc2  and sign up for election alerts\par \par \uc2 As it stands, organizers and activists in states that have proto-Project 2025 policies are able to push for change on a state and local level. If Project 2025 were implemented, however, many of those policies could become federally enshrined, drastically changing the way lawmakers and advocates can push to repeal such laws.\par \par \uc2 Nsombi Lambright-Haynes, the executive director of One Voice Mississippi, a civil rights organization, said that the non-profit has been encouraging people to vote by educating them about what Project 2025 would do to the public education system and to \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are pointing out what we already have and then pointing out the danger that can come if something like this is fully implemented,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s really like a wake-up call.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A \u8216\'20\'18beacon\u8217\'20\'19 to get people \u8216\'20\'18fervent in their racism\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Two years ago, Jackson, Mississippi\u8217\'20\'19s capital and the Blackest city in the country, was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/sep/01/jackson-mississippi-clean-drinking-water-flooding-problems"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 without water}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.splcenter.org/news/2023/06/28/timeline-jackson-mississippi-water-problems"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 for more than a month}}}\uc2  due to decades of the state refusing to invest in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/jan/07/jackson-mississippi-water-outage-neighbors-helping"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 infrastructure}}}\uc2 . Danyelle Holmes, an organizer with the non-profit Poor People\u8217\'20\'19s Campaign, said that implementing Project 2025 nationwide would worsen the rest of the country\u8217\'20\'19s infrastructure woes.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CProject 2025 supports removing clean water protection,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat puts marginalized communities really at a very vulnerable place and position, as we\u8217\'20\'19re feeling the impact of not having access to clean and safe drinking water.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Project 2025 would downgrade per- and polyfluoroalkyl substances (PFAS) from being classified as \u8220\'20\'1Chazardous\u8221\'20\'1D to \u8220\'20\'1Ccontaminants\u8221\'20\'1D, and it would eliminate the \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s (\uc2 EPA\uc2 ) Toxic Substances Control Act, preventing the government from adequately monitoring the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.newsweek.com/heres-what-makes-project-2025-so-dangerous-our-environment-opinion-1946400"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cumulative effect of toxins.}}}\par \par \uc2 The plan could {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.salon.com/2023/09/05/dark-right-wing-network-recruits-maga-army-to-replace-50k-workers-plans-to-purge/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cerode the country\u8217\'20\'19s system }}}\uc2  of checks and balances\u8221\'20\'1D, according to an analysis by Salon, increasing the president\u8217\'20\'19s power over all of the federal government. But many states have already given such extreme powers to their state officials.\par \par \uc2 In Texas, for instance, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://newrepublic.com/article/183101/texas-death-star-summer-heat-effect"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDeath Star\u8221\'20\'1D bill}}}\uc2  prevents cities and counties from passing measures that are stronger than those passed at the state level across a broad range of policy areas. While in Florida, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , the governor, has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/nation/2023/04/16/florida-ron-desantis-governor-legislature-power/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 augmented his own power}}}\uc2  by using the state\u8217\'20\'19s republican supermajority to cement his ideas into law.\par \par \uc2 Project 2025 would eliminate {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.americanprogress.org/article/project-2025-would-end-dhs-law-enforcement-training/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Federal Law Enforcement Training Centers (FLETC),}}}\uc2  an interagency law enforcement training body, increase the use of the federal death penalty, eliminate the use of consent decrees and increase the use of mandatory minimum sentences, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tminstituteldf.org/project-2025-public-safety-criminal-legal-system/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an analysis}}}\uc2  by the Thurgood Marshall Institute, the research arm of the Legal Defense Fund.\par \par \uc2 In Mississippi, police departments across the state have already been embroiled in controversy. Six law enforcement officers in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/10/mississippi-goon-squad-officers-sentencing"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Rankin county}}}\uc2  were convicted for torturing two Black men, while a federal investigation found that police in a majority-Black town elsewhere in the state have \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/lexington-mississippi-police-department-civil-rights-investigation-c494a06af8b45841d280a65be3ca055c"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 created a system }}}\uc2  where officers can relentlessly violate the law\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Project 2025 would make it so that the rest of the country experiences the restrictive, conservative lawmaking that many southerners have been organizing against for years, said Courtney Jones, a writer and researcher with \u8216\'20\'18SippTalk Media, a digital media platform, said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s no part of this nation that is untouched by the harm that racism does. Project 2025 is more of a beacon to get people to be more fervent in their racism,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CInstead of whispering about it or doing political loopholes, now they\u8217\'20\'19re just directly saying, \u8216\'20\'18We\u8217\'20\'19re going to take these small things that we\u8217\'20\'19ve been doing to these specific populations and now we\u8217\'20\'19re just going to amplify them. And we\u8217\'20\'19re going to make this happen across the entire country.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Jones noted that organizers in the state and region had long been trying to warn the rest of the country about what was happening and what might soon come for them. Their warnings were met with dismissal, he said, as people believed \u8220\'20\'1Cthat\u8217\'20\'19s just Mississippi for you\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe people here that are doing the work have always been doing the work,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CA lot of people in Mississippi recognize that because we\u8217\'20\'19ve always been overlooked, that we have to kind of look within in order to save ourselves. There is no grand agency or political candidate that\u8217\'20\'19s ever going to come here and suddenly fix things for us.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241103ekb3000un\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
