\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Contested state supreme court seats are site of hidden battle for  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  access\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,327 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 November 2024\par 12:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Judges are on ballot in election swing states such as Arizona and Florida whose courts recently upheld \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be on the ballot in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/sep/26/election-abortion-ballot-measures-states"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 10 states}}}\uc2  on Tuesday, and it\u8217\'20\'19s one of the top issues in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/us-elections-2024"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 presidential contest}}}\uc2  between {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/kamala-harris"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Kamala Harris}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2 . But it is also key to less publicized but increasingly contested races for seats on state supreme courts, which often have the last word on whether a state will ban or protect access to the procedure.\par \par \uc2 This year, voters in 33 states have the chance to decide who sits on their state supreme courts. Judges will be on the ballot in Arizona and Florida, where supreme courts have recently ruled to uphold \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. They are also up for election in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/article/2024/aug/21/montana-becomes-eighth-state-to-put-measure-protecting-abortion-on-ballot"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Montana}}}\uc2 , where the supreme court has backed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the face of a deeply \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -hostile state legislature.\par \par \uc2 In addition, supreme court judges are on the ballot in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/oct/28/abortion-rights-parental-rights"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Maryland}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/sep/13/nebraska-supreme-court-abortion-ballot-measures"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Nebraska}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/may/20/nevada-signatures-abortion-vote-november"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Nevada}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 all of which are holding votes on measures that could enshrine access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in their state constitutions. Should those measures pass, state supreme courts will almost certainly determine how to interpret them.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups are already gearing up for lawsuits.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re all going to end up in court, because they\u8217\'20\'19re going to take vague language from these ballot initiatives to ask for specific things like funding for all abortions, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/oct/28/abortion-rights-parental-rights"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  for minors without parental consent}}}\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kristi Hamrick, chief media and policy strategist for the powerful anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group Students for Life of America, which is currently campaigning around state supreme court races in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/oct/30/arizona-voters-election-trump-harris"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Arizona}}}\uc2  and Oklahoma. \u8220\'20\'1CJudges have become a very big, important step in how \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law is actually realized.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In Michigan and Ohio, which voted in 2022 and 2023 respectively to amend their state constitution to include \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, advocates are still fighting in court over whether those amendments can be used to strike down \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions. Come November, however, the ideological makeup of both courts may flip.\par \par \uc2 Spending in state supreme court races has surged since Roe fell. In the 2021-2022 election cycle, candidates, interest groups and political parties spent more than $100m, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/research-reports/politics-judicial-elections-2021-2022"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to the Brennan Center for Justice}}}\uc2 . After adjusting for inflation, that\u8217\'20\'19s almost double the amount spent in any previous midterm cycle.\par \par \uc2 In 2023, a race for a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/apr/04/wisconsin-supreme-court-election-results"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 single seat}}}\uc2  on the Wisconsin supreme court alone cost $51m \u8211\'20\'13 and hinged on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, as the liberal-leaning candidate talked up her support for the procedure. (As in many other \u8211\'20\'13 but not all \u8211\'20\'13 state supreme court races, the candidates in Wisconsin were technically non-partisan.) After that election, liberals assumed a 4-3 majority on the Wisconsin supreme court. The court is now set to hear a case involving the state\u8217\'20\'19s 19th-century \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, which is not currently being enforced but is still on the books.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s too early to tally up the money that has been dumped into these races this year, especially because much of it is usually spent in the final days of the election. But the spending is all but guaranteed to shatter records.\par \par \uc2 In May, the National Democratic Redistricting Committee and Planned Parenthood Votes {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2024/05/20/us/politics/democrats-state-supreme-courts.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced}}}\uc2  that they were teaming up this cycle to devote $5m to ads, canvassing and get-out-the-vote efforts in supreme court races in Arizona, Michigan, Montana, North Carolina, Ohio and Texas. Meanwhile, the \uc2 ACLU\uc2  and its Pac, the ACLU Voter Education Fund, has this year spent $5.4m on non-partisan advertising and door-knocking efforts in supreme court races in Michigan, Montana, North Carolina and Ohio. The scale of these investments was unprecedented for both \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  and the \uc2 ACLU\uc2 , according to Douglas Keith, a senior counsel in the Brennan Center for Justice\u8217\'20\'19s Judiciary Program who tracks supreme court races.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Don\u8217\'20\'19t miss important US election coverage. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Get our free app}}}\uc2  and sign up for election alerts\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFor a long time, judicial campaign ads often were just judges saying that they were fair and independent and had family values, and that was about it. Now, you\u8217\'20\'19re seeing judges talk about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights or voting rights or environmental rights in their campaign ads,\u8221\'20\'1D Keith said.\par \par \uc2 By contrast, rightwing judicial candidates are largely avoiding talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Keith said, as the issue has become ballot box poison for Republicans in the years since Roe fell. Still, the Judicial Fairness Initiative, the court-focused arm of the Republican State Leadership Committee, announced in August that it would make a \u8220\'20\'1Cseven-figure investment\u8221\'20\'1D in judicial races in Arizona, Michigan, Montana, North Carolina, Ohio and Texas.\par \par \uc2 Balancing the federal bench\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is far from the only issue over which state courts hold enormous sway. They also play a key role in redistricting, LGBTQ+ rights, voting rights and more. And with the \uc2 US Congress\uc2  so gridlocked, state-level legislation and its legality has only grown in importance.\par \par \uc2 This is the most direct way in which Texas voters can have their voices heard on this issue\par \par \uc2 Gina Ortiz Jones\uc2 , leader of Find Out Pac on state supreme court seats\par \par \uc2 For years, conservative operatives have focused on remaking the federal judiciary in their ideological image \u8211\'20\'13 an effort that culminated in \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s appointments of three \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  justices and has made federal courts generally more hostile to progressive causes. Now, the \uc2 ACLU\uc2  hopes to make state supreme courts into what Deirdre Schifeling, its chief political and advocacy officer, calls a \u8220\'20\'1Ccounterbalance\u8221\'20\'1D to this federal bench.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have a plan through 2030 to work to build a more representative court,\u8221\'20\'1D said Schifeling, who has a spreadsheet of the supreme court races that will take place across eight states for years to come. (As a non-partisan organization, the \uc2 ACLU\uc2  focuses on voter education and candidates\u8217\'20\'19 \u8220\'20\'1Ccivil rights and civil liberties\u8221\'20\'1D records.) This cycle, the organization\u8217\'20\'19s messaging has centered on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNationally, you\u8217\'20\'19re seeing polling that shows the top thing that voters are voting on is the economy. But these judges don\u8217\'20\'19t really influence the economy,\u8221\'20\'1D Schifeling said. \u8220\'20\'1COf the issues that they can actually influence and have power over, \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  is by far the most important to voters.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters are testing out this strategy even in some of the United States\u8217\'20\'19 most anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  states. In Texas, where {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.propublica.org/article/nevaeh-crain-death-texas-abortion-ban-emtala"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ProPublica}}}\uc2  this week reported two women {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/oct/30/texas-woman-death-abortion-ban-miscarriage"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 died}}}\uc2  after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/nov/01/teen-dies-abortion-ban-texas-neveah-crain"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 being denied}}}\uc2  emergency care due to the state\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, former \uc2 US air force\uc2  undersecretary \uc2 Gina Ortiz Jones\uc2  has launched the Find Out Pac, which aims to unseat three state supreme court justices.\par \par \uc2 Justices Jane Bland, Jimmy Blacklock and John Devine, the Pac has declared, \u8220\'20\'1Cfucked around with our reproductive freedom\u8221\'20\'1D in cases upholding Texas\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions. Now, Jones wants them out.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhy would we not try to hold some folks accountable?\u8221\'20\'1D Jones said. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is the most direct way in which Texas voters can have their voices heard on this issue.\u8221\'20\'1D (There is no way for citizens to initiate a ballot measure in Texas.) The Pac has been running digital ads statewide on how the Texas ban has imperiled access to medically necessary care.\par \par \uc2 However, since state supreme court races have long languished in relative obscurity, voters don\u8217\'20\'19t always know much about them and may very well default to voting on party lines in the seven states where the ballots list the affiliations of nominees for the bench. Although the majority of Texans believe abortions should be legal in all or some cases, nearly half of Texans don\u8217\'20\'19t recall seeing or hearing anything about their supreme court in the last year, according to Find Out Pac\u8217\'20\'19s own polling.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis conversation that we\u8217\'20\'19re having in Texas, around the importance of judicial races, is new for us as Democrats,\u8221\'20\'1D Jones said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not for the Republicans.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241102ekb2001e3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
