\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Vaginas in Times Square: birth control has been rebranded, but are women the ones benefiting?\b0\par\par\uc2 Emily Shugerman \par \uc2 1,808 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 October 2024\par 18:46 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 A host of slickly marketed women\u8217\'20\'19s health companies are changing the way life-altering medication is accessed \u8211\'20\'13 and commodifying it at the same time\par \par \uc2 Karley Sciortino, a sex influencer, former Vice columnist and the founder of the blog {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://slutever.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Slutever}}}\uc2 , is known for her unfiltered writing on kink and dating (a sample advice column is titled {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://slutever.com/should-i-have-a-threesome/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CShould I Be Double-Teamed by a Couple Twice My Age?\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  ). On \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , she occasionally posts sponsored content promoting personal lubricants and vibrators: short, confessional clips shot against her bright pink bedspread.\par \par \uc2 Last month, a company called Wisp approached her about promoting its emergency contraception product, which can be ordered online and delivered straight to patients\u8217\'20\'19 doors. The ad she wound up producing for it is almost indistinguishable from her vibrator content, featuring Sciortino sprawled on her bed in hot-pink lipstick, recalling her visits to judgmental doctors to get Plan B as a teen. \u8220\'20\'1CSometimes they\u8217\'20\'19d be like: \u8216\'20\'18Why do you need it?\u8217\'20\'19 And it\u8217\'20\'19s like: \u8216\'20\'18Take a wild guess, bro,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D she jokes.\par \par \uc2 The video is in keeping with Wisp\u8217\'20\'19s other marketing: pastel-pink \uc2 Instagram\uc2  posts, pop-art posters bearing images of blurred-out, cherry-red vulvas. On its website, birth control pills and emergency contraception are arranged appealingly on colorful blocks, a perfectly manicured hand holding a NuvaRing descending from the sky. The overall effect is \u8220\'20\'1Ca little bit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/books/2023/sep/19/glossier-beauty-brand-book-emily-weiss"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Glossier}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D, Sciortino said with a laugh.\par \par \uc2 Since the fall of Roe v Wade almost two-and-a-half years ago, direct-to-consumer women\u8217\'20\'19s health companies like Wisp have flourished, offering birth control and emergency contraceptives on demand with the same glossy branding found in a Sephora aisle. The companies claim they remove much of the inconvenience, shame and stigma that often accompany a trip to the doctor or pharmacy, and provide alternate methods of access to contraception at a time when clinics are shuttering and legislators threaten to make providing even the most basic of women\u8217\'20\'19s healthcare a crime.\par \par \uc2 But they also turn what is often a life-altering medication into a trendy consumer product; commodifying its use and spinning the social and political conversation around it into a marketing strategy. \u8220\'20\'1CThese companies have a financial interest in patients they serve utilizing the medications they promote,\u8221\'20\'1D said Suzanne Bollmeier, a professor of pharmacy practice at the University of Health Sciences and Pharmacy in St Louis. \u8220\'20\'1CThese are unchartered waters.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Patients have been able to access contraceptives via telehealth for nearly a decade; \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  started offering virtual care as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2016/06/20/health/birth-control-options-websites.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 early as 2016}}}\uc2 . But after the fall of Roe v Wade in June 2022, as lawmakers began restricting access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and threatened to do the same for contraceptives, online providers said demand surged. (The telehealth service Nurx said it saw a tenfold increase in requests for emergency contraception in the months after Roe fell, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2022/07/06/health/contraceptives-demand-after-roe/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to CNN}}}\uc2 .) There was a corresponding rush of interest from venture capital investors: \u8220\'20\'1CThat crisis put everyone on alert,\u8221\'20\'1D said Erika Seth Davies, the CEO of Rhia Ventures, a company focused on funding \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThere was actual attention on \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  needs because \u8230\'20\'26 all of a sudden it was under threat.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Brands like Wisp, Nurx, Hey Jane and other playfully named competitors rolled out new services and bold marketing campaigns. The online pregnancy test retailer Stix started selling emergency contraceptives the same week Roe fell, later rebranding as Winx, a one-stop sexual health shop. Julie Schott, the marketing whiz behind Starface acne stickers, co-founded her own eponymous line of emergency contraceptives in September 2022, with the explicit purpose of reducing stigma around the product for younger generations. And Hey Jane, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill delivery service inspired in part by the success of direct-to-consumer erectile dysfunction drug providers like Hims, expanded to providing birth control and Plan B just this year.\par \par \uc2 Let\u8217\'20\'19s be honest, if men got pregnant, we would have \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills at \uc2 Starbucks\par \par \uc2 Amy Hagstrom Miller\uc2 , founder of Whole Woman\u8217\'20\'19s Health\par \par \uc2 These brands promise discreet, efficient care, ordered online \u8211\'20\'13 and in many cases, delivered straight to your doorstep. Some, like Julie, offer only over-the-counter medications like Plan B, while others, like Wisp, Winx and Nurx, offer online consultations with physicians, either on staff or outsourced from other companies. All avoid the kind of awkward interactions with physicians and pharmacists that Sciortino described, or the hassle of getting to a doctor\u8217\'20\'19s office in an ever-shrinking landscape of women\u8217\'20\'19s health clinics. \u8220\'20\'1CI didn\u8217\'20\'19t realize that now you can just have the morning-after pill in your cabinet and you don\u8217\'20\'19t have to talk to anybody,\u8221\'20\'1D Sciortino said of her interaction with Wisp. \u8220\'20\'1CI really do believe that is life-changing.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Though delivered discreetly, the products are marketed boldly, with powder-pink packaging meant to be displayed and PR campaigns designed to start conversations. Julie, Schott\u8217\'20\'19s emergency contraceptive brand, handed out free products at an Olivia Rodrigo concert this spring (although Rodrigo stopped the practice at later shows following a rightwing backlash). They also recently debuted a small, hot-pink handbag meant for carrying the product in public (\u8220\'20\'1CSaturday morning nightstand check\u8221\'20\'1D, read a recent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.instagram.com/p/DBUG-RfSpFU/?hl=en&img_index=1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Instagram post}}}\uc2 , showing the bag on a bedside table). Wisp, meanwhile, became the first company ever to use the word \u8220\'20\'1Cvagina\u8221\'20\'1D on a billboard in Times Square last year. The audacious campaigns are part mission statement, part marketing strategy \u8211\'20\'13 a means of inserting themselves into the growing national conversation about \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to Wisp\u8217\'20\'19s vice-president of marketing, Jennifer Dwork. \u8220\'20\'1CWe really just try to be as bold as we can be, because we only have a couple of seconds to grab people\u8217\'20\'19s eyes and ears,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Other brands have leaned into the politics around their products, using the rollback of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  as fodder for TikTok and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  content. Winx hosted an activation at a \u8220\'20\'1CHotties for Harris\u8221\'20\'1D event at the Democratic national convention and recently released a line of sweatshirts reading \u8220\'20\'1Cvote with ur v@g\u8221\'20\'1D. (Its homepage currently features links to both buy the sweatshirt and check your voter registration.) The company\u8217\'20\'19s founders, Cynthia Plotch and Jamie Norwood, said they felt called to this kind of activism: \u8220\'20\'1CWe can\u8217\'20\'19t morally and ethically run this business and advocate for what we advocate for without being involved in this conversation,\u8221\'20\'1D Plotch said. It\u8217\'20\'19s also what a new generation of consumers demands: the brand\u8217\'20\'19s social impressions increased more than 300% in the week after the Democratic convention, Norwood said.\par \par \uc2 The result has been a sea change not only in the way these products are delivered, but in how they\u8217\'20\'19re viewed: not as shameful, or a secret, but something to be shopped and shown off. \u8220\'20\'1CThe way women are delivered healthcare at the clinical level and the consumer level, it\u8217\'20\'19s embroiled with shame,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kaitlyn Barclay, a branding and communications expert who works with companies including Hey Jane. These brands are \u8220\'20\'1Cusing really savvy marketing tools to destigmatize healthcare that everyone should have access to\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 But this shift also means that the first interaction many young women may have when accessing healthcare won\u8217\'20\'19t be with a doctor, but a brand.\par \par \uc2 In some ways, this can be helpful: for relatively low-risk products, like UTI medication or even \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, and especially for over-the-counter medication, like Plan B, there\u8217\'20\'19s little risk to patients self-selecting their treatment. (As Sciortino points out, most women don\u8217\'20\'19t need a doctor to tell them when they have a yeast infection.) With clinics overrun and healthcare costs surging, Anna Wexler, a professor of medical ethics and health policy at the \uc2 University of Pennsylvania\uc2 , said there was an argument to be made that contraception should be easier to access. But this model also risks breaking down the physician-patient relationship and encouraging \u8220\'20\'1Cmedication shopping\u8221\'20\'1D rather than a \u8220\'20\'1Cholistic look at the patient\u8217\'20\'19s overall health needs\u8221\'20\'1D, Wexler said.\par \par \uc2 Other researchers have pointed out similar concerns, noting that telehealth companies don\u8217\'20\'19t provide the services offered by OB-GYNs in person, like pap smears, breast exams and even blood-pressure checks. Ordering birth control online, said the Rand researcher Lori Uscher-Pines, \u8220\'20\'1Ccould be a missed opportunity to get much-needed preventative care\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Breaking down the doctor-patient relationship also means less of a barrier between patients and the companies trying to sell them a cure. \uc2 The Food and Drug Administration\uc2  (\uc2 FDA\uc2 ) rules that regulate how pharmaceutical manufacturers advertise their products do not apply to the doctors and telehealth companies that sell them, noted Bollmeier. Neither are required to name an \uc2 FDA\uc2 -approved use of the drug or list its side effects, or balance a drug\u8217\'20\'19s efficacy and its risks. A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/telehealth-cerebral-done-ads-mental-health-adhd-11672161087"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Wall Street Journal investigation}}}\uc2  into ads for online mental health companies found many of them used paid actors posing as patients and promoted uses other than those approved by the \uc2 FDA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Amy Hagstrom Miller\uc2 , the founder of Whole Woman\u8217\'20\'19s Health, a national chain of women\u8217\'20\'19s health clinics, said she wasn\u8217\'20\'19t concerned about these drugs being prescribed online. \u8220\'20\'1CLet\u8217\'20\'19s be honest \u8211\'20\'13 if men got pregnant, we would have \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D she said, noting that the two-pill regime is proven to be safer than Tylenol. For even lower-risk drugs like emergency contraception and birth control, she added: \u8220\'20\'1CThe reason they\u8217\'20\'19re being withheld is rooted in stigma, it\u8217\'20\'19s rooted in shame.\u8221\'20\'1D Her clinics have been providing both in-person and telehealth services since 2009, she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThere are some people who have been on a prescription, have had all the screenings, who know what they need, and others who need a doctor\u8217\'20\'19s visit,\u8221\'20\'1D she explained. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m always a both-and kind of person.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Indeed, the founders of many telehealth startups see their services as a complement to the work of brick-and-mortar clinics, not a competitor. Plotch, the Winx co-founder, noted more than a third of counties in the US are currently without an OB-GYN, a number that is only rising as clinicians flee states with restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, fearing they will be arrested for simply doing their jobs. Brands like Winx are simply filling the gap that this dwindling number of in-person providers creates, she argued, adding: \u8220\'20\'1CWe can\u8217\'20\'19t pick one thing and say clinics are the only way to do it, because that\u8217\'20\'19s not feasible.\u8221\'20\'1D Davies, the Rhia Ventures CEO, agreed, arguing that it \u8220\'20\'1Cshouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have to be this false choice of this binary\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe need all of it,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd we\u8217\'20\'19re still half the population, so we should get all of it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241030ekau001xi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
