\par\par \uc2 Australia news\par \b \uc2 Stan Grant takes shots at \u8216\'20\'18political elites\u8217\'20\'19 in US \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Cait Kelly and Rafqa Touma \par \uc2 12,333 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 October 2024\par 06:58 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 This blog is now closedBoy, 11, dies and four other children injured after car crashes into Melbourne schoolAustralia\u8217\'20\'19s pandemic plans were \u8216\'20\'18grossly inadequate\u8217\'20\'19, an inquiry has found. What lessons can be learned?Get our breaking news email, free app or daily news podcast\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.25am GMT\par \par \uc2 What we learned today, Tuesday 29 October\par \par \uc2 And with that, we are going to put the blog to bed. Thank you for spending part of your day with us. Before we go let\u8217\'20\'19s recap the big headlines:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Anthony Albanese announces two more clean energy-related projects in New South Wales and Victoria aimed at transitioning Australia\u8217\'20\'19s energy production and workforce {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/oct/29/clean-energy-renewables-solar-thermal-house-prices-property-market-victoria-nsw-e-scooters-qld-election-cost-of-living?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-671f67a78f080a42be7581f0#block-671f67a78f080a42be7581f0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 away from fossil fuels.}}}\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The pace of property price growth is slowing, with Domain recording a 0.8% growth in the quarter and slightly more for units. Experts say homebuyer budgets are finding a ceiling.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The Victorian Greens are called on the state government to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/oct/29/clean-energy-renewables-solar-thermal-house-prices-property-market-victoria-nsw-e-scooters-qld-election-cost-of-living?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-671f680c8f080a42be7581f2#block-671f680c8f080a42be7581f2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 enter the property development game}}}\uc2 , saying Labor\u8217\'20\'19s plan to get more millennials into the housing market will not work without such an intervention.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Robbie Katter, the Katter\u8217\'20\'19s Australia party leader, says babies deserve representation in parliament. He wants to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/oct/29/clean-energy-renewables-solar-thermal-house-prices-property-market-victoria-nsw-e-scooters-qld-election-cost-of-living?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-671fff208f086ba8381d4e0e#block-671fff208f086ba8381d4e0e"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wind back \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights.}}}\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The foreign affairs minister, \uc2 Penny Wong\uc2 , criticised new Israeli laws restricting the \uc2 UN\uc2  relief agency \uc2 Unrwa\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s access to Gaza, saying that \uc2 Unrwa{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/oct/29/clean-energy-renewables-solar-thermal-house-prices-property-market-victoria-nsw-e-scooters-qld-election-cost-of-living?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-672016b88f08eb7baa5ebb32#block-672016b88f08eb7baa5ebb32"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cdoes life-saving work\u8221\'20\'1D.}}}\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The prime minister, Anthony Albanese, said he hasn\u8217\'20\'19t had to declare flights taken on the private jets of billionaires, answering questions on reports he {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/28/albaneses-reported-requests-for-qantas-upgrades-a-bit-strange-dutton-says-while-defending-own-upgrades"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sought free Qantas flight upgrades directly from Alan Joyce while serving as transport minister}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Tenants Advice and Advocacy Services in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/new-south-wales"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New South Wales}}}\uc2  are set to receive $4m in funding over four years from \uc2 NSW Fair Trading\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Journalist and academic Stan Grant took shots at \u8220\'20\'1Cpolitical elites\u8221\'20\'1D in the United States, saying neither \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  were the answer to the \u8220\'20\'1Cexistential crisis of America\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Australians lost trust in governments as the Covid pandemic went on, particularly due to poor communication of the reasons for lockdowns and other restrictions, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/29/covid-inquiry-albanese-government-report-findings"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an inquiry found.}}}\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Mark Butler\uc2  announced $251m to establish a \uc2 Centre for Disease Control\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 And a group of children were injured after a car crashed through a fence at Auburn South primary school this afternoon and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/oct/29/clean-energy-renewables-solar-thermal-house-prices-property-market-victoria-nsw-e-scooters-qld-election-cost-of-living?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-6720689f8f08c0c03443b6c5#block-6720689f8f08c0c03443b6c5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 collided into a bench.}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.58am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.17am GMT\par \par \uc2 BoM confirms prospects for a La Ni\u241\'00\'F1a event continue to subside\par \par \uc2 As we flagged way back at the start of today\u8217\'20\'19s blog, the Bureau of Meteorology \u8217\'20\'19s main model only gave a 4% chance of a La Ni\u241\'00\'F1a forming in December.\par \par \uc2 The bureau this afternoon updated its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.bom.gov.au/climate/enso/#tabs=Overview"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fortnightly climate drivers}}}\uc2  report to provide a bit more detail. Conditions in the Pacific Ocean remain in neutral and look like remaining so, even though the BoM is maintaining its \u8220\'20\'1CLa Ni\u241\'00\'F1a watch\u8221\'20\'1D rating.\par \par \uc2 The bureau noted only one of the international models it compares its ACCESS model to is forecasting its La Ni\u241\'00\'F1a threshold is likely to be breached throughout November to February.\par \par \uc2 enltrAs expected, the chance of a La Nina this year continues to ebb, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BOM_au?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @bom_au}}}\uc2 . The bureau's model is no longer the outlier, too, with Europe's ECMWF model giving a La Nina event even less of a chance of forming over the coming summer. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/D9wctt7Nee"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/D9wctt7Nee}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 @phannam@mastodon.green (@p_hannam) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/p_hannam/status/1851143428308193725?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 29, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 As for the Indian Ocean, the negative phase of that basin\u8217\'20\'19s dipole (which compares sea-surface temperatures between the west and the east) looks likely to be exceeded next month. A negative-IOD, as it is known, typically contributes to wetter than usual conditions for much of the country.\par \par \uc2 But BoM notes the background warming from global heating means past experiences may not be such a good guide to what\u8217\'20\'19s happening now.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CGlobal [sea surface temperatures] remain at near record levels, with temperatures since July being just short of the record temperatures observed during 2023, yet above all other years since observations began in 1854,\u8221\'20\'1D BoM said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe sustained nature of this significant global ocean heat suggests that climate indicators such as [El Ni\u241\'00\'F1o southern oscillation in the Pacific] and IOD may not behave or evolve as they have in the past.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.23am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.02am GMT\par \par \uc2 More renewables connecting to the grid as project pipeline grows, \uc2 Aemo\uc2  says\par \par \uc2 In recent years we\u8217\'20\'19ve heard a lot about the need for a faster pace of deployment of large-scale renewable energy to shore up the grid as aging coal-fired power stations drop out.\par \par \uc2 Well, there\u8217\'20\'19s some light (so to speak) at the end of the tunnel, with a jump of registrations in wind and solar farms and big batteries during the September quarter. The tally of 3.5 gigawatts of capacity was more than the 2.4GW of registrations for all of 2023, the \uc2 Australian Energy Market Operator\uc2  says.\par \par \uc2 Aemo\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://aemo.com.au/en/energy-systems/electricity/national-electricity-market-nem/participate-in-the-market/network-connections/connections-scorecard"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 connections scorecard}}}\uc2  said the past quarter\u8217\'20\'19s tally included three windfarms (1.8GW), three battery projects (1.25GW) and four (relatively small) solar farms (0.4GW).\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese projects are built, connected to the grid and can now move to the final commissioning phase,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Aemo\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s executive general manager, Merryn York, said.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps more usefully, seven projects with a capacity of 1.3GW (or about the total of the Liddell coal-fired power station when it shut in April 2023) reached full generation during the September quarter, an increase of about two-thirds on the same quarter last year. (This collection included four windfarms, a hybrid battery and solar farm, and a gas-fired generator.)\par \par \uc2 Early-stage application approvals also rose 160% to 2.6GW in the quarter from a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 Aemo\uc2  said 45.6GW of generation and storage capacity was working through various stages of the connections process as of the end of September, or equal to 70% of the total generation capacity in the national electricity market. That result was 36% more than a year ago.\par \par \uc2 Wonder if we\u8217\'20\'19ll still have room for nuclear power plants from the mid-2030s (or later)?\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.08am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.23am GMT\par \par \uc2 One child in critical condition and four others also in hospital after Hawthorn East collision\par \par \uc2 Victoria police have provided an update for the Hawthorn East incident, in which a number of children have been injured after a car crashed into a school fence.\par \par \uc2 Victoria police said:\par \par \uc2 Emergency services attended a collision in Hawthorn East this afternoon. It\u8217\'20\'19s believed a vehicle was traveling along Burgess Street when it left the road and crashed through a school fence just after 2.30pm.\par \par \uc2 One child has been taken to hospital in a critical condition and four others have been transported to hospital with non-life-threatening injuries. The female driver of the vehicle has been arrested and will be interviewed.\par \par \uc2 The exact cause of the incident is being investigated. Major collision investigation unit detectives are enroute to the scene. Anyone with information is urged to contact Crime Stoppers on 1800 333 000.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.09am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.00am GMT\par \par \uc2 The Labor-turned-independent senator, Fatima Payman, has accused her former party of giving \u8220\'20\'1Ccomplete impunity\u8221\'20\'1D to Israel after it voted {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/oct/28/israeli-lawmakers-pass-bill-that-could-halt-unwra-relief-work-in-gaza"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in favour of a ban against the UN\u8217\'20\'19s Palestinian aid body}}}\uc2  overnight.\par \par \uc2 On social media, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.instagram.com/p/DBsS2aOP_rR/?igsh=dm5heTNubnBqaWVy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Payman said}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 Waking up to this news and wondering why everyone is shocked?\par \par \uc2 This is what happens when this Labor government gives complete impunity to its so-called friend, Israel and in return, they incrementally increase their human rights violations and continue breaching international humanitarian laws.\par \par \uc2 This is another low for Israel to cut off humanitarian aid provided by \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  to Palestinian refugees!\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.46am GMT\par \par \uc2 Children injured after collision at Melbourne school\par \par \uc2 Victoria\u8217\'20\'19s education department has confirmed a collision has occurred at a school in Melbourne\u8217\'20\'19s inner east.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson has told Guardian Australia a car crashed through a fence at Auburn South primary school this afternoon and collided into a bench, injuring a number of students.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.14am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.37am GMT\par \par \uc2 Paramedics at Hawthorn East collision\par \par \uc2 Victoria police and paramedics have rushed to a car crash involving multiple \u8220\'20\'1Cpatients\u8221\'20\'1D in the Melbourne suburb of Hawthorn East.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for \uc2 Ambulance Victoria\uc2  says paramedics are responding to a collision:\par \par \uc2 Our priority right now is our patients and we do not have confirmed information from the scene at this early stage.\par \par \uc2 Victoria police have confirmed emergency services are at the scene. More to come.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.47am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.29am GMT\par \par \uc2 Dutton says PM should refer himself to integrity commission over \uc2 Qantas\uc2  flights\par \par \uc2 Dutton says the PM should refer himself to the integrity commission over the flight upgrades.\par \par \uc2 It is clear he has breached the standards of the ministerial code of conduct in the Gillard code of conduct when he was transport minister.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.32am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.27am GMT\par \par \uc2 Dutton has attacked the PM on the \uc2 Qantas\uc2  upgrades and the lack of competitive air fares in Australia.\par \par \uc2 He has been asked about his own access to a private jet and relationship with a billionaire \uc2 Gina Rinehart\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I have been very transparent, declared all of that, which is why [Albanese] knows about it and I was not the transport ministe, picking up the phone to \uc2 Alan Joyce\uc2 , the CEO of Australia\u8217\'20\'19s biggest airline, to ask for free up-rates for me and my family and then refusing to detail it in an honest way.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.33am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.25am GMT\par \par \uc2 Dutton says PM is focused only on \u8216\'20\'18what he can get from the system\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2  is talking in Queensland. He has accused the PM of being \u8220\'20\'1Cfocused on himself\u8221\'20\'1D:\par \par \uc2 This government talks a lot and Anthony Albanese promised a lot before the election but doesn\u8217\'20\'19t do anything except for himself and, unfortunately, Australians are missing out. And the prime minister is focused only on himself and what he can get from the system.\par \par \uc2 He says the treasurer has handed down \u8220\'20\'1Cinflationary\u8221\'20\'1D budgets:\par \par \uc2 When we were in government during the Covid period, \uc2 Jim Chalmers\uc2  and Anthony Albanese proposed spending more money, so if their argument was inflation was a problem, their argument at the time was to fuel it further, and this is something that Australians understand.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.35am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.06am GMT\par \par \uc2 Thanks for tuning into the blog today. Handing over now to Cait Kelly, who will roll your live news updates this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.08am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.04am GMT\par \par \uc2 The independent ACT senator, David Pocock, has urged the Albanese government to place targeted sanctions on officials within the Israeli government and its armed forces after it banned the \uc2 UN\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Palestinian aid body from entering Gaza, the West Bank and Israel.\par \par \uc2 Overnight, Israel\u8217\'20\'19s parliament {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/oct/28/israeli-lawmakers-pass-bill-that-could-halt-unwra-relief-work-in-gaza"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voted to ban the UN relief and works agency}}}\uc2  (\uc2 UNRWA\uc2 ) from its territory within 90 days.\par \par \uc2 The aid body provides food, water and medicine for the more than 1.9 million displaced Palestinians, who face severe shortages in the Gaza strip.\par \par \uc2 Pocock said he held concerns about the potential \u8220\'20\'1Chumanitarian apocalypse\u8221\'20\'1D in Gaza and called for targeted sanctions against Israeli government and defence force officials \u8220\'20\'1Cresponsible for war crimes against the people of Gaza and the West Bank\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is one of the only levers available to us,\u8221\'20\'1D Pocock said.\par \par \uc2 In July, Australia {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/article/2024/jul/25/australia-sanctions-israeli-youth-group-settler-violence-west-bank"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 imposed financial sanctions and travel bans}}}\uc2  on seven Israelis and a youth group for their involvement in violent attacks on Palestinians in the West Bank.\par \par \uc2 The move came after the \uc2 UN\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s international court of justice {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/article/2024/jul/19/israels-settlement-policies-break-international-law-court-finds"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ordered Israel to end its occupation}}}\uc2  of the Palestinian territories \u8220\'20\'1Cas rapidly as possible\u8221\'20\'1D and make full reparations for its \u8220\'20\'1Cinternationally wrongful acts\u8221\'20\'1D in an advisory opinion that declares the occupation violates international law.\par \par \uc2 In a press conference earlier, Pocock said the federal government\u8217\'20\'19s failure to do more amounted to a \u8220\'20\'1Creal failure of moral courage\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 At some point, countries like Australia, [must] say \u8216\'20\'18that\u8217\'20\'19s not good enough\u8217\'20\'19, and we can actually make a small stand as a middle power.\par \par \uc2 Read more about the Knesset\u8217\'20\'19s decision here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/oct/28/israeli-lawmakers-pass-bill-that-could-halt-unwra-relief-work-in-gaza"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Israeli parliament votes to ban Unrwa from Israel within 90 days}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.58am GMT\par \par \uc2 Mark Butler\uc2  says trust difficult to restore after Covid pandemic\par \par \uc2 The health minister, \uc2 Mark Butler\uc2 , says it \u8220\'20\'1Cis going to be a long hard road to get trust to the level it was before the pandemic\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He points to mis- and disinformation in health as something that is \u8220\'20\'1Cnot going to be easy to battle\u8221\'20\'1D:\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s easily lost, it\u8217\'20\'19s hard to build it back up, I think is the insight. The establishment of an independent authoritative body that is seen as distant from government, that is able to provide fearless advice to the community and to governments, which I think will have a reporting function to parliament, that will be transparent, I think is a critical central ingredient in rebuilding that trust.\par \par \uc2 But I \u8211\'20\'13 I think \u8211\'20\'13 as all of you know, you know, the pandemic did fuel a lot of misinformation and disinformation which \u8211\'20\'13 which particularly was fertile in health, and that is not going to be easy to battle, frankly. So we need to build the structures, we need to give the community confidence that, in the future, decisions will be taken at an appropriate time on the basis of real data, Australian data, not overseas data, which the report makes clear we had to use because we had no data of our own, even though UK and US data was telling a very different picture to a population like Australia\u8217\'20\'19s that had different vaccination status. We use our own data and we use evidence-based approaches that \u8230\'20\'26 balance risks and benefits, takes the proportionate approach and thinks about non-health impacts as well as the direct health impacts.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.02am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.50am GMT\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Billions and billions of dollars were wasted\u8217\'20\'19 during Covid crisis, \uc2 Jim Chalmers\uc2  says\par \par \uc2 The treasurer, \uc2 Jim Chalmers\uc2 , also addressed press on the Covid inquiry report.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBig decisions were taken and big mistakes were made,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThose mistakes were costly and they were inflationary.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe consequences of the economic policies developed through the pandemic also continue to be felt right across our economy even today.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Chalmers quotes the report in saying \u8220\'20\'1Cthere was excessive fiscal and monetary policy stimulus provided throughout 2021 and 2022, especially in the construction sector, combined with supply-side disruptions this contributed to inflationary pressures coming out of the pandemic\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He continued:\par \par \uc2 The modelling cited in the report says that the extended policy support which didn\u8217\'20\'19t always move in line with the health advice meant that peak inflation was at least two percentage points higher than it could have been.\par \par \uc2 On the design of the economic response, we saw some very good ideas badly implemented and poorly targeted. Labor called for and supported programs like jobkeeper but we were very, very clear at the time that the support should have been rolled out faster and that it should have been better targeted because, if it was better targeted, we could have provided more assistance to those who genuinely needed it by wasting less on the businesses in particular who didn\u8217\'20\'19t need jobkeeper and we know subsequently that billions and billions of dollars were wasted.\par \par \uc2 On the home builders\u8217\'20\'19 program, the report makes it clear that overheated the industry and contributed to inflation in the post-pandemic era with many Australians experiencing the consequences of this now through higher inflation and through lack of access to housing.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s also particularly critical of the early release of superannuation, which was not an appropriate policy response and shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be deployed again. They see the existing hardship provisions as sufficient for these kinds of events.\par \par \uc2 It also concludes that the slow vaccine rollout had economic consequences as well. It delayed the reopening of our economy by months at great cost to the economy.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.55am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.42am GMT\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s more on the newly announced \uc2 Centre for Disease Control\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The health minister, \uc2 Mark Butler\uc2 , told the media a short while ago:\par \par \uc2 The first priority of the CDC will be to establish a comprehensive joined-up data and surveillance system in partnership with states and territories, but to have a single comprehensive real-time data and surveillance system and for our surveillance capability to be world-leading including our use of wastewater surveillance, which we know has been so important. The CDC will be also responsible for providing independent evidence-based advice about particularly a pandemic response, but more generally our responses to communicable diseases. It will also be responsible for engagement with regional and international partners.\par \par \uc2 It will also obviously be responsible for l eading pandemic planning and testing or stress-testing of our ability to respond to a pandemic in partnership with relevant departments, including my own, the Department of Health, and the National Emergency Management Authority.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.44am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.38am GMT\par \par \uc2 Federal government announces $251m funding for \uc2 Centre for Disease Control\par \par \uc2 Mark Butler\uc2  has announced $251m to establish a \uc2 Centre for Disease Control\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He told the media:\par \par \uc2 The government is investing $251m over the forward estimates as well as funding ongoing to establish an independent authoritative \uc2 Centre for Disease Control\uc2  to commence from 1 January 2026.\par \par \uc2 Legislation will be introduced into the parliament next year to set out its independence and functions, he said. It will be based out of Canberra, and its functions will \u8220\'20\'1Cclosely reflect\u8221\'20\'1D recommendations from the Covid inquiry report.\par \par \uc2 Butler talked through the report\u8217\'20\'19s recommendations a short while ago:\par \par \uc2 There are 26 recommended actions for the national government, 19 of them are recommended to be prioritised over the next 12 to 18 months. Seven in the longer-term. Cabinet considered this report yesterday and appointed PM&C to lead a cross-government taskforce to work through those recommendations and come back to the cabinet. I think, without doubt, though, the most important recommendation from this report is the establishment of a centre for disease control \u8211\'20\'13 a CDC. As you know, Australia at the beginning of this pandemic was pretty much the only \uc2 OECD\uc2  nation without a central authoritative centre for disease control and it was an election promise from Anthony Albanese, made in one of his budget reply speeches to close that gap and to establish a CDC here in Australia.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.47am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.33am GMT\par \par \uc2 Australia now \u8216\'20\'18arguably worse-placed\u8217\'20\'19 to deal with a pandemic than in 2020\par \par \uc2 The health minister, \uc2 Mark Butler\uc2 , says the Covid inquiry report concludes that, for a range of reasons, \u8220\'20\'1Cwe are arguably worse-placed as a country to deal with a pandemic than we were in early 2020\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He told the media:\par \par \uc2 There has been really significant scarring on our healthcare systems and the health workforce, a workforce that is exhausted by the demands of a once-in-a-century pandemic that\u8217\'20\'19s gone on for years. The APS has lost \u8211\'20\'13 the \uc2 Australian Public Service\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 has lost key personnel who learnt a lot through that pandemic but have moved on from their positions, often through exhaustion. As I\u8217\'20\'19m sure the treasurer will talk about, governments are in much more significant debt than they were before the pandemic. But crucially, crucially, this report says that a number of the points I have already made about the lack of real-time evidence-based policy and the lack of transparency has driven a large decline in trust \u8230\'20\'26 which the panel members say, and these are their words \u8211\'20\'13 which many of the measures taken during Covid-19 are unlikely to be accepted by the population again.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.38am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.28am GMT\par \par \uc2 Health minister says Covid report criticises pace of vaccine rollout as well as aged care and communication failures\par \par \uc2 Health minister \uc2 Mark Butler\uc2  told press the Covid-19 inquiry report identified the \u8220\'20\'1Cslowness of our vaccine rollout,\u8221\'20\'1D \u8220\'20\'1Cfailures in aged care,\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cstark inequity\u8221\'20\'1D between different community groups \u8220\'20\'1Cparticularly different groups from culturally linguistically diverse backgrounds but also temporary visa holders\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He also said the report \u8220\'20\'1Cpoints to a lack of transparency around the rationale and the evidence behind decisions that were taken by governments that had such a profound impact on the lives of Australians and the freedom of Australians\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.30am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.18am GMT\par \par \uc2 Covid inquiry finds Australia's pandemic plans 'grossly inadequate'\par \par \uc2 The Covid-19 inquiry report found Australia\u8217\'20\'19s pandemic plans were \u8220\'20\'1Cgrossly inadequate for the scale of the challenge that Covid-19 presented to us\u8221\'20\'1D, the health minister, \uc2 Mark Butler\uc2 , has told the media:\par \par \uc2 The first lesson from this report is that like most countries, frankly, our pandemic plans were grossly inadequate for the scale of the challenge that Covid-19 presented to us. The report makes clear, for example, that our plans, such as they were, included no plan that would deal with the closure of the international border, which was such a central part of our response. No plan to deal with quarantine, which was also incredibly important. No plan to deal with the workforce demands of a pandemic that went on for as long as it did. And, as a result, to use the words of the report, our response to the pandemic was not as effective as it could have been.\par \par \uc2 As a result of the lack of plans, leaders, particularly, were placed in the invidious position, to use the words of the report, of building the plane while it was flying. The disease surveillance systems were frankly simply not up to the task. The report talks about the use of paper systems, the use of facsimile machines.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.26am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.07am GMT\par \par \uc2 Mark Butler\uc2  says Covid took lives directly of 20,000 Australians and caused immense \u8216\'20\'18separation, anxiety and grief\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 The health minister, \uc2 Mark Butler\uc2 , is addressing a press conference after the release of the Covid-19 inquiry report:\par \par \uc2 It almost goes without saying that the Covid-19 pandemic for anyone born after the end of World War II was the biggest national and global emergency in our lifetimes. As we know it, it took the lives directly of more than 20,000 Australians, but its indirect impacts on the health system resulted in thousands more losing their lives as well. There was immense dislocation, separation, anxiety, grief and, as the treasurer will point out, enormous economic impacts from the pandemic and our response to it.\par \par \uc2 We have a responsibility, particularly as a government, we have a responsibility to examine our response to the pandemic, to learn what we did well and, in particular, to learn what we could have done better. Importantly, to build as this report describes it, \u8220\'20\'1Ca high-level playbook for the next pandemic\u8221\'20\'1D, because we know there will be a next pandemic.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.22am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.04am GMT\par \par \uc2 In releasing the report of the Covid inquiry, the federal government is also announcing $251m to deliver an Australian Centre for Disease Control.\par \par \uc2 The idea was a Labor election commitment and an \u8220\'20\'1Cinterim\u8221\'20\'1D body has been operating inside the health department since the start of 2024. But the health minister, \uc2 Mark Butler\uc2 , is today pledging $251.7m to deliver the proposal as a standalone institution.\par \par \uc2 The Covid inquiry said a CDC would be a key trusted source of information in future, especially around health emergencies, helping to track statistics and communicate information as a central authority. The government said Australia is the only \uc2 OECD\uc2  country without an equivalent body.\par \par \uc2 The interim body is already operating, and working on biosecurity measures for avian flu. The standalone body is slated to be located in Canberra and launch in January 2026, pending passage of legislation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe establishment of the Australian CDV will ensure we are prepared next time,\u8221\'20\'1D Butler said.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.12am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.03am GMT\par \par \uc2 Australians lost trust in governments during Covid pandemic, inquiry finds\par \par \uc2 The long-awaited Covid inquiry into Australia\u8217\'20\'19s response to the pandemic has been released. A panel of experts found Australians lost trust in governments as the pandemic went on, particularly due to poor communication of the reasons for lockdowns and other restrictions.\par \par \uc2 The report also warned Australians are unlikely to abide by such strict pandemic restrictions in future health emergencies, and stressed that governments needed to work hard to rebuild public trust in institutions \u8211\'20\'13 with concerns that secrecy led to misinformation growing rife on the health response.\par \par \uc2 Many Australians still feel \u8220\'20\'1Cresentment about what they lost\u8221\'20\'1D during the pandemic Covid period and governments need to \u8220\'20\'1Crebuild the social fabric\u8221\'20\'1D, respondents told the inquiry.\par \par \uc2 The report said:\par \par \uc2 Focus group participants said that governments resisted releasing information that may have contradicted the policies they were pursuing. This opinion led to a view that government did not trust the public to understand or interpret information correctly.\par \par \uc2 A lack of transparency around vaccination prioritisation decisions reduced trust in government, particularly among people with disability. The panel heard that a lack of transparency also increased the perception that the government was hiding adverse information. This view fuelled the spread of misinformation and disinformation.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/29/covid-inquiry-albanese-government-report-findings"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Government secrecy during Covid pandemic eroded public trust, inquiry finds}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.12am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.49am GMT\par \par \uc2 Stan Grant says neither Trump or Harris are answer to 'existential crisis of America'\par \par \uc2 Journalist and academic Stan Grant has taken shots at \u8220\'20\'1Cpolitical elites\u8221\'20\'1D in the United States, as well as coverage of the US election, in a speech made at a social cohesion conference in western Sydney.\par \par \uc2 The former host of ABC\u8217\'20\'19s Q+A told attendees that neither \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  were the answer to the \u8220\'20\'1Cexistential crisis of America\u8221\'20\'1D and then made reference to Robin Hood:\par \par \uc2 The American political elites have turned their backs on poor Americans. They are bought and paid for by lobbyists. They pass laws that put more money into rich people\u8217\'20\'19s hands. Healthcare is a scandal. Machines are making people redundant. Big pharma has people hooked on drugs.\par \par \uc2 If we think that Harris or Trump are going to be the answer to the existential crisis of America, then we need to know that the Sheriff of Nottingham has taken up residence in Washington DC, and the good cops have left town. Robin Hood is nowhere to be seen.\par \par \uc2 He said coverage of the election had been \u8220\'20\'1Cabominable\u8221\'20\'1D in that \u8220\'20\'1Cevery lie\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cevery hatred\u8221\'20\'1D has been \u8220\'20\'1Camplified.\u8221\'20\'1D He said the media has only been interested in the outrage and star power of the campaigns, and less so the people \u8220\'20\'1Cholding that broken country together\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 I barely watched much of the news coverage because it has been abominable.\par \par \uc2 Every lie is amplified, every hatred is broadcast. And I\u8217\'20\'19m just not talking about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I\u8217\'20\'19m talking about the entire picture. If we reduce the American election to just an idea of who may be more decent or who has bad manners, but better manners, we\u8217\'20\'19re missing the point.\par \par \uc2 The point is that we are talking about a nation where human beings are expendable and the media doesn\u8217\'20\'19t tell us that story because they\u8217\'20\'19re too interested in the latest entertainment, the latest pop star, the latest movie star, the latest business person, the latest Trump outrage, the latest inane comment from \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , and never speak to the people who were doing the back-breaking, dirty, gut-busting work of holding that broken country together while politicians have absolutely exploited them.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.54am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.36am GMT\par \par \uc2 $4m funding boost to NSW tenants' support services\par \par \uc2 Tenants Advice and Advocacy Services in New South Wales are set to receive $4m in funding over four years from \uc2 NSW Fair Trading\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The service network \u8211\'20\'13 made up of 21 not-for-profits that support tenants in negotiations, dispute resolution and advocacy \u8211\'20\'13 will be boosted by $1m extra a year until 2028, as put in a statement from the minister for fair trading and better regulation, Anoulack Chanthivong. Funding has increased to $16.2m in 2024-25.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Tenants Advice and Advocacy Service provides local support through a network of highly skilled advocates who work to ensure quality advice and advocacy is available to all renters in NSW,\u8221\'20\'1D Chanthivong said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe funding boost will mean the services can reach even more renters and keep this critical information service free.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The chief executive of the Tenants\u8217\'20\'19 Union of NSW, Leo Patterson Ross, welcomes the additional funding, and says it \u8220\'20\'1Cwill make it easier for renters\u8221\'20\'1D to seek free advice and advocacy:\par \par \uc2 This funding increase will ensure services can continue to provide the same high-quality advice we have for the past 30 years.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.49am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.10am GMT\par \par \uc2 Third person charged in alleged Bluey coin theft\par \par \uc2 A third person has been charged today with allegedly stealing tens of thousands of unreleased limited edition Bluey coins from a warehouse in Sydney\u8217\'20\'19s west.\par \par \uc2 Police received a report that a large amount of currency had been stolen from a warehouse in Wetherill Park on Monday 12 July this year, according to a NSW Police statement.\par \par \uc2 63,000 unreleased limited edition $1 Bluey coins \u8211\'20\'13 produced by the Australian Mint \u8211\'20\'13 had been stolen, officers who arrived at the scene were told.\par \par \uc2 An investigation into the incident was commenced by State Crime Command\u8217\'20\'19s Robbery and Serious Crime Squad under Strike Force Bandit. Two men have been charged and remain before the courts.\par \par \uc2 Around 6.55am today, a 27-year-old woman was arrested, after detectives executed a search warrant in Green Valley.\par \par \uc2 She taken to Liverpool Police Station where she was charged with \u8220\'20\'1Cthree counts of aggravated break & enter commit serious indictable offence, and dispose property-theft=serious indictable offence <=$5000\u8221\'20\'1D, as put in the NSW Police Statement.\par \par \uc2 She was refused bail to appear before Liverpool Local Court today. Police will allege in court the woman was the driver in the alleged break and enter.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.56am GMT\par \par \uc2 Class action against Homes Victoria won\u8217\'20\'19t be decided until next year\par \par \uc2 Residents in public housing towers slated for demolition will not learn if they are successful in their fight against the Victorian government until next year, \uc2 AAP\uc2  has reported.\par \par \uc2 The class action claims Homes Victoria did not properly consider the residents\u8217\'20\'19 human rights before deciding to demolish the towers in Carlton, Flemington and North Melbourne.\par \par \uc2 The trial began in the Victorian supreme court on Monday, where the Homes Victoria chief executive, Simon Newport, conceded \uc2 Daniel Andrews\uc2  made the announcement before residents were notified.\par \par \uc2 The trial was due to continue on Tuesday but the case was instead adjourned so the parties could fight over the production of secret documents.\par \par \uc2 Justice Melinda Richards said another judge would need to decide on the document question, with a hearing date yet to be fixed.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.30am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.47am GMT\par \par \uc2 Julia Gillard\uc2  library to be established at \uc2 University of Adelaide\par \par \uc2 Former prime minister \uc2 Julia Gillard\uc2  and the \uc2 University of Adelaide\uc2  have agreed to establish the Julia Gillard Prime Ministerial Library on the University\u8217\'20\'19s North Terrace campus.\par \par \uc2 The uni will work with \uc2 National Archives of Australia\uc2  to bring the trove of documents from Gillard\u8217\'20\'19s career and prime ministership closer to the public.\par \par \uc2 Gillard said:\par \par \uc2 I am deeply honoured that plans are under way to establish the \uc2 Julia Gillard\uc2  Prime Ministerial Library on the grounds of the university where my tertiary education journey began.\par \par \uc2 I have many fond memories from my time at the \uc2 University of Adelaide\uc2 , where I studied law, economics and literature. I have continued my relationship with the University as an honorary professor and through the annual \uc2 Julia Gillard\uc2  lecture.\par \par \uc2 I am excited to see the library take shape in the coming years and become a dynamic, living space for students, staff and the general public.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.53am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.29am GMT\par \par \uc2 Circling back to Albanese\u8217\'20\'19s press conference \u8211\'20\'13 Labor is trying to change the topic (or at least muddy the waters) around the prime minister\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Qantas\uc2  upgrades by pointing out \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s own disclosures of accepting flights related to mining billionaire \uc2 Gina Rinehart\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Albanese referenced Dutton taking flights \u8220\'20\'1Corganised to private parties\u8221\'20\'1D. It seemed to be a reference to a story we broke in January, where the opposition leader was flown to a party \u8211\'20\'13 at Rinehart\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Hancock Prospecting\uc2  mine in the Pilbara \u8211\'20\'13 by another billionaire rich-lister.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/jan/10/peter-dutton-gina-rinehart-pilbara-party-flown-roy-hill-mine-warburton-group"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Peter Dutton was flown by a billionaire to Gina Rinehart\u8217\'20\'19s Pilbara party \u8211\'20\'13 where he had a message for parents}}}\par \par \uc2 Senior Labor minister Murray Watt referenced this story last night, tweeting a screenshot with the caption \u8220\'20\'1Cspeaking of flights\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 enltrSpeaking of flights\u8230\'20\'26 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/ViXDQ4rWP9"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/ViXDQ4rWP9}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Senator Murray Watt (@MurrayWatt) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/MurrayWatt/status/1850783222373941358?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 28, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Watt yesterday also noted Dutton had accepted flights in 2022 from \uc2 Gina Rinehart\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Hancock Prospecting\uc2 , including a Perth-to-Pilbara air fare and flights between Sydney, Rockhampton and Mackay to attend a Bali bombing memorial.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, Albanese defended his actions by saying all the upgrades were disclosed, but he didn\u8217\'20\'19t directly answer several questions about whether he\u8217\'20\'19d asked the then chief executive, \uc2 Alan Joyce\uc2 , for the upgrades himself.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI didn\u8217\'20\'19t have to declare any flights on private jets owned by billionaires like \uc2 Gina Rinehart\uc2 , because I haven\u8217\'20\'19t engaged in it. As simple as that. And it\u8217\'20\'19s up to Mr Dutton to explain how those flights were organised to private parties,\u8221\'20\'1D the PM said at a press conference.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.57am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.22am GMT\par \par \uc2 Stamp duty bill introduced to Victorian parliament\par \par \uc2 Earlier this morning, the Victorian premier, \uc2 Jacinta Allan\uc2 , held a news conference in Docklands, where she announced the approval of three new residential towers in the area.\par \par \uc2 She says two permits for 915 new homes to be built in three towers at Collins Wharf were approved by the planning minister, Sonya Kilkenny, on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The $521m Lendlease development includes two 28-storey buildings with 349 and 375 apartments, and a 16-storey building with 191 apartments.\par \par \uc2 She also announced the government will be introducing the Duties Amendment (More Homes) bill 2024 to parliament to allow for the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/21/victoria-stamp-duty-tax-cuts-apartments-off-plan-jacinta-allan-housing"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 12-month off-the-plan stamp duty concessions she announced}}}\uc2  last week to come into effect.\par \par \uc2 Under the plan, anyone buying off-the-plan apartments, townhouses and units will receive a huge discount on their stamp duty.\par \par \uc2 The bill will be debated in the lower house this week before it goes to the upper house in the following sitting week, where Allan urged both the Liberals and Greens to support it.\par \par \uc2 Allan told reporters:\par \par \uc2 We need to take this action immediately and that\u8217\'20\'19s why, too, I\u8217\'20\'19m calling on the Liberal party, the Greens political party, to not stand in the way of this action that we are taking in the parliament this week. Industry, home builders and homebuyers deserve the certainty from all political parties in the parliament that they will support this legislation to move through the parliament as quickly and effectively as possible, and that\u8217\'20\'19s why we are bringing it to the parliament this week. We don\u8217\'20\'19t want to see any games. We want to see it introduced straight away. Homebuyers are relying on it. So too are home builders.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.34am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.16am GMT\par \par \uc2 Tony Burke says Labor didn\u8217\'20\'19t try to legislate against hate out of fears of ugly debate\par \par \uc2 The minister for immigration, Tony Burke, has said the government introduced envoys to Islamophobia and antisemitism, as opposed to legislating against such hate, to avoid an \u8220\'20\'1Cugly\u8221\'20\'1D political debate.\par \par \uc2 Burke was speaking at a conference on social cohesion in western Sydney earlier and said that while there were state laws to address hate based on faith, there was no federal law that protects communities from such hate.\par \par \uc2 But he said that should the government try to change that, it would result in \u8220\'20\'1Cthe ugliest debate I could imagine\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Instead, the government appointed Jillian Segal as the first antisemitism envoy and Aftab Malik as the first anti-Islamophobia envoy:\par \par \uc2 In the absence of federal law, and certainly at the moment, should we try to change that, the debate in the parliament would be one of the ugliest debates I could imagine, which we aren\u8217\'20\'19t wanting to bring on to people. So the way of being able to make clear statements against discrimination on the basis of people\u8217\'20\'19s faith was to create two positions which did not require a vote of parliament.\par \par \uc2 The reason is very simple, antisemitism has an element of racist bigotry and bigotry on faith, Islamophobia is entirely about faith \u8211\'20\'13 neither of them have the federal protection that is there against racism, against sexism, against other forms of bigotry. So in establishing those envoys, we are establishing a very clear principle that these forms of bigotry and hatred are completely unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 Burke also dismissed claims refugee arrivals from Gaza did not go through the proper security checks, saying such claims only \u8220\'20\'1Cadded kerosene to the fire\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Finally, he also addressed the government\u8217\'20\'19s decision to reject the visa of American rightwing pundit Candace Owens because she would undermine social cohesion. He said it was \u8220\'20\'1Cone of the easiest decisions\u8221\'20\'1D of his life.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.01am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.07am GMT\par \par \uc2 Men think sexual harassment is improving, women don\u8217\'20\'19t\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s a stark difference between male business leaders and female employees in their perceptions about sexual harassment in Australian workplaces, a recent survey of 500 leaders and 1,000 employees has found.\par \par \uc2 More than 70% of male leaders said they had high confidence a colleague felt safe to report sexual harassment. This dropped to only one-in-two women, the survey commissioned by the violence prevention organisation Our Watch found.\par \par \uc2 Almost 80% of male bosses said they had a high degree of confidence appropriate action would be taken by employers if a report was made, compared with about one-in-two female workers.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 4 million women have experienced workplace sexual harassment in the past five years, which is a much higher rate than men and meant women had a more personalised experience with sexual harassment, the Our Watch vhief executive, Patty Kinnersly, said.\par \par \uc2 Workplace leaders thought the insidious problem was improving but that isn\u8217\'20\'19t what the data reflected, she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 Australian Associated Press\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 In Australia, support is available at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.1800respect.org.au/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 1800Respect}}}\uc2  (1800 737 732). In the UK, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://rapecrisis.org.uk/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Rape Crisis}}}\uc2  offers support on 0808 500 2222. In the US, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.rainn.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Rainn}}}\uc2  offers support on 800-656-4673. Other international helplines can be found at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://ibiblio.org/rcip/internl.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ibiblio.org/rcip/internl.html}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.15am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.53am GMT\par \par \uc2 PM asked if he called \uc2 Alan Joyce\uc2  directly about flight upgrades\par \par \uc2 Anthony Albanese has been asked a few times whether he called the \uc2 Qantas\uc2  chief executive, \uc2 Alan Joyce\uc2 , directly about flight upgrades.\par \par \uc2 He said:\par \par \uc2 There is no accusations being made with any specifics at all about any of this. None. None. People are \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2  is making this statement. If there is a suggestion to be made, let it be made specifically and I will answer it, but I have said very clearly that I recall direct discussions with \uc2 Alan Joyce\uc2  over the flights, the Emirates, \uc2 Qantas\uc2  flight to Dubai and of course the first flight to Perth. They are the only times I was on a plane with \uc2 Alan Joyce\uc2 . Of the 22 flights, 10 of them were during the leadership ballot between myself and \uc2 Bill Shorten\uc2  in 2013 over a one-month period where both \uc2 Qantas\uc2  and \uc2 Virgin\uc2  provided upgrades for flights that were paid for by the Australian Labor party to make sure there was not any cost to taxpayers for what was internal business.\par \par \uc2 Albanese reiterated his claim that his financial arrangements are completely transparent:\par \par \uc2 In terms of transparency, \uc2 Bridget McKenzie\uc2  can answer questions herself about things. \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2  can answer questions about his flights. I assume, his flights upgrades, the same circumstances I am in with the exception that I don\u8217\'20\'19t have access to a private jet on call, which has happened with Mr Dutton, and it is up to him to explain that. Just like all of my financial arrangements are completely transparent.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.03am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.44am GMT\par \par \uc2 PM says he \u8216\'20\'18didn\u8217\'20\'19t have to declare any flights taken on private jets owned by billionaires\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 The prime minister, Anthony Albanese, says he hasn\u8217\'20\'19t had to declare flights taken on the private jets of billionaires, answering questions on reports he {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/28/albaneses-reported-requests-for-qantas-upgrades-a-bit-strange-dutton-says-while-defending-own-upgrades"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sought free Qantas flight upgrades directly from Alan Joyce while serving as transport minister}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He spoke to press from Newcastle a short while ago:\par \par \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2  has declared some of his flights. There is a difference between both of us. My flights were commercial, with \uc2 Qantas\uc2  or \uc2 Virgin\uc2 , or Emirates. They were all declared in the appropriate way. I didn\u8217\'20\'19t have to declare any flights on private jets owned by billionaires like \uc2 Gina Rinehart\uc2 , because I haven\u8217\'20\'19t engaged in it. As simple as that. And it\u8217\'20\'19s up to Mr Dutton to explain how those flights were organised to private parties.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.24am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.14am GMT\par \par \uc2 More than 10% of Australian teenagers found to identify as gay, bisexual, pansexual or asexual\par \par \uc2 More than one in 10 Australian teenagers identify as gay, bisexual, pansexual or asexual, a survey of high school students has found.\par \par \uc2 Researchers surveyed 6,388 year 8 students between 2019 and 2021, finding that 12% of the teenagers reported diverse sexualities, while 3.3% identified as gender-diverse.\par \par \uc2 The findings, the study\u8217\'20\'19s authors say, highlight an \u8220\'20\'1Curgent need\u8221\'20\'1D for support services in schools and healthcare settings to mitigate against an increased risk of stigma, discrimination and violence.\par \par \uc2 Read the full story here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/28/diverse-sexuality-australian-high-school-students-gay-bisexual-pansexual"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Diverse sexuality reported by more than one in 10 Australian high school students}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.20am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.57pm GMT\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Immense\u8217\'20\'19 contribution\par \par \uc2 More on Dr \uc2 Michael Brand\uc2 , director of the \uc2 Art Gallery of NSW\uc2 , stepping down.\par \par \uc2 Brand oversaw the $344m Sydney Modern project that included the opening of the award-winning new building, Naala Badu, and the revitalisation of the 153-year-old original building, Naala Nura, both completed almost two years ago.\par \par \uc2 Brand did not reveal what his next move would be.\par \par \uc2 The president of the board of trustees, \uc2 David Gonski\uc2 , who is stepping down from his role in December, said Brand\u8217\'20\'19s contribution to the AGNSW had been immense.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.07am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.56pm GMT\par \par \uc2 AGNSW director to step down next year\par \par \uc2 The director of the \uc2 Art Gallery of New South Wales\uc2 , Dr \uc2 Michael Brand\uc2 , is stepping down from in July 2025.\par \par \uc2 Brand joined the gallery as director in 2012. He said:\par \par \uc2 When we unveiled the Sydney Modern vision in 2013, I said it would be a green light for art, artists and audiences. I am extremely proud of all that we have achieved together in the last 12 years to create a unique art museum experience on Gadigal Country in Sydney for our local and international audiences.\par \par \uc2 This ambitious project is what called me back to Australia so my decision to step down next year follows much reflection. As we are now building on the success of the Sydney Modern transformation, it feels like the right time to transition to a new generation of leadership for the next exciting chapter in the history of our 153-year-old art museum.\par \par \uc2 The president of the board of trustees, David Gonski AC, said:\par \par \uc2 We are indebted to [Brand\u8217\'20\'19s] ambition and vision to create a globally significant art museum in Sydney.\par \par \uc2 A national and international search for a new director will commence soon, Gonski said.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.07am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.43pm GMT\par \par \uc2 More on the overhaul of committal hearings in Victoria\par \par \uc2 For the accused, audiovisual link appearances will become default position in committal proceedings, while the committal hearing process will be bypassed altogether for homicide matters after a successful pilot, which fast-tracked cases to the supreme court.\par \par \uc2 The Victorian attorney general, \uc2 Jaclyn Symes\uc2 , says the reforms act on a number of recommendations made by the \uc2 Victorian Law Reform Commission\uc2  in its 2020 report into committal hearings.\par \par \uc2 She says she knows her changes will be met with \u8220\'20\'1Cmixed reviews\u8221\'20\'1D from the legal community but she was taking into account victim\u8217\'20\'19s experiences.\par \par \uc2 Symes told reporters outside parliament:\par \par \uc2 The most compelling evidence put to me is that when you have sexual offence cases in particular, but [also] family violence and stalking matters, where witnesses are being cross examined in a committal process, then having to repeat that process because the case is proceeding, that\u8217\'20\'19s unacceptable. There is lack of evidence to suggest that cases are being knocked out in the committal stage. It\u8217\'20\'19s just a rerun and so [the reform is] avoiding the retraumatisation of victims having to give repeated evidence and particularly be cross examined.\par \par \uc2 There is no doubt the cross examination process, is an important process of a trial, but it is the most stressful for a victim and a witness and if we can avoid them having to do that twice, I think this is called for.\par \par \uc2 She says there will be a 12-month implementation period and a review of the changes once they\u8217\'20\'19re in place.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 In Australia, the national {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.1800respect.org.au/help-and-support/telephone-and-online-counselling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 family violence counselling service}}}\uc2  is on 1800 737 732. Other international helplines may be found via {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.befrienders.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.befrienders.org}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.50pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.40pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Overhaul of committal hearings in Victoria\par \par \uc2 Traumatic pre-trial cross-examinations will be banned in Victoria as part of a push to protect victims and witnesses in family violence, sexual offence and stalking cases.\par \par \uc2 The Victorian attorney general, \uc2 Jaclyn Symes\uc2 , is announcing new laws she says will guarantee that victims of particularly traumatic cases will only have to give evidence at committal hearings.\par \par \uc2 It will reduce the number of cross-examinations, which Symes says is one of the most challenging parts of the criminal trial process.\par \par \uc2 The justice legislation amendment (committals) bill 2024, will also significantly reduce the amount of pre-trial evidence given by all victims and witnesses, ensuring only questioning that is essential to resolve matters earlier is permitted.\par \par \uc2 The laws will also prevent child victims, witnesses and those with cognitive impairments from having to testify multiple times in family violence cases by allowing them to give prerecorded video evidence, as is already done in sexual offence proceedings.\par \par \uc2 Keep an eye out for more detail in the next blog post.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 In Australia, the national {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.1800respect.org.au/help-and-support/telephone-and-online-counselling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 family violence counselling service}}}\uc2  is on 1800 737 732. Other international helplines may be found via {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.befrienders.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.befrienders.org}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.52pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.33pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Mehreen Faruqi calls on government to sanction Israel\par \par \uc2 Greens deputy leader Mehreen Faruqi is calling on the Albanese government to sanction Israel after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/oct/28/israeli-lawmakers-pass-bill-that-could-halt-unwra-relief-work-in-gaza"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 its parliament voted to ban UN relief agency Unrwa from the country and declared it a terrorist organisation}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Faruqi said:\par \par \uc2 Undermining and stopping \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  from providing aid to Palestinians has been a long term goal of Israel and it\u8217\'20\'19s outrageous that now they are even closer to achieving this because the world has refused to take action to stop them.\par \par \uc2 The Labor government has been \u8216\'20\'18urging\u8217\'20\'19 Israel to abide by international law for a year, and all we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen is its war crimes and genocide escalating.\par \par \uc2 Enough with the weasel words. It\u8217\'20\'19s time for Albanese and Wong to impose sanctions and expel the Israeli ambassador.\par \par \uc2 The foreign affairs minister, \uc2 Penny Wong\uc2 , condemned Israel\u8217\'20\'19s ban on \uc2 Unrwa\uc2  operations {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/oct/29/clean-energy-renewables-solar-thermal-house-prices-property-market-victoria-nsw-e-scooters-qld-election-cost-of-living?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-672016b88f08eb7baa5ebb32#block-672016b88f08eb7baa5ebb32"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 earlier today}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Read more here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/oct/28/israeli-lawmakers-pass-bill-that-could-halt-unwra-relief-work-in-gaza"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Israeli parliament votes to ban Unrwa from Israel within 90 days}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.15am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.18pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Myer to buy multiple brands including Just Jeans\par \par \uc2 Department store chain Myer will buy Premier Investment\u8217\'20\'19s apparel brands in Australia and New Zealand, which includes Just Jeans, Jay Jays, Portmans, Dotti and Jacqui E, the companies announced today.\par \par \uc2 The transaction involves Premier shareholders receiving Myer stock, which will give Premier chairman \uc2 Solomon Lew\uc2  control of just under one-third of Myer shares.\par \par \uc2 Lew said:\par \par \uc2 Myer and our apparel brands will be stronger together \u8211\'20\'13 delivering vertical integration, scale, additional margins and loyalty opportunities.\par \par \uc2 Premier\uc2  will retain the Peter Alexander and Smiggle brands along with its stake in \uc2 Breville Group\uc2  and various property assets.\par \par \uc2 The transaction comes at a challenging time for Myer, which reported lower profits and sales as customers grappled with rising living costs, prompting the department store operator to discount its fashion brands.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.02am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.07pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Wong condemns Israeli ban on \uc2 Unrwa\uc2  operations\par \par \uc2 The foreign affairs minister, \uc2 Penny Wong\uc2 , has criticised new Israeli laws restricting the \uc2 UN\uc2  relief agency \uc2 Unrwa\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s access to Gaza, saying that \uc2 Unrwa\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cdoes life-saving work\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAustralia opposes the Israeli Knesset\u8217\'20\'19s decision to severely restrict \uc2 UNRWA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s work,\u8221\'20\'1D Wong said in a statement posted to the social media site X, formerly \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 On Sunday, Australia joined Canada, France, Germany, Japan, South Korea and the UK to urge Israel\u8217\'20\'19s Knesset not to proceed with this legislation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Unrwa\uc2 , the United Nations Relief Works Agency for Palestine Refugees in the Near East, is the main provider of humanitarian aid to Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Israel\u8217\'20\'19s prime minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  has accused \uc2 Unrwa\uc2  of engaging in \u8220\'20\'1Cterrorist activities\u8221\'20\'1D against Israel, which the agency has denied.\par \par \uc2 Overnight, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/oct/28/israeli-lawmakers-pass-bill-that-could-halt-unwra-relief-work-in-gaza"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Israeli parliament passed legislation }}}\uc2  designating the agency a terrorist organisation, severing ties with it, stripping staff of diplomatic immunity and banning it from operating on Israeli soil \u8211\'20\'13 a move that severely hampers its capacity to deliver aid to Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/oct/28/israeli-lawmakers-pass-bill-that-could-halt-unwra-relief-work-in-gaza"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Israeli parliament votes to ban Unrwa from Israel within 90 days}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.12pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.56pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Australian Energy Regulator\uc2  investigates power outages around Broken Hill\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Australian Energy Regulator\uc2  is investigating \u8220\'20\'1Cpower system events\u8221\'20\'1D that left {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/24/transgrid-nsw-inquiry-failed-backup-generators-broken-hill-outage"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 20,000 people without power}}}\uc2  in Broken Hill and nearby areas, after severe weather hit far west NSW and the region\u8217\'20\'19s only working large-scale emergency generator failed.\par \par \uc2 The investigation will review whether there has been any potential breaches of the national electricity rules, as put in a statement from the regulator. The rules require transmission and distribution networks to \u8220\'20\'1Censure their systems and infrastructure are properly operated and maintained\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 AER\uc2  will coordinate with the \uc2 Independent Pricing and Regulatory Tribunal\uc2  investigation into \uc2 Transgrid\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s compliance with licence conditions and other obligations under state legislation.\par \par \uc2 You can read up on the outage here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/24/transgrid-nsw-inquiry-failed-backup-generators-broken-hill-outage"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Transgrid under pressure as NSW inquiry examines how failed backup generators left Broken Hill in the dark}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.02am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.36pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Reaction to PM\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Qantas\uc2  flight upgrades \u8216\'20\'18a complete pile-on\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 The minister for social services, Amanda Rishworth, and Nationals senator \uc2 Bridget McKenzie\uc2  went back and forth on the Today show this morning, talking reports of Anthony Albanese seeking free \uc2 Qantas\uc2  flight upgrades directly from \uc2 Alan Joyce\uc2  while serving as transport minister.\par \par \uc2 Rishworth said the fallout \u8220\'20\'1Chas been a complete pile-on on the prime minister\u8221\'20\'1D:\par \par \uc2 I mean, he\u8217\'20\'19s publicly declared very, very clearly what he has been gifted. I think there\u8217\'20\'19s something to if you are in a glass house, don\u8217\'20\'19t throw stones.\par \par \uc2 Asked if she has ever called in for an upgrade, Rishworth said:\par \par \uc2 Me personally? No, no, I haven\u8217\'20\'19t rung \uc2 Alan Joyce\uc2 , anyone, [for] an upgrade. But, you know, quite frankly. Well, no, that is not something that I\u8217\'20\'19ve done.\par \par \uc2 Host Karl Stefanovic responded to Rishworth, saying:\par \par \uc2 It wasn\u8217\'20\'19t your most convincing answer, Amanda, I mean, it was a pretty simple question.\par \par \uc2 He then asked McKenzie if she had ever called \uc2 Qantas\uc2  management for an upgrade. McKenzie said:\par \par \uc2 Amanda\u8217\'20\'19s answers aside, this is a serious problem \u8230\'20\'26 The problem is that I don\u8217\'20\'19t know of another transport minister who had a hotline to the CEO of \uc2 Qantas\uc2  to request freebies for family holidays.\par \par \uc2 Pressed to answer the question about calling in for an update, McKenzie said, \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m happy to answer it,\u8221\'20\'1D then went on:\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s a difference to receive a gift and declare it on your register to actually getting on the blower and saying, \u8220\'20\'1CListen, mate, the missus and I are going overseas on a holiday. How about upgrading those economy tickets?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Again, McKenzie was asked whether she has ever called Joyce or \uc2 Qantas\uc2  management for an upgrade. She said:\par \par \uc2 I do not have a hotline to request upgrades. I have received an upgrade in 2018 that I declared, but to my knowledge, no transport minister other than Anthony Albanese has done this.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.01am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.06pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Jeffrey Pope to be new acting Australian electoral commissioner\par \par \uc2 Special minister of state Don Farrell has announced Jeffrey Pope as the new acting Australian electoral commissioner, to take on that role from mid-December.\par \par \uc2 Pope has been the deputy AEC commissioner since 2016 and made \u8220\'20\'1Csignificant contributions\u8221\'20\'1D to the 2023 referendum process and recent federal elections, according to Farrell:\par \par \uc2 This appointment marks the end of a successful 11-year term for the current electoral commissioner, Mr \uc2 Tom Rogers\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Farrell thanked Rogers for his work at the 2016, 2019 and 2022 elections, as well as the 2023 referendum, and praised him for overseeing significant reforms in Australia\u8217\'20\'19s electoral process:\par \par \uc2 Mr Rogers leaves a legacy of stable leadership and a stronger democracy.\par \par \uc2 Farrell appoints Pope as parliament watchers eagerly await the final details of the government\u8217\'20\'19s new electoral reforms, which will propose changes surrounding spending caps and donations at elections. We understand that legislation is now slated to appear in mid-November, just before the parliament finishes for the year.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.55pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.50pm GMT\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18I haven\u8217\'20\'19t worked that out\u8217\'20\'19: Robbie Katter asked about his \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy in Queensland\par \par \uc2 Back to Robbie Katter on ABC RN a short while ago.\par \par \uc2 Asked whether he would amend Queensland\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation or repeal it, Katter said he hasn\u8217\'20\'19t worked it out. He said he had been \u8220\'20\'1Cpulled\u8221\'20\'1D into the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate and that it wasn\u8217\'20\'19t meant to be a priority of the campaign:\par \par \uc2 I was pushed to give a response saying that there\u8217\'20\'19s backbenchers that would say, if they were given the opportunity and they would to turn things around, they would. And they said, \u8220\'20\'1CWould you entertain that?\u8221\'20\'1D I said, \u8220\'20\'1COf course I would, like, that\u8217\'20\'19s what we do.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 And so I responded to a question, and then, you know, blew up from there. And everyone started hitting me up for details on that \u8230\'20\'26 this one isn\u8217\'20\'19t one of my priorities for the new government.\par \par \uc2 But if you ask me, yes, I would try and turn things around, and to what degree, whether that\u8217\'20\'19s, you know, 22 weeks thing, or 16 weeks or 22 weeks, or repeal or new bill and amendment, I haven\u8217\'20\'19t worked that out, Steve, and I\u8217\'20\'19m sorry I can\u8217\'20\'19t give you more detail, but it wasn\u8217\'20\'19t sort of front of centre of my consciousness when I entered the campaign and I got pulled into the debate.\par \par \uc2 The Katter\u8217\'20\'19s Australia party leader has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/15/queensland-state-election-abortion-policy-labor-lnp-robbie-katter"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 vowed to introduce a private member\u8217\'20\'19s bill to wind back \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights and access}}}\uc2  that would notionally spark a conscience vote.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.58pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.33pm GMT\par \par \uc2 One person dead after car crash in Victoria\par \par \uc2 One person has died in a two-vehicle collision in Nyora about 5.20am this morning, according to a statement from Victoria police.\par \par \uc2 It is believed a male driver in a Holden sedan was travelling along the South Gippsland Highway when it collided head-on with a silver \uc2 Mazda\uc2  hatchback at the intersection of Nyora-St Helier Road.\par \par \uc2 The driver of the sedan, yet to be formally identified, died at the scene.\par \par \uc2 The driver of the hatchback, a Poowong man in his 30s, sustained serious injuries and was airlifted to hospital for treatment.\par \par \uc2 Investigations are ongoing. The exact cause of the collision is yet to be determined.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.57pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.24pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Babies deserve representation in parliament, Robbie Katter says\par \par \uc2 Robbie Katter, the Katter\u8217\'20\'19s Australia party leader, says babies deserve representation in parliament.\par \par \uc2 In the final weeks of the Queensland state election campaign, Katter vowed to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/15/queensland-state-election-abortion-policy-labor-lnp-robbie-katter"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 introduce a private member\u8217\'20\'19s bill to wind back \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights and access}}}\uc2  that would notionally spark a conscience vote.\par \par \uc2 He was asked on ABC RN this morning:\par \par \uc2 An argument you hear in the US, and it\u8217\'20\'19s an argument you hear in Australia, that it\u8217\'20\'19s always men who seem to want to change laws about women\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . What would you say to them?\par \par \uc2 Katter replied:\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19d say there\u8217\'20\'19s a lot of babies that are males and females, and there\u8217\'20\'19s 93 members of parliament in Queensland, and looks like [there will] again be four that vote against that vote for pro life. So there\u8217\'20\'19s pretty strong representation for inverted commas, women, those women.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, I think that the babies deserve to have some representation as well. And four is a pretty small number in the Queensland parliament, but I think those babies deserve representation.\par \par \uc2 A lot of those women you speak of, they were babies once too, and I\u8217\'20\'19m sure they would have preferred to have some representation when they were babies as well.\par \par \uc2 Labor decriminalised \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2018 and has sought to put the issue at the forefront of the campaign, saying there is a \u8220\'20\'1Cvery real risk\u8221\'20\'1D that \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be wound back if the LNP wins government. Read more from Ben Smee \u8217\'20\'19s report here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/15/queensland-state-election-abortion-policy-labor-lnp-robbie-katter"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Greens accuse Queensland Labor of \u8216\'20\'18pure hypocrisy\u8217\'20\'19 on \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  after Katter party election preferences}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.56pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.13pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The significance of Thorpe\u8217\'20\'19s royal protest\par \par \uc2 Independent senator for Victoria \uc2 Lidia Thorpe\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s protest before \uc2 King Charles\uc2  in the Great Hall of Parliament House last week caught news attention around the globe.\par \par \uc2 While it prompted a few reactions, it also started a conversation about the British monarchy\u8217\'20\'19s role in the lasting legacy of colonisation for First Nations people.\par \par \uc2 For today\u8217\'20\'19s Full Story podcast, Reged Ahmad speaks to political editor Karen Middleton about why Australians can\u8217\'20\'19t stop talking about the royal protest and what it means for Thorpe\u8217\'20\'19s role as a senator.\par \par \uc2 Listen here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/audio/2024/oct/28/why-lidia-thorpes-royal-protest-continues-to-hit-a-nerve-full-story-podcast"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Why Lidia Thorpe\u8217\'20\'19s royal protest continues to hit a nerve - podcast}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.35pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.00pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Bowen deflects question about PM\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Qantas\uc2  flight upgrades\par \par \uc2 Climate and energy minister Chris Bowens says Anthony Albanese is not focused on political debates \u8220\'20\'1Cabout what conversations he may or may not have had\u8221\'20\'1D, after reports of the prime minister {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/28/albaneses-reported-requests-for-qantas-upgrades-a-bit-strange-dutton-says-while-defending-own-upgrades"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 seeking free Qantas flight upgrades}}}\uc2  directly from \uc2 Alan Joyce\uc2  while serving as transport minister and opposition leader.\par \par \uc2 He said on ABC Radio National:\par \par \uc2 The opposition can focus on the cost of \uc2 Qantas\uc2  flights for their life. The Labor party and the prime minister is focused on cost of living for ordinary Australians. Whether it be the cost of housing and a housing package we want to get through cost of energy and our reforms there.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Anthony Albanese is, I can tell you, as a senior member of his cabinet, focused like the laser on these issues, not on these sort of political debates about what he may conversations he may or may not have had, you know, 10 or 15 years ago. He\u8217\'20\'19s focused on issues today.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.03pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.50pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Bowen derides Coalition\u8217\'20\'19s \u8216\'20\'18nuclear fantasy\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Chris Bowen, minister for climate change and energy, is speaking on ABC Radio National this morning.\par \par \uc2 He says going \u8220\'20\'1Cdown the nuclear road\u8221\'20\'1D for power is not playing to Australia\u8217\'20\'19s strengths:\par \par \uc2 If I was the energy minister of another country, I would consider the opportunities that I had in that country \u8211\'20\'13 but a country saying to Australia, with our excellent renewable resources, that we should go down the nuclear road when we have no nuclear industry, no nuclear expertise of the scale that we would need for a nuclear power industry, is like us going to Finland or Scandinavia and saying, \u8220\'20\'1CListen, we know [you have] a lot of snow, but you should really try beach surfing.\u8221\'20\'1D It just doesn\u8217\'20\'19t make any sense.\par \par \uc2 We have to play to our strengths in Australia, and we have the best renewable resources in the world, and the opposition wants to stop us using them, and in turn, keep coal in the system for longer. They\u8217\'20\'19re quite explicit about that while we wait for this nuclear fantasy to come on board. That would be terrible for emissions and fatal for energy reliability.\par \par \uc2 The opposition leader \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s plan to build seven nuclear power plants has been scrutinised by government agencies and department officials. Read about it here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/24/peter-dutton-nuclear-plant-sites-issues"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Potential issues\u8217\'20\'19 with Coalition\u8217\'20\'19s planned nuclear reactor sites, safety expert warns}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.39pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.34pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Victorian Greens criticise Labor\u8217\'20\'19s housing plan\par \par \uc2 The Victorian Greens leader, Ellen Sandell, has dismissed \uc2 Jacinta Allan\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s criticism, arguing her party has been campaigning for years for more housing:\par \par \uc2 The difference between the Greens and Labor is that the Greens want this housing to be genuinely affordable, yet Labor\u8217\'20\'19s plan will only build expensive, luxury apartments. Labor\u8217\'20\'19s plan leaves things totally in the hands of profit-driven developers who have no interest in building homes for young people, nurses, teachers \u8211\'20\'13 the people who actually need them.\par \par \uc2 Dr David Hayward, an emeritus professor of public policy at \uc2 RMIT\uc2 , welcomed the Greens\u8217\'20\'19 plan, arguing the private sector undersupplied housing to maximise profits:\par \par \uc2 You can deregulate as much as you want, but unless those private actors can get their financial return, they\u8217\'20\'19re not going to build what you want. The direct intervention proposed by the Greens is a way of plugging that gap. A state-owned builder wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be the main player, just an additional one. It could even be a not-for-profit that does it, it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t really matter, as long as the motivation is not financial return. The main driver is the need to provide housing.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.33pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.23pm GMT\par \par \uc2 La Ni\u241\'00\'F1a looking more unlikely for the coming summer\par \par \uc2 As we\u8217\'20\'19ve flagged for a while, it\u8217\'20\'19s looking unlikely that we\u8217\'20\'19ll see a La Ni\u241\'00\'F1a weather event in the Pacific this summer.\par \par \uc2 The latest Bureau of Meteorology models place just a 4% chance that La Ni\u241\'00\'F1a thresholds will be crossed in January (and less of a chance in December and February).\par \par \uc2 enltrLatest model runs by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BOM_au?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @bom_au}}}\uc2  indicate conditions in the Pacific will continue to be neutral with only about a 4% chance of a La Nina threshold being reached. La Nina not happening, in other words... {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/hWrCzMqaxL"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/hWrCzMqaxL}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 @phannam@mastodon.green (@p_hannam) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/p_hannam/status/1850798585375494388?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 28, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 The bureau will provide a fuller update of the main climate drivers this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 La Ni\u241\'00\'F1as tend to mean we get wetter than usual weather over much of the country, particularly in eastern Australia (and more tropical cyclones than the average).\par \par \uc2 But conditions in the Indian Ocean are tilted towards the negative phase of the ocean\u8217\'20\'19s dipole (which measures temperature differences across the basin). These indicate we might still get a relatively damp end to spring going into summer.\par \par \uc2 enltrMeanwhile, out in the Indian Ocean, a negative phase of the dipole looks to be underway. That tends to mean wetter-than-average conditions in spring for much of Australia. (Via {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BOM_au?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @bom_au}}}\uc2  ) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/8AdggOfD39"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/8AdggOfD39}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 @phannam@mastodon.green (@p_hannam) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/p_hannam/status/1850798960086237242?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 28, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 And that\u8217\'20\'19s what {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.bom.gov.au/climate/outlooks/#/overview/summary"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the bureau\u8217\'20\'19s outlooks}}}\uc2  indicate too. That summer umbrella might turn out to be handy for both the rain and the sun.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.34pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.20pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Greens policy costings\par \par \uc2 The policy is similar to a federal Greens proposal announced in March. Based on a federal Parliamentary Budget Office costing conducted at the time, it was forecast renters could save more than $122 a week a household and first home buyers $260,157 on a home, compared with median prices.\par \par \uc2 But the costing rested on the assumption that state and territory governments would also agree to waive stamp duty, which the PBO warned was unlikely to occur.\par \par \uc2 The Victorian Greens are also calling for more homes to be sold than the federal plan, in which 75% would be rentals.\par \par \uc2 Last week \uc2 Jacinta Allan\uc2  made several announcements designed to stimulate development in Melbourne\u8217\'20\'19s inner suburbs, with the most significant being a plan to densify 50 areas near train and tram stations.\par \par \uc2 Allan on Thursday vowed to be \u8220\'20\'1Cthe premier who gets millennials into homes\u8221\'20\'1D and described both the Liberals and Greens as \u8220\'20\'1Cblockers\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.10pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Victorian Greens call for state-owned housing developer\par \par \uc2 The Victorian Greens are calling on the state government to enter the property development game, saying Labor\u8217\'20\'19s plan to get more millennials into the housing market will not work without such an intervention.\par \par \uc2 In response to a series of housing announcements made last week by the premier, \uc2 Jacinta Allan\uc2 , the minor party is calling on the government to set up a \u8220\'20\'1Cpublic builder\u8221\'20\'1D to construct affordable homes.\par \par \uc2 Under the Green\u8217\'20\'19s plan, a state-owned developer would build 200,000 homes over the next 20 years and sell half for just 5% above the cost of construction. The remainder would be rented out for a maximum of 25% of household income.\par \par \uc2 Distinct from public housing, the homes would be available for the wider public to buy or rent, though the party wants households on the social housing waitlist to be prioritised for rentals.\par \par \uc2 They say the homes would be located near train stations and in regional areas and would be funded initially through \u8220\'20\'1Ctaxing big corporations\u8221\'20\'1D, revenue collected from the state\u8217\'20\'19s vacant property and short-stay taxes, and a contribution from the commonwealth.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.07pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Good morning\par \par \uc2 Thank you Martin Farrer for kicking off the blog this morning!\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19ll be rolling your live news updates throughout the day \u8211\'20\'13 if you see anything you don\u8217\'20\'19t want us to miss, shoot it my way on X (formerly \uc2 Twitter\uc2 ) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/At_Raf_"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @At_Raf}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.38pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.56pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Brisbane, Adelaide and Perth property markets performing better than other capital cities\par \par \uc2 More on that housing price story from \uc2 AAP\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 Domain\u8217\'20\'19s Nicola Powell said apartments performing better than houses in cities such as Sydney and Melbourne was evidence of stretched affordability and buyers seeking out the less expensive options.\par \par \uc2 Inflation and wage growth \u8211\'20\'13 robust but not enough to keep up with the housing market \u8211\'20\'13 was weighing on demand, along with high interest rates clipping borrowing capacity.\par \par \uc2 Powell said buyers were \u8220\'20\'1Cwaiting on the sidelines\u8221\'20\'1D for firmer signals on interest rate moves.\par \par \uc2 In the September quarter, Brisbane, Adelaide and Perth continued to do better than other capital city property markets.\par \par \uc2 House prices in Adelaide gained 4.2%, with 3.1% in Perth and 1.5% in Brisbane.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.37pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.55pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Property market losing heat, Domain says\par \par \uc2 Homebuyer budgets are finding a ceiling, slowing the pace of property price growth, \uc2 Australian Associated Press\uc2  reports.\par \par \uc2 Domain\u8217\'20\'19s chief of research and economics, Nicola Powell, says the housing market appears to be shifting in favour of buyers:\par \par \uc2 Supply is rising, days on the market are lengthening, discounting is also increasing.\par \par \uc2 Everything points to a slowdown overall, and it\u8217\'20\'19s very possible that we could see a dip in price even over the final quarter of this year, on that aggregate capital city level.\par \par \uc2 Prices were still going up in the three months to September. Domain recorded 0.8% growth in the quarter and slightly more for units, with both finding record highs.\par \par \uc2 But prices are not rising as fast, with the pace of quarterly house prices in Sydney and Perth growing half as quickly as in the previous quarter.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.36pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.55pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Albanese to announce clean energy projects in NSW and Victoria\par \par \uc2 Anthony Albanese is due to announce two more clean energy-related projects in NSW and Victoria aimed at transitioning Australia\u8217\'20\'19s energy production and workforce away from fossil fuels.\par \par \uc2 Albanese will announce federal funding for both projects under his government\u8217\'20\'19s Future Made in Australia scheme and contrast them with the Coalition\u8217\'20\'19s plans to develop nuclear power stations at seven sites around Australia if it wins office.\par \par \uc2 Albanese will travel to the NSW Hunter region on Tuesday to announce a new $60m \u8220\'20\'1Cnet zero manufacturing centre of excellence\u8221\'20\'1D at a Tafe campus in Newcastle, to be jointly funded with the NSW government. The centre will develop a new apprenticeship model focused on the skills required for modern, clean-energy manufacturing, training workers for jobs in the Hunter-Central Coast renewable energy zone.\par \par \uc2 He will then travel to Wodonga in Victoria to announce a $17m federal funding injection for the nation\u8217\'20\'19s first concentrated solar thermal heat plant, expanding the application of solar power beyond electricity to heat generation.\par \par \uc2 The government says the project will involve an 18 megawatt thermal plant with up to 10 hours\u8217\'20\'19 storage capacity which it says will halve the use of gas where it is installed and generate 80 jobs during construction. He will say:\par \par \uc2 Creating jobs, investing in our regions, reducing emissions and bringing down power prices \u8211\'20\'13 that\u8217\'20\'19s what we\u8217\'20\'19re delivering.\par \par \uc2 Renewable energy is bringing new jobs and new industry across our country, including regional Australia.\par \par \uc2 He is expected to demand again that opposition leader, \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2 , reveal the costings for his nuclear-energy plans.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.05pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.30pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Welcome\par \par \uc2 Good morning and welcome to our live news blog. I\u8217\'20\'19m Martin Farrer with the best of the overnight stories before Rafqa Touma picks up the slack.\par \par \uc2 Australian schools have an \u8220\'20\'1Curgent need\u8221\'20\'1D for support services to help pupils at risk of stigma, discrimination and violence as a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/28/diverse-sexuality-australian-high-school-students-gay-bisexual-pansexual"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 major survey indicates}}}\uc2  that more than 10% are LGBTQ+ or gender-diverse. More than one in 10 Australian teenagers in the survey identified as gay, bisexual, pansexual or asexual, with 3.3% of the more than 6,000 questioned identifying as gender-diverse.\par \par \uc2 The Victorian Greens are calling on the state government to enter the property development game, saying Labor\u8217\'20\'19s plan to get more millennials into the housing market will not work without such an intervention. We\u8217\'20\'19ll have a full post coming up and we also have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/commentisfree/2024/oct/29/australian-cities-are-desperate-for-the-missing-middle-of-housing-density-but-its-not-as-simple-as-the-nimby-yimby-debate-suggests"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an op-ed piece today from housing policy expert}}}\uc2  Peter Mares who argues that only government intervention can redirect development to the kind of \u8220\'20\'1Cmiddle-density\u8221\'20\'1D type needed to solve the crisis.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Domain\u8217\'20\'19s chief economist says the property market is beginning to cool and move in favour of buyers. Research by the property company shows that prices rose in the last quarter by 0.8% but the rate of increase is slowing, with the pace in Sydney and Perth, for example, half as quick as in the previous quarter. More coming up.\par \par \uc2 Anthony Albanese is due to announce two more clean-energy-related projects in NSW and Victoria today, aimed at transitioning Australia\u8217\'20\'19s energy production and workforce away from fossil fuels. Details coming up.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241028ekas002s7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
