\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18I\u8217\'20\'19m not afraid. Let\u8217\'20\'19s do it\u8217\'20\'19: the Arizona  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  clinic testing the limits of the state\u8217\'20\'19s ban\b0\par\par\uc2 Cecilia Nowell in Phoenix with photographs by Kasia Strek \par \uc2 2,548 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 October 2024\par 16:45 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The owner of the Camelback Family Planning is willing to take risks for patients that other doctors won\u8217\'20\'19t, while staying within the bounds of the state\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban\par \par \uc2 Gabrielle Goodrick\u8217\'20\'19s day has barely begun and she\u8217\'20\'19s already poring over a stack of legal documents. As the nurses and other doctors at her clinic start seeing the first of what will be upwards of 40 patients that day, the 59-year-old family medicine doctor and owner of Camelback Family Planning \u8211\'20\'13 one of Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s few \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics \u8211\'20\'13 has a decision to make.\par \par \uc2 Since the supreme court overturned Roe v Wade in June 2022, Arizona has banned all abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy \u8211\'20\'13 with a small but vague carveout for medical emergencies. In November, Arizona voters will get to weigh in on a ballot measure that would {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/article/2024/aug/13/arizona-abortion-november-vote"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 enshrine in the state constitution the right to \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  until viability}}}\uc2 , or around 24 weeks.\par \par \uc2 But that ballot initiative won\u8217\'20\'19t help the 32-year-old woman in Goodrick\u8217\'20\'19s waiting room. The woman had been thrilled five months ago when she\u8217\'20\'19d become pregnant, seven years after the birth of her first child, until her water broke prematurely 17 weeks into her pregnancy \u8211\'20\'13 far too early for her baby to survive. With no amniotic fluid left in her uterus, it\u8217\'20\'19s only a matter of time until an infection sets in.\par \par \uc2 In the clinic\u8217\'20\'19s break room, Goodrick sets down the stack of papers \u8211\'20\'13 which include {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.azag.gov/sites/default/files/2024-06/R24-011.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a letter}}}\uc2  four state representatives wrote to Arizona\u8217\'20\'19s attorney general this summer asking what qualified as a \u8220\'20\'1Cmedical emergency\u8221\'20\'1D and the attorney general\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.azag.gov/opinions/i24-009-r24-011"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 response}}}\uc2 . Dressed in turquoise corduroy trousers and a white button-up, Goodrick pulls her hair into a claw clip before she begins describing the case to a few of the clinic\u8217\'20\'19s nurses. Across the room, her colleague Barbara Zipkin, an obstetrician-gynecologist, listens in as she strokes her 5lb, 16-year-old dog Scooter. The maltese-yorkie mix is a fixture at the clinic, regularly accompanying Zipkin into patient rooms to provide emotional support.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNo one will do it,\u8221\'20\'1D Goodrick says. So far, the only known abortions after 15 weeks in the state have been performed when a patient develops a severe infection, but Goodrick doesn\u8217\'20\'19t want to make this patient wait that long.\par \par \uc2 The woman\u8217\'20\'19s healthcare network has already said it won\u8217\'20\'19t end the pregnancy, and so she and her husband have started talking to a doctor in Las Vegas. But the six-hour drive to Nevada is no small hurdle. And that clinic \u8211\'20\'13 like most offering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in the second and third trimester in the United States \u8211\'20\'13 only offers a procedure called dilation and evacuation. During a D&E, a patient\u8217\'20\'19s cervix is dilated, often using small rods called laminaria that slowly expand over the course of one to two days, before a doctor empties the patient\u8217\'20\'19s uterus using suction or surgical instruments. It\u8217\'20\'19s difficult for providers to remove the fetus intact during a D&E \u8211\'20\'13 but this woman wants to hold her baby to say goodbye.\par \par \uc2 Camelback is one of only a handful of clinics in the US offering an alternative called induction \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Like a medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in which patients take the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills mifepristone and misoprostol to end a pregnancy in the first trimester, an induction \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  involves taking the same pills in the second or third trimester. Because the fetus is larger than in the first trimester, the misoprostol triggers contractions that are more akin to labor than an earlier medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the procedure usually occurs in a hospital instead of at home. (Camelback manages the pain of that labor \u8211\'20\'13 which is quicker and less severe than childbirth \u8211\'20\'13 with painkillers and conscious sedation). The result is that patients \u8211\'20\'13 like this one \u8211\'20\'13 can hold and grieve the fetus afterwards, and even take home footprints.\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s legal risk to helping this patient \u8211\'20\'13 Goodrick\u8217\'20\'19s not sure she would do it if the state didn\u8217\'20\'19t have a Democratic governor and attorney general \u8211\'20\'13 but she\u8217\'20\'19ll remark later how frustrating she finds it that more doctors aren\u8217\'20\'19t willing to use their position to more aggressively challenge \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions. She is in a bind countless doctors have faced in the last two years, as stories of women denied emergency abortions in their home states have piled up.\par \par \uc2 Zipkin agrees they need to act. From her perch on a black office chair, she responds, defiant: \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m not afraid. Let\u8217\'20\'19s do it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 In the aftermath of Roe, patients across the United States are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jamanetworkopen/fullarticle/2819135"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 seeking \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  care later in pregnancy}}}\uc2 , as a growing web of restrictions {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9382185/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 makes it increasingly difficult}}}\uc2  for them to access care sooner. And the procedure most physicians offer after the first trimester, a D&E, is not always the procedure patients want \u8211\'20\'13 for example, if they\u8217\'20\'19d prefer to hold their fetus after or avoid cervical dilation with laminaria.\par \par \uc2 When she found out that we could see her, she started crying. She was just overwhelmingly relieved\par \par \uc2 In the United States, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills are \uc2 FDA\uc2 -approved through 10 weeks of pregnancy \u8211\'20\'13 but it\u8217\'20\'19s still legal to use them \u8220\'20\'1Coff label\u8221\'20\'1D later. While they\u8217\'20\'19re not widely prescribed later in the US, they are the standard for second- and third-trimester care in much of northern Europe \u8211\'20\'13 and the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care guidelines describe how to use the pills up to and beyond 24 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Goodrick and her colleagues are hopeful that educating more physicians on the use of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills later in pregnancy could expand access to care across the US by allowing more physicians with labor and delivery training, rather than only doctors with the surgical skills required to perform a D&E, to offer the procedure. Although conservative groups have called the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill \u8220\'20\'1Cunsafe\u8221\'20\'1D in an attempt to undo the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s approval of the medication, Goodrick believes it could be an equalizer for physicians.\par \par \uc2 In that spirit, this year they published {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2590151624000017"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a study}}}\uc2  sharing their experience providing hundreds of induction abortions to end pregnancies between 18 and 24 weeks before Roe fell. They also regularly host and train medical students.\par \par \uc2 Offering that care has been harder \u8211\'20\'13 but not impossible \u8211\'20\'13 since Arizona banned abortions after 15 weeks. Since the ban went into effect, Camelback has directed patients whose pregnancies are past that mark to a clinic in southern California, where they\u8217\'20\'19re able to receive and take mifepristone, which stops the pregnancy from progressing. The next day, after they\u8217\'20\'19ve returned to Arizona, Goodrick and her team administer misoprostol, which induces contractions. They\u8217\'20\'19re able to do so because the pregnancy has already ended, Goodrick says, meaning they\u8217\'20\'19re essentially treating a stillbirth. It\u8217\'20\'19s a loophole that Goodrick\u8217\'20\'19s attorneys have reassured her is legal \u8211\'20\'13 and it helps patients avoid the cost of a surgical procedure and out-of-state hotel.\par \par \uc2 But this patient, who has found her way to Camelback one day in mid-September, is the first the clinic will treat entirely in Arizona since the state\u8217\'20\'19s 15-week ban went into effect in 2022.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen she found out that we could see her, she started crying,\u8221\'20\'1D Goodrick said. \u8220\'20\'1CShe was so upset still about the hospital not helping her. She was just overwhelmingly relieved.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI know it\u8217\'20\'19s scary\u8221\'20\'1D for doctors to push back against restrictions, says Goodrick \u8211\'20\'13 and she understands that hospitals and their legal teams are even more risk averse. But she\u8217\'20\'19s steadfast: \u8220\'20\'1CI think it\u8217\'20\'19s up to the medical field to say [to the state], \u8216\'20\'18You don\u8217\'20\'19t know what you\u8217\'20\'19re talking about.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 Goodrick never intended to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care \u8211\'20\'13 but early in her career she saw the need.\par \par \uc2 She remembers Henry Morgentaler\u8217\'20\'19s name appearing in the news when she was an undergraduate student at \uc2 McGill University in Montreal\uc2  in the 1980s. A Holocaust survivor, Morgentaler had emigrated to Canada, where he began his career, like Goodrick, as a general practitioner. Over time, he became one of the first Canadian doctors to offer vasectomies, IUDs, birth control to unmarried women, and eventually abortions \u8211\'20\'13 and his repeated challenges of Canadian law would lead to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  being legalized nationwide in 1988. The next year, Goodrick enrolled in medical school.\par \par \uc2 At the \uc2 University of Vermont\uc2 , Goodrick felt drawn to women\u8217\'20\'19s health \u8211\'20\'13 but not obstetrics and gynecology, a surgical specialty. Instead, she gravitated toward family medicine, where she could receive some obstetrics training but follow patients through their full lives. In 1990 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/1990/05/07/us/abortion-foes-clog-vermont-courts.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 anti-\b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  protesters descended}}}\uc2  on Burlington with the intention of closing two clinics there. That year, she co-founded a pro-choice alliance of medical students at the university.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI didn\u8217\'20\'19t really think of abortions like, \u8216\'20\'18This is what I\u8217\'20\'19m going to do,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D she said. But when Goodrick began her residency at Phoenix Baptist hospital (now Abrazo Central) in 1993, she did a rotation at the local \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  and enjoyed it. She would ultimately open a family practice clinic, where she also offered occasional abortions, though they weren\u8217\'20\'19t the focus of her work.\par \par \uc2 That changed after 2000, when the Food and Drug Administration approved mifepristone for use in the US. Goodrick says she was the first provider in the south-west to offer the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill \u8211\'20\'13 and she remembers patients flying in from across the country to access it.\par \par \uc2 In those years, even though Roe was still standing, there were few physicians providing second-trimester abortions in the US, and one of the most prominent, George Tiller, was murdered in 2009. As she developed her skills in D&E, Goodrick began to wonder, \u8220\'20\'1CHow am I going to get help? How am I going to be doing this five days a week for the rest of my life?\u8221\'20\'1D The surgical procedure required advanced training, and she worried about finding other physicians to fill in at and eventually take over her practice.\par \par \uc2 In 2018, Goodrick was attending the International Federation of \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Contraception Professionals\u8217\'20\'19 biannual conference in France when she walked into a session led by Swedish midwives. She discovered that it\u8217\'20\'19s standard in Sweden to provide second trimester abortions not with surgical instruments, but with pills.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe VERY rarely use D&E and almost exclusively use medical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D or pills, in Sweden, said Kristina Gemzell Danielsson, a professor of obstetrics and gynecology at the Karolinska Institutet in Stockholm, via email. In the 1980s, she helped develop what would become the WHO protocol for using \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills in the second trimester.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDue to our geography and [the fact] that many live far from a hospital, surgical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the second trimester is not feasible,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. Today, medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the second trimester, a rarity in the US, is available \u8220\'20\'1Cin all OB-GYN clinics in the whole country\u8221\'20\'1D, she adds. It is often performed by midwives.\par \par \uc2 When Goodrick returned to Arizona, she told the doctors at her clinic she wanted to bring the practice to the clinic. They were wary at first. D&Es had been in use in the US since the 1970s and were by far the most common way to end a pregnancy in the second trimester; today, they make up about 95% of abortions after 14 weeks in the US. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21422848/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Studies}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/pdf/10.1016/S0968-8080%2808%2931378-0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in the late 2000s}}}\uc2  had found that D&Es resulted in fewer complications than inductions (though {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bmcwomenshealth.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s12905-020-0889-9"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 research}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0301211522004602"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 today}}}\uc2  suggests they\u8217\'20\'19re equally safe) and many providers preferred a 20-minute procedure to hours-long labor.\par \par \uc2 But Zipkin would get on board and today prefers the inductions. While the risk of perforating the uterus with surgical instruments during a D&E is low, with induction abortions it disappears \u8211\'20\'13 and patients don\u8217\'20\'19t have to spend the day before in discomfort as laminaria dilates their cervix.\par \par \uc2 Although training in D&E is still vital for any clinic offering care later in pregnancy \u8211\'20\'13 in case, for example, a patient\u8217\'20\'19s uterus doesn\u8217\'20\'19t expel all of the pregnancy tissue with pills alone \u8211\'20\'13 Goodrick believes the medication might allow more providers to offer abortions in areas where OB-GYNs are few and far between.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 There are about half a dozen protesters outside Camelback when Goodrick returns to work the morning after meeting with the patient who needs help as her pregnancy approaches 20 weeks. The sight isn\u8217\'20\'19t unusual, and Goodrick notes that they\u8217\'20\'19ll be gone by the time the Arizona sun is high in the sky, pushing temperatures into the 90s on this relatively cool summer day. Two cheerful clinic escorts greet patients at their cars and use large umbrellas to block them from the protesters\u8217\'20\'19 views.\par \par \uc2 As she holds the clinic door open for patients, one of the escorts \u8211\'20\'13 a 66-year-old sporting a rainbow vest, straw witch\u8217\'20\'19s hat and a bevy of buttons and pins promoting feminism and voting rights \u8211\'20\'13 recalls the second-trimester \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  she received in 1999. She\u8217\'20\'19d been trying to get pregnant and, at one of her regular prenatal appointments, her doctor realized her fetus had stopped growing, probably two weeks earlier. Her regular OB-GYN had been able to offer her a D&E. \u8220\'20\'1CNow in Arizona I would not be able to do that,\u8221\'20\'1D she says, declining to give her name.\par \par \uc2 The 32-year-old mother in need of care is now resting in a recliner inside. When she arrived at the clinic that morning, Zipkin gave her a small dose of misoprostol to induce labor and painkillers to ease the ensuing cramps. By lunchtime, when Zipkin sits down to sip an orange Sunkist, it\u8217\'20\'19s delivery time: \u8220\'20\'1CDoctor!\u8221\'20\'1D a nurse beckons down the hall. Within a few minutes, Goodrick joins her in the patient\u8217\'20\'19s room.\par \par \uc2 Less than an hour later, they\u8217\'20\'19re both back in the break room. The woman is coming out from under sedation; her husband is about to join her to say goodbye to their baby. \u8220\'20\'1CShe did great,\u8221\'20\'1D Zipkin says.\par \par \uc2 Goodrick has by now spoken with the Arizona attorney general, who has reassured her the case falls under her definition of a medical emergency. But she worries the Arizona department of health will still flag the case after Camelback reports it \u8211\'20\'13 and that the conservative county attorney could decide to prosecute the clinic despite the state AG\u8217\'20\'19s position.\par \par \uc2 She\u8217\'20\'19s hopeful she won\u8217\'20\'19t have to deal with these kinds of concerns after Arizonians vote in November on whether to enshrine the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until 24 weeks. \u8220\'20\'1CObviously what happens federally is very important, but every state needs their own ballot initiative,\u8221\'20\'1D Goodrick said.\par \par \uc2 In moments like these, Goodrick reflects on the legacies of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers who came before, like Dr Tiller. \u8220\'20\'1CA lot of us still think of him. \u8216\'20\'18What would he do now? How would he act? How would he be calm and sure of himself?\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D she says.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s why, she says, she told the attorney general, \u8220\'20\'1CIn the future, if you hear of these cases and they can\u8217\'20\'19t get done in the hospital, tell us. We\u8217\'20\'19ll see them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241028ekas001gu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
