\par\par \uc2 Film\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18We just have to keep fighting\u8217\'20\'19: a shocking new film on the danger of US  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  laws\b0\par\par\uc2 Adrian Horton \par \uc2 1,440 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 October 2024\par 08:26 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 In the Hilary Clinton and Jennifer Lawrence-produced documentary Zurawski v Texas, women whose lives have been brutally upended have their say\par \par \uc2 In August 2022, Amanda and Josh Zurawski were 18 weeks into a much-wanted pregnancy with their first child when her water broke early. The complication ended her chances of delivering a healthy baby and imperiled her health \u8211\'20\'13 but doctors in Austin, where the couple live, said they could not end her pregnancy under Texas law, because they could still detect fetal cardiac activity.\par \par \uc2 In the wake of the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision in Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization in June 2022, which reversed half a century of precedent and overturned Roe v Wade, the Texas legislature, like {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2024/jul/29/abortion-laws-bans-by-state"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 those in 13 other states}}}\uc2 , passed a near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. Though the ban allowed for medical exceptions, doctors have said that the law \u8211\'20\'13 written by politicians, not medical professionals \u8211\'20\'13 is so vaguely worded, and the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/sep/24/abortion-prosecutions-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 criminal penalties so severe}}}\uc2  (up to 99 years in prison for violating state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law), that it was unworkable in practice, blocking doctors from helping patients.\par \par \uc2 So Zurawski, against her wishes and the common standard of care, remained pregnant, knowing her daughter stood no chance of survival, as she grew sicker and sicker. As she testified to the state in a lawsuit that bears her name \u8211\'20\'13 the first post-Roe v Wade to be filed by women who said that their health, lives or fertility had been endangered by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, and the subject of a new documentary executive produced by Hillary and \uc2 Chelsea Clinton\uc2  and Jennifer Lawrence \u8211\'20\'13 she listened to the cardiac activity with a terrible mix of love, for the child she and Josh desperately wanted, and dread, for her deteriorating health. Doctors eventually performed an emergency induction \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 but only after she developed sepsis and nearly died. The infection kept her in the ICU for days and cost her one of her fallopian tubes, permanently compromising her future ability to have children.\par \par \uc2 Aided by the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Zurawski sued the state of Texas, looking for clarity in the law as written, and responsibility for a policy that has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/jun/24/texas-abortion-ban-infant-deaths"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 led to a 13% increase}}}\uc2  in infant deaths, forced doctors to provide substandard care and inflicted {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2024/oct/21/idaho-abortion-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 incalculable trauma on women seeking basic healthcare}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSomebody had to file the first suit,\u8221\'20\'1D Zurawski says in the film named after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://reproductiverights.org/zurawski-v-texas-plaintiffs-stories-remarks/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 her lawsuit}}}\uc2 , Zurawski v Texas, released just before a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/sep/26/election-abortion-ballot-measures-states"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pivotal election}}}\uc2  in which \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  across the country are on the line.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat we\u8217\'20\'19re asking for in this lawsuit is the bare minimum human decency requires,\u8221\'20\'1D says \uc2 Molly Duane\uc2 , the Center\u8217\'20\'19s leading attorney for the suit, in the film. \u8220\'20\'1CPeople are going to die. And the question is, does anyone in the state of Texas in a position of power actually care?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The film, directed by Texas-bred film-makers Abbie Perrault and Maisie Crow, captures with journalistic clarity the chaos, uncertainty and unnecessary pain and suffering that ensues when a state bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 what happens in court, in hospitals, and in the private aftermath. It is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2024/jan/24/us-miscarriage-laws-abortion-rights-options"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one particular slice of post-Roe America}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 life-saving or simply dignifying healthcare denied, because of legal uncertainty, to women who wanted to be pregnant in the first place.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s like we\u8217\'20\'19re in triage mode,\u8221\'20\'1D Crow told the Guardian. \u8220\'20\'1CIf someone has to go septic before they can get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that means that for everyone else who needs \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for all the reasons that women seek \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, they are not getting that care either. We really felt like we needed to highlight the most dire situation that was unfolding in front of us to emphasize the fact that if the women in this lawsuit aren\u8217\'20\'19t getting care, then no one else is.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 And the situation, as the film-makers found, was dire. As soon as Zurawski filed her suit, dozens of women, along with their partners and families, reached out with similarly harrowing stories. In a collage of voicemails played during the film, others share similar experiences: an ectopic pregnancy denied care until her fallopian tube ruptured, another inviable pregnancy carried to term. Share my story, tell people what is happening. \u8220\'20\'1CThe state absolutely wants to sweep their experiences under the rug,\u8221\'20\'1D says Duane. \u8220\'20\'1CThey just want to pretend like real people don\u8217\'20\'19t exist at all.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Zurawski v Texas follows three of the eventual 22 plaintiffs, who provided in-person testimony on their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stories, something that was happening in court for the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://kffhealthnews.org/news/article/how-the-texas-trial-changed-the-story-of-abortion-rights-in-america/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 first time since the 1970s}}}\uc2 . Samantha Casiano, who lived in East Texas with her partner and their children, learned at her 20-week scan that her daughter, Halo, would be born without most of her brain \u8211\'20\'13 a defect called anencephaly that was incompatible with life. Unable to afford travel out of state for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , she was forced to carry the pregnancy to term while planning for her daughter\u8217\'20\'19s funeral, then watch as Halo gasped for air for four hours after birth. Dr Austin Dennard, an OB-GYN based in Dallas, also learned her long-awaited third child had anencephaly, and had to travel out of state for the medical care she used to be able to provide her patients in her home community. \u8220\'20\'1CHaving these laws cripple our ability to provide care \u8211\'20\'13 it feels like a slap in the face,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. Zurawski, traumatized by her medical experience, resorts to surrogacy in another state.\par \par \uc2 The film is part behind the scenes of their court hearing \u8211\'20\'13 nerves, fury, pride, inexpressible pain (Casiano, recounting Halo\u8217\'20\'19s short life after birth, vomited on the stand) \u8211\'20\'13 and part documentation of trauma, healing and action. \u8220\'20\'1CWe didn\u8217\'20\'19t want to politicize it, because this is a healthcare issue that\u8217\'20\'19s been politicized, but it should be a nonpartisan issue,\u8221\'20\'1D said Perrault. \u8220\'20\'1CWe really just wanted to present what\u8217\'20\'19s happening to people under these laws and let the audience take that in on a very human, intimate, personal level from each of these women.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 For the participants \u8211\'20\'13 Zurawski, Dennard, Casiano, along with their partners and families \u8211\'20\'13 \u8220\'20\'1Cthe horror of what they had to experience and the impact of that on their families and their own reproductive journeys was evident throughout the film-making process,\u8221\'20\'1D said Crow. \u8220\'20\'1CBut we also were just incredibly in awe of their bravery and their willingness to stand up for women across the country in filing this lawsuit and taking the stand to share their testimony.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The state, ultimately, did not listen to them. On 31 May, the Texas supreme court unanimously {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/may/31/texas-abortion-ban-medical-exceptions-supreme-court-ruling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rejected}}}\uc2  the women\u8217\'20\'19s legal challenge, declaring that the state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban could stand as is, without clarifying when medically necessary abortions can be performed. The decision barely mentioned the women. \u8220\'20\'1CTo know that people in the position of power feel that pregnant individuals should simply be vessels,\u8221\'20\'1D says Dennard in the film. \u8220\'20\'1CWe just have to keep fighting, and we have to keep talking about it, and we have to keep telling our stories, without fear.\u8221\'20\'1D The film will be released directly for home viewing to do just that, as an explicitly urgent call to understand the stakes of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans that many who do not experience them first hand do not understand. \u8220\'20\'1CTwenty-five million women in America live under bans like this,\u8221\'20\'1D said Perrault. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not only these women, it\u8217\'20\'19s also their partners and their children and their parents. Understanding that this issue could affect you in your lifetime is so key.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The landscape for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  captured in Zurawski v Texas is staggeringly bleak, even as similar lawsuits in other four other states with near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, including Idaho and Tennessee, remain in legal limbo. But Duane, along with Perrault and Crow, see power in what they witnessed with the lawsuit: people \u8211\'20\'13 such as Zurawski\u8217\'20\'19s conservative family members \u8211\'20\'13 changing their minds and their votes through hearing the women\u8217\'20\'19s experiences.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing now that we didn\u8217\'20\'19t see pre-Dobbs is that families are talking about this,\u8221\'20\'1D said Crow. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s going to take a lot of time before \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is accessible again. But in a pre-Dobbs era, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not accessible to so many people who needed it to begin with. If there is a restructuring of how \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  becomes more accessible, I think that that\u8217\'20\'19s something to be hopeful towards.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Zurawski v Texas is out now in New York and Los Angeles with more cities to follow\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241027ekar000gq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
