\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 This race could affect Pennsylvanians more than the presidential election. Can Democrats win?\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Stein in Bethel Park, Pennsylvania \par \uc2 1,424 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 October 2024\par 01:06 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nicole Ruscitto, Democratic challenger of Republican incumbent Delvin Robinson, hopes to flip the state senate\par \par \uc2 In a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/pennsylvania"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Pennsylvania}}}\uc2  suburb whose voters are coveted by both {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/kamala-harris"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Kamala Harris}}}\uc2 , Nicole Ruscitto walked from house to house on a gloomy Tuesday afternoon, informing residents that there is another important race in November.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m Nicole, I\u8217\'20\'19m running for state senate in your district,\u8221\'20\'1D she told voters on the doorsteps of their red brick houses in Bethel Park, a town about 30 minutes from downtown Pittsburgh, which, if yard signs and election results are any gauge, is about evenly divided between Trump and Harris supporters. \u8220\'20\'1CI want to go to Harrisburg to help our families.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In a swing state that\u8217\'20\'19s considered by both Trump and Harris as perhaps the most important to deciding the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/us-elections-2024"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 presidential election}}}\uc2 , Ruscitto is running for an office that receives less attention than the occupant of White House or members of Congress, yet may have far more impact on the day-to-day life of Pennsylvanians.\par \par \uc2 For three decades, Democrats have been locked out of power in the state\u8217\'20\'19s general assembly. On 5 November, the party is hoping the elections of Ruscitto, a school teacher and former town councilmember, and three other candidates to the state senate will change that.\par \par \uc2 Should they wind up with control of the senate and the house of representatives \u8211\'20\'13 the party\u8217\'20\'19s majority in the latter is just one seat \u8211\'20\'13 Democrats will finally be able to send {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/aug/02/josh-shapiro-kamala-harris"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Josh Shapiro}}}\uc2 , the Pennsylvania governor, a host of legislation that Republicans are currently blocking, ranging from increasing the minimum wage to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf we could have that trifecta, Governor Shapiro would be able to do so many great things for the people here in Pennsylvania, and I plan on doing that 100%,\u8221\'20\'1D Ruscitto said in an interview at her campaign office.\par \par \uc2 While many Americans are fixated on the presidential election, there are 5,808 legislative seats in 44 states up for grabs in November, according to the National Conference of State Legislatures, and voters\u8217\'20\'19 choices may be more impactful than ever before. State governments across the United States have in recent years picked up the policymaking slack from Washington DC, where partisan gridlock and uncooperative Congresses have meant successive presidents have failed to enact many of their campaign promises.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are at a natural disadvantage when it comes to the geography of a state like Pennsylvania, because they waste more votes\par \par \uc2 John J Kennedy, political science professor\par \par \uc2 The results can be seen in the stark differences between laws in red and blue states.\par \par \uc2 Where Democrats rule, governors have enacted laws to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, cut down on emissions tied to climate change, curb gun violence and streamline the process to cast ballots. Republican-led states, meanwhile, have banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to varying degrees, targeted gender-affirming care for transgender youths, restricted cities from passing gun control measures and expanded the role of religion in public education.\par \par \uc2 Many states have legislatures and governor\u8217\'20\'19s mansions controlled by the same party. A smaller group of states, including Wisconsin, Kansas and North Carolina, have governors of one party and legislatures controlled by the other. Pennsylvania is the only state in the union where the two houses of the general assembly are held by different parties.\par \par \uc2 Considered a part of Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 \u8220\'20\'1Cblue wall\u8221\'20\'1D along the Great Lakes, the Keystone state has supported the party\u8217\'20\'19s presidential candidates in most recent elections, though this year\u8217\'20\'19s polls show Trump and Harris essentially tied. Democrats have also seen victories at the state level with the election of Shapiro and the US senator \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  in 2022.\par \par \uc2 But control of the general assembly has eluded them since 1994. John J Kennedy, a political science professor at West Chester University and an expert on the state legislature, credited that to a push by Republicans to draw district maps in their favor, as well as Democratic voters\u8217\'20\'19 tendency to be clustered in states\u8217\'20\'19 urban areas.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDemocrats are at a natural disadvantage when it comes to the geography of a state like Pennsylvania, because they waste more votes,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CDemocrats are so concentrated, they\u8217\'20\'19re at a sort of a natural disadvantage when it comes to accumulating a majority of seats.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The tide began to turn two years ago, when Democrats barely took control of the house in midterm elections that saw the party perform far better than expected nationwide, fueled by voters\u8217\'20\'19 outrage at the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  for overturning Roe v Wade. But Republican control of the state senate has meant many of their legislative ambitions \u8211\'20\'13 including {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pahouse.com/InTheNews/NewsRelease/?id=131520"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a bill}}}\uc2  intended to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  seekers in the state, where the procedure is legal up until about 24 weeks of pregnancy \u8211\'20\'13 have gone nowhere.\par \par \uc2 Vincent Hughes, a Democratic senator who is the campaign chair of the Pennsylvania senate Democratic campaign committee, said he believes more voters are aware of the importance of legislative races, citing Trump\u8217\'20\'19s attempts to get Republicans in Pennsylvania and other swing states to go along with his plan to block {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/joebiden"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Joe Biden}}}\uc2  from taking office in 2020.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat has happened is that the importance of state legislators nationally has become much clearer in the last four or five years, and I think that will lead to more folks getting more engaged in down-ballot races at the state legislative level,\u8221\'20\'1D he said in an interview.\par \par \uc2 Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 hopes for a majority hinge on winning purple districts around the city of Erie, the state capital Harrisburg, and two in Pittsburgh\u8217\'20\'19s suburbs \u8211\'20\'13 including the one in its western outskirts where Ruscitto is running against incumbent Devlin Robinson. A Marine Corps veteran and businessman, Robinson unseated a Democratic officeholder four years ago and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.inquirer.com/politics/election/spl/electoral-college-certification-pennsylvania-senate-letter-20210106.html#loaded"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 promptly signed}}}\uc2  a letter, along with many of his colleagues, encouraging the top Republicans in Congress to delay certifying Pennsylvania\u8217\'20\'19s election results as part of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2020/dec/08/us-supreme-court-republican-pennsylvania-election-results-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 disproven campaign}}}\uc2  of election denialism.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Don\u8217\'20\'19t miss important US election coverage. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apps.apple.com/us/app/the-guardian-live-world-news/id409128287?mt=8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Get our free app}}}\uc2  and sign up for election alerts\par \par \uc2 Ruscitto hopes her emphasis on cost-of-living issues and personal experience as a teacher, as well as attacks on Robinson for voting for a state constitutional amendment that could be used to curb \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, will give her an edge.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have the lowest minimum wage, and it sits in our state senate, and it\u8217\'20\'19s not getting passed. And, to me, that\u8217\'20\'19s absurd,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Residents of the district say Robinson has been stepping up his outreach to constituents as election day approaches. Joyce Reinoso, a retired teacher and campaigner for candidates who backs organized labor and public education, said he also has the power of incumbency.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t care what party, it\u8217\'20\'19s always harder to beat the incumbent because the name recognition, if nothing else,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 The Ruscitto campaign\u8217\'20\'19s internal polling has found her leading Robinson by a mere two percentage points, within their survey\u8217\'20\'19s margin of error. This week, the \uc2 University of Virginia\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s center for politics {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://centerforpolitics.org/crystalball/the-state-legislatures-several-key-battleground-chambers-remain-toss-ups/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rated}}}\uc2  Pennsylvania\u8217\'20\'19s house as a toss-up, but said the GOP has the edge in keeping the senate.\par \par \uc2 In Bethel Park, which was briefly thrust into the national spotlight in July when {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/jul/15/trump-rally-shooter-bethel-park-pennsylvania"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a man from the town}}}\uc2  tried to kill the former president, houses with Trump yards signs and flags sit across the street from those backing Harris, and the two candidates\u8217\'20\'19 ads are ubiquitous online and on television. But signs for Ruscitto and Robinson are relatively scarce \u8211\'20\'13 as are strong opinions.\par \par \uc2 As she sat down for an early dinner at Ma and Pop\u8217\'20\'19s Country Kitchen, Sandy Messiner, a retired bookkeeper, expressed no doubts about voting for Trump again.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf Trump gets in, my investments will go up. We need a businessman to run this country,\u8221\'20\'1D the 70-year-old said. And though she knew less about them, she planned to vote for all the other Republicans on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t care who gets in as long as they\u8217\'20\'19re Republican.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Sitting at the other end of the counter was Pam Cirucci, an 83-year-old retired nurse who was sure she would not be voting for Trump, because \u8220\'20\'1Che doesn\u8217\'20\'19t respect females\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 A former Republican, Cirucci was less concerned with who controlled the legislature \u8211\'20\'13 or what the lawmakers in Harrisburg were up to at all.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are so many things that are more important,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241024ekao0028l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
