\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 The Maga legal networks that could topple \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  and gut women\u8217\'20\'19s healthcare\b0\par\par\uc2 Melissa Segura \par \uc2 5,621 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 October 2024\par 22:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Revealed: outcome of coming election may determine staying power of hard-right activist \uc2 Leonard Leo\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s crusade to reshape US courts\par \par \uc2 In the second year of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s presidency, a young lawyer with crisply shorn blond hair approached the podium at a gathering for Texas members of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=f-_5bPKuIRg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Federalist Society}}}\uc2 , a conservative legal group that wields immense power in the US judicial system. As vice-president of the group\u8217\'20\'19s Fort Worth chapter, Matthew Kacsmaryk had the honor of presenting the first speaker.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are blessed to have Judge Edith Jones,\u8221\'20\'1D Kacsmaryk announced. Jones, a longtime judge on the US fifth circuit court of appeals, stepped on stage to introduce the evening\u8217\'20\'19s guest, her friend, the supreme court justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 . In her introduction, Jones also hailed the four new conservative judges Trump had appointed to join her on the appeals court.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19ve raised the bar for the fifth circuit since I got on,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd that\u8217\'20\'19s thanks to the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2 , to Leonard.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Leonard Leo\uc2  needed no last name in his introduction to this crowd as he took his seat in a black leather chair across from Thomas. The justice was the featured speaker but Leo may have been the most important person in the American legal system in that room \u8211\'20\'13 a conservative activist who had built the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2  into a political powerhouse and helped Trump create the supreme court majority that, in 2022, erased federal protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 His influence continues to be on display now in one of the most consequential cases moving through the American legal system \u8211\'20\'13 one that seeks to strike another blow to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and could possibly bankrupt \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , one of the nation\u8217\'20\'19s leading providers of healthcare for women. It\u8217\'20\'19s a lawsuit that has been filed by an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activist tied to Leo and heard by judges \u8211\'20\'13 from the lower courts to the fifth circuit appeals court \u8211\'20\'13 who are also linked to Leo.\par \par \uc2 Three of the people on the stage at the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2  event in Fort Worth in 2018 \u8211\'20\'13 Kacsmaryk, Jones and Leo \u8211\'20\'13 have all played key roles in the case. Though the stakes in this case couldn\u8217\'20\'19t be higher for one of the nation\u8217\'20\'19s oldest healthcare providers, it is about more than \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or healthcare. The lawsuit is a parable about Leo\u8217\'20\'19s power amid a presidential election season whose outcome will probably determine to what extent Leo will continue to reshape the makeup and ideology of the nation\u8217\'20\'19s courts.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 The case was filed in February 2021 by an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activist who had conducted what he described as an \u8220\'20\'1Cextensive undercover investigation\u8221\'20\'1D of the organization. He accuses \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  of fraud \u8211\'20\'13 claiming that it owes $1.8bn in fines, fees and reimbursements to the Medicaid program. It\u8217\'20\'19s an amount that could force the 108-year-old non-profit healthcare provider to shutter clinics across the country.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit is titled USA v \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  because it was filed under a federal whistleblower law that allows citizens to sue on behalf of the \uc2 US government\uc2  over allegations that federal programs have been defrauded. It is the latest in a series of legal actions that started in 2015 after Texas\u8217\'20\'19s health officials used footage from the activist\u8217\'20\'19s hidden-camera recordings as a basis to expel \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  from the state\u8217\'20\'19s Medicaid program.\par \par \uc2 The activist and his allies claimed the videos showed \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  was illegally selling fetal tissue and endangering pregnant people\u8217\'20\'19s health.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  repeatedly denied wrongdoing. Investigations in multiple states triggered by the video resulted in no disciplinary action against the healthcare provider.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US government\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 the source of 90% of the Medicaid funds paid to \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  clinics in Texas \u8211\'20\'13 disputes that \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  owes the federal government money. Federal officials say in a court filing that they found no evidence that \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  had improperly billed for its services and that they found no reason \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  should have been removed from Medicaid. Experts in healthcare law expected the case to be dismissed quickly.\par \par \uc2 Yet none of these facts are as important to understanding the significance of this case as knowing where it was filed: in the federal courthouse in Amarillo, Texas \u8211\'20\'13 home to zero Planned Parenthood clinics.\par \par \uc2 This wasn\u8217\'20\'19t an accident.\par \par \uc2 The US district court in Amarillo is under the purview of the US fifth circuit court of appeals, making it likely that any upward appeals in \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s case would be heard in the hard-right appeals court, including by judges appointed by Trump or other conservative stalwarts like Jones. And by the time the \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  case was filed, Kacsmaryk \u8211\'20\'13 that young attorney on stage making introductions at the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2  event in Texas \u8211\'20\'13 had been serving as the federal district court judge for Amarillo for nearly two years. Kacsmaryk had been appointed by Trump, who had famously consulted Leo in picking judges for federal appointments.\par \par \uc2 Kacsmaryk was the only judge in the Amarillo court district, and all but guaranteed to hear the \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  case.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of court files, tax records and judges\u8217\'20\'19 financial disclosure forms reviewed by the Guardian show that Kacsmaryk and most of the other key players in the case to topple \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  are tied to Leo via a vast network of non-profit and for-profit organizations involved in the fight against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or other conservative causes.\par \par \uc2 Tax records analyzed by the Guardian show that a conservative legal non-profit where Kacsmaryk worked before he was appointed to the bench made at least three payments totaling more than $350,000 to a public relations firm, CRC Advisors, that Leo acquired in early 2020.\par \par \uc2 That conservative legal group, \uc2 First Liberty Institute\uc2 , made a payment of $137,000 before Kacsmaryk was confirmed as a judge in mid-2019 and before Leo took over the PR firm, tax records show. The next two payments \u8211\'20\'13 totaling more than $230,000 \u8211\'20\'13 were made after Kacsmaryk was sworn in as a district judge, the records show. At least one of those payments was made after Leo had taken over the PR firm, according to \uc2 First Liberty\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s tax filings for 2020 and 2021.\par \par \uc2 First Liberty\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s general counsel Hiram Sasser said his organization has worked with CRC Advisors at least dating back to 2015, long before Leo took over the company. Sasser told the Guardian he had never worked with Leo and that Leo\u8217\'20\'19s business partner, Greg Mueller, was the organization\u8217\'20\'19s point of contact.\par \par \uc2 Records also revealed that \uc2 First Liberty\uc2  had {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://projects.propublica.org/nonprofits/organizations/751403169/202421149349301027/full"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 paid}}}\uc2  the plaintiff\u8217\'20\'19s attorneys who filed USA v Planned Parenthood, Heather Hacker and Andrew Stephens, more than $500,000 in 2023 in consulting fees. \uc2 First Liberty\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Sasser said the payment was for the attorneys\u8217\'20\'19 work in another, unrelated case.\par \par \uc2 In other words, Kacsmaryk is ruling in a case in which the attorneys arguing in front of him have received substantial payments from his former employer. \uc2 First Liberty\uc2  also employs the spouse of one of the plaintiff\u8217\'20\'19s attorneys.\par \par \uc2 Hacker and Stephens did not respond to multiple attempts to reach them for comment.\par \par \uc2 Some legal ethicists and court watchdog organizations contacted by the Guardian say that this case is another example of the ambiguities of the judiciary\u8217\'20\'19s rules about conflict of interest and recusal rules.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/455"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rules}}}\uc2  require that a judge \u8220\'20\'1Cshall disqualify himself in any {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.law.cornell.edu/definitions/uscode.php?width=840&height=800&iframe=true&def_id=28-USC-408818804-1019605827&term_occur=999&term_src="}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 proceeding}}}\uc2  in which his impartiality might reasonably be questioned\u8221\'20\'1D. But it is unclear whether any of the connections between \uc2 First Liberty\uc2 , Leo and the attorneys on this case meet that threshold.\par \par \uc2 This case \u8220\'20\'1Craises the shortcomings in the recusal law when the ties that exist in this case seem fairly obviously to impugn and implicate the bias of the judge who\u8217\'20\'19s hearing the case\u8221\'20\'1D, said Gabe Roth, executive director of Fix the Court, a nonpartisan organization that advocates for increased transparency and accountability within the court system.\par \par \uc2 Kacsmaryk did not respond to a detailed list of questions provided to him through a court spokesperson. Leo did not reply to most questions sent to him by the Guardian but a spokesperson for him said: \u8220\'20\'1CMr Leo has not spoken to Matthew Kacsmaryk since a public encounter in 2019.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Records also show that Leo, a lawyer, provided key legal advice to the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activist suing \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Through generous grants he has provided to law schools and legal organizations, Leo has also helped to underwrite luxury trips to judicial conferences for judges on the fifth circuit court of appeals who have ruled on the \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  case.\par \par \uc2 When nearly every person on one side of a legal case and the judge are connected politically or ideologically, it is called court capture \u8211\'20\'13 a term some legal scholars use to describe USA v \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a captured court, specific judges, lawyers and even plaintiffs align to produce a specific outcome of a case \u8211\'20\'13 often an outcome that supports beliefs or preferences that tie each of these legal actors together.\par \par \uc2 The shared ideology in this case is ending \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a lawsuit that has no merit, but somehow it\u8217\'20\'19s progressing anyway,\u8221\'20\'1D said Nicole Huberfeld, the chair of the health law program at \uc2 Boston University\uc2  and the co-director of the university\u8217\'20\'19s program on reproductive justice. \u8220\'20\'1CIt seems to me an attempt to try to limit the activities of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  clinics in Texas and Louisiana by any means possible.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The presidential election in November will determine who will nominate the next generation of judges to the federal courts: the former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , whose nominees to the supreme court played a key role in overturning Roe v Wade \u8211\'20\'13 or the vice-president, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , who has staked much of her presidential campaign on restoring \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights nationwide.\par \par \uc2 There are currently 44 federal {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.uscourts.gov/judges-judgeships/judicial-vacancies/future-judicial-vacancies"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 judgeships vacant}}}\uc2 , with another 23 judges announcing their plans to step down from the bench.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 Lena Zwarensteyn, the fair courts campaign director for the \uc2 Leadership Conference on Civil and Human Rights\uc2 , describes court capture as a \u8220\'20\'1Cmanufactured and coordinated effort determined to produce specific outcomes\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Those manufactured efforts, she said, are \u8220\'20\'1Cjeopardizing the health of pregnant people, jeopardizing even access to contraception, jeopardizing the government\u8217\'20\'19s ability to set forth and enforce civil and human rights protection for access to clean air and water\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Zwarensteyn said the right\u8217\'20\'19s focus on the courts was a reaction to major wins by the civil rights movement in the 1960s and early 70s, including victories expanding voting rights.\par \par \uc2 Groups like the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 an organization dedicated to reforming the court \u8220\'20\'1Cto place a premium on individual liberty, traditional values, and the rule of law\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 created a pipeline of legal talent ready to fill federal judgeships. In 1986, as a young law student at Cornell Law School, Leo founded a chapter of the group. He eventually ascended the ranks of the organization to become co-chair of its board of directors and build a network of conservative lawyers and law students throughout the country who could be candidates for judgeships throughout the federal system.\par \par \uc2 After Trump was elected president in 2016, he {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/oct/23/leonard-leo-us-judiciary-wisconsin-north-carolina"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 leaned}}}\uc2  on a list of judicial nominees provided by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/oct/23/leonard-leo-us-judiciary-wisconsin-north-carolina"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Leo}}}\uc2  to fill vacancies, stacking the courts with conservative judges from the district level, to the appellate courts and the supreme court. Trump named {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.uscourts.gov/sites/default/files/apptsbypres.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 245 federal judges}}}\uc2  to the bench in his one term, compared with 334 over \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s eight years in office. Trump ultimately appointed six hard-right judges to the fifth circuit appeals court along with appointing 28 judges to district courts in Texas, Louisiana and Mississippi that are under the fifth circuit\u8217\'20\'19s umbrella.\par \par \uc2 The six circuit court appointments helped create an appeals court that is even more Maga than the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 , which itself has been widely condemned for destroying federal protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and other decisions favored by Trump and his supporters. In its most recent term, the supreme court overruled eight of the 11 fifth circuit decisions it took up for consideration \u8211\'20\'13 including reversing rulings by the district judge Kacsmaryk and the fifth circuit appeals court that sought to limit access to mifepristone, a drug used in medication abortions. The fifth circuit\u8217\'20\'19s reversals by the high court were the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theatlantic.com/ideas/archive/2024/07/how-fifth-circuit-won-losing/678918/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 most for any circuit}}}\uc2  for the second year in a row.\par \par \uc2 While much public attention is focused on the nominations of supreme court justices and the 100 to 150 cases the high court takes up each year, the workings of the fifth circuit \u8211\'20\'13 and the roughly 5,000 cases it decides each year \u8211\'20\'13 get far less scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 The John Minor Wisdom courthouse in downtown New Orleans where the the fifth circuit is headquartered has become the preferred venue for much of the conservative movement \u8211\'20\'13 so much so that businesses and advocates outside the fifth circuit\u8217\'20\'19s jurisdiction have filed paperwork to incorporate an organization in Texas that only exists on paper or recruited plaintiffs who are tangential to a case but reside within the court\u8217\'20\'19s boundaries.\par \par \uc2 One Trump-appointed district court judge, frustrated by the US chamber of commerce filing a case in Texas by recruiting a Texas bank as a plaintiff even though most of the major players in the suit were in Washington DC, wrote that the \u8220\'20\'1Cvenue is not a continental breakfast; you cannot pick and choose on a Plaintiff\u8217\'20\'19s whim where and how a lawsuit is filed\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 District and appeals court judges in the fifth circuit are well known to big businesses trying to skirt financial and environmental regulations, political operatives looking to dilute the voting power of marginalized communities, anti-immigration advocates looking to undercut asylum law and anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists seeking to limit legal access to reproductive care \u8211\'20\'13 as in the case in Amarillo that attempts to force \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  to pay out more than $1.8bn.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 The origins of USA v \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  trace back to a conservative activist from California named David Daleiden who described his anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions as his birthright. In media {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/22/us/with-planned-parenthood-videos-activist-ignites-abortion-issue.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 interviews}}}\uc2  he {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.c-span.org/video/?403139-4/planned-parenthood-videos"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 described himself}}}\uc2  as a \u8220\'20\'1Cchild of a crisis pregnancy\u8221\'20\'1D and said \u8220\'20\'1Cthere were people who said I should be aborted\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Daleiden formed an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group at his high school. In college he co-authored an article about the emotional toll of abortions on physicians performing them.\par \par \uc2 By 2013, when was in his mid-20s, he founded a non-profit called the Center for Medical Progress with the mission of using undercover journalism to \u8220\'20\'1Cactively oppose the exploitation of any class of human beings, whether born or unborn\u8221\'20\'1D, according to its tax filings.\par \par \uc2 In a draft proposal for a hidden-camera investigation of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , Daleiden crafted a plan for a \u8220\'20\'1Ccutting edge expos\u233\'00\'E9 of the commercial exploitation of aborted fetuses\u8221\'20\'1D using \u8220\'20\'1Cgotcha undercover videos\u8221\'20\'1D that would show doctors \u8220\'20\'1Ccannibalizing the young and playing God\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 By 2014, he was secretly recording healthcare providers discussing fetal tissue procedures at \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  conferences in California and Maryland.\par \par \uc2 Though the Center for Medical Progress listed Daleiden as its only employee on its tax forms, he had help from Leo and other conservative activists during his undercover investigation in 2014 and 2015. In an affidavit, Daleiden said he consulted Leo \u8220\'20\'1Cabout how to ensure successful prosecutions of various entities illegally profiting from the sale of fetal tissue\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 To handle public relations for his group, Daleiden hired Leo\u8217\'20\'19s business partner, Mueller, a political consultant and messaging specialist.\par \par \uc2 Daleiden did not reply to multiple emails and phone messages seeking comment for this story.\par \par \uc2 In April 2015, Daleiden talked his way into a tour of a \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  office in Houston. He and another activist posed as representatives of a company that specialized in procuring fetal tissue for researchers. Scientists use the tissue to try to develop cures for diseases such as Alzheimer\u8217\'20\'19s and cancer. The advocates surreptitiously recorded their conversations with Melissa Farrell, a Planned Parenthood research coordinator, and a physician with the clinic.\par \par \uc2 Daleiden asked Farrell about how the tissue would be treated and preserved. Farrell gave a rambling, sometimes confusing answer, telling Daleiden the Planned Parenthood Gulf Coast affiliate had extensive experience in working with researchers to meet their needs.\par \par \uc2 At one point, she told Daleiden that in meeting the needs of researchers, there were times when \u8220\'20\'1Cwe deviate from our standards\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Daleiden saw that phrase and other parts of the recordings as evidence that \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  was violating federal laws governing the collection of fetal tissue.\par \par \uc2 Farrell later explained in a sworn statement that she meant clinic procedures \u8211\'20\'13 not medical procedures \u8211\'20\'13 could be altered to minimize disruption at the clinic and \u8220\'20\'1Cwould never be done in a way that involves suboptimal care for patients\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In July and August 2015, Daleiden shared the videos with state officials in Texas and released them publicly. He went on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HzMx4ieyF64"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Fox News}}}\uc2  to accuse \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  of \u8220\'20\'1Charvesting and selling\u8221\'20\'1D baby parts.\par \par \uc2 The officials who received the videos included \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , the state attorney general, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://georgewbush-whitehouse.archives.gov/results/leadership/bios/bowens.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Stuart Bowen}}}\uc2 , the then inspector general for the state\u8217\'20\'19s health and human services department. Bowen was a longtime Republican operative in Texas who had worked for \uc2 George W Bush\uc2  when Bush was governor and later president.\par \par \uc2 Bowen later testified that he watched the roughly eight hours of video Daleiden provided five times. He determined that Planned Parenthood Gulf Coast did not follow mandatory safety requirements for handling fetal tissue and that the clinic violated medical standards by agreeing to alter the method or timing of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in order to obtain fetal tissue.\par \par \uc2 Federal law prohibits changing the time or manner of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the purposes of collecting fetal tissue. The idea behind these provisions is to ensure the pregnant person\u8217\'20\'19s wellbeing is prioritized over the researcher\u8217\'20\'19s interest in getting tissue samples.\par \par \uc2 Bowen sought a second opinion from his agency\u8217\'20\'19s chief medical officer \u8211\'20\'13 an orthopedic surgeon who admitted he was not familiar with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedures \u8211\'20\'13 before concluding that the state\u8217\'20\'19s Planned Parenthood clinics were \u8220\'20\'1Cno longer capable of performing medical services in a professionally competent, safe, legal, and ethical manner\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Bowen\u8217\'20\'19s office sent a letter in October 2015 terminating the \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  clinic in Houston, where the video was filmed, from receiving funds though the Texas Medicaid program. The same termination letter also went to \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  chapters based in Dallas and San Antonio \u8211\'20\'13 which oversee dozens of clinics across the state that had not been included in the undercover videos and were not involved in fetal tissue donations.\par \par \uc2 Bowen\u8217\'20\'19s decision meant that roughly 11,000 Texas women enrolled in Medicaid would have to seek care somewhere other than \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 not only for abortions but also for breast cancer screenings, wellness exams and other services provided by the clinics.\par \par \uc2 The clinics with headquarters in Houston, Dallas and San Antonio operate under the \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  name and follow marketing, legal and safety requirements set by the national federation, but say they are separate entities, each with its own board of directors and leadership teams.\par \par \uc2 Bowen did not return multiple messages seeking comment.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  affiliates in Texas, with help from lawyers from the national federation, responded to Bowen\u8217\'20\'19s edict by filing a lawsuit in federal court based in Austin. Along with the affiliates, the suit listed 11 Jane Doe patients as plaintiffs. The suit \u8211\'20\'13 Planned Parenthood v Smith \u8211\'20\'13 charged that these Medicaid patients were being denied the right, under federal law, to choose a qualified medical provider.\par \par \uc2 In January 2017, the US district court judge Sam Sparks convened a hearing to determine whether \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  should be allowed to remain in the Medicaid program while the case was pending.\par \par \uc2 Sparks, a Republican appointed to the bench in 1991 by George HW Bush, listened to three days of testimony from Bowen, \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  employees and other witnesses.\par \par \uc2 In a 42-page opinion, Sparks wrote that the secretly recorded videos and aliases used by the film-makers gave the case the feel of a \u8220\'20\'1Cbest-selling novel rather than a case involving the interplay of federal and state authority through the Medicaid program. Yet rather than a villain plotting to take over the world, the subject of this case is the State of Texas\u8217\'20\'19s efforts to expel a group of health care providers from a social health care program for families and individuals with limited resources.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He summarized Daleiden\u8217\'20\'19s video as \u8220\'20\'1Cnothing more than confused and ambiguous dialogue\u8221\'20\'1D. The Planned Parenthood employee who commented about deviating from the organization\u8217\'20\'19s standard shifted between talking about administrative and medical processes, making the comments \u8220\'20\'1Copen to interpretation\u8221\'20\'1D, the judge wrote.\par \par \uc2 All that was certain, according to Sparks, was that Bowen and his department did not have \u8220\'20\'1Ceven a scintilla of evidence\u8221\'20\'1D to prove \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  had done anything wrong.\par \par \uc2 Sparks granted \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  an injunction that allowed the Texas clinics to continue providing care to Medicaid patients \u8211\'20\'13 at least until the entire case could be heard.\par \par \uc2 Lawyers for the state of Texas did not give Sparks a chance to hold a full hearing of the case.\par \par \uc2 Instead, they pushed the case to a higher authority: the fifth circuit court of appeals.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 In June 2019, about a year after the fifth circuit judge Edith Jones took the microphone to introduce Leo and the supreme court justice Thomas at the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2  event in Fort Worth, she and two other appeals judges took the bench to hear the state of Texas argue that Sparks was wrong in granting \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  an injunction.\par \par \uc2 Jones, who had been appointed to the fifth circuit by Ronald Reagan in 1985, had stirred some controversies during her more than three decades on the court. In 2013, attendees at a talk she gave at a \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2  event in Pennsylvania alleged she told the crowd that Black and Latino people were more \u8220\'20\'1Cprone\u8221\'20\'1D to commit acts of violence.\par \par \uc2 An investigation led by the future US attorney general \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  determined there was insufficient evidence to establish whether Jones had violated any rules or substantiate what she had told the crowd.\par \par \uc2 Hacker, who at the time was serving as a lawyer for the state attorney general\u8217\'20\'19s office, told the three judges that Sparks had erred in determining that there wasn\u8217\'20\'19t evidence that \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  had ever sold any fetal tissue or broken any laws in obtaining it. He should have looked at the case strictly from an administrative perspective and how the policies and procedures allow the state of Texas to control its Medicaid program, Hacker said.\par \par \uc2 As Planned Parenthood\u8217\'20\'19s attorney, Jennifer Sandman, tried to lay out why Sparks\u8217\'20\'19s ruling was sound, Jones interrupted her with an example that seemed far afield from the dispute over fetal tissue and Medicaid payments.\par \par \uc2 She brought up a Philadelphia doctor convicted of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2013/may/15/philadelphia-abortion-doctor-kermit-gosnell-sentenced-life"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 first-degree murder }}}\uc2  for killing three babies born alive by stabbing them with scissors.\par \par \uc2 Using the Philadelphia doctor as an example, Jones asked if a state could ever be allowed to make a judgment to expel a provider.\par \par \uc2 Then she switched gears and described a \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  provider on one of the undercover videos who \u8220\'20\'1Cgiggled\u8221\'20\'1D as she discussed procuring fetal tissues.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYour honor, I would deeply disagree with that characterization,\u8221\'20\'1D Sandman said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CShe definitely smirked,\u8221\'20\'1D Jones shot back.\par \par \uc2 Six months after the hearing, the panel issued its ruling. The decision, written by Jones, acknowledged that Judge Sparks had not found any evidence of wrongdoing by the Planned Parenthood Gulf Coast employees captured on video, but complained that the judge had accepted \u8220\'20\'1Cself-serving\u8221\'20\'1D testimony from \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s witnesses while \u8220\'20\'1Crefusing to consider\u8221\'20\'1D evidence from Bowen\u8217\'20\'19s office.\par \par \uc2 The case wasn\u8217\'20\'19t about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , she wrote, \u8220\'20\'1Cdespite being litigated with the trappings of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate, this is fundamentally a statutory construction case, not an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case\u8221\'20\'1D. Twenty-six pages earlier in her opinion, she included a screengrab from Daleiden\u8217\'20\'19s video that showed fetal parts \u8211\'20\'13 with a tiny finger and partial limb visible swimming in a clear, glass baking dish.\par \par \uc2 Through a court spokesperson, Jones declined to comment for this story.\par \par \uc2 Jones and the other two judges on the panel voted 3-0 to vacate the injunction that allowed \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  to remain in Medicaid. Jones\u8217\'20\'19s opinion also called for the entire fifth circuit \u8211\'20\'13 not just a three-judge panel \u8211\'20\'13 to consider an issue that Texas\u8217\'20\'19s attorney, Hacker, had raised in her argument. Did the women receiving care at \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  even have a right to sue over the state\u8217\'20\'19s decision to block the healthcare provider from Medicaid?\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 By 2020, Trump, with help from Leo, has pushed the fifth circuit court of appeals from a conservative court to a far-right bastion. All six of the judges that Trump appointed to the appeals court had claimed membership in Leo\u8217\'20\'19s Federalist Society.\par \par \uc2 One of them, James Ho, wrote on a questionnaire during his confirmation process that he had not missed the group\u8217\'20\'19s annual conference in 20 years and had met his future wife at a gathering of the group.\par \par \uc2 Four of the Trump appointees also accepted trips to legal conferences underwritten by the law school at \uc2 George Mason University\uc2 , to which Leo was a regular and generous contributor.\par \par \uc2 Most of the judges appointed to the fifth circuit by Trump also had ties to conservative politics or politicians. Ho served in the George W Bush White House in the Office of Legal Counsel and as solicitor general of Texas, a post in which he fought against same-sex marriage. Stuart Kyle Duncan had also come out of the Texas solicitor general\u8217\'20\'19s office known as a laboratory in which to test ultra conservative legal strategies in cases involving transgender rights, voting rights, and same-sex marriage. \uc2 Don Willett\uc2  worked on the 2000 Bush-Cheney presidential campaign and transition team before landing on the Texas state supreme court.\par \par \uc2 All six came to the court with avowed anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stances.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 Appeals from district court cases to federal circuit courts generally are decided by a three-judge panel selected from the larger pool of appellate judges within the circuit court \u8211\'20\'13 like the panel led by Jones in 2019 that ended \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s bid to remain in the Medicaid program while the case was pending. But rulings by these three-judge panels can be reviewed by the full contingent of appellate judges in that circuit.\par \par \uc2 In November 2020, four of the Trump-appointed judges were among the 16 fifth circuit jurists who addressed Jones\u8217\'20\'19s request that the full circuit court determine whether \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s patients had the right to sue in federal court to challenge Texas authorities\u8217\'20\'19 decision to banish the healthcare provider from Medicaid.\par \par \uc2 The lawyers arguing the Medicaid recipients\u8217\'20\'19 claims had a large body of law supporting their position. Six federal circuit courts had previously ruled that Medicaid beneficiaries have a right to sue if they had been denied access to their medical providers \u8211\'20\'13 including a ruling three years earlier by a three-judge panel within the fifth circuit. Only one circuit court had ruled in the other direction.\par \par \uc2 The fifth circuit ruled that the Jane Doe plaintiffs did not have standing to sue in federal court, saying that most other circuits\u8217\'20\'19 reasoning on the issue was \u8220\'20\'1Cdemonstrably incorrect\u8221\'20\'1D. That majority ruling was supported in whole or in part by 14 judges, swamping the two judges who voted against the decision.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s appeals to remain in Texas\u8217\'20\'19s Medicaid program were over.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 For Daleiden, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activist, the appeals court ruling was the first victory after a string of defeats in other venues.\par \par \uc2 Investigations inspired by his videos in at least 13 states did not produce actions against \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  affiliates in their jurisdictions. Probes by the \uc2 Texas Rangers\uc2  and the Harris county district attorney\u8217\'20\'19s office \u8211\'20\'13 whose jurisdiction includes Houston \u8211\'20\'13 also found no wrongdoing by the clinics.\par \par \uc2 Daleiden also suffered repercussions in his home state. In 2016, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , then the California attorney general, sent investigators to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/story/2016/04/california-investigators-search-daleidens-home-221604"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 raid}}}\uc2  Daleiden\u8217\'20\'19s home. That probe provided the evidence used by Harris\u8217\'20\'19s successor, \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2 , to file a 15-count criminal complaint against Daleiden accusing him of illegal eavesdropping and recording people without their consent. His {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sfchronicle.com/politics/article/antiabortion-activists-face-criminal-trial-top-17867476.php"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 criminal trial }}}\uc2  is slated for December. Daleiden has pleaded not guilty.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, a federal judge in San Francisco hit Daleiden and the Center for Medical Progress with a $2m judgment in a civil case filed by the \uc2 National \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Federation\uc2 , ruling he had violated the law by secretly recording \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  employees and other attendees at conferences organized by the NAF around the country.\par \par \uc2 But as the defeats piled up, two members of the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2  advanced a new strategy that tested the strength of the captured courts Leo had spent over two decades building \u8211\'20\'13 and pushed forward Daleiden\u8217\'20\'19s campaign against \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  on a new legal front.\par \par \uc2 Hacker, the attorney who had once represented Texas \u8211\'20\'13 arguing in front of Judge Jones that the Jane Doe patients of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  did not have the standing to sue \u8211\'20\'13 had left the attorney general\u8217\'20\'19s office and entered private practice.\par \par \uc2 She and another former attorney in the attorney general\u8217\'20\'19s office, Stephens, had formed their own private firm. Both were members of the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2 , and have represented organizations that have received funding from Leo\u8217\'20\'19s non-profit network.\par \par \uc2 Through a spokesperson, Leo said he had never met Hacker or Stephens.\par \par \uc2 In 2021, Hacker and Stephens used the US False Claims Act to file a suit in Kacsmaryk\u8217\'20\'19s federal district court in the Texas Panhandle alleging \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  owes more than $1.8bn to the government for the payments it received from the time \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  clinics were first given notice of their termination in 2015 until the fifth circuit ended Sparks\u8217\'20\'19s injunction in 2020.\par \par \uc2 In court records, the person initiating the case is identified by a pseudonym, Alex Doe. But the details described in the lawsuit and other court documents \u8211\'20\'13 including the undercover video sting \u8211\'20\'13 make it clear that Alex Doe is Daleiden.\par \par \uc2 By filing in Amarillo, the lawyers landed Daleiden\u8217\'20\'19s case in a courtroom presided over by a judge who embraces a family legacy of staunch anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  beliefs \u8211\'20\'13 Kacsmaryk\u8217\'20\'19s mother worked at crisis pregnancy centers that pushed alternatives to abortions, and his wife {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2023/04/08/abortion-pill-ruling-judge-matthew-kacsmaryk/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 once posted }}}\uc2  a photo online of one of their children in a T-shirt that read \u8220\'20\'1CI survived Roe v Wade.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Hacker and Stephens\u8217\'20\'19s former boss, Paxton, a member of the Republican Attorneys General Association that receives funding from Leo\u8217\'20\'19s organizations and had appointed Leo to his transition team after taking office, signed on to the lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 Experts interviewed by the Guardian struggled to come up with other examples of when a court ordered an organization to pay back funds it had collected during an injunction.\par \par \uc2 Lawyers for \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  have pointed out that the \uc2 US government\uc2  has repeatedly asserted in court records that \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  should not have been disqualified from the program.\par \par \uc2 The False Claims Act usually applies when healthcare providers bill for services they did not provide.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is just not an appropriate False Claims Act case,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr Abigail R Moncrieff, a professor of health and constitutional law at \uc2 Cleveland State University\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CIf Texas and Louisiana wanted their money back, they should just ask for their money back. But they didn\u8217\'20\'19t.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe whole concept of a whistleblower is that it\u8217\'20\'19s supposed to reveal secret information that the state didn\u8217\'20\'19t know about, and the states here knew about it, and they have not asked for their money back,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Kacsmaryk seems unconvinced. In October 2023 he declined to dismiss the case, writing that \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  clinics \u8220\'20\'1Cwere not entitled to the payments and were obligated to return them at least within 60 days\u8221\'20\'1D of their expulsion, he wrote. \u8220\'20\'1CNo other argument advanced by Defendants convinces the Court otherwise.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Kacsmaryk reached back to a 1919 supreme court decision about injunctions to support his reasoning. That case predated women\u8217\'20\'19s right to vote by nearly a year.\par \par \uc2 The case is on hold in Kacsmaryk\u8217\'20\'19s court as the \uc2 Planned Parenthood Federation of America\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 the administrative headquarters rather than medical providers \u8211\'20\'13 fights Kacsmaryk\u8217\'20\'19s refusal to dismiss the federation from the lawsuit. Since the federation in New York does not provide procedures or bill Medicaid, it should not be subject to Daleiden\u8217\'20\'19s suit, the national group has argued.\par \par \uc2 The appeals court\u8217\'20\'19s decision could come any day. Then the larger questions about whether \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  should have to repay Medicaid will resume in Kacsmaryk\u8217\'20\'19s courtroom. If Kacsmaryk finds in Daleiden\u8217\'20\'19s favor, the activist can collect as much as 30% of the fees and fines imposed on \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On the other side, legal fees keep mounting for the clinics.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEvery dollar, every moment, spent on anything but our mission to provide \u8230\'20\'26 care is a distraction,\u8221\'20\'1D said Laura Terrill, Planned Parenthood South Texas\u8217\'20\'19s chief executive officer.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, as \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s fate depends on the courts, the teen birth rate in Texas rose in 2022 for the first time in 15 years. Infant deaths have increased nearly 13%, compared with a 2% jump nationwide. Researchers from the \uc2 University of Texas\uc2  found that terminating \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  from Medicaid \u8220\'20\'1Cadversely affected low-income residents\u8217\'20\'19 access to essential \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, resulting in delayed care and increases in births\u8221\'20\'1D, many of which researchers identified as \u8220\'20\'1Clikely unintended\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Should Planned Parenthood lose the case in Kacsmaryk\u8217\'20\'19s court, it would find itself in familiar territory again: appealing in front of the fifth circuit court.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241022ekam001s0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
