\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 If Trump wins the election, Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s extreme  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ban could go nationwide\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman in Boise, Idaho \par \uc2 2,361 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 October 2024\par 16:27 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Women and doctors describe heart-wrenching decisions under what may be the US\u8217\'20\'19s strictest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in Idaho. \u8216\'20\'18Everything that\u8217\'20\'19s happening is a playbook for the rest of the country\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 When Jennifer Adkins and her husband were considering having a second child in Idaho, they vaguely thought how the state\u8217\'20\'19s near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban could affect them. But Adkins\u8217\'20\'19 first pregnancy had gone so smoothly, she didn\u8217\'20\'19t even use an epidural when she gave birth. Her next pregnancy, she expected, would be similar.\par \par \uc2 But in April 2023, 12 weeks into her second pregnancy, an ultrasound scan shattered that hope.\par \par \uc2 The ultrasound indicated a litany of issues with the fetus, whom Adkins and her husband had taken to calling \u8220\'20\'1CSpooky\u8221\'20\'1D, since Adkins\u8217\'20\'19 due date was Halloween. Spooky, Adkins learned, showed multiple signs of a chromosomal disorder as well as dangerously severe swelling. Together, these conditions usually lead to a miscarriage or death shortly after delivery, Adkins\u8217\'20\'19 doctors told her. And if the pregnancy continued, Adkins herself was at risk of developing life-threatening pre-eclampsia and swelling.\par \par \uc2 As the mother of a toddler son, Adkins was not willing to take that chance.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think that you understand, until you are in that situation, how scary and how awful that actually is,\u8221\'20\'1D Adkins said during a recent interview at a coffee shop outside Boise.\par \par \uc2 Under Idaho law, abortions are permitted in medical emergencies. But although Adkins\u8217\'20\'19 health was in jeopardy, her doctors didn\u8217\'20\'19t believe her case was enough of an emergency to legally end the pregnancy. They were too afraid of Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s ban to even refer her to an out-of-state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic.\par \par \uc2 So Adkins and her husband frantically called clinics in Washington, Oregon and Utah, trying to find somewhere within driving distance that was not booked up for weeks with other patients fleeing the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans now blanketing the US. The couple also scrambled for financial support to pay for the procedure and travel. If they had to shoulder all those costs on their own, the family would be at risk of being unable to afford their mortgage.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re thinking about all these logistics when all you want to think about is grieving the loss of a baby that you really wanted,\u8221\'20\'1D Adkins said. \u8220\'20\'1CNone of us feel good about making that decision. But it is a loving choice that we should be able to make for our family.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Adkins is far from the only woman to come forward to say she was denied a medically necessary \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the two years since the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade and paved the way for more than a dozen states to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2024/jul/29/abortion-laws-bans-by-state"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ban almost all abortions}}}\uc2 . Although these bans technically permit the procedure in emergencies, their exceptions are worded so vaguely that doctors across the country say it\u8217\'20\'19s not clear when they can legally intervene. Fearful of the criminal consequences of violating bans, doctors are forced to wait and watch as patients get sicker and sicker \u8211\'20\'13 and then attempt to pull them back from the brink of death.\par \par \uc2 As the home of what may be the strictest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in the country, Idaho is a cautionary tale about what can happen when the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  disappears: droves of doctors have fled the state, which already had rising rates of maternal and infant mortality, and the state has become a laboratory for testing other laws meant to limit support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThose opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and reproductive healthcare access have had a playbook for a really long time, and Idaho is a textbook example of their playbook,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jessica Waters, an \uc2 American University\uc2  professor who studies \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CEverything that\u8217\'20\'19s happening in Idaho is a playbook, I think, for what could happen in the rest of the country.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 What a Trump administration could mean for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationally\par \par \uc2 If \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wins the presidency in November, even \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access for women in blue states may be under threat. Although Trump \u8211\'20\'13 who appointed three of the justices who overturned Roe \u8211\'20\'13 recently claimed that he would veto a national ban, he has repeatedly flip-flopped on the issue. And a Trump administration would not need Congress to wreak havoc on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access nationwide or to in effect ban the procedure.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/aug/05/project-2025-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Project 2025}}}\uc2 , the controversial policy playbook for a future conservative administration, proposes using the Comstock Act, an 1873 anti-vice law that outlaws the mailing of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related materials, to ban people from shipping \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. These pills currently account for about two-thirds of US abortions; they can also be used to safely self-induce an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first trimester of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Some anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists believe the Comstock Act can do even more: if enacted to its fullest extent, it could not only ban pills but the very equipment that clinics need to do their jobs.\par \par \uc2 Without ever involving Congress, Trump could use the Comstock Act to implement a nationwide de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. And if he doesn\u8217\'20\'19t, the courts could: anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists have begun to seed lawsuits throughout the country that cite Comstock, while the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s far-right justices, \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/mar/26/us-supreme-court-abortion-pill-case-mifepristone"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 both brought up the 151-year-old law}}}\uc2  in recent arguments.\par \par \uc2 Trump could also step back from enforcing a federal law that protects access to emergency abortions. Over the last year, Idaho has been at the center of the debate over this law, a debate that recently {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2024/apr/24/us-supreme-court-abortion-rights-emtala"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 made its way to the US}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2024/apr/24/us-supreme-court-abortion-rights-emtala"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 supreme court}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The law \u8211\'20\'13 the Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act, or Emtala \u8211\'20\'13 requires hospitals to stabilize patients who show up at their doors in the midst of a medical emergency or transport them to a hospital that can do so. But Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban only permits abortions in medical emergencies when a patient\u8217\'20\'19s life is at stake \u8211\'20\'13 an exception, the Biden administration argued to the supreme court, that is so narrow it violates Emtala. Idaho pushed back, arguing in part that the federal government can\u8217\'20\'19t compel doctors to offer care that is illegal under state law.\par \par \uc2 The supreme court essentially punted on whether Emtala extends to emergency abortions. The justices {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2024/jun/27/supreme-court-allows-emergency-abortions-idaho"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 restored a lower court order}}}\uc2  that lets Idaho doctors perform a broader range of emergency abortions, but left the door open to reconsider Emtala in the future.\par \par \uc2 Without a Democrat in the White House to fight it, Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s interpretation of Emtala could end up becoming a widespread standard.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18These are not normal conversations\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Many doctors have left Idaho rather than work under its ban. In the 15 months after Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s ban took effect, 22% of Idaho obstetricians stopped working in the state, according to a report by the Idaho Physician Well-Being Action Collaborative and the Idaho Coalition for Safe Healthcare. Three {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://idahocapitalsun.com/2024/04/22/idaho-doctor-who-worked-at-closed-maternity-ward-says-abortion-ban-harmed-recruiting/#:~:text=Caldwell's%20West%20Valley%20Medical%20Center,abortion%20ban%20in%20August%202022."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 labor and delivery wards have closed}}}\uc2 . More than half of the state\u8217\'20\'19s nine maternal fetal medicine specialists have left or stopped working in the state full-time.\par \par \uc2 Dr Kylie Cooper is one of them.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was a very real concern of mine that a situation would come up that somebody requires \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care that I cannot provide, or that I am providing and at risk of being charged with a felony and going to prison,\u8221\'20\'1D Cooper said. \u8220\'20\'1CI lost a lot of sleep over it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Cooper and her husband tried to game out the logistics of Cooper being arrested. Would she go to jail immediately? Could she get bail? What would that mean for their small children? Eventually, Cooper thought: \u8220\'20\'1CWhat are we even doing?\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese are not normal conversations. These are not conversations that I ever anticipated having in my medical career, in my life,\u8221\'20\'1D she continued. \u8220\'20\'1CThinking about myself and my family and my future patients, we made the really difficult decision to leave the state because of the bans.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She now works in Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 In interviews by the Guardian with nearly a dozen Idaho-based OB-GYNs and family medicine physicians \u8211\'20\'13 two specialties that tend to handle pregnancy \u8211\'20\'13 over the last year, every one said that they had considered leaving the state, if not the country. Experts are still trying to calculate the impact of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans on infant and maternal mortality, but {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/jun/24/texas-abortion-ban-infant-deaths"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one study linked a Texas \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban}}}\uc2  to a 13% increase in the number of infants who died in their first year of life.\par \par \uc2 At least two women, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.propublica.org/article/georgia-abortion-ban-amber-thurman-death"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Amber Nicole Thurman}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.propublica.org/article/candi-miller-abortion-ban-death-georgia"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Candi Miller}}}\uc2 , died after being unable to access legal abortions in their home state of Georgia, which bans abortions after six weeks of pregnancy, according to recent reporting by \uc2 ProPublica\uc2 . Experts deemed both of their deaths \u8220\'20\'1Cpreventable\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Idaho has also sought to limit access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in other ways. In 2023, the state created the crime of \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  trafficking\u8221\'20\'1D when it passed a law banning people from taking minors across state lines for legal abortions if their parents had not consented. That same year, Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s Republican attorney general, \uc2 Ra\u250\'00\'FAl Labrador\uc2 , issued a legal opinion claiming that Idaho law blocked medical professionals from even referring people for legal out-of-state abortions.\par \par \uc2 Separately, Idaho professors and teachers\u8217\'20\'19 unions sued over a law that makes it a crime to use public funds to \u8220\'20\'1Cpromote\u8221\'20\'1D or \u8220\'20\'1Ccounsel in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D..\par \par \uc2 Courts have since blocked the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  trafficking law and the attorney general\u8217\'20\'19s legal opinion from taking effect, while dismissing the teachers\u8217\'20\'19 lawsuit after Labrador said academic speech about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not prohibited under Idaho law. However, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://idahocapitalsun.com/2024/08/26/helping-a-minor-travel-for-an-abortion-some-states-have-made-it-a-crime/#:~:text=Last%20year%2C%20Idaho%20became%20the%20first%20state%20to%20outlaw%20%E2%80%9Cabortion,abortion%20medication%20without%20parental%20permission."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 four other states}}}\uc2  have since introduced \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  trafficking legislation. One of those states, Tennessee, has passed it.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18It\u8217\'20\'19s scary and it\u8217\'20\'19s disappointing\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Rebekka Schaffner lay on an exam bed in an Idaho clinic, pale pink and blue straps curving over her pregnant stomach, as she listened to the rapid \u8220\'20\'1Cwhoosh\u8221\'20\'1D-ing of her in-utero baby\u8217\'20\'19s heart.\par \par \uc2 Because Schaffner\u8217\'20\'19s pregnancy is high risk, the 34-year-old mother regularly undergoes tests to check that all is well. The threat of Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, and how it may affect her life if the pregnancy goes wrong, weighs on Schaffner. She\u8217\'20\'19s already felt the effects of the ban \u8211\'20\'13 twice.\par \par \uc2 Last year, Schaffner had a miscarriage that left fetal remains inside her body, she said. Before Roe fell, doctors might have treated Schaffner with a routine procedure known as a dilation and curettage, or D&C \u8211\'20\'13 but Schaffner said they were too fearful of Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s ban to perform the procedure. She ended up in the hospital with an infection, Schaffer said. \u8220\'20\'1CHad they handled it properly prior, I wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have gotten sick,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Dr Loren Colson, a family medicine doctor in Boise, Idaho, said that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws are so confusing, some medical providers are unwilling to treat miscarriages. When his own wife had a miscarriage, Colson said, a pharmacist refused to fill a prescription to treat it.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEven if it does seem clear cut, some people are like: \u8216\'20\'18If the punishment is jail time, I\u8217\'20\'19m just not going to do this,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D Colson said. When he\u8217\'20\'19s working with patients, \u8220\'20\'1Cif there\u8217\'20\'19s any concern that a patient might need an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or might need a D&C for stabilizing care, we don\u8217\'20\'19t even come close to that line. We send them out to Seattle or even Utah, [which] has better laws.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 When Schaffner realized she was pregnant again, she became profoundly anxious. \u8220\'20\'1CI didn\u8217\'20\'19t want to potentially end up having another miscarriage and be right back in that position again, of being told what I could and couldn\u8217\'20\'19t do, what was available to me,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Twenty weeks into her pregnancy, Schaffner\u8217\'20\'19s cervix started to dilate, she said. Her doctors stitched her cervix closed to keep her from delivering too early, but the procedure carried a small risk of breaking Schaffner\u8217\'20\'19s water too early. Had that happened \u8211\'20\'13 a condition known as premature prelabor rupture of membranes, or PPROM \u8211\'20\'13 Schaffner\u8217\'20\'19s pregnancy would have been in effect over, because there would be no way for her to deliver a healthy baby.\par \par \uc2 Complications from untreated PPROM can be deadly, but since Roe fell, many women have been denied treatment for the condition. When one Texan woman, Amanda Zurawski, developed PPROM, she was unable to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , contracted sepsis and ended up spending three days in the ICU. Other women have suffered life-threatening blood loss after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/article/2024/sep/09/state-abortion-bans-doctor-care-pregnancy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 doctors delayed treating their miscarriages}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Should she develop PPROM, Schaffner said, she planned to go out of state for care.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s scary and it\u8217\'20\'19s disappointing, knowing that if something went wrong in the process of trying to save the baby, that there wasn\u8217\'20\'19t anything else they could do,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CI would have to be sent to another state to have my daughter and lose her.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Adkins was ultimately able to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a clinic in Oregon, a six-hour drive away from her home in the Boise area, in April 2023. An \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund helped her cover the costs, but being away from her home state and the providers who knew her was wrenching. As she spoke, Adkins started to cry. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s an incredibly cruel side-effect of these bans, to put bereaved parents through a loss like that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In September 2023, Adkins became one of four women to sue Idaho over being denied a medically necessary \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Her lawsuit urges the state to clarify the scope of its medical exceptions, but it has not yet gone to trial. A similar lawsuit, in Texas, was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/may/31/texas-abortion-ban-medical-exceptions-supreme-court-ruling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shut down in May}}}\uc2  by the state\u8217\'20\'19s supreme court.\par \par \uc2 Adkins has since given birth to a second child. Afterward, Adkins got an IUD.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNow that the stakes are extremely high with this election, we decided to take a little bit firmer stance on how we were going to prevent pregnancy from happening,\u8221\'20\'1D said Adkins, as she nursed her infant. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t know what kind of access to care I\u8217\'20\'19m going to have.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241021ekal001mf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\