\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Florida sued over attempts to shut down TV ad in favor of  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 782 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 October 2024\par 15:48 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 State sending cease-and-desists to news stations was \u8216\'20\'18unconstitutional government interference\u8217\'20\'19, coalition says\par \par \uc2 The coalition behind a Florida ballot measure to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights into the state constitution sued the state\u8217\'20\'19s health department on Wednesday over its attempts to shut down an television advertisement supporting the measure.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit is the latest volley in an escalating war between the coalition, Floridians Protecting Freedom, and Florida\u8217\'20\'19s rightwing government, which has alarmed civil rights groups, fair elections organizations and even the \uc2 Federal Communications Commission\uc2  over its tactics to undermine the ballot initiative, which will appear before voters in November.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, the \uc2 Florida health department{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/oct/09/florida-news-abortion-ballot-coverage"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sent cease-and-desist letters}}}\uc2  to local news stations over an ad featuring a woman, identified as Caroline from Tampa, Florida, who speaks about being diagnosed with brain cancer while pregnant.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe doctors knew if I did not end my pregnancy, I would lose my baby, I would lose my life, and my daughter would lose her mom,\u8221\'20\'1D Caroline says in the ad. \u8220\'20\'1CFlorida has now banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  even in cases like mine.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The letters, which were signed by John Wilson, \uc2 Florida health department\uc2  general counsel, said the claim that women can\u8217\'20\'19t get life-saving abortions in Florida was \u8220\'20\'1Ccategorically false\u8221\'20\'1D and the state had the authority to criminally prosecute media outlets that continued to run Caroline\u8217\'20\'19s ad. ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.miamiherald.com/news/politics-government/state-politics/article293882159.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Wilson left that job}}}\uc2  days after sending those letters.) At least one TV station stopped airing it, according to the lawsuit filed by Floridians Protecting Freedom.\par \par \uc2 Although Florida\u8217\'20\'19s six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban permits the procedure in medical emergencies, doctors across the country say such exceptions are worded so vaguely that they force doctors to deny patients abortions or wait until they get sick enough to treat them legally. A New York doctor recently told the Guardian that she had {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/aug/30/whole-womans-health-abortion-clinic-virginia"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 treated a woman with an ectopic pregnancy}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 which is nonviable and potentially life-threatening if left untreated \u8211\'20\'13 who had been turned away from a Florida hospital.\par \par \uc2 If Floridians successfully pass the pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights ballot measure \u8211\'20\'13 which needs 60% of the vote to succeed \u8211\'20\'13 the state\u8217\'20\'19s ban would be overturned.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe state of Florida\u8217\'20\'19s crusade against amendment 4 is unconstitutional government interference \u8211\'20\'13 full stop,\u8221\'20\'1D Lauren Brenzel, director of the campaign for the ballot measure, said in a statement on Wednesday. \u8220\'20\'1CThe state cannot coerce television stations into removing political speech from the airwaves in an attempt to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in place.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Over the last several weeks, Florida\u8217\'20\'19s government, led by its Republican governor, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , has levied a series of attacks on the measure and its backers. Law enforcement officials {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/sep/13/florida-abortion-petition-investigation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have investigated people}}}\uc2  who signed a petition to get the measure on the ballot, while the Florida agency for healthcare administration created a website attacking the amendment. Floridians Protecting Freedom sued over that website, but a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.tampabay.com/news/florida-politics/elections/2024/10/01/judge-refuses-block-floridas-website-abortion-amendment/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 judge ruled it could}}}\uc2  remain up.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the Florida office of election crimes and security issued a report claiming that the agency believed Floridians Protecting Freedom may have \u8220\'20\'1Csubmitted a large number of forged signatures or fraudulent petitions\u8221\'20\'1D in support of the measure. In a statement, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/florida-abortion-ballot-measure-under-fire-by-state-government"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Brenzel denied the claims}}}\uc2  and said: \u8220\'20\'1CWhat we are seeing now is nothing more than dishonest distractions and desperate attempts to silence voters.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In its Wednesday lawsuit, which was filed in the US district court for the northern district of Florida in Tallahassee, Floridians Protecting Freedom asks the court to declare the cease-and-desist letters to be \u8220\'20\'1Cunconstitutional coercion and viewpoint discrimination\u8221\'20\'1D and to block Florida\u8217\'20\'19s government from following through on their threats of prosecution of media outlets.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COpponents of the measure are, of course, free to engage in their own oppositional speech,\u8221\'20\'1D the lawsuit reads, \u8220\'20\'1Cbut the state cannot use its coercive powers as the state to attempt to chill or suppress the speech of FPF or others who would speak in support of the amendment or facilitate that speech\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit names Wilson as a defendant in his individual capacity, as well as the Florida surgeon general, Joseph Ladapo. \uc2 Florida\u8217\'20\'19s health department\uc2  did not immediately respond to a request for comment.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/sep/26/election-abortion-ballot-measures-states"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Florida is one of 10 states}}}\uc2  with an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related measure on the ballot this year. Although Nevada and Arizona \u8211\'20\'13 two key presidential battlegrounds \u8211\'20\'13 also have similar measures, Florida\u8217\'20\'19s may be the most high-profile, because it would restore \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access to a state that had become a southern \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  haven after the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade in 2022. Its six-week ban {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/may/03/florida-abortion-ban-clinic"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 went into effect earlier this year}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241016ekag0028q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
