\par\par \uc2 Australia news\par \b \uc2 Coalition wants to scrutinise \u8216\'20\'18national security risk\u8217\'20\'19 of Chinese-made EVs \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Royce Kurmelovs \par \uc2 7,195 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 October 2024\par 06:44 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 This blog is now closedSteven Miles bats away accusations of pinching policies from Greens ahead of Queensland polls openingGet our breaking news email, free app or daily news podcast\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.32am BST\par \par \uc2 What we learned, 13 October 2024\par \par \uc2 With that we\u8217\'20\'19re wrapping up the blog. Before we go, here are the major stories from Sunday:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/oct/13/australia-news-live-politics-closing-the-gap-anthony-albanese-peter-dutton-cost-of-living-voice-referendum?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-670aeaf58f08e1594c64bbac#block-670aeaf58f08e1594c64bbac"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Repatriation flights from Lebanon }}}\uc2  are expected to wind down as the Australian government seeks to end its evacuation scheme;\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The Coalition has doubled down on its support for Israel\u8217\'20\'19s attacks on southern Lebanon and Gaza, despite its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/13/james-paterson-says-coalition-at-odds-with-biden-administration-on-middle-east-ceasefire"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 views being out of step }}}\uc2  with Australia\u8217\'20\'19s international counterparts;\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The Coalition wants to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/oct/13/australia-news-live-politics-closing-the-gap-anthony-albanese-peter-dutton-cost-of-living-voice-referendum?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-670af8c38f087ed1d5a8a508#block-670af8c38f087ed1d5a8a508"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 scrutinise Chinese electric vehicle imports}}}\uc2  over \u8220\'20\'1Cnational security risks\u8221\'20\'1D;\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Queensland Labor leader \uc2 Steven Miles{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/13/queensland-election-steven-miles-promises-free-primary-school-lunches"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has promised free school lunches}}}\uc2  as part of his bid to seek re-election and fend of a resurgent LNP challenge;\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Sussan Ley{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/13/sussan-ley-responds-to-white-supremacist-rally-corowa-nsw-ntwnfb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has condemned a white supremacist rally }}}\uc2  in Corowa, saying \u8220\'20\'1Cracism and hate has no place\u8221\'20\'1D in the border town;\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 A man is dead after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/oct/13/australia-news-live-politics-closing-the-gap-anthony-albanese-peter-dutton-cost-of-living-voice-referendum?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-670aff548f087ed1d5a8a523#block-670aff548f087ed1d5a8a523"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 allegedly being hit by a car in Melbourne.}}}\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ll pick things up again tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.38am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.18am BST\par \par \uc2 Olympian posts first marathon win in Melbourne\par \par \uc2 Some more from today\u8217\'20\'19s Melbourne marathon, where Genevieve Gregson scored her first marathon win, two months after running in the event at the Paris Olympics.\par \par \uc2 The Queenslander won the Melbourne Marathon on Sunday in two hours, 28 minutes and 13 seconds, beating local Sarah Klein by nearly three minutes. Kate Mason, also from Melbourne, completed the women\u8217\'20\'19s podium in 2:34:08.\par \par \uc2 Gregson\u8217\'20\'19s husband, Ryan Gregson, finished second in the men\u8217\'20\'19s race behind Jack Rayner, who clocked 2:11:49 after winning the Melbourne half-marathon for the past three years.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.58am BST\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Steep\u8217\'20\'19 rent hikes pricing more Australians out of homes, advocates fear\par \par \uc2 Renters in Australian capital cities are on average spending nearly $15,000 more a year to rent a house since the pandemic, analysis has revealed.\par \par \uc2 Research from the advocacy organisation Everybody\u8217\'20\'19s Home showed on average renters in capitals were paying $14,700 more annually to rent a house and $9,600 more to rent a unit compared with 2020.\par \par \uc2 Sydney and Perth have endured the steepest rent rises, with annual increases well above capital city averages for units ($10,452 and $14,508 respectively) and houses ($18,512 and $18,304). Adelaide and Brisbane unit rents are also above average.\par \par \uc2 Everybody\u8217\'20\'19s Home analysed rental data from SQM Research to build its report.\par \par \uc2 Maiy Azize, a spokesperson from Everybody\u8217\'20\'19s Home, said \u8220\'20\'1Ckeeping a roof over their head\u8221\'20\'1D was the biggest cost-of-living expense for most people in Australia.\par \par \uc2 The steep rise in rents is pushing more people into severe housing stress and homelessness.\par \par \uc2 For more on this story, read the full report by Guardian Australia\u8217\'20\'19s Cait Kelly :\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/13/more-australians-being-priced-out-of-homes-by-big-rent-hikes-advocates-fear"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 More Australians being \u8216\'20\'18priced out\u8217\'20\'19 of homes by big rent hikes, advocates fear}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.31am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.50am BST\par \par \uc2 Auction activity remains stable this weekend\par \par \uc2 There were 2,525 auctions expected to be held this weekend, well above the 1,955 held last week and a gain on the 2,275 auctions at the same time last year.\par \par \uc2 Based on results collected so far, \uc2 CoreLogic\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s summary found that the preliminary clearance rate was 66.9% across the country, which is slightly higher than the 63.9% preliminary rate recorded last week but only just above the 65.2% actual rate on final numbers.\par \par \uc2 Across the capital cities:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Sydney : 736 of 1004 auctions with a preliminary clearance rate of 69%\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Melbourne : 785 of 1044 auctions with a preliminary clearance rate of 64.8%\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Brisbane : 152 0f 216 auctions with a preliminary clearance rate of 62.5%\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Adelaide : 83 of 164 auctions with a preliminary clearance rate of 67.5%\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Canberra : 52 of 83 auctions with a preliminary clearance rate of 71.2%\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Tasmania : One auction held\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Perth : Nine of 13 auctions held\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.54am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.43am BST\par \par \uc2 Jobs market on display as inflation picture brightens\par \par \uc2 Australia\u8217\'20\'19s labour market is tipped to keep softening little-by-little as persistently high interest rates weigh on demand for workers.\par \par \uc2 Some economists predict another tick higher in the official jobless rate on Thursday when the \uc2 Australian Bureau of Statistics\uc2  unveils its monthly labour force report.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  has jacked up interest rates and kept them high to slow the economy and tame inflation.\par \par \uc2 Yet in a promising sign for borrowers waiting for cuts, August consumer price data showed all-important underlying inflation heading in the right direction, falling to 3.4% in August from 3.8% in July.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 RBA\uc2  maintains it wants to be sure inflation is moving sustainably back within the target band before reducing rates.\par \par \uc2 Further nuggets of insight into the central bank\u8217\'20\'19s thinking may be revealed in a speech by assistant governor Sarah Hunter, who is due to speak on Wednesday at the Citi Australia and New Zealand Investment Conference in Sydney.\par \par \uc2 - \uc2 AAP\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.51am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.21am BST\par \par \uc2 Traffic fines should be income-based to avoid \u8216\'20\'18criminalising poverty\u8217\'20\'19, finds new \uc2 Australia Institute\uc2  report\par \par \uc2 Australia\u8217\'20\'19s traffic fine systems are \u8220\'20\'1Ccriminalising poverty\u8221\'20\'1D finds a new report calling for fairer fines proportional to a driver\u8217\'20\'19s income.\par \par \uc2 While it might be an annoyance for high income earners, amid cost-of-living pressures a traffic fine can mean lower income earners are forced to choose between essentials or paying it off, \uc2 The Australia Institute\uc2  says.\par \par \uc2 Fines are the same for every driver caught speeding based on their jurisdiction, with NSW sometimes offering a 50% discount for people on government benefits.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://australiainstitute.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/P1539-Proportional-Speeding-Fines-WEB-2.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The think tank report}}}\uc2  is calling for a more equitable Finnish traffic fine model to be implemented in Australia.\par \par \uc2 Having a billionaire pay the same $200 speeding fine as a low income earner is unfair, the institute\u8217\'20\'19s research manager and report co-author Alice Grundy said.\par \par \uc2 Australia\u8217\'20\'19s regressive speeding fine system effectively criminalises poverty.\par \par \uc2 The plan proposes a flat fine based on the speeding range, with drivers accruing more based on their income and whether they have dependents.\par \par \uc2 NSW drivers currently pay $361 in fines if caught speeding between 10km/h to 19km/h but under the proposal, drivers could receive a fine ranging from $75 to $885 based on their income.\par \par \uc2 Accrued unpaid fines can trigger a vicious circle, where having the driver\u8217\'20\'19s car registration or licence cancelled leads to a reduced ability to work and pay fines.\par \par \uc2 The report noted that income-based fines were aimed at fairness, rather than changing driver behaviour, suggesting warning signs and physical road modifications were more effective.\par \par \uc2 - \uc2 AAP\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.41am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.08am BST\par \par \uc2 Smoke and toxic fumes erupt on Nullarbor highway due to burning semi-trailer carrying household insecticides\par \par \uc2 A burning semi-trailer carrying household insecticides is causing smoke and toxic fumes to erupt after an incident on a highway along the Nullarbor, roughly 88km east of the Western Australian border.\par \par \uc2 South Australian Country Fire Service volunteers are on site with a bulk-water carrier and one truck. SA and WA police and WA fire services are supporting the effort.\par \par \uc2 An exclusion zone has been set up to protect the public from toxic smoke.\par \par \uc2 The truck is expected to burn for at least 10 to 24 hours, and sections of the Eyre Highway between Eucla and Yalata have been closed to traffic. It is unknown when the roads will reopen.\par \par \uc2 Emergency service workers have asked the public not to enter the area due to the injury risk firefighters as visibility is poor.\par \par \uc2 People in the area are advised to remain indoors and in vehicles, with windows and doors closed and any air conditioning set to recirculation to not draw any fumes or smoke into enclosed spaces.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.24am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.49am BST\par \par \uc2 Jeff Kennett\uc2  apologises for derogatory comment at Young Liberals function\par \par \uc2 Former Victorian premier \uc2 Jeff Kennett\uc2  has apologised for calling a woman a \u8220\'20\'1Cbitch\u8221\'20\'1D for speaking during his speech at a Young Liberals function on Saturday night.\par \par \uc2 The Nine papers report that Kennett said he regretted making the comment during the Young Liberal Annual Gala held at the Melbourne Arts Centre.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to the Age, Kennett said the woman was \u8220\'20\'1Cvery, very distracting\u8221\'20\'1D. He said she was apart of a group who continued to talk through his speech, despite being asked to be \u8220\'20\'1Crespectful\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 I was giving my speech, [the woman] was sitting at the table immediately in front of me, and speaking continually, and I could hear her, and it was distracting.\par \par \uc2 Kennett said he had been trying to calling the woman to apologise directly but hadn\u8217\'20\'19t \u8220\'20\'1Cbeen able to make contact\u8221\'20\'1D, saying he suspected they were not taking his calls.\par \par \uc2 I apologise for using the word.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.26am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.40am BST\par \par \uc2 Dutton\u8217\'20\'19s focus on \u8216\'20\'18dividing Australians\u8217\'20\'19 isolating Libs from international community on Gaza/Lebanon ceasefire\par \par \uc2 The foreign minister, \uc2 Penny Wong\uc2 , says the Coalition is \u8220\'20\'1Cat odds\u8221\'20\'1D with the international community and Australia\u8217\'20\'19s key allies over its opposition to calls for a ceasefire in Lebanon and the Gaza Strip.\par \par \uc2 Responding to an interview earlier on Sunday by the shadow home affairs minister, \uc2 James Paterson\uc2 , Wong suggested the concession showed Coalition \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2  was \u8220\'20\'1Cisolated\u8221\'20\'1D on this issue and was more concerned with \u8220\'20\'1Cdividing Australians\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 A few days ago, Mr Dutton said the prime minister should be condemned for calling for a ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 Now the Liberals finally realise they\u8217\'20\'19re at odds with the international community, including the United States, who are all pressing for peace \u8211\'20\'13 but he still can\u8217\'20\'19t bring himself to do so.\par \par \uc2 He\u8217\'20\'19s so focused on dividing Australians over this conflict that he doesn\u8217\'20\'19t realise how isolated his position is.\par \par \uc2 We know Mr Dutton is negative about everything but it\u8217\'20\'19s pretty extreme to be negative about a ceasefire that saves civilian lives and prevents further disastrous escalation of conflict.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.52am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.17am BST\par \par \uc2 They are relics of the Gondwana age but five years after Australia\u8217\'20\'19s black summer these trees are dying a \u8216\'20\'18long, slow death\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Thousands of years of accumulated leaf fall makes the ground bounce like a mattress underfoot and, high above, rainforest coachwood trees form a dense canopy that dresses the understory in permanent shade.\par \par \uc2 Some of the unique plants in this rainforest can trace their lineage back 40 or 50m years when the Gondwana supercontinent was breaking apart and Australia was detaching itself from what is now Antarctica.\par \par \uc2 Take only a few steps from the cool shade and you emerge to something altogether different. Still rainforest, but changing fast.\par \par \uc2 Soil and rocks are exposed and, above, the leafless branches of tall and dead trees let the sunlight strike the forest floor.\par \par \uc2 This is a place packed with threatened species, including the nightcap oak \u8211\'20\'13 a true Gondwanan relic that grows nowhere else.\par \par \uc2 On the ground and at the base of the thin-barked trees is charcoal. Five years ago, chunks of this in rainforest not evolved to burn, did burn, during what became known as Australia\u8217\'20\'19s black summer bushfires.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt can take a lot of these trees a long time to die. It is a long, slow death,\u8221\'20\'1D says Dr Robert Kooyman, an evolutionary ecologist who has spent more than 40 years working and studying the rainforests in and around Nightcap national park in northern New South Wales.\par \par \uc2 For more on this story, read the full feature from Guardian Australia\u8217\'20\'19s Graham Readfern :\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/13/nightcap-national-park-rainforest-trees-age-bushfires-impact"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 They are relics of the Gondwana age but five years after Australia\u8217\'20\'19s black summer these trees are dying a \u8216\'20\'18long, slow death\u8217\'20\'19}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.36am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.59am BST\par \par \uc2 Anti-war protesters rally in Sydney CBD\par \par \uc2 Protesters have gathered in Sydney\u8217\'20\'19s Hyde Park as part of anti-war protests, calling for an end to Israel\u8217\'20\'19s attacks on southern Lebanon and a ceasefire in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Large crowds gathered from 1pm to attend the rally, the latest in a year of consistent protests.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 A march has been held in Melbourne for the 53rd straight week.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 A crowd had also begun to grow in Brisbane for an anti-war rally.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The Australian Friends of Palestine Association in South Australia has held a Run Ride Walk 4 Palestine event at Glenelg.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Rallies were also held in Perth and Hobart on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.57am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.30am BST\par \par \uc2 NZ Labour leader lays out economic plan on first anniversary of party\u8217\'20\'19s election loss\par \par \uc2 New Zealand Labour\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Chris Hipkins\uc2  has managed to stay on as leader of his party and is now looking forward to the next election, saying he intends to make \uc2 Chris Luxon\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Nationals a one-term government.\par \par \uc2 Speaking on the eve of the first anniversary of his party\u8217\'20\'19s election loss, Hipkins, who has just visited the UK where Labour has returned to power, laid out his ideas for New Zealand\u8217\'20\'19s future economy to \uc2 AAP\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 If we actually want to grow the economy, government does have a role.\par \par \uc2 Government investment on missions with a clear purpose can actually generate huge spin-off benefits.\par \par \uc2 And we shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be focused on growth for growth\u8217\'20\'19s sake \u8230\'20\'26 we should be saying our goal is to ensure that everybody has a good well-paying job.\par \par \uc2 The economy has been growing \u8211\'20\'13 for the last 40 years \u8211\'20\'13 but the vast majority of the people who are generating the growth aren\u8217\'20\'19t feeling the benefit.\par \par \uc2 To implement that economic strategy, Labour needs to win in 2026.\par \par \uc2 Its odds are poor \u8211\'20\'13 no Kiwi government has been turfed after a single term in half a century \u8211\'20\'13 and Hipkins concedes Labour was \u8220\'20\'1Cout-campaigned\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8211\'20\'13 with \uc2 AAP\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.45am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.15am BST\par \par \uc2 NZ government says coalition \u8216\'20\'18working well\u8217\'20\'19 one year in\par \par \uc2 A year on from the election that brought \uc2 Chris Luxon\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s National\u8217\'20\'19s to power in a right-wing coalition, New Zealand\u8217\'20\'19s prime minister has defied expectations of a dysfunctional government.\par \par \uc2 Luxon\u8217\'20\'19s coalition partner, the ACT leader and transport minister \uc2 David Seymour\uc2 , says the experience so far has been \u8220\'20\'1Cmuch less obstructive and keener to get along than I\u8217\'20\'19d anticipated\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Chris Bishop, National\u8217\'20\'19s campaign chair and the housing and infrastructure minister, insists the coalition is working well behind closed doors.\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s a real willingness from everybody in the coalition to make it work. Differences are resolved amicably and congenially.\par \par \uc2 Many of the policies embraced by the coalition have drawn a mighty backlash, particularly those supported by ACT and NZ First, the minority parties which secured 8.6% and 6.1% of the vote respectively.\par \par \uc2 Public sector cuts \u8211\'20\'13 particularly in health \u8211\'20\'13 and the abolition of smoke-free strategies, fast-tracked consent for mining projects, gun law reform, and most controversially, Treaty of Waitangi reform, have angered many.\par \par \uc2 The future remains uncertain with New Zealand mired in recession and GDP per capita falling by 4.6% over the past seven quarters \u8211\'20\'13 a larger fall than it experienced during the global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 The economic downturn is largely due to the Reserve Bank ratcheting up interest rates to contain high post-pandemic inflation \u8211\'20\'13 a challenge which appears contained given the centre bank is now easing rates.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate has grown by 1.4% in the last two years and is likely to keep growing.\par \par \uc2 - \uc2 AAP\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.47am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.00am BST\par \par \uc2 Man dead after allegedly being hit by car in Melbourne\par \par \uc2 Victorian police are investigating after two pedestrians were run down in a car park early on Sunday morning.\par \par \uc2 Officers were initially called to reports of an illegal rave in a park on Glenferrie Road where they witnessed two pedestrians being struck by a car at 1.10am, killing one.\par \par \uc2 The second person, who is yet to be identified, was taken to hospital with non-life-threatening injuries.\par \par \uc2 Police arrested the driver at the scene.\par \par \uc2 The investigation remains ongoing.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.17am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.52am BST\par \par \uc2 PM to give speech in Melbourne shortly on centenary of Australia\u8217\'20\'19s Greek Orthodox Archdiocese\par \par \uc2 Prime minister Anthony Albanese will deliver a speech at 1.3opm during a visit to Melbourne to commemorate the centenary of the Greek Orthodox Archdiocese of Australia.\par \par \uc2 We will bring you the latest as it happens.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.08am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.42am BST\par \par \uc2 Manhunt afoot for alleged attacker after man dies from hit to head while walking in Melbourne\u8217\'20\'19s CBD\par \par \uc2 A manhunt is under way after an alleged deadly assault in Melbourne\u8217\'20\'19s central business district.\par \par \uc2 Police say a man from Seddon died in hospital on Saturday after he was allegedly attacked by another man earlier in the week.\par \par \uc2 The 44-year-old had been walking on Elizabeth Street from Flinders Street when police allege he was approached and hit in the end.\par \par \uc2 He sustained significant injuries and was taken to hospital where he later died.\par \par \uc2 Homicide Squad detectives are appealing for public assistance to find a man named Todd Menegaldo after the alleged assault.\par \par \uc2 Detectives have conducted a significant search for Menegaldo in the past few days, police said.\par \par \uc2 Menegaldo, who also goes by the nickname \u8220\'20\'1CRooster\u8221\'20\'1D, is described as approximately 180 to 185cm tall with a thin build.\par \par \uc2 Police said he had a black and white crossbreed Staffordshire terrier and was known to frequent the Melbourne CBD, in particular the areas around Elizabeth Street and Flinders Street station, as well as Mildura and Bendigo.\par \par \uc2 Menegaldo was last seen wearing a zip-up purple jumper, dark jeans and a yellow beanie.\par \par \uc2 Police said people should not approach Menegaldo and instead contact triple zero immediately.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.00am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.24am BST\par \par \uc2 Melbourne Marathon winners cross finishing line\par \par \uc2 The 2024 Melbourne Marathon is under way with thousands taking part in across the CBD.\par \par \uc2 Here are a few images from on the ground:\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.37am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.59am BST\par \par \uc2 US official accuses Russia and China of blocking Asia leaders\u8217\'20\'19 statement\par \par \uc2 Russia and China blocked a proposed consensus statement for the East Asia Summit drafted by south-east Asian countries, mainly over objections to language on the contested South China Sea.\par \par \uc2 A draft statement, the 10-nation \uc2 Association of Southeast Asian Nations\uc2  arrived at by consensus, was put to the 18-nation East Asia Summit meeting in Laos on Thursday evening, a US official told Reuters on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAsean presented this final draft and said that, essentially, this was a take-it-or-leave-it draft,\u8221\'20\'1D the official said on condition of anonymity.\par \par \uc2 Australia, the United States, Japan, South Korea and India all said they could support it, the official said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Russians and the Chinese said that they could not and would not proceed with a statement,\u8221\'20\'1D the official said.\par \par \uc2 Russian Foreign Minister Sergei Lavrov told a news conference in Vientiane on Friday the final declaration had not been adopted because of \u8220\'20\'1Cpersistent attempts by the United States, Japan, South Korea, Australia and New Zealand to turn it into a purely political statement\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 China\u8217\'20\'19s Washington embassy did not immediately respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 The US official said there were a couple of issues of contention, but the main one was how it referred to the UN Convention on the Law of the Sea (Unclos), going further than in the previous 2023 EAS statement.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere was certainly no language that was getting into the nitty gritty of any particular standoff, no language that was favouring any claimant over any other,\u8221\'20\'1D the official said.\par \par \uc2 China claims nearly all of the South China Sea and has stepped up pressure on rival claimants, including several Asean countries, notably the Philippines.\par \par \uc2 \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 AAP\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.44am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.42am BST\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18If you want us to die, we need to know\u8217\'20\'19: hundreds of thousands of Afghans are waiting for visas to Australia \u8211\'20\'13 and time is running out\par \par \uc2 Some managed to evacuate. Many left behind, including the family of Afghan-Australians, live in fear of \u8220\'20\'1Cbeatings\u8221\'20\'1D or of simply \u8220\'20\'1Cdisappearing\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Afghan-Australian Sara* is sobbing as she speaks via video call from Iran, a bandage on her nose that she explains was caused by flying missile debris raining down on traffic.\par \par \uc2 About a month ago, Sara and her husband, Fadi *, left their Adelaide home to help their Afghan family members, in Iran illegally, in hiding and in danger after fleeing the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  after the fall of Kabul in 2021.\par \par \uc2 Now, Israel is contemplating retaliation for Iran\u8217\'20\'19s rocket attacks amid its escalating war with Iranian proxies \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Australian government has told citizens not to travel to Iran. There are risks of missile strikes, terrorist attacks, kidnapping and a \u8220\'20\'1Chigh risk of arbitrary detention or arrest,\u8221\'20\'1D according to the Smartraveller website.\par \par \uc2 *Names have been changed\par \par \uc2 For more on this story, read the full report from Guardian Australia\u8217\'20\'19s Tory Shepherd :\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/13/if-you-want-us-to-die-we-need-to-know-hundreds-of-thousands-of-afghans-are-waiting-for-visas-to-australia-and-time-is-running-out"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18If you want us to die, we need to know\u8217\'20\'19: hundreds of thousands of Afghans are waiting for visas to Australia \u8211\'20\'13 and time is running out}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.59am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.24am BST\par \par \uc2 Sussan Ley\uc2  condemns white supremacist rally in Corowa, saying \u8216\'20\'18racism and hate has no place\u8217\'20\'19 in NSW border town\par \par \uc2 The deputy leader of the opposition, \uc2 Sussan Ley\uc2 , says she has been \u8220\'20\'1Cheartened\u8221\'20\'1D by how Corowa residents, on the New South Wales and Victorian border, have \u8220\'20\'1Ccondemned\u8221\'20\'1D a rally of white supremacists.\par \par \uc2 A group of white supremacists held a rally in the historic town, considered the birthplace of federation, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.abc.net.au/news/2024-10-12/white-supremacists-rally-in-country-nsw-town-of-corowa/104465704"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 on Saturday.}}}\uc2  The rally was moved on by police but no arrests have been made.\par \par \uc2 Ley, whose electorate includes Corowa, described the rally attenders as \u8220\'20\'1Cthugs\u8221\'20\'1D and said \u8220\'20\'1Cit is clear this is part of a strategy to shock and provoke using regional towns.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 I am heartened by reports that members of the community made it clear to these individuals that racism and hate has no place in Corowa. But I am saddened that this hate was forced upon members of my community, including many families who were simply enjoying their weekend, the day before the Corowa Show.\par \par \uc2 I want to thank the New South Wales Police for dispersing this group of thugs and sending them out of our community.\par \par \uc2 Ley said Australians \u8220\'20\'1Ccan not and should not tolerate gangs of balaclava covered thugs spouting hate anywhere in this country\u8221\'20\'1D. She said the Coalition would give its support to federal and state authorities to \u8220\'20\'1Cto take whatever action is necessary to dismantle this organisation and stop these sorts of activities occurring\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Australians should all be proud of the diversity of our multicultural nation. We must remember this group only represents an isolated minority made up of radicalised individuals, and that the vast majority of our country stands against them.\par \par \uc2 We must redouble efforts to counter this radicalisation and equip our authorities with every necessary support to take stronger action.\par \par \uc2 For every day of the past 23 years, I have been so privileged to represent the incredible people of Corowa, but as I reflect on the community\u8217\'20\'19s strength yesterday, I have never been prouder of them than I am today.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.02am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.07am BST\par \par \uc2 Labor should \u8216\'20\'18practise what they preach\u8217\'20\'19 handling \uc2 Richard Marles\uc2  office bullying allegation, says \uc2 Dan Tehan\par \par \uc2 Shadow immigration minister \uc2 Dan Tehan\uc2  has accused the Albanese government of not living up to its own rhetoric on the treatment of women in parliamentary workplaces. It comes after the deputy prime minister\u8217\'20\'19s chief of staff alleged she was moved out of her job and sidelined when she raised a complaint with her boss about bullying in the office.\par \par \uc2 Guardian Australia reported last week that Jo Tarnawsky alleged \uc2 Richard Marles\uc2  forced her to take leave and told her to find another job when she raised her concerns with him on 30 April and has not spoken to her in five months.\par \par \uc2 Tarnawsky, the government\u8217\'20\'19s second most senior chief of staff, subsequently made a public statement, accusing unnamed staff of bullying her and alleging the government\u8217\'20\'19s handling of the situation had been \u8220\'20\'1Ccowardly, cruel and completely unnecessary\u8221\'20\'1D and had caused her deep distress. She did not accuse Marles of bullying.\par \par \uc2 Marles said, through a spokesperson, that \u8220\'20\'1Ca number of the assertions and recollections are contested\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Tarnawsky remains employed as chief of staff but was moved into a specially created temporary job that expired on 30 September. She said her employment future was unclear.\par \par \uc2 Speaking on Sky News on Sunday, Tehan said prime minister Anthony Albanese should clarify what his office knew about the situation and how long it would continue.\par \par \uc2 There does seem to be serious questions that need to be answered. We just hope the government \u8211\'20\'13 because when they were in opposition they called for full transparency on these types of issues \u8211\'20\'13 will practise what they preach.\par \par \uc2 Tehan suggested the issue demonstrated that Albanese had been inconsistent on the issue of workplace standards.\par \par \uc2 They talked a big game when they were in opposition. So let\u8217\'20\'19s hope it can be dealt with respectfully and transparently.\par \par \uc2 Marles told parliament on Thursday that Tarnawsky was a \u8220\'20\'1Cwonderful person\u8221\'20\'1D and he was \u8220\'20\'1Cvery sad that events have got to where they have\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Ms Tarnawsky remains a member of staff. Ms Tarnawsky has been treated with respect and courtesy. At all times, the wellbeing of staff, including Ms Tarnawsky, has been front of mind.\par \par \uc2 For more, read the Guardian Australia\u8217\'20\'19s previous reporting here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/11/sussan-ley-says-richard-marles-should-resign-if-he-failed-to-provide-safe-workplace-for-chief-of-staff-ntwnfb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sussan Ley says Richard Marles should resign if he failed to provide safe workplace for chief of staff}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.29am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.47am BST\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  wasn\u8217\'20\'19t on Queensland election agenda. So why is it now a threat to the LNP campaign?\par \par \uc2 On front doorsteps in Brisbane\u8217\'20\'19s middle suburbs, LNP candidates campaigning for the Queensland election have started getting questions about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s becoming an issue \u8230\'20\'26 in places we thought we might win,\u8221\'20\'1D says one party member.\par \par \uc2 Two weeks ago, as the Queensland election campaign began, Labor social media posts suggesting the LNP had a \u8220\'20\'1Csecret plan\u8221\'20\'1D to limit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and access were dismissed as a scare campaign.\par \par \uc2 The LNP leader, David Crisafulli, says his party has \u8220\'20\'1Cruled out\u8221\'20\'1D changes to Labor\u8217\'20\'19s 2018 laws that decriminalised \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is, as Crisafulli says, \u8220\'20\'1Cnot a priority\u8221\'20\'1D for the LNP. But it has quickly become a problem.\par \par \uc2 For more on this story, read the full report by Guardian Australia\u8217\'20\'19s Ben Smee :\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/13/queensland-election-2024-lnp-abortion-policy-david-crisafulli"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  wasn\u8217\'20\'19t on the Queensland election agenda. So why is it now a threat to the LNP campaign?}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.56am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.28am BST\par \par \uc2 QLD Labor promises free primary school lunches in bid to defy polling as it officially launches election campaign\par \par \uc2 Seeking to defy polling and deliver Labor a fourth straight term in government, Queensland premier \uc2 Steven Miles\uc2  will officially launch the party\u8217\'20\'19s election campaign on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 The government has enjoyed almost a decade in power since \uc2 Annastacia Palaszczuk\uc2  ousted \uc2 Campbell Newman\uc2  in 2015, and with just 13 days until voters head to the poles, Miles is seeking to extend his party\u8217\'20\'19s tenure.\par \par \uc2 Labor has governed Queensland for 30 of the past 35 years but polling has the party on track for defeat by 56 to 44 per cent on a two-party-preferred basis.\par \par \uc2 Liberal National Party leader David Crisafulli is tipped to end Labor\u8217\'20\'19s nine-year reign after consistently leading the polls, campaigning hard on the election\u8217\'20\'19s key issues \u8211\'20\'13 health, youth crime, homes and cost of living pressures.\par \par \uc2 Miles has promised to make school lunches free for every student in prep through to year six if re-elected. The proposal would kick off next year, saving parents $1600 a child, a year and benefiting 326,000 students.\par \par \uc2 The proposal forms part of a broad progressive agenda that Miles is hoping will overcome the \u8220\'20\'1Cit\u8217\'20\'19s time\u8221\'20\'1D factor weighing on his government.\par \par \uc2 \u8211\'20\'13 with \uc2 AAP\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.51am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.10am BST\par \par \uc2 PM\u8217\'20\'19s Tourette syndrome gaffe and subsequent apology shows he\u8217\'20\'19s a \u8216\'20\'18genuine bloke\u8217\'20\'19, assistant trade minister tells \uc2 Sky\par \par \uc2 The government is seeking to make a virtue of a difficult parliamentary week and the first anniversary of the failed Voice referendum. It is arguing the latter shows the Albanese government \u8220\'20\'1Cdoes what it says\u8221\'20\'1D and that the PM\u8217\'20\'19s parliamentary gaffe about Tourette syndrome \u8211\'20\'13 for which he apologised \u8211\'20\'13 showed he was a \u8220\'20\'1Cgenuine bloke\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Assistant trade minister Tim Ayres told Sky News on the eve of the referendum anniversary that proceeding with the vote was the government keeping a promise.\par \par \uc2 One of the things you have to do is do what you say you\u8217\'20\'19re going to do and explain your way through these changes.\par \par \uc2 Ahead of Monday\u8217\'20\'19s first anniversary of the referendum that rejected constitutionally enshrining an Indigenous Voice to parliament, some Indigenous leaders have said the vote should have been deferred.\par \par \uc2 Ayres said:\par \par \uc2 We argued the case for constitutional change. We respect the result that we got. I think a government that does what it says it is going to do is something that people should value and understand.\par \par \uc2 He also defended the prime minister\u8217\'20\'19s leadership, both in the context of last week\u8217\'20\'19s parliamentary jibe accusing the shadow treasurer of having Tourette syndrome and then apologising, and more broadly since becoming Labor leader in 2019.\par \par \uc2 The prime minister\u8217\'20\'19s like any other Australian. He said something in the heat of parliamentary debate. He didn\u8217\'20\'19t have to be dragged to a withdrawal \u8230\'20\'26 He\u8217\'20\'19s a genuine bloke who responded genuinely when he said something that he knew he shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have said.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.27am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.54pm BST\par \par \uc2 Protesters dressed in red to \u8216\'20\'18represent the blood that connects all species\u8217\'20\'19 outside APGA convention to test SA\u8217\'20\'19s anti-protest laws\par \par \uc2 Extinction Rebellion associated protesters will hold a demonstration outside the annual convention of the \uc2 Australian Pipelines and Gas Association\uc2  (APGA) in Adelaide on Sunday in what may become a test of the state\u8217\'20\'19s stringent anti-protest laws.\par \par \uc2 A group dressed in red to \u8220\'20\'1Crepresent the blood that connects all species\u8221\'20\'1D will \u8220\'20\'1Cwalk slowly to the beat of a single drum\u8221\'20\'1D outside the Adelaide Convention Centre to \u8220\'20\'1Chighlight the devastating impact of fracking and gas extraction\u8221\'20\'1D in the Beetaloo Basin in the NT, Extinction Rebellion SA spokesperson Anna Slynn said.\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s action is a message for the Adelaide community. The planet is heating up. Extreme weather events like the devastating cyclones in the US are increasing. It is not in our interest to be welcoming the fossil fuel industry to our state while we face the threat of another killer bush-fire season and long term drought.\par \par \uc2 APGA is supported by companies including Santos, \uc2 Woodside\uc2  and \uc2 Chevron\uc2 , and the SA energy and mining minister, \uc2 Tom Koutsantonis\uc2 , is expected to give a keynote address.\par \par \uc2 In May last year, Koutsantonis told the annual national oil and gas industry conference that the South Australian state government was \u8220\'20\'1Cat your disposal\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 For more on that story, read Guardian Australia\u8217\'20\'19s previous reporting:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2023/may/16/south-australia-gas-industry-appea-national-conference-2023"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 South Australia tells gas industry the state is \u8216\'20\'18at your disposal\u8217\'20\'19 }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.28am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.38pm BST\par \par \uc2 Coalition would consider mirroring US action on TikTok due to \u8216\'20\'18foreign interference risk\u8217\'20\'19, says Paterson\par \par \uc2 Paterson has implied that the Coalition would consider taking similar action to the US on TikTok.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US government\uc2  recently passed a bill requiring TikTok\u8217\'20\'19s parent company, ByteDance, to divest itself of the social media platform owing to its relationship to the Chinese government.\par \par \uc2 TikTok will pose a national security risk and particularly a foreign interference risk in our democracy. The US has taken action. The worrying thing is you might end up with a safer version of TikTok in the United States and a more dangerous version in Australia because we have been unwilling to take that action.\par \par \uc2 And that\u8217\'20\'19s a wrap.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.06am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.34pm BST\par \par \uc2 Coalition wants to scrutinise \u8216\'20\'18national security risk\u8217\'20\'19 of Chinese-made EVs\par \par \uc2 Paterson says the Albanese government deserves credit for ending China\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Ceconomic coercion campaign\u8221\'20\'1D but has sought to cast the achievement as \u8220\'20\'1Ca bipartisan achievement\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt started in the Morrison government, it is continuing under the Albanese government,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Of course, it happened on the Albanese government\u8217\'20\'19s watch. They deserve some of credit for that, but it is a credit to Australia as a whole because the reason why the Chinese government has walked away from these sanctions is because they didn\u8217\'20\'19t work.\par \par \uc2 Paterson says he wants to \u8220\'20\'1Clook closely\u8221\'20\'1D about whether to ban Chinese-made electric vehicles in Australia because \u8220\'20\'1Cinternet connected vehicles are a national security and cybersecurity risk\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 So much so that the Biden administration is taking very tough action to eliminate them from their market. If our closest ally and friends think it is a concern, we should address it as well.\par \par \uc2 However, Paterson said there were other ways of mitigating the risks, which may not necessarily involve an outright ban.\par \par \uc2 For example, you can impose minimum cybersecurity standards that would lift the bar that all Internet devices like that have to meet, not just cars but other devices connected to the Internet.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.37am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 Tweet in support of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  enough ground to cancel visas, says Paterson\par \par \uc2 Pushed on where the Coalition\u8217\'20\'19s position lands compared to Australia\u8217\'20\'19s international counterparts, Paterson said:\par \par \uc2 Of course we aim to get to [a] ceasefire and aim to get to peace. I don\u8217\'20\'19t think that can happen unless Israel removes the very serious and existential problems it faces.\par \par \uc2 On the cancellation of Palestinian visas, Patterson refers to statements by \uc2 Asio\uc2  head \uc2 Mike Burgess\uc2 , who said that anyone who liked a tweet showing support for \uc2 Hamas\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cconstitutes a security threat to Australia\u8221\'20\'1D. He said that this should be enough ground to cancel someone\u8217\'20\'19s visa.\par \par \uc2 Paterson said that visas can be refused not just on security grounds but on character grounds and that should be used to cancel the visas of those who express support for groups like \uc2 Hamas\uc2  or \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.52pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.26pm BST\par \par \uc2 Wounding of \uc2 UN\uc2  peacekeepers in southern Lebanon \u8216\'20\'18unfortunate \u8230\'20\'26 if that is the case\u8217\'20\'19, says Paterson\par \par \uc2 Paterson says he would have concerns about the wounding of \uc2 UN\uc2  peacekeepers in southern Lebanon \u8220\'20\'1Cif that is the case, that [it was] \uc2 IDF\uc2  action that wounded US peacekeepers\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 That would have been very unfortunate and very regrettable. It should be investigated and should not happen.\par \par \uc2 Paterson is canvassed on the position of other world leaders, particularly the G7 leaders, who have called for a ceasefire and whether that means the Coalition is out of step with Australia\u8217\'20\'19s international allies.\par \par \uc2 Paterson:\par \par \uc2 On 26 September, Australia with some other nations, called for a ceasefire in Lebanon. Had Israel followed that advice, \uc2 Hassan Nasrallah\uc2  would still be alive today, the former head of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.48pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.23pm BST\par \par \uc2 Lebanon ceasefire can only happen once \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  \u8216\'20\'18degraded and defeated\u8217\'20\'19: \uc2 James Paterson\par \par \uc2 Asked about the Coalition position on a ceasefire in southern Lebanon, Paterson said that it could only happen when \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  had been \u8220\'20\'1Cdegraded and defeated\u8221\'20\'1D. This is at odds with the position of US President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and vice-president \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , who have called for a de-escalation and ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 Patterson said: \u8220\'20\'1Cit wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be remarkable for an Australian politician to disagree with an American politician.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The administration under Biden and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is centre left. I\u8217\'20\'19m from centre [the] right. We can be open and honest as mature adults about where we disagree.\par \par \uc2 Pushed on whether the Coalition is out of step with the position of Australia\u8217\'20\'19s biggest ally, the US, Patterson said:\par \par \uc2 Yes, on questions of principle, [the Coalition] are very strong supporters of Israel. We understand the circumstances they face. God forbid Australia ever faces challenges that Israel faces. God forbid that Australian politicians are ever faced with these choices, but if we would expect their Government to respond as decisively as Israel has.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.29am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.19pm BST\par \par \uc2 PM must have \u8216\'20\'18have the courage of his convictions\u8217\'20\'19 and \u8216\'20\'18op up\u8217\'20\'19 when in disagreement with US, \uc2 James Paterson\uc2  says\par \par \uc2 Shadow home affairs minister \uc2 James Paterson\uc2  says prime minister Anthony Albanese should \u8220\'20\'1Chave the courage of his convictions\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cjust be honest and op up\u8221\'20\'1D about where he disagrees with the United States.\par \par \uc2 Paterson said the PM \u8220\'20\'1Cclearly\u8221\'20\'1D disagreed with the United States over Israel\u8217\'20\'19s actions in Gaza and southern Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s not a remarkable thing that Australia could come to a different judgment from the United States. We are our own sovereign country. The problem here is that the prime minister doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have the courage of his own convictions.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.36pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.08pm BST\par \par \uc2 Regressive steps and broken promises failing to close the gap, says peak Aboriginal group\par \par \uc2 Monday will mark the first anniversary of the national referendum that voted against creating an indigenous voice to parliament. The peak body for Aboriginal community-controlled groups has taken the moment to check progress against a previous key moment \u8211\'20\'13 the signing of the national Closing the Gap agreement in 2020.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.coalitionofpeaks.org.au/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Coalition of Peaks}}}\uc2 , representing more than 80 peak groups, says there have been regressive steps on youth justice and broken promises on funding and steps to make government services accountable.\par \par \uc2 Patricia Turner, the coalition\u8217\'20\'19s lead convener and Gudanji-Arrernte woman, says there has also been \u8220\'20\'1Creal progress\u8221\'20\'1D in areas, such as employment and early childhood education.\par \par \uc2 But the changes have to be systemic and they have to be long-term. There\u8217\'20\'19s important work still to do to make sure the rubber\u8217\'20\'19s hitting the road, funding is getting to communities, and governments are truly changing.\par \par \uc2 The new NT government wants to cut the age of criminal responsibility to 10 and Victoria had walked back plans to lift the age to 14. Turner said:\par \par \uc2 Imprisoning vulnerable children, many of whom have developmental delays that make their decision-making capacity that of seven or eight-year-olds, flies in the face of what State and Territory Governments signed up to under the National Agreement on Closing the Gap.\par \par \uc2 A 2020 agreement signed by all governments pledged that by the end of 2023 each would create Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander-led bodies to improve the way they work with those communities. The coalition said:\par \par \uc2 To date, none of them have.\par \par \uc2 Turner said dedicated funding for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander community-controlled organisations was also falling short, seeing \u8220\'20\'1Cmoney wasted on programs that aren\u8217\'20\'19t designed by us, and don\u8217\'20\'19t work for us\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.24pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.57pm BST\par \par \uc2 Lebanon repatriation flights land in Sydney\par \par \uc2 About 2,400 evacuees from Lebanon have now landed in Australia as the federal government prepares to end its evacuation program.\par \par \uc2 Demand for help leaving Beirut in the wake of Israel\u8217\'20\'19s attacks on southern Lebanon has fallen in recent days, with less than 180 people on board flights to Cyprus that left on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The last two government-chartered flights will leave Beirut on Sunday before the Albanese government ends the operation.\par \par \uc2 enltr194 Australians and family members have arrived in Australia overnight.\par \par \uc2 2,135 Australians and their family members have now returned to Australia from Lebanon on assisted-departure flights. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/AvY8929i5P"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/AvY8929i5P}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade ???? (@dfat) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/dfat/status/1844840442149675457?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 11, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Two more government-chartered repatriation flights landed in Sydney on Saturday evening, carrying hundreds of Australians and their family members.\par \par \uc2 One final \uc2 Qantas\uc2  flight is due to leave Cyprus on Wednesday evening.\par \par \uc2 The arrived passengers add to the 2,135 who have already arrived on long-haul flights chartered by Australia.\par \par \uc2 Australians and their family members still in Lebanon will need to resort to local flag carrier \uc2 Middle East Airlines\uc2  or other commercial outfits.\par \par \uc2 More than 2,200 people have been killed and another 1.2 million have been displaced across Lebanon, a nation about the size of Melbourne or Sydney.\par \par \uc2 - \uc2 AAP\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.01pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.46pm BST\par \par \uc2 The assistant trade minister Tim Ayres and shadow immigration minister \uc2 Dan Tehan\uc2  have appeared on Sky News this morning, and the Coalition\u8217\'20\'19s shadow home affairs minister \uc2 James Paterson\uc2  is speaking to ABC Insiders host David Speers shortly.\par \par \uc2 enltrComing up on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/ABCInsiders?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #ABCInsiders}}}\uc2  with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/David_Speers?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @David_Speers}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 Shadow Home Affairs Minister \uc2 James Paterson\uc2  interviewed??\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 ?? Jack the Insider\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 ??? Carly Williams, Mark Kenny and James Massola\par \par \uc2 See you at 9am {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/auspol?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #auspol}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/bn0uHIbZKN"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/bn0uHIbZKN}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 ABC News (@abcnews) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/abcnews/status/1845190700843008411?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 12, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 We will bring you the latest as it happens.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.48pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.35pm BST\par \par \uc2 Good morning\par \par \uc2 And welcome to another Sunday Guardian live blog.\par \par \uc2 Almost 2,500 people have returned to Australia from Lebanon as the government is preparing to end its repatriation flights. The program began during Israel\u8217\'20\'19s attacks on southern Lebanon but with falling numbers taking up the opportunity to evacuate, the last two government-chartered flights are expected to leave Beirut on Sunday after two landed in Sydney on Saturday night.\par \par \uc2 Monday will mark the one-year anniversary of the failed voice to parliament referendum, with Australia\u8217\'20\'19s peak Aboriginal group using the moment to check in on the country\u8217\'20\'19s efforts to close the gap. A body representing 80 peak groups says the last year has been characterised by backward steps and broken promises.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m Royce Kurmelovs and I\u8217\'20\'19ll be taking the blog through the day.\par \par \uc2 With that, let\u8217\'20\'19s get started...\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.59pm BST\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241012ekac002e5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
