\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Texas man drops lawsuit against women accused of helping ex-wife get  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 404 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 October 2024\par 18:50 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Marcus Silva asked court for $1m damages in case that was legally questionable but worried \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists\par \par \uc2 A Texas man who sued his ex-wife\u8217\'20\'19s friends for allegedly helping her get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropped his lawsuit on Thursday after the case was settled.\par \par \uc2 Marcus Silva first {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/mar/11/texas-women-sued-wrongful-death-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sued Jackie Noyola and Amy Carpenter}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 both friends of his ex-wife \u8211\'20\'13 along with Aracely Garcia in 2023, alleging that the three had helped her obtain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills in July 2022, about two months after she filed for divorce. Silva asked a Texas district court to award him more than $1m in damages for their \u8220\'20\'1Ccriminal and murderous actions\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans typically target providers, not patients, and Texas, like the vast majority of states, does not criminalize self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (Silva\u8217\'20\'19s ex-wife was not named as a defendant in the lawsuit.) However, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates feared that, even if the first-of-its-kind lawsuit was not legally sound, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2023/03/10/texas-abortion-lawsuit/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 it would still intimidate people}}}\uc2  out of helping one another get abortions.\par \par \uc2 The case had been set to go to trial before Silva dropped the lawsuit. In court filings, Silva did not explain his reason for the decision, but a notice of settlement was submitted to the court. Carpenter {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/nation/2024/10/11/texas-man-abandons-suit-against-women-he-claimed-helped-ex-wife-get-abortion/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told the Washington Post}}}\uc2  that no money had been exchanged as part of Silva\u8217\'20\'19s abandonment of the lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 Silva was represented in the lawsuit by Jonathan Mitchell, a lawyer who masterminded the Texas six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that deputized ordinary people to sue one another over suspected illegal abortions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile we are grateful that this fraudulent case is finally over, we are angry for ourselves and others who have been terrorized for the simple act of supporting a friend who is facing abuse,\u8221\'20\'1D Noyola said in a statement. \u8220\'20\'1CNo one should ever have to fear punishment, criminalization, or a lengthy court battle for helping someone they care about.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis case was about using the legal system to harass us for helping our friend, and scare others out of doing the same,\u8221\'20\'1D Carpenter added. \u8220\'20\'1CAfter two years of being entangled in Mitchell and Silva\u8217\'20\'19s campaign of abusive litigation, we were ready to fight this baseless suit in court. But the claims were dropped because they had nothing. We did nothing wrong, and we would do it all again.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241011ekab002h0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
