\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Will white women abandon Republicans and vote for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ?\b0\par\par\uc2 Carter Sherman \par \uc2 1,417 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 October 2024\par 08:32 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 22\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Trump led this demographic handily in 2016 and 2020 but younger white women are moving decidedly to the leftUS voters in swing states: if you\u8217\'20\'19re still undecided how to vote, tell us why\par \par \uc2 White female voters have been the backbone of the GOP for decades \u8211\'20\'13 but polls indicate their support for the party may erode this November, thanks to younger white women who are moving left at breakneck speed.\par \par \uc2 In the weeks after the 2016 presidential election, after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  stunned the world by defeating \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/01/us/politics/white-women-helped-elect-donald-trump.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 media outlets }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/01/us/politics/white-women-helped-elect-donald-trump.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 seized}}}\uc2  on white women to explain his shock win. Forty-seven per cent of white women voted for Trump, while 45% backed Clinton, according to an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/politics/2018/08/09/an-examination-of-the-2016-electorate-based-on-validated-voters/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 analysis of validated voter files}}}\uc2  by the \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s success with white women highlighted a longstanding truth: this group votes for Republicans. Over the last 72 years, a plurality of white women have voted for the Democratic candidate in only two presidential elections \u8211\'20\'13 in 1964, when Lyndon Johnson won 44 states, and in 1996, when \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  ran in a three-way race. Trump\u8217\'20\'19s lead with white women {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/politics/2021/06/30/behind-bidens-2020-victory/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 even grew in 2020}}}\uc2 , when 53% supported him. In contrast, 95% of Black women voted for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in 2020, along with 61% of Hispanic women, Pew found.\par \par \uc2 But quite a bit has changed since 2020 \u8211\'20\'13 especially for women. The \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade in 2022, transforming \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights into a major election issue. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  took over as the Democratic candidate from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , becoming the first woman of color to secure a major-party nomination for president. All this raises the question: will 2024 be the year that white women, who make up almost {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2020/11/03/929478378/understanding-the-2020-electorate-ap-votecast-survey"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 40% of the national electorate}}}\uc2 , finally join women of color in supporting the Democrats?\par \par \uc2 Well, not necessarily. But the gap very well may shrink.\par \par \uc2 There are signs that younger white women are peeling off from the GOP \u8211\'20\'13 a trend that is linked to a steady drift by all young women to the left.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYoung women of color and young white women, in my research, are pretty uniformly liberal and feminist,\u8221\'20\'1D said Melissa Deckman, CEO of the Public Religion Research Institute and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2024/aug/07/gen-z-voters-political-ideology-gender-gap"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 author of the recent book}}}\uc2  The Politics of Gen Z: How the Youngest Voters Will Shape Our Democracy. \u8220\'20\'1CI think Harris\u8217\'20\'19s selection as the nominee now \u8211\'20\'13 as opposed to Biden \u8211\'20\'13 has really further made them enthusiastic about voting. So I strongly suspect that young, white women voters are going to defy the longer-term trend of white women in general voting for Republicans.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Young women are increasingly queer, increasingly secular and getting married later in life \u8211\'20\'13 all characteristics that tend to be linked with liberalism and support for the Democratic party. (People who identify as liberal are very likely to be Democrats, though the inverse is not necessarily true \u8211\'20\'13 not all Democrats identify as liberal.)\par \par \uc2 Between 2011 and 2024, liberal identification among white women rose by 6%, according to a Gallup analysis shared with the Guardian. Such identification also rose by 6% among Black women, but fell by 2% among Hispanic women.\par \par \uc2 Gen Z is the most diverse generation of Americans yet, but {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/649826/exploring-young-women-leftward-expansion.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Gallup research}}}\uc2  suggests that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t explain young women\u8217\'20\'19s leftward drift. Between 2017 and 2024, 41% of white women between the ages of 18 and 29 identified as liberals \u8211\'20\'13 2 percentage points more than their peers of color.\par \par \uc2 Young women are also unusually involved in politics. Women have long outvoted men, but in 2020, 60% of 18- to 29-year-old white women voted \u8211\'20\'13 more than any other group of youth voters, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://circle.tufts.edu/latest-research/2020-youth-voter-turnout-raceethnicity-and-gender"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to an analysis}}}\uc2  of voter file and American Community Survey population data by the Center for Information & Research on Civic Learning and Engagement. Fifty-five per cent voted for Biden , the center found, after analyzing data from AP VoteCast.\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s 2016 victory may have something to do with these trends. Raised by Democratic-leaning independents, Chloe Fowler said Trump\u8217\'20\'19s election was a critical inflection point in her political evolution. She was a sophomore in high school when Trump won; the day after, somebody in her school hallway shouted gleefully: \u8220\'20\'1CGrab \u8217\'20\'19em by the pussy!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThings like that stick with us,\u8221\'20\'1D recalled Fowler , who is white. A few months later, her mom took her to the Women\u8217\'20\'19s March in Omaha, Nebraska. \u8220\'20\'1CThat was a very pivotal moment for me, honestly \u8211\'20\'13 doing a bunch of chants with her and wearing the pink cat ear hats.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Fowler is now the vice-president of Nebraska Young Democrats. The 23-year-old has been phone-banking furiously in her home district \u8211\'20\'13 Nebraska\u8217\'20\'19s second congressional district, which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/jul/07/nebraska-presidential-election-congressional-district"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 may end up deciding}}}\uc2  whether Trump or Harris becomes president.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Why is this race so close?\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/17ItHAe2KFPF8fueuhQJMWAUgxdvFKbYK/edit?gid=1454020452#gid=1454020452"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 19th News/SurveyMonkey poll}}}\uc2  in September found that white women narrowly prefer Harris to Trump, 42% to 40%. (The poll has a margin of error of plus or minus 1%.) The remaining 18% can make or break the election, of course. The gender gap between white women and white men is larger. Fifty per cent of white men prefer Trump, with only 36% supporting Harris.\par \par \uc2 A majority of Black women support Harris, that poll found, as do pluralities of Hispanic and Asian American women.\par \par \uc2 Jane Junn, a political science professor at the \uc2 University of Southern California\uc2 , says what is often misunderstood as a \u8220\'20\'1Cgender gap\u8221\'20\'1D between male and female voters is really a race gap. While women as a whole may end up voting for Harris \u8211\'20\'13 a September {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2024/09/19/us/elections/times-siena-poll-likely-electorate-crosstabs.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New York Times/Siena poll}}}\uc2  showed that 54% of women planned to vote for Harris, compared with 40% of men \u8211\'20\'13 white women, Junn predicted, will remain Republicans in 2024. \u8220\'20\'1CIf all of a sudden, the white women were like: \u8216\'20\'18Oh, my God, I\u8217\'20\'19m burning my bra and my Barbie shoes and my long fingernails and all the plastic sprays I put into my body\u8217\'20\'19 \u8211\'20\'13 we\u8217\'20\'19re not seeing that,\u8221\'20\'1D Junn said. \u8220\'20\'1CWhy is this race so close? It\u8217\'20\'19s so close because these groups remain fairly consistent in their partisan loyalty.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Polling from Galvanize Action, an organization that seeks to mobilize moderate women \u8211\'20\'13 especially in the critical \u8220\'20\'1Cblue wall\u8221\'20\'1D states of Pennsylvania, Wisconsin and Michigan \u8211\'20\'13 has found the race in a dead heat among moderate white women, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://playbook.galvanizeaction.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/August-2024-Tracking-Survey-Wave-3.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 who are split 43% to 44%}}}\uc2  in favor of the former president. These women, who Galvanize Action defines as not ideologically entrenched as Democrats or Republicans, account for more than 5 million voters in those three states .\par \par \uc2 Trump has the edge when it comes to these women\u8217\'20\'19s top issues of the economy and immigration, but the women polled by Galvanize Action trust Harris more on democracy and reproductive freedom.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEven among women who say that economy or democracy is their No 1 issue, a good segment of those people also say: \u8216\'20\'18I\u8217\'20\'19m not going to vote for anyone that won\u8217\'20\'19t protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D said Jackie Payne, Galvanize Action\u8217\'20\'19s executive director and founder.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are hoping that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights-related {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/sep/26/election-abortion-ballot-measures-states"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ballot measures}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 which voters will decide on in the battlegrounds of Arizona, Nevada and Nebraska\u8217\'20\'19s second congressional district \u8211\'20\'13 will spur turnout among their base. However, white women may in effect vote split-ticket, simultaneously voting for a pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights measure and for Republicans. More than half of white women voted for Ohio\u8217\'20\'19s 2023 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related ballot measure \u8211\'20\'13 but {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/election/2022/exit-polls/ohio/general/governor/21"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than 60% of white women}}}\uc2  supported \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2 , the Republican governor who signed a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban into law, in 2022, just months after Roe fell.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is going to be all about turnout. This is going to be a very, very close election , \u8221\'20\'1D said Debbie Walsh , director of the Center for American Women and Politics at Rutgers. \u8220\'20\'1CThe Democratic party counts on women. They count particularly on Black women to turn out. Will they be more energized?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 This article was amended on 8 October 2024 to correct some figures from the 19th News/\uc2 SurveyMonkey\uc2  poll, including a figure introduced during the editing process that suggested the percentage of white women who had not shown a voting preference for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  or \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was 8%; it was 18%. Also, to add that some information about 2020 youth voters was analyzed using voter file and American Community Survey population data, in addition to AP VoteCast, by the Center for Information & Research on Civic Learning and Engagement.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241008eka8001e1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
