\par\par \uc2 Australia news\par \b \uc2 Veteran activist arrested at campaign to protect Bulga state forest \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Josh Taylor and Amy Remeikis (earlier) \par \uc2 16,316 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 October 2024\par 07:28 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 This blog is now closedExclusive: Crisafulli wedged on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as Katter party flags vote to criminalise terminationsGet our breaking news email, free app or daily news podcast\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.59am BST\par \par \uc2 What we learned: Wednesday 9 October\par \par \uc2 We will wrap the live blog here for today. Here\u8217\'20\'19s what made the news:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The prime minister, Anthony Albanese, apologised a second time {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/oct/09/australia-politics-live-labor-public-ownership-nbn-albanese-apology-tourette-question-time-peter-dutton-labor-liberal-angus-taylor-gambling-ad-ban-greens?page=with:block-670584348f083b19333a7e30#block-670584348f083b19333a7e30"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 for referring to Tourette syndrome}}}\uc2  during an exchange with the shadow treasurer, \uc2 Angus Taylor\uc2 , during question time yesterday.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 More than 120 academics as well as mental health and youth organisations wrote an open letter to the prime minister arguing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/oct/09/australia-politics-live-labor-public-ownership-nbn-albanese-apology-tourette-question-time-peter-dutton-labor-liberal-angus-taylor-gambling-ad-ban-greens?page=with:block-670597f98f08f2b9f7a4984d#block-670597f98f08f2b9f7a4984d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 against plans to ban younger teens from social media}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The federal government introduced legislation to make it harder for a future government to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/oct/09/australia-politics-live-labor-public-ownership-nbn-albanese-apology-tourette-question-time-peter-dutton-labor-liberal-angus-taylor-gambling-ad-ban-greens?page=with:block-6705b2308f08f65f2b7004be#block-6705b2308f08f65f2b7004be"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 privatise the NBN}}}\uc2 , as the opposition questioned why it was needed.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Patricia Karvelas announced she was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/oct/09/australia-politics-live-labor-public-ownership-nbn-albanese-apology-tourette-question-time-peter-dutton-labor-liberal-angus-taylor-gambling-ad-ban-greens?page=with:block-6705bbbc8f083b19333a7fb8#block-6705bbbc8f083b19333a7fb8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 leaving Radio National Breakfast}}}\uc2  after three years, but will take on a new role at the ABC.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Former Labor turned independent senator for WA, Fatima Payman launched her political party, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/oct/09/australia-politics-live-labor-public-ownership-nbn-albanese-apology-tourette-question-time-peter-dutton-labor-liberal-angus-taylor-gambling-ad-ban-greens?page=with:block-6705da058f083b19333a8075#block-6705da058f083b19333a8075"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Australia\u8217\'20\'19s Voice}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Labor re-introduced its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/oct/09/australia-politics-live-labor-public-ownership-nbn-albanese-apology-tourette-question-time-peter-dutton-labor-liberal-angus-taylor-gambling-ad-ban-greens?page=with:block-6705e6ce8f08f65f2b7005fd#block-6705e6ce8f08f65f2b7005fd"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 help-to-buy housing legislation}}}\uc2  into the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\uc2  (\uc2 RBNZ\uc2 ) governor Adrian Orr{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/oct/09/australia-politics-live-labor-public-ownership-nbn-albanese-apology-tourette-question-time-peter-dutton-labor-liberal-angus-taylor-gambling-ad-ban-greens?page=with:block-670600b48f083b19333a8141#block-670600b48f083b19333a8141"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cut the official cash rate to 4.75%}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Two students {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/oct/09/australia-politics-live-labor-public-ownership-nbn-albanese-apology-tourette-question-time-peter-dutton-labor-liberal-angus-taylor-gambling-ad-ban-greens?page=with:block-670605408f08f65f2b7006a7#block-670605408f08f65f2b7006a7"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 were arrested}}}\uc2  at \uc2 Western Sydney University\uc2  (\uc2 WSU\uc2 ) after a pro-Palestine protest on campus.\par \par \uc2 One more sitting day this week tomorrow, and we will be back in the morning with all the news.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.05am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.22am BST\par \par \uc2 Hail lashes south-east Queensland and NSW in wake of severe storms\par \par \uc2 There has been hail up to 7cm in size after severe thunderstorms in southern Queensland and northern NSW on Wednesday afternoon, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.weatherzone.com.au/news/giant-hail-and-intense-rain-lash-qld-nsw/1889977"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Weatherzone has reported}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There was reportedly 35mm of rain in Inverell in 30 minutes, and flooding in Southport, Queensland, as well as between 50 to 70mm in rainfall across northern NSW and Southern Queensland over the seven hours from 9am.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bureau of Meteorology\uc2  has warnings in place for areas between the Capricornia and the southern downs in Queensland, and the northern slopes and plains in NSW.\par \par \uc2 BoM has said the thunderstorm risk will subside tomorrow, but there will still be showers along the east coast of Australia, with anywhere between 50 and 100mm of rain in those hardest hit across Wednesday and Thursday.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.32am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.11am BST\par \par \uc2 The bill to introduce a national student ombudsman has passed the House of Representatives. It will now go to the Senate.\par \par \uc2 enltrBREAKING: Our bill to establish an independent National Student Ombudsman has just passed the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Jason Clare MP (@JasonClareMP) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JasonClareMP/status/1843888691233861950?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 9, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 enltr1 in 20 students have been sexually assaulted since they started university and 1 in six have been sexually harassed.\par \par \uc2 Not enough has been done to address sexual violence in our universities and for too long students haven\u8217\'20\'19t been heard\par \par \uc2 With this legislation that begins to change\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Jason Clare MP (@JasonClareMP) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JasonClareMP/status/1843888694220185703?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 9, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.51am BST\par \par \uc2 Victoria police have identified a man wanted in connection to the alleged assault of a 20-year-old British tourist whose jaw was broken during the altercation.\par \par \uc2 Police on Wednesday morning had released CCTV that showed a man they wanted to speak to in relation to the alleged incident. Sr Const Elissa Dykstra said the alleged incident was \u8220\'20\'1Chorrific\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cnot great to see, especially on a busy Saturday night\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Victoria police said inquiries were ongoing:\par \par \uc2 The person we were seeking to identify following an assault in Richmond has made himself known to police.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.57am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.42am BST\par \par \uc2 Here are some photos from the Bulga forest protest this morning that we reported on earlier.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.45am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.28am BST\par \par \uc2 I am going to hand you over to Josh Taylor now, who will take you through the evening\u8217\'20\'19s news.\par \par \uc2 We will be back with the last sitting day from early tomorrow morning. It is the last sitting day until November (when estimates will also be held) so make sure you get your fill.\par \par \uc2 Until then \u8211\'20\'13 as always \u8211\'20\'13 take care of you.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.22am BST\par \par \uc2 Veteran activist 12th person arrested at community campaign to protect Bulga state forest from logging\par \par \uc2 Veteran forest campaigner Susie Russell has become the 12th person to be arrested in a community campaign to protect the Bulga state forest in mid-north New South Wales from logging.\par \par \uc2 Russell, the vice-president of the North East Forest Alliance, locked herself on to logging machinery at the forest on Wednesday until she was cut off.\par \par \uc2 It comes a day after Russell and other campaigners criticised the government for hypocrisy and \u8220\'20\'1Cbullshit\u8221\'20\'1D for hosting a nature summit in Sydney while habitat for the endangered greater glider was logged just 400km away.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2024/oct/08/environment-summit-taking-place-in-sydney-while-greater-glider-habitat-is-logged-is-bullshit-advocates-say"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Environment summit taking place in Sydney while greater glider habitat is logged is \u8216\'20\'18bullshit\u8217\'20\'19, advocates say}}}\par \par \uc2 She said today:\par \par \uc2 Every hour those machines don\u8217\'20\'19t work is 50 trees saved. Our community will throw itself at the machines for as long as we can. We don\u8217\'20\'19t want to give up any chance to save this precious forest and the greater gliders and koalas that live there.\par \par \uc2 I have no doubt that our actions are on the right side of history. All the science, all the reports, all the experts say that if we want to repair the damage to our planet, we need to start by not doing more damage. That is the most straightforward and cheapest way to make progress.\par \par \uc2 She added she was \u8220\'20\'1Csickened at the inertia of the environment departments and their ministers, at state and federal level. They have no vision, and no appetite for action.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Logging at the Bulga state forest, inland from Port Macquarie, is expected to continue for weeks.\par \par \uc2 The operations this week have occurred close to an area of the forest where Guardian Australia photographed a greater glider emerging from its den in July.\par \par \uc2 The Forestry Corporation of NSW has said trained ecologists had undertaken nocturnal surveys and put exclusion zones in place around greater glider den trees.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.34am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.20am BST\par \par \uc2 High court hears cases relating to federal court re-detention decision\par \par \uc2 The high court is hearing a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/mar/08/australias-high-court-to-hear-two-appeals-over-legality-of-re-detaining-more-than-100-non-citizens"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 trio of cases relating to a retrospective law re-detaining more than 100 unlawful non-citizens}}}\uc2  who were released from immigration detention by the Pearson full federal court decision.\par \par \uc2 In February 2023, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/feb/09/labor-condemned-for-abhorrently-cruel-decision-to-put-100-people-back-in-immigration-detention"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Labor and the Coalition teamed up to pass laws authorising}}}\uc2  the cancellation of visas of people who were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2022/dec/29/up-to-100-people-released-from-immigration-detention-after-government-loses-federal-court-case"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 released from immigration detention}}}\uc2  due to the decision that aggregate sentences did not count for the automatic cancellation of visas for serving a sentence of more than 12 months in prison.\par \par \uc2 Bret Walker, counsel for one of the applicants, said that the legislation had gone beyond \u8220\'20\'1Cunexceptional corrective legislation\u8221\'20\'1D to an \u8220\'20\'1Cimpermissible interference\u8221\'20\'1D or \u8220\'20\'1Cusurpation\u8221\'20\'1D of judicial functions. The legislation \u8220\'20\'1Crequired that Pearson be considered incorrectly decided\u8221\'20\'1D, he submitted.\par \par \uc2 Craig Lenehan, representing the commonwealth respondents, said the cases run counter to a \u8220\'20\'1Clong line of authority\u8221\'20\'1D that there is \u8220\'20\'1Cno interference with judicial power merely because [legislation] declares or amends substantive rights of parties in litigation\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Lenehan said the legislation does not declare that \u8220\'20\'1Cthe law was different than what the court in Pearson declared it to be\u8221\'20\'1D but rather it declares \u8220\'20\'1Cparticular effects\u8221\'20\'1D about validity of things including visa cancellation.\par \par \uc2 Lenehan argued if the original Pearson decision was correct, a person convicted of murder and grievous bodily harm and sentenced to an aggregate 25 years in prison \u8220\'20\'1Cwould not fail the character test\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The high court justices queried why the commonwealth was only arguing Pearson was wrong in one of the three cases. Lenehan replied they were worried it would be an abuse of process because leave to appeal had been denied. The judges noted leave was denied only because the new law overturned its effect. They recommended he reconsider his position, resulting in Lenehan receiving instructions to argue Pearson was wrong in all three cases.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.53am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.17am BST\par \par \uc2 What is Fatima Payman\u8217\'20\'19s position on recognising a Palestinian state?\par \par \uc2 The senator tells the ABC:\par \par \uc2 That would definitely be something that I would advocate strongly for. Something that is overdue. This government has been sitting on its hands.\par \par \uc2 You cannot have a two-state solution when they are advocating without recognising one of those two states.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.19am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.04am BST\par \par \uc2 Powers to take over social media accounts to gather evidence of criminal activity unused\par \par \uc2 Social media account takeover powers granted to federal law enforcement officers haven\u8217\'20\'19t been used once in the 2023-24 financial year, a document tabled shows.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Australian Criminal Intelligence Commission\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s report, tabled Wednesday, shows the powers to take over social media accounts to disrupt criminal activity weren\u8217\'20\'19t used for the entire year.\par \par \uc2 The powers, known as the identity takeover disrupt laws, were introduced in 2021 under the Morrison government and also give \uc2 ACIC\uc2  and the Australian federal police access to other online accounts, including online banking and online forum accounts.\par \par \uc2 The powers are due to sunset on 4 September 2026. The Independent National Security Legislation Monitor {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.inslm.gov.au/news-and-media/media-release-independent-reviewer-consider-special-law-enforcement-surveillance-powers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced in July}}}\uc2  it would conduct a review of the powers to determine how they were actually being used, including for which offences and their effectiveness.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.20am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.03am BST\par \par \uc2 Senate question time, as seen by Mike Bowers:\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.06am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.41am BST\par \par \uc2 Watt reaffirms security checks carried out for Gaza visas\par \par \uc2 While the house question time (which still ran as a repeat on ABC TV) focused on security checks for visas (again) the Senate QT was more of a mixed bag.\par \par \uc2 Business insolvencies, home builds, the cost of living and apprentice numbers were all canvassed.\par \par \uc2 On the issues raised in the House of Representatives earlier today, the answers were the same.\par \par \uc2 Asked whether \uc2 Asio\uc2  cleared a visa (along with the implication these questions were not answered in the House of Representatives), the employment and workplace relations minster, Murray Watt said:\par \par \uc2 These matters have been canvassed quite extensively this morning at question time in the House of Representatives, and I\u8217\'20\'19m very well aware there were answers given. They might not just be the answers that you are hoping for, but there were answers, accurate answers, that were provided.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m very happy to repeat the comments that Minister Burke made, and they were that they that we are working closely with our security agencies on this matter, as people would expect, everyone from Gaza granted a visa since October the 7th has been checked by our security agencies.\par \par \uc2 Every single person has been checked by our security agencies.\par \par \uc2 I think the disturbing thing that we continue to see from the opposition is a complete lack of faith in our security agencies. We have seen that over and over again, and I think, I think, I think it\u8217\'20\'19s very concerning to see this creeping authoritarianism within the opposition.\par \par \uc2 Last week we had the leader of the opposition, Mr Dutton, and other frontbenchers demanding that police, state police, go and arrest certain people this week, we\u8217\'20\'19ve got the opposition trying to direct the security agencies as to what they would do.\par \par \uc2 So that\u8217\'20\'19s a little shadow and a little insight into the kind of operation we would have under a Dutton government, if, God forbid, we ever had a Dutton government.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.26am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.29am BST\par \par \uc2 Richard Marles\uc2  is officially acting prime minister \u8211\'20\'13 Anthony Albanese has taken off for Laos and the Asean conference.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.23am BST\par \par \uc2 Two students arrested during pro-Palestine protest at \uc2 Western Sydney University\par \par \uc2 Two students have been arrested at \uc2 Western Sydney University\uc2  (\uc2 WSU\uc2 ) following a pro-Palestine protest on campus. About 50 people were in attendance at the protest, NSW police estimate.\par \par \uc2 Police say about 11.20am, the two protesters, 24 and 21, were arrested after allegedly assaulting campus security. They allege as police were arresting the pair, one allegedly assaulted and resisted police.\par \par \uc2 Students were gathering to call for the university to cut its ties with Israel and weapons manufacturers, citing financial investments revealed under FoI in weaponised planes, naval weapons and lethal drones.\par \par \uc2 In footage of the protest distributed by the social media page, \uc2 WSU\uc2  4 Palestine Collective, police are seen attempting to remove a banner from protesters while they resist, amid cries of \u8220\'20\'1Cit\u8217\'20\'19s a banner, it\u8217\'20\'19s not a weapon\u8221\'20\'1D. In another video, five police are seen arresting one of the men.\par \par \uc2 Both students have been taken to Gladesville police station where they are assisting police with inquiries.\par \par \uc2 Asked whether it called the police to attend the event and if it was condoned, a spokesperson for \uc2 WSU\uc2  said it was \u8220\'20\'1Caware\u8221\'20\'1D of an incident at its Parramatta South campus during a rally.\par \par \uc2 We are committed to free speech, and respectful and civil debate. Our priority is always the safety, wellbeing and security of every single person across our university community.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.39am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.17am BST\par \par \uc2 A very big thank you to Josh Taylor for picking up the blog for me for that moment.\par \par \uc2 You have Amy Remeikis back with you \u8211\'20\'13 I\u8217\'20\'19ll give you a brief whoosh whoosh of QT.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.18am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.13am BST\par \par \uc2 New Zealand lowers cash rate to 4.75%\par \par \uc2 Interest rates are on the way down in New Zealand, where the central bank has cut 50 basis points from the official cash rate, \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports.\par \par \uc2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\uc2  (\uc2 RBNZ\uc2 ) governor Adrian Orr took the OCR to 4.75% on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 He said:\par \par \uc2 Economic activity in New Zealand is subdued, in part due to restrictive monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Business investment and consumer spending have been weak, and employment conditions continue to soften.\par \par \uc2 The New Zealand economy is now in a position of excess capacity, encouraging price- and wage-setting to adjust to a low-inflation economy.\par \par \uc2 The [\uc2 RBNZ\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s monetary policy] committee agreed that it is appropriate to cut the OCR by 50 basis points to achieve and maintain low and stable inflation, while seeking to avoid unnecessary instability in output, employment, interest rates and the exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 With headline consumers price index (CPI) inflation at 3.3% and almost back in \uc2 RBNZ\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s target band of 1-3%, the central bank has opted to start cutting.\par \par \uc2 Orr said he believed the CPI had lowered beyond that 3.3% figure, last measured in July, with fresh figures due next week.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.17am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.08am BST\par \par \uc2 The Australian Communications Consumer Action Network (Accan) has welcomed the government\u8217\'20\'19s move to introduce legislation to keep the NBN in public ownership.\par \par \uc2 Accan CEO, Carol Bennett, said:\par \par \uc2 Retaining public ownership of this critical public asset will provide the opportunity for continued improvement in the communications services Australians need now and into the future. The NBN in public hands provides ongoing certainty that will ensure Australians\u8217\'20\'19 economic and social wellbeing for years to come.\par \par \uc2 Bennett said the legislation would remove a pathway for future governments to sell off the network.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.12am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.24am BST\par \par \uc2 Over in the Senate, the focus is on the nature-positive laws the government is trying to get support for (the Greens are not a fan).\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s the same answer we heard in yesterday\u8217\'20\'19s QT in the house.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.35am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.23am BST\par \par \uc2 NBN should be sold off when \u8216\'20\'18time is right\u8217\'20\'19, Liberal MP says\par \par \uc2 The Senate is busy being the Senate, so we are just taking a look at Liberal MP \uc2 Zoe McKenzie\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s interview with Sky News.\par \par \uc2 Q: Would you sell it [NBN] off? Was that your plan?\par \par \uc2 McKenzie:\par \par \uc2 To be fair, I\u8217\'20\'19ve never heard that that\u8217\'20\'19s our plan, but it has been in the public debate, the public discussion, that eventually it would be privatised. Remember? It\u8217\'20\'19s interesting to me, you do know that the NBN just paid back the last tranche of public loan money it had to the commonwealth. There was about 5bn paid, I think, on the 30th of June of this year. So now the NBN has technically paid back the commonwealth for the loan it gave of some $20bn. Now all of a sudden it\u8217\'20\'19s urgent that it be in public hands. I don\u8217\'20\'19t get it. It\u8217\'20\'19s like, thanks for the cash, but I want to keep the title \u8211\'20\'13\par \par \uc2 Q: So you\u8217\'20\'19d be relaxed with it being privatised?\par \par \uc2 McKenzie:\par \par \uc2 It has always been in the contemplation that it would be privatised when the time is right.\par \par \uc2 Q: So you think it should be?\par \par \uc2 McKenzie:\par \par \uc2 Eventually, when the time is right, but that\u8217\'20\'19s not now.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.34am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.59am BST\par \par \uc2 The Senate question time is just about to begin and because we have had already brunch QT, we will be covering as much of Senate QT as we can stomach.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.02am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.55am BST\par \par \uc2 We are just about to head into Senate question time, but here is the question time exchange between Tony Burke and \uc2 Dan Tehan\uc2  that we covered earlier in the blog:\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.59am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.47am BST\par \par \uc2 On the campaign trail in Queensland\par \par \uc2 On the Queensland election trail, both major parties spent the day making announcements on their respective core issue: youth crime for the LNP and health for Labor.\par \par \uc2 In Townsville, the LNP promised a new $40m \u8220\'20\'1Cvictims\u8217\'20\'19 advocate service\u8221\'20\'1D, spruiked as a \u8220\'20\'1Cone-stop shop\u8221\'20\'1D for victims of crime. Their leader, David Crisafulli :\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19ll give people updates of the court proceedings. It\u8217\'20\'19ll give continuity of advice so they don\u8217\'20\'19t have to retell their story every time to someone different in a different part of government.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19ll help with financial support, and often we speak to victims who fall between the cracks during a difficult period of time \u8230\'20\'26 and probably above all else, it will connect them with those service providers who do great work in this space.\par \par \uc2 In Brisbane, the premier, \uc2 Steven Miles\uc2 , told journalists \u8220\'20\'1Cwe already have one\u8221\'20\'1D, pointing to the victims\u8217\'20\'19 commissioner set up under Labor.\par \par \uc2 The Labor leader was in the nation\u8217\'20\'19s capital to announce a backflip on payroll tax for GP clinics. The party will now abandon a plan to help clinics transition to new financial arrangements to evade the tax.\par \par \uc2 The new approach is the same as the LNP \u8211\'20\'13 to pass a new legal exemption.\par \par \uc2 Miles said the reversal was necessary \u8220\'20\'1Cbecause of the LNP\u8217\'20\'19s successful misinformation campaign, they were unfortunately successful at convincing GPs that some kind of new tax was coming and that that would affect them and their patients. We want to allay that fear, and we will do so by legislating specifically\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.06am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.37am BST\par \par \uc2 Ramos-Horta says ideal world is one of \u8216\'20\'18peace and cooperation\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Ramos-Horta\uc2  continued his \uc2 National Press Club\uc2  answer by saying Timor-Leste did not discuss \u8220\'20\'1Chigh-stakes security issues with the Chinese\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He said that when he visited China the issue he wanted to discuss most was agriculture and \u8220\'20\'1Chow China can replicate in Timor-Leste the Chinese miracle of eliminating poverty, increasing food production\u8221\'20\'1D. Ramos-Horta said the Chinese president, Xi Jinping, \u8220\'20\'1Cresponded very well\u8221\'20\'1D to that policy discussion.\par \par \uc2 Ramos-Horta acknowledged that there were \u8220\'20\'1Csensitive issues in the South China Sea\u8221\'20\'1D. He said his vision \u8220\'20\'1Cideally, in a romantic world, would be a sea of peace and cooperation\u8221\'20\'1D. He also believed that disputes in the South China Sea \u8220\'20\'1Cshould be discussed bilaterally between the claimant states\u8221\'20\'1D. Ramos-Horta added:\par \par \uc2 To say that, \u8216\'20\'18oh, this is not acceptable because China would put so much pressure on each country,\u8217\'20\'19that they are smaller, weaker\u8217\'20\'19 \u8211\'20\'13 that is patronising to these countries. They may be small, but they have an incredible sense of sovereignty, of pride, and the more pressure you put on them, the more they resist. So it\u8217\'20\'19s total nonsense, these arguments.\par \par \uc2 Ramos-Horta said Timor-Leste currently had an Australian, Portuguese and US military presence. He attracted more laugher with this line:\par \par \uc2 Imagine if we had one or two Chinese military personnel there. No one would sleep in peace in Australia!\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.44am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.27am BST\par \par \uc2 Timor-Leste president \uc2 Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Ramos-Horta\uc2  speaks at \uc2 National Press Club\par \par \uc2 The president of Timor-Leste, \uc2 Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Ramos-Horta\uc2 , has joked that if Australian intelligence services continued to spy on his country\u8217\'20\'19s political leaders \u8220\'20\'1Cthey would find it very boring\u8221\'20\'1D. He told the \uc2 National Press Club\uc2  in Canberra that such snooping would only reveal politicians bad-mouthing each other.\par \par \uc2 His comments are an oblique reference to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2019/aug/10/witness-k-and-the-outrageous-spy-scandal-that-failed-to-shame-australia"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a 2004 scandal when Australian spies bugged Timor-Leste\u8217\'20\'19s cabinet room during sensitive negotiations over access to oil and gas reserves}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 When asked for his perspective about China\u8217\'20\'19s position in the region and whether he was \u8220\'20\'1Cleveraging China against Australia at the moment\u8221\'20\'1D in regard to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/08/ramos-horta-says-75bn-timor-sea-gas-project-could-begin-within-months-if-australia-signs-treaty"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the development of the Greater Sunrise project}}}\uc2 , Ramos-Horta began by suggesting that Australian newspapers sought to increase their sales by focusing on China \u8220\'20\'1Cin a negative way\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Ramos-Horta said he believed that \u8220\'20\'1CAustralian intelligence, Australian military people and the politicians who are really, really informed and educated \u8230\'20\'26 would know that China is not in any shape or form or imagination a threat to Australia\u8221\'20\'1D, adding:\par \par \uc2 It is an economic trade rival. Yes. China steals technology. Who doesn\u8217\'20\'19t steal technology?\par \par \uc2 Ramos-Horta recalled a conversation with a British ambassador when they were both in West Africa:\par \par \uc2 Then there was the issue of espionage, Australians spying on Timor government, and the ambassador, with a British sense of humour, he said, \u8216\'20\'18God, if because of bugging phones people cut [diplomatic] relations, the US wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have relations with anybody, because they bug everybody\u8217\'20\'19s phone around the world.\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 And if, at all, the Australian intelligence continued to bug our phones \u8211\'20\'13 or the Chinese \u8211\'20\'13 they would find it very boring, very tedious, because the only thing you end up finding out is Timorese politicians and leaders bad-mouth each other all the time.\par \par \uc2 The comment attracted laughter from the \uc2 National Press Club\uc2  audience.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.47am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.24am BST\par \par \uc2 Rowland holds press conference on gambling and NBN\par \par \uc2 Michelle Rowland\uc2  has also held a (belated) press conference (the first one was cancelled because it clashed with Fatima Payman\u8217\'20\'19s presser) about the legislation to keep the NBN in public hands.\par \par \uc2 There is an argument that this legislation is fixing to address a problem that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t exist \u8211\'20\'13 no one is proposing to sell the NBN. So you could say that this is something the government has created so it has something to talk about.\par \par \uc2 But on the flip side, there is also the point that we are very late in the electoral cycle and if you wanted to do something, well now is the time to do it.\par \par \uc2 The Rowland presser though, was mostly focused on the gambling reforms and when that response was coming.\par \par \uc2 Rowland told Josh Butler:\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ll work with the states and territories on how to do it effectively, we will be taking advice from the department and from the regulator on the best way in which to achieve that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But there was no real answer beyond that \u8211\'20\'13 a timeless statement.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.29am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.17am BST\par \par \uc2 Labor reintroduces Help to Buy legislation in the House of Representatives\par \par \uc2 Over in the House, Clare O\u8217\'20\'19Neil has reintroduced the shared equity scheme Help to Buy (mark II) as part of the government\u8217\'20\'19s broader aim to have the focus shift back to domestic issues.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/search/display/display.w3p;page=0;query=BillId%3Ar7251%20Recstruct%3Abillhome"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Help to Buy (Consequential Provisions) Bill 2023 [No. 2] is now in the parliament.}}}\uc2  May Dolly have mercy on it.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.22am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.12am BST\par \par \uc2 Gerard Rennick\uc2  to announce People First party\u8217\'20\'19s monetary policy today\par \par \uc2 It is a day of new political parties. \uc2 Gerard Rennick\uc2  has announced he will be unveiling his new political party\u8217\'20\'19s policy on monetary/capital markets policy very soon. That\u8217\'20\'19s the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/article/2024/aug/26/gerard-rennick-quits-lnp-liberal-national-launch-people-first-party"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 People First party}}}\uc2 , if you missed that news.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.17am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.42am BST\par \par \uc2 Fatima Payman: a new breed of politician?\par \par \uc2 Fatima Payman certainly knows how to attract a crowd in this place.\par \par \uc2 Payman is also part of a new breed of politicians who don\u8217\'20\'19t mind calling out questions they think go beyond respectability at press conferences. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  made recent comments about seeing young women immediately calling out questions and behaviour they disagreed with and how it was one of the things she admired most about younger generations.\par \par \uc2 Payman has at least another five years in the Senate \u8211\'20\'13 might be something reporters have to get used to.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.55am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.35am BST\par \par \uc2 Payman says Australia\u8217\'20\'19s Voice party is not about \u8216\'20\'18whether you sit on the left or right\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Asked where Australia\u8217\'20\'19s Voice sits within the Overton window, Fatima Payman says:\par \par \uc2 The ideological spectrum of whether you sit on the left or right, this is not what we\u8217\'20\'19re talking about here. This is a party for all Australians. We\u8217\'20\'19re going to ensure that everyone is represented, whether it\u8217\'20\'19s the mums and dads who are trying to make and make ends meet, or the young students out there, or whether it\u8217\'20\'19s the grandparents who want to have, you know, dignity and respect as they age.\par \par \uc2 Q: That\u8217\'20\'19s a pretty broad group with lots of different interests. How do you make sure that all these different competing interests are represented?\par \par \uc2 Payman:\par \par \uc2 Isn\u8217\'20\'19t that beautiful? That Australia, being one of the most multicultural, mature democracies in our world, we\u8217\'20\'19re able to represent people of all walks of life \u8230\'20\'26 and that\u8217\'20\'19s what Australia\u8217\'20\'19s voice is going to do. It\u8217\'20\'19s not going to be an easy task. I appreciate that, but we need to capture everyone\u8217\'20\'19s concerns and make sure that they\u8217\'20\'19ve got a voice here in Canberra.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.38am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.34am BST\par \par \uc2 Payman promises future MPs a conscience vote on every matter\par \par \uc2 Fatima Payman says she has not had conversations with any other crossbenchers about joining her party and will be considering all options.\par \par \uc2 As to what would happen if there was a disagreement with any future MP, Payman says:\par \par \uc2 As a person of principle, as somebody who voted on her conscience, we will definitely have a conscience vote. And I will support anyone else\u8217\'20\'19s, should Australia\u8217\'20\'19s Voice have a member elected. We will ensure that they have the opportunity to exercise a conscience vote. We\u8217\'20\'19re a beautiful democracy, and we should exercise on every matter a conscience vote on every matter.\par \par \uc2 Well, on matters that are important, obviously, you know, as a party we would have value alignment. But at the same time, that\u8217\'20\'19s something that even if I disagree with them on, it would be important to appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.38am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.29am BST\par \par \uc2 Payman says she admires Greens but Voice party will be more practical and pragmatic\par \par \uc2 You can find the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.australiasvoice.com.au/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 website for Fatima Payman\u8217\'20\'19s party here}}}\par \par \uc2 The senator is asked about the party name \u8211\'20\'13 Australia\u8217\'20\'19s Voice \u8211\'20\'13 and whether it is related to the voice referendum. She is also asked what will make it different to the Greens :\par \par \uc2 Payman says:\par \par \uc2 In regards to what makes us different from the Greens, well, I admire the Greens for their passion. But I\u8217\'20\'19ve heard from Australians that they think sometimes the Greens go way too far. And that, you know, when it comes to practicality or pragmatism, there needs to be that level of engagement with what\u8217\'20\'19s possible and what can be achieved.\par \par \uc2 And in regards to the name, Australia\u8217\'20\'19s Voice, we\u8217\'20\'19ve consulted with First Nations people. We\u8217\'20\'19ve consulted with elders from that community who actually feel like the current government is not representing them, and they are being treated as electoral poison.\par \par \uc2 Any form of Indigenous issues is being treated as electoral poison when it comes to the government and I\u8217\'20\'19ve heard first-hand from First Nations elders that, you know, they didn\u8217\'20\'19t realise and would never have fathomed that they would be far worse off post a failed referendum and that was a constitutional matter.\par \par \uc2 But what we\u8217\'20\'19re proposing and perhaps even asking for it to be considered, is maybe there is an opportunity to have that level of engagement with First Nations elders throughout, through our parliamentary processes.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.32am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.26am BST\par \par \uc2 Payman says no decision on whether to run candidate in PM\u8217\'20\'19s seat\par \par \uc2 Fatima Payman says Australia\u8217\'20\'19s Voice may run a candidate in Anthony Albanese\u8217\'20\'19s seat, but has not made any decisions.\par \par \uc2 In terms of the target seats, we have not had those decisions yet. But, you know, the prime minister challenged me the other day, so we might consider that maybe.\par \par \uc2 Maybe not.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.32am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.25am BST\par \par \uc2 Payman says \u8216\'20\'18not ruling out anyone\u8217\'20\'19 in terms of candidates\par \par \uc2 Q: What kind of backgrounds are you looking for? We have heard in the past interest from some Muslim groups to engage with you. Will you be hoping to recruit some people from Muslim backgrounds to run as part of your party?\par \par \uc2 Fatima Payman:\par \par \uc2 The candidate selection will occur in due course \u8230\'20\'26 it will be based on merit and value alignment when it comes to selecting those candidates. We are not ruling out anyone again. Australia\u8217\'20\'19s voice is for each and every person, and we welcome candidates.\par \par \uc2 And to just add to that, we\u8217\'20\'19ve already received so much interest from disenfranchised Labor candidates, former Labor candidates. We\u8217\'20\'19ve had people from the National party reach out and express interest. So there\u8217\'20\'19s already a growing number of people putting their hand up.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.34am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.23am BST\par \par \uc2 Payman says Voice party\u8217\'20\'19s policy platform will come \u8216\'20\'18in due course\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 What will Australia\u8217\'20\'19s Voice be focused on, in terms of issues?\par \par \uc2 Fatima Payman:\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re obviously launching the party today. The policy platform will come in time, and I\u8217\'20\'19m very excited to publicly announce them in due course.\par \par \uc2 All you have to do is look back at a few of my speeches since becoming an independent, whether that\u8217\'20\'19s negative gearing and capital gains tax in terms of housing affordability, or whether it\u8217\'20\'19s looking at our aged care reform bills or whether it\u8217\'20\'19s looking at early childhood education, making sure that parents and families are able to put food on the table while ensuring their kids get a good education.\par \par \uc2 So there\u8217\'20\'19s lots to look at. But obviously we will be announcing it in due course.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.34am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.22am BST\par \par \uc2 Payman says Labor party has \u8216\'20\'18lost its way\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Fatima Payman, the leader of Australia\u8217\'20\'19s Voice (which was registered last week, according to ABN records), says:\par \par \uc2 Australians are fed up with the major parties having a duopoly, a stranglehold over our democracy. If we need to drag the two major parties kicking and screaming to do what needs to be done, we will do it. I\u8217\'20\'19m reminded of the quote by the great \uc2 Gough Whitlam\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18There are some people who are so frightened to put a foot forward, to put a foot wrong, that they won\u8217\'20\'19t put a foot forward.\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 This comment was made in 1985 and applies so much to the current Labor party, who has lost its way.\par \par \uc2 Australia\u8217\'20\'19s Voice believes in a system where people come first, where your concerns are not just heard, but acted upon.\par \par \uc2 We reject the status quo that serves the powerful and ignores the rest, the forgotten people.\par \par \uc2 As Robert Menzies put it, Australia\u8217\'20\'19s voice is here to build a future where every Australian, regardless of their background, their colour, their faith or circumstances, have a voice that matters. This is more than a party. It is a movement for a fairer, more inclusive Australia.\par \par \uc2 Together we will hold our leaders accountable and ensure that your voice, Australia\u8217\'20\'19s voice, will never be silenced.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.35am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.20am BST\par \par \uc2 Payman says new party Australia\u8217\'20\'19s Voice will be \u8216\'20\'18for the disenfranchised and the unheard\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Fatima Payman is announcing her new political party.\par \par \uc2 The WA senator left the Labor party after a disagreement with Labor\u8217\'20\'19s position on Gaza.\par \par \uc2 She has named the party Australia\u8217\'20\'19s Voice.\par \par \uc2 [So many have felt] a growing frustration, a feeling of being left behind. Of shouting into the void. Only for their concerns to fall on deaf ears. So many of you have told me with emotions in your hearts, we need something different. We need a voice. It is this cry for change that has brought us here today. Because we can no longer sit by while our voices are drowned out by the same old politics.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s time to stand up, to rise together and to take control of our future. And so it is with great humility and deep responsibility that I announce the formation of Australia\u8217\'20\'19s Voice, a new political party for the disenfranchised, the unheard, and those yearning for real change.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.35am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.14am BST\par \par \uc2 David McBride allowed to appeal over jail sentence\par \par \uc2 The former army lawyer David McBride will be allowed to appeal his jail sentence for stealing defence documents and leaking them to the ABC after launching a late court application.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday morning, McBride appeared in the ACT supreme court in his application to file an appeal outside of the usual 28-day window.\par \par \uc2 After the Commonwealth Director of Public Prosecutions said it would not oppose, the court\u8217\'20\'19s registrar granted approval for the appeal in a packed courtroom full of McBride\u8217\'20\'19s supporters.\par \par \uc2 McBride\u8217\'20\'19s legal team said they were seeking to have the appeal heard in March 2024 for a day.\par \par \uc2 Both legal teams will return to court next Thursday to confirm what dates they agree to hold the appeal.\par \par \uc2 McBride said he was grateful to the supreme court registrar for allowing him to attend and said he would probably not appear again in the following week.\par \par \uc2 As the former military lawyer exited the courtroom, the crowd of a few dozen clapped and cheered, shouting support.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/05/david-mcbride-whistleblower-conviction-appeal-adf-ntwnfb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Former army lawyer David McBride appeals conviction claiming duty to disclose military secrets}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.32am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.03am BST\par \par \uc2 How has \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  become a political issue again?\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has become a political issue again, being discussed in the Queensland election campaign.\par \par \uc2 Queensland Labor decrimalised it in a very, very fraught campaign where LNP MPs were given a conscience vote. Those that voted with the government \u8211\'20\'13 which already had the numbers \u8211\'20\'13 found themselves facing preselection battles for what had been very safe seats. They faced the challengers down, but it was one of those \u8216\'20\'18battle for the heart of the party\u8217\'20\'19 moments.\par \par \uc2 Finance minister Katy Gallagher was asked about it on ABC radio this morning and said:\par \par \uc2 I look forward to the day when women\u8217\'20\'19s health isn\u8217\'20\'19t a political football that\u8217\'20\'19s used and pulled out at election time. I think there\u8217\'20\'19s been a lot of legislative reform, rightly, across states and territories.\par \par \uc2 And it is primarily an issue for states and territories around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation, to make sure it\u8217\'20\'19s not a criminal offence and to put in place the legislative framework that allows women to make choices about their own \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  or involve doctors as appropriate.\par \par \uc2 And I really think it\u8217\'20\'19s a matter between a woman and her health professional, and it\u8217\'20\'19s unfortunate we see it rise up and down at election times, but I look forward to a day when we don\u8217\'20\'19t have that political football brought out and used, and that this is treated, really as it should be, as a health matter between a woman and her health professional.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.27am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.54am BST\par \par \uc2 Queensland opposition leader asked about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws \u8211\'20\'13 again\par \par \uc2 Queensland\u8217\'20\'19s opposition leader has refused to say which way he would personally vote on a Katter\u8217\'20\'19s Australian party \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bill to be introduced next parliament.\par \par \uc2 David Crisafulli spent the third day straight giving the same answers on the issue: \u8220\'20\'1Cthere will no change\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cit\u8217\'20\'19s not part of our plan\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He again refused to say whether his party \u8211\'20\'13 most of whom opposed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legalisation in 2018 \u8211\'20\'13 would be granted a conscience vote on the issue. Two LNP MPs have in recent weeks said in public that they would vote to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , if it came before parliament.\par \par \uc2 KAP leader Robbie Katter told Sky News this morning \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m your huckleberry, the KAP\u8217\'20\'19ll put it in\u8221\'20\'1D by introducing a private members\u8217\'20\'19 bill to bring the issue before parliament.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m up for anything,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Crisafulli repeated on Wednesday: \u8220\'20\'1CNot part of our plan.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CKatter\u8217\'20\'19s priority is recriminalising \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Labor\u8217\'20\'19s priority is running a scare campaign to brush over their failings in crime. Our plan is dealing with youth crime. So over to you Queensland.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Labor leader \uc2 Steven Miles\uc2  said his party would be granted a conscience vote.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s been our long-held practice to have a conscience vote on those matters, but you know what our conscience says. Labor MPs support a women\u8217\'20\'19s right to choose. And you know what the LNP MPs\u8217\'20\'19 consciences say too; they are overwhelmingly opposed to a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to choose.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Miles pointed out that all LNP MPs voted against a bill making \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  more available earlier in 2024.\par \par \uc2 Now we know that there will be a bill next term, and MPs should tell their constituents, or candidates should tell voters how they would vote on that bill.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.27am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.50am BST\par \par \uc2 RBA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Covid-era lending fund cost $9bn but \u8216\'20\'18helped prevent dire economic outcomes\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Christopher Kent, a Reserve Bank assistant governor, is giving a speech this morning in Sydney related to the central bank\u8217\'20\'19s term funding facility, one of its key tools to keep the economy afloat during the Covid pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The funding provided low-cost three-year funding to commercial banks and in so doing, helped lower borrowing costs at a time when markets were seizing up. The \uc2 RBA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s cash rate was already on the way to just 0.1%, or about as low as it could go.\par \par \uc2 By the end of its operations, in mid-2021, the facility extended $188bn, with the major banks collecting $133bn and foreign banks $22bn.\par \par \uc2 All the money was repaid, and on average, outstanding lending rates fell by almost 100 basis points (or 1 percentage point).\par \par \uc2 In sum, the facility \u8220\'20\'1Cmet the objectives we set out for it at the start of the pandemic. It helped prevent dire economic outcomes at a time when the outlook was bleak and highly uncertain, and there was limited scope for further cuts to the cash rate,\u8221\'20\'1D Kent said.\par \par \uc2 The cost to the \uc2 RBA\uc2 , though, wasn\u8217\'20\'19t small, at $9bn. One reason for the loss size was that the lending rate was fixed. When the economy recovered faster than expected and the \uc2 RBA\uc2  hiked its cash rate, what had been profitable until May 2022 started losing money for the bank.\par \par \uc2 Similarly, the extension of the facility in September 2020 turned out to have been unnecessary (although officials had little idea then how Covid variants would play out at the time).\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 RBA\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cwould consider\u8221\'20\'1D using the TFF again \u8220\'20\'1Cin extreme circumstances when the cash rate target had been lowered to the full extent possible\u8221\'20\'1D, Kent said. And next time, a flexible rate would be on offer since the \uc2 RBA\uc2  and the banks have since upgraded their systems - one lesson learnt.\par \par \uc2 What might trigger the next meltdown? Who knows - and Kent doesn\u8217\'20\'19t speculate - but perhaps that will come up in the speech Q&A. (US elections from 5 November, sadly, may be the next big market challenge.)\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.03am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.47am BST\par \par \uc2 Fatima Payman to announce new political party\par \par \uc2 For those asking, yes, Fatima Payman will absolutely be announcing her new party at the 12.15 press conference.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.54am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.46am BST\par \par \uc2 Albanese: \u8216\'20\'18When people fire on Israel, Israel has a right to defend itself\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 The interview moves on.\par \par \uc2 Q: The \uc2 US State Department\uc2  overnight said that Israeli incursions to degrade \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  infrastructure [the US supports that], do you?\par \par \uc2 Anthony Albanese:\par \par \uc2 I support Israel\u8217\'20\'19s right to defend itself. We\u8217\'20\'19ve done that from the very beginning. But I also, along with all of the \uc2 G7\uc2  nations, including the United States, including us \u8211\'20\'13 we were part of the statement of 13 countries, the United States, Australia, Canada, Japan, the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , as well as Saudi Arabia and the UAE, we have said that there needs to be a deescalation.\par \par \uc2 They called for a ceasefire in order to have peace in that Israeli Lebanon border, and they call for the implementation of the \uc2 UN Security Council\uc2  resolution that was carried on.\par \par \uc2 Q: Just to clarify, or do you support the US position\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Albanese:\par \par \uc2 We have supported, always supported, the same position as the United States have put here. And the United States have consistently said that there needs to be an agreement, and there were negotiations with Israel and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  prior to the current escalation, and you need a deescalation in order to have a diplomatic solution.\par \par \uc2 If you don\u8217\'20\'19t say that that\u8217\'20\'19s the case are you saying that this conflict should just continue to escalate ad infinitum with no end?\par \par \uc2 \u8230\'20\'26.Now we support Israel\u8217\'20\'19s right to defend itself, and how it defends itself matters.\par \par \uc2 So we have always said that from the very beginning, from October 8, I said that on the Insiders program, and we did that in the resolution that was carried in the parliament that I moved.\par \par \uc2 Q: Just is that contradictory?\par \par \uc2 Albanese:\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s the same position that the \uc2 G7\uc2  countries have had. It\u8217\'20\'19s the same position that everyone in terms of world leaders, it\u8217\'20\'19s the same position that \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  in the UK have said, that President Biden in the United States has said, that all of our allies and partners who we work with have said, that they want a deescalation of this conflict, that they want a ceasefire that enables Israel to continue to exist with security. Now, Israel is taking this action because there isn\u8217\'20\'19t security there, and when people fire on Israel, Israel has a right to defend itself.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.56am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.39am BST\par \par \uc2 Ask Amy: what\u8217\'20\'19s the deal with party preferences?\par \par \uc2 In case it needs to be made clear, voters decide their preferences in the voting booth.\par \par \uc2 Political parties can make suggestions and list the order they would like their supporters to place candidates in particular races, and there is evidence that some voters, particularly those considered \u8220\'20\'1Crusted-on\u8221\'20\'1D, do follow those how-to-vote cards.\par \par \uc2 But there are also large numbers of people who make their own decisions and the political parties just have to see where those votes end up.\par \par \uc2 Much like accepting a vote of someone voted into the parliament, politicians have no choice but to accept what voters decide to do in terms of preferences. You cannot reject a vote (although sometimes political parties will send one of their own out of the chamber to offset a vote, which makes a point but doesn\u8217\'20\'19t alter the voting record) and you cannot reject a voter\u8217\'20\'19s preferences.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.57am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.35am BST\par \par \uc2 Albanese reiterates goal of Labor in majority government\par \par \uc2 Back to the Sky News interview, Anthony Albanese is asked:\par \par \uc2 If you\u8217\'20\'19ve given your view of the Greens and you believe that many of their views are repugnant, how do you accept preferences from them?\par \par \uc2 (That sound you hear is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Psephology"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 psephologists}}}\uc2  around the nation screaming.)\par \par \uc2 Anthony Albanese:\par \par \uc2 Well, I don\u8217\'20\'19t accept preferences \u8211\'20\'13 people go into polling booths and put numbers next to names. So the preference allocation, of course, will be done by the organisational wing.\par \par \uc2 I make it very clear, as occurred when I was deputy prime minister in 2013 under \uc2 Kevin Rudd\uc2 , that there were no deals done.\par \par \uc2 That was a minority parliament, no deals were done in 2013 and I certainly am seeking to be prime minister of governing in my own right \u8230\'20\'26with a majority, I want people to vote [No 1] Labor.\par \par \uc2 There is something called One Nation whose views on race are pretty repugnant as well.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.39am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.31am BST\par \par \uc2 Fatima Payman calls press conference for today\par \par \uc2 Looks like an announcement from Fatima Payman on her new political party is imminent.\par \par \uc2 The independent senator has called a press conference in about an hour\u8217\'20\'19s time.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.48am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.30am BST\par \par \uc2 Albanese says \u8216\'20\'18no deals with the Greens\u8217\'20\'19 even in minority government\par \par \uc2 The prime minister is sitting down for an interview with Sky News ahead of his trip to Laos. It is focused on Australia\u8217\'20\'19s position on Israel and the Greens :\par \par \uc2 Q: The leaders of two peak Jewish groups have written to you and the opposition leader, urging you to preference each other above the Greens, given their repugnant position on the Middle East, would you consider that?\par \par \uc2 Anthony Albanese:\par \par \uc2 Well, of course, preferences will be done by the organisation, but I make this point, or a few points here.\par \par \uc2 One is, the only reason why the Greens are in the Queensland Parliament is because the LNP chose to put them there to give them preferences.\par \par \uc2 My objective, of course, is to lead, on an ongoing basis, the majority Labor government. That is what we will do.\par \par \uc2 The LNP in Queensland, in the election that\u8217\'20\'19s underway at the moment, talking and giving preferences to One Nation above the Labor Party. I find that extraordinary, and that contradicts something that began under \uc2 John Howard\uc2  of putting One Nation last.\par \par \uc2 Q: You said it\u8217\'20\'19s a matter for the party, and it is, but it\u8217\'20\'19s also a matter for moral leadership, isn\u8217\'20\'19t it? Would you consider doing that, and also in terms of the preferences, but also in terms of the minority government, if we do end up in that situation, would you rule out bringing in the Greens in any way, given what you yourself believe, their views on the Middle East are repugnant? They\u8217\'20\'19re accusing you of being complicit in genocide.\par \par \uc2 Albanese:\par \par \uc2 Absolutely, I rule that out. I\u8217\'20\'19m the only candidate for prime minister seeking a government in our own right, and there\u8217\'20\'19ll be no deals with the Greens. I want a majority Labor government, and the best way that that can be achieved is by people voting Labor in the election when it\u8217\'20\'19s held.\par \par \uc2 Unlike the Coalition that seem to have \u8211\'20\'13 they, of course, are a coalition themselves, with the Liberals and the Nationals, often having different objectives \u8211\'20\'13 and they seem to have written off a range of the seats that they\u8217\'20\'19ve held historically, don\u8217\'20\'19t seem to be even campaigning in them or trying to appeal to voters in those seats.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.36am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.17am BST\par \par \uc2 Albanese details Asean summit agenda as he prepares to depart\par \par \uc2 Anthony Albanese:\par \par \uc2 I will be heading to Laos attend meetings of the East Asia Summit. While there, I will be meeting with Chinese Premier Li, building on the patient, delivered and calibrated work that this government has in stabilising the relationship with China.\par \par \uc2 They will also be bilateral meetings with the prime ministers of Canada, New Zealand, Vietnam, the president of the Republic of Korea as well as meeting for the first time with new prime ministers. Prime Minister \uc2 Shigeru Ishiba\uc2  \u8230\'20\'26 I congratulate him in his election as a prime minister of Japan, that is a critical relationship and I rang him and had a good conversation with him initially, the relationship which in Japan is not just an economic one but increasingly one that\u8217\'20\'19s important for our national security cooperation as well.\par \par \uc2 As well, I\u8217\'20\'19ll be meeting the new prime minister of Thailand as well as having other meetings with leaders from our region.\par \par \uc2 The economy and national security will be the focus of the meetings.\par \par \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2  adds his \u8220\'20\'1Ctravel well, be successful\u8221\'20\'1D team Australia message and the chamber moves on.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.20am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.02am BST\par \par \uc2 Question time ends\par \par \uc2 Morning question time is even more unhinged than afternoon question time, it seems.\par \par \uc2 It ends with everyone annoyed. The prime minister is now giving a speech on indulgence about his upcoming trip to the Asean conference (which he is leaving for in a matter of hours).\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.10am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.55am BST\par \par \uc2 Burke challenges opposition over confidence in security agencies\par \par \uc2 Tony Burke continues that answer after a point of order that was not a point of order. He is very fired up:\par \par \uc2 The guarantee of the government gets, and the guarantee in our security agencies. is the same guarantee [the] leader of the opposition would have given when he [oversaw] on visitor visas 4,994 people from Iraq, controlled by its Islamic State, described to other people who stood here as a death cult.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s the same guarantee the leader of the opposition would have given when he held my job about the 1,505 people he [took] in from Syria on visitor visas.\par \par \uc2 The same guarantee of the leader of the opposition would have given with respect to 864 people who came here from the Palestinian territories.\par \par \uc2 If you want to be on the side of the chamber, and you don\u8217\'20\'19t have confidence in our security agencies, say so.\par \par \uc2 Because this government has confidence in our security agencies and those opposite, those opposite are now wanting to demand a whole lot of examples which, [if] tested against their record, they would fail.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.25am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.50am BST\par \par \uc2 Sparring continues over Palestinian visas\par \par \uc2 Dan Tehan\uc2  will not be deterred.\par \par \uc2 Can the prime minister guarantee his government has not granted a visa to any individual \u8230\'20\'26 [who] has links to a terrorist organisation?\par \par \uc2 Tony Burke takes this one as well:\par \par \uc2 I said earlier in terms of terrorist organisations that I didn\u8217\'20\'19t want to add to the words of \uc2 Mike Burgess\uc2  or bury them, I have the quotes, I will read it directly:\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18If you think terrorism is OK, if you think the destruction of the state of Israel is OK, if you think \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and what they did on 7 October is OK, I can tell you that is not OK and from security assessment point of view you will not pass muster.\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 That is the test, that is a security test provided. The members [asks] what guarantee can be provided? I tell you the guarantee is the guarantee of our security agencies being responsible and a government that has confidence in them.\par \par \uc2 Which I might say is the same guarantee the leader of the opposition would have provided with respect to 1991 people coming from \uc2 Taliban\uc2  controlled Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.53am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.39am BST\par \par \uc2 Liberals continue visa attacks over Palestinian artist\par \par \uc2 Dan Tehan\uc2  asks:\par \par \uc2 Prime minister, does supporting \uc2 Hamas\uc2  pass the character test for an Australian visa?\par \par \uc2 Tony Burke:\par \par \uc2 The question is with respect to character and different flags put forward by \uc2 Asio\uc2  which have been answered by \uc2 Mike Burgess\uc2  clearly.\par \par \uc2 I am not going to change a word of what has been said with respect to that.\par \par \uc2 Tehan then asks:\par \par \uc2 The Albanese government granted a visa to Fayez Elhasani, linked to \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and Islamic Palestinian jihad. Can the prime minister guarantee that his government is not granting visas to any other individuals from the war zone who have links to listed terrorist organisations? I give the call to the Leader of the House.\par \par \uc2 Burke looks pretty fed up by now and says:\par \par \uc2 There are seven or eight different ways of asking the same question so I guess we get the same answer again. Which is to say that our security agencies never stop collecting information, never stop collecting information and it is exactly as it should be.\par \par \uc2 And when people first apply for a visa, the movement best represents the information they already had, the different alerts that can be resolved in an Asio referral.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.43am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.37am BST\par \par \uc2 The Coalition continues to ask about Palestinian visas and the security checks.\par \par \uc2 So it is worth keeping this story from Daniel Hurst in mind, about the realities of the time it took for visas to be approved.\par \par \uc2 It was not, as claimed by the Coalition, 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/05/visas-for-palestinians-taking-average-of-four-months-data-shows-not-24-hours-as-coalition-claimed-ntwnfb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Visas for Palestinians take median time of four months to process, despite Coalition claims}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.35am BST\par \par \uc2 Speaker calls for order after continual opposition interjections\par \par \uc2 Milton Dick again has to tell the opposition to shut it, after even more interjections during a dixer. He calls out Paul Fletcher for \u8220\'20\'1Cyelling\u8221\'20\'1D which he says, again \u8220\'20\'1Cis disrespectful\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 This is the last QT the prime minister will be at this week, and it is the last QT until the first week of November, so everyone is feeling a bit rowdy.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.36am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.29am BST\par \par \uc2 Burke says visa concellations relating to Gaza to date have been offshore\par \par \uc2 The Liberal\u8217\'20\'19s Julian Leeser takes the next non-government question and asks:\par \par \uc2 Have any of the tourist visas granted to those coming from the terrorist-controlled Gaza war zone since 7 October 2023 been cancelled?\par \par \uc2 Tony Burke also takes this one:\par \par \uc2 I will deal with the question into parts.\par \par \uc2 Specifically first with reference to the issue of visa cancellations. Secondly with the concept of visas coming from war zones.\par \par \uc2 First of all with respect to cancellations, the situation is the same as what I last advised parliament which is some cancellations have occurred and may well occur offshore but that does not change the fact that the government, the security agencies never stopped collecting information.\par \par \uc2 As has happened, no matter who is in government, and we continue to conduct various checks on people who have visas whether they are onshore or offshore.\par \par \uc2 Specific to the question, the cancellations so far have been offshore.\par \par \uc2 On the issue which has been raised in the preface to the question and raised a number of times in this house, is about the use of visitor visas for people coming from war zones.\par \par \uc2 Because when the people from Syria who came on humanitarian visas were separately interviewed, there\u8217\'20\'19s been a presumption that they were the only people that came from the war zone during that time.\par \par \uc2 It is to be remembered that during the period those opposite were in office and a large portion of that, the leader of the opposition was the chief minister in charge of these programs \u8211\'20\'13 Afghanistan, large portions of which were controlled by the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , did they put a ban on visitor visas from Afghanistan?\par \par \uc2 No \u8211\'20\'13 1,991 visitor visas [were] granted from Afghanistan during that period.\par \par \uc2 From Iraq and Syria, during the period that \uc2 Islamic State\uc2  controlled large parts of Iraq and Syria, did they put a ban on visitor visas in the region?\par \par \uc2 No. Over 4,000 visitors [from the region], 1,500 by visitors from Syria.\par \par \uc2 And similarly, during the entire period where the Gaza Strip was controlled by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , 864 visitor visas from the Palestinian territory.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.32am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.25am BST\par \par \uc2 Opposition rages on during government dixers on the NBN\par \par \uc2 The dixers on the NBN bill continue and the opposition cannot hide their contempt. The rowdiness from the coalition side of the chamber is growing \u8211\'20\'13 as our Josh Butler, who is in the chamber, points out, they are now openly mocking government MPs during the dixer answers.\par \par \uc2 (If you need an explainer on dixers, have a peek below.)\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2022/jul/28/australian-parliament-101-your-questions-about-key-words-and-processes-answered"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Australian parliament 101: your questions about key words and processes, answered}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.30am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.20am BST\par \par \uc2 Barbs continue to fly in question time\par \par \uc2 That moment of unity came directly after the prime minister took a dixer on the NBN legislation, where he spoke about \u8220\'20\'1Cwhat the NBN has been reduced to\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Our own Daniel Hurst hears \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2  interject with \u8220\'20\'1Cwhat have you been reduced to, prime minister?\u8221\'20\'1D so it seems the early QT has done nothing to improve moods.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.23am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.19am BST\par \par \uc2 House honours former Tasmanian premiere \uc2 Peter Gutwein\par \par \uc2 There is a moment of unity to honour former Tasmanian premier \uc2 Peter Gutwein\uc2 , who is a guest in the chamber today.\par \par \uc2 Anthony Albanese:\par \par \uc2 I had a meeting with the former premier of Tasmania. He is on a mission to celebrate the role that migrants have played in [Australia\u8217\'20\'19s] story up until now, but also in Australia\u8217\'20\'19s future.\par \par \uc2 And Mr Gutwein has, through his experience in Tasmania, wanted to reaffirm the role of multiculturalism in this country and who we are in 2024.\par \par \uc2 He spoke to me about the extraordinary contribution of people particularly from the subcontinent making the delivery of services in Tasmania but we spoke about the range of services and the role that migrants are playing essentially in agriculture in the great story that is modern Australia.\par \par \uc2 And I certainly reaffirm my government\u8217\'20\'19s commitment to social inclusion \u8211\'20\'13 a commitment to support full multiculturalism and cohesion as well as one of the reasons why I appointed Peter Khalil as the special envoy on social inclusion.\par \par \uc2 We cannot take this for granted and I congratulate him on his leadership in undertaking this big journey. His journey, his walk is similar to the walk Australia has taken towards this modern, vibrant, dynamic multicultural society that we are. We cannot take it for granted, we have to cherish it and nurture it and I thank Mr Gutwein for his contribution [and his] post-parliamentary life, his ongoing contribution [in] public life. Well done.\par \par \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2  joins with the prime minister\u8217\'20\'19s words in support.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.22am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.15am BST\par \par \uc2 Opposition continue attacks over visa of Palestinan refugee\par \par \uc2 Dan Tehan\uc2  is back:\par \par \uc2 The Albanese government granted a visa to Fayez Elhasani, an individually openly linked to terrorist organisations \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and \uc2 Palestinian Islamic Jihad\uc2  in 2019. Al Hassani reportedly hosted a summit of terror organisation leaders, which he opened by stating they must quote, I quote, \u8220\'20\'1Cconfront the occupation by all possible means\u8221\'20\'1D.Prime Minister, has the visa of this individual been canceled \u8230\'20\'26 ?\par \par \uc2 Tony Burke takes this one too:\par \par \uc2 The question is not different to the last one, and the answer is not different to the last one either. If at any point we receive advice from our security agencies that a visa should be canceled, that is what will happen. That is what will happen. But this concept that shadow ministers should \u8230\'20\'26 be the proxies for our security agencies is just a bizarre way to operate.\par \par \uc2 Our security agencies work through the evidence that they have. They never stop collecting information. Even after people are here, they never stop collecting information, and this government stands with full confidence in our security agencies. I\u8217\'20\'19m not sure what the position of the opposition is on that.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.03am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.12am BST\par \par \uc2 Tony Pasin ejected from question time chamber\par \par \uc2 The communications minister, \uc2 Michelle Rowland\uc2  took a dixer on the NBN legislation and the opposition gets rowdy. The speaker, Milton Dick, calls for order and says:\par \par \uc2 That wall of noise is completely disrespectful. Everyone on the frontbench, the member [for] Barker, the member for [for] O\u8217\'20\'19Connor, just completely yelling is unparliamentary, but it\u8217\'20\'19s also disrespectful, particularly when a female minister is on her feet.\par \par \uc2 Tony Pasin, the Liberal MP for Barker pipes up at this, scoffing:\par \par \uc2 Really? Rubbish.\par \par \uc2 Milton Dick immediately becomes Dugald Dick (here\u8217\'20\'19s the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2023/sep/07/australia-politics-live-qantas-catherine-king-aviation-policy-qatar-anthony-albanese-jakarta-li-qiang-question-time-peter-dutton-richard-marles?page=with:block-64f94fd98f08229294ee42d0#block-64f94fd98f08229294ee42d0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 deep lore}}}\uc2  behind that) and orders Pasin from the chamber, but first makes him apologise. Pasin:\par \par \uc2 I apologise. I apologise.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.20am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.10am BST\par \par \uc2 Patricia Karvelas leaving Radio National Breakfast program\par \par \uc2 Patricia Karvelas is leaving Radio National Breakfast after three years in the agenda-setting role.\par \par \uc2 She will continue hosting Q&A and will present national politics on the ABC News Channel. She said in a statement:\par \par \uc2 After a decade it\u8217\'20\'19s time to change it up and I\u8217\'20\'19m ready to lean into a new cross-platform role.\par \par \uc2 I want to thank the ABC for the opportunities it has provided me through my decade here. It continues to be the most important public institution in the country in my view and I want to help build its connections with voters across the country.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.15am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.09am BST\par \par \uc2 Question time begins\par \par \uc2 Dan Tehan\uc2  opens up the questions:\par \par \uc2 The Daily Telegraph has this morning reported the Albanese government granted a visa to Fayez Elhasani, an individual proudly and openly linked to \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and \uc2 Palestinian Islamic Jihad\uc2 , both listed terrorist organisations in this country, and that Elhasani is now living in Australia. Prime minister, has the visa for this individual been canceled?\par \par \uc2 Tony Burke takes this one:\par \par \uc2 The process for all of these visas for people who\u8217\'20\'19ve come from Palestine has been exactly the same for every one of them.\par \par \uc2 And the engagement with security agencies has been, has been the same the whole way through.\par \par \uc2 And I have to say, if the choice between who I\u8217\'20\'19ll take advice from, between the shadow minister who asked the question, or the director general of Asio. I\u8217\'20\'19m sorry, but I\u8217\'20\'19m going to listen to the director general of Asio.\par \par \uc2 So the process the visa goes through is this, every single applicant is first of all checked against the movement alert list.\par \par \uc2 The movement alert list is updated every 24 hours involving \uc2 Asio\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s information.\par \par \uc2 Then if additional flags are raised at any point, separate referrals happen to Asio, then for people who were getting out of that part of the world, they had to go through a border that was controlled by both Egypt and Israel, with the relevant checks that would happen at that point as well.\par \par \uc2 In addition to that, it is now public that there was a further check that was made in March of every single visa holder within this caseload, either onshore or offshore, all of those checks have been made.\par \par \uc2 If at any point, if at any point, our security agencies gave advice to the government that a visa should be canceled, then the government, of course, would take seriously that advice, but the concept that we should abandon our security agencies and defer instead to \uc2 Google\uc2  searches from the opposition is not something this government will not do.\par \par \uc2 Elhasani was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.canberratimes.com.au/story/8716461/maimed-gaza-artist-in-nsw-after-10-of-his-family-killed/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 granted a visa in August}}}\uc2  after he fled Gaza when 10 of his family members were killed in an Israeli airstrike, including five grandchildren.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.30am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.55pm BST\par \par \uc2 Amy\u8217\'20\'19s analysis: Liberal Aaron Violi\u8217\'20\'19s response to the NBN legislation\par \par \uc2 This is a speech that really should have been an email.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.59pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.53pm BST\par \par \uc2 Aaron Violi is now saying it is hypocritical for Labor to criticise the Coalition\u8217\'20\'19s nuclear plan while saying the NBN should be publicly owned, and then ranges on to the cost of living, to how out of touch Labor MPs are because they have been in the parliament for too long.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.58pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.48pm BST\par \par \uc2 Aaron Violi is saying that it is \u8220\'20\'1Camazing\u8221\'20\'1D that the prime minister can \u8220\'20\'1Cget everyone to come in for his speech\u8221\'20\'1D today, but couldn\u8217\'20\'19t get everyone in the chamber for when he apologised for his Tourette\u8217\'20\'19s comment.\par \par \uc2 Question time is in just over 10 minutes, which helps.\par \par \uc2 He\u8217\'20\'19s now back on the issues with the NBN. Which the Coalition built.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.52pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.46pm BST\par \par \uc2 Coalition continue NBN criticism\par \par \uc2 Aaron Violi says the NBN is not \u8220\'20\'1Cdelivering as it is meant to\u8221\'20\'1D but he seems to forget that it was his side of politics which built it.\par \par \uc2 And chose {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2018/apr/27/nbns-speed-slowed-by-reliance-on-copper-network-its-ceo-admits"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 copper over fibre}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2021/nov/10/secret-figures-reveal-coalitions-cut-down-nbn-tech-three-times-more-expensive-than-forecast?CMP=Share_iOSApp_Other"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in charge of the rollout}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Seems memories are very short at times.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.51pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.45pm BST\par \par \uc2 Liberals accuse Labor of \u8216\'20\'18rushing\u8217\'20\'19 on NBN laws to distract from PM\u8217\'20\'19s gaffe\par \par \uc2 The Liberal MP for Casey, Aaron Violi, is accusing the government of \u8220\'20\'1Crushing\u8221\'20\'1D to introduce the NBN legislation (the opposition has not yet been briefed on the bill) to create a \u8220\'20\'1Cdistraction\u8221\'20\'1D from his Tourette\u8217\'20\'19s comment yesterday.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.50pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.38pm BST\par \par \uc2 PM says NBN legislation will safeguard service from future Coalition government\par \par \uc2 Anthony Albanese says the legislation is about safeguarding the NBN from a future Coalition government.\par \par \uc2 Albanese:\par \par \uc2 For those opposite, it\u8217\'20\'19s always the same; let the market rip, and if people are disadvantaged or miss out, well, so be it. So be it.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.44pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.35pm BST\par \par \uc2 Albanese: in 2024 ever child needs access to the NBN\par \par \uc2 Anthony Albanese:\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 National Broadband Network\uc2  is a vital national asset, and it delivers an essential public service. It was built by Australians, for Australians, and it belongs to every Australian citizen, and it belongs in public hands.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s what this legislation is about, safeguarding the future of the NBN, making sure it cannot be privatised. It can\u8217\'20\'19t be hollowed out, it can\u8217\'20\'19t be sold off to overseas interests, and making sure that every Australian whether they live in the city or in the regions or in the outer suburbs can count on the affordable, reliable and fast internet that they need and that they deserve, publicly owned and affordable for all.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister \uc2 Gough Whitlam\uc2  once said that his definition of equality was every child in Australia having a quiet room to study in and a desk with a lamp. That was in the 1970s.\par \par \uc2 In 2024 every Australian child \u8230\'20\'26 needs to be sitting at their desk with access to the NBN.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.43pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.31pm BST\par \par \uc2 Albanese claims keeping the NBN public is a cost-of-living measure\par \par \uc2 There we go \u8211\'20\'13 Anthony Albanese is speaking on the introduction NBN bill (the one which will keep it in public hands) which it seems the opposition is opposing. The Liberal\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 David Coleman\uc2  said the government should be focused on cost of living.\par \par \uc2 Anthony Albanese says the opposition has opposed every cost-of-living measure the government has brought forward. He says ensuring the NBN stays public is a cost-of-living measure, because it won\u8217\'20\'19t go to a private company who has obligations to maximise profits for shareholders.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.42pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.28pm BST\par \par \uc2 Anthony Albanese arrives in House chamber early\par \par \uc2 Anthony Albanese has entered the chamber 30 minutes ahead of question time (it is unusual for the prime minister to arrive so early) so he may be about to speak on a motion.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.14pm BST\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Cassidy has an update on the school funding agreement \u8211\'20\'13 which looks like another headache for the Albanese goverment:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/09/australias-biggest-states-hold-out-for-greater-public-school-funding-as-deadline-passes"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Australia\u8217\'20\'19s biggest states hold out for greater public school funding as deadline passes}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.03pm BST\par \par \uc2 Question time looms \u8230\'20\'26 early\par \par \uc2 We hope you are getting those mimosas and bloody Mary\u8217\'20\'19s ready, because we are an hour away from the special brunch QT, where all your favourite (and not-so-favourite) politicians will be bright-eyed and bushy tailed and wondering what to do with the rest of their day.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.13pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.51pm BST\par \par \uc2 Twelve years on since Gillard\u8217\'20\'19s powerful speech on misogyny\par \par \uc2 It is 12 years since \uc2 Julia Gillard\uc2  delivered her misogyny speech to the parliament.\par \par \uc2 The speech, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fCNuPcf8L00"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 which was delivered in response to a question from then opposition leader Tony Abbott about Peter Slipper}}}\uc2 , took on a second life of its own after it was discovered by Gen Z and turned into a TikTok trend.\par \par \uc2 It is also widely regarded as a moment the press gallery at the time missed, with members of the public embracing the words, while much reporting focused on the politics of the time. Everyone involved in that parliamentary moment has since left the parliament, but the speech continues to have an impact.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.instagram.com/gedkearney/reel/DA4GqqKT-U-/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ged Kearney did her own take for social media.}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/sep/30/i-will-not-how-julia-gillards-words-of-white-hot-anger-reverberated-around-the-world"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18I will not\u8217\'20\'19: how Julia Gillard\u8217\'20\'19s words of white-hot anger reverberated around the world}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.55pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.35pm BST\par \par \uc2 More evacuation flights arrive from Lebanon\par \par \uc2 Another 220 people have arrived in Australia from Beirut in the latest evacuation flights overnight. \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports:\par \par \uc2 Trading smoke and fear in the Middle East to the warm hugs of her grandchildren, Houda Ammoun says she is thankful to be back in Australia. The Sydney woman was among 220 Australians and permanent residents to touch down in Sydney late on Tuesday night after being evacuated from Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 But, like many, she\u8217\'20\'19 i concerned for the family left behind. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s very hard, I can\u8217\'20\'19t explain it, the smoke and everything,\u8221\'20\'1D she said, breaking down in tears.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m very happy to be back home because over there is very, very sad. It\u8217\'20\'19s not fair, why should we do this? [It\u8217\'20\'19s] our home, our houses, our families.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Beginning with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/sep/17/hundreds-of-hezbollah-members-hurt-in-lebanon-after-pagers-explode"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a pager attack targeting militant group Hezbollah}}}\uc2 , Israel\u8217\'20\'19s bombing campaign has killed more than 1,400 Lebanese people since 17 September. It follows a \uc2 Hamas\uc2  invasion of Israel in October 2023 that killed 1,200 people and a counterattack that has all but levelled Gaza and killed at least 42,000 people.\par \par \uc2 The government-chartered flight, the first operated by \uc2 Qantas\uc2 , is among several ferrying 1,200 people out as Israeli strikes rain down on the country. Commercial flights have been cancelled in recent weeks due to the recent conflict.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.43pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.26pm BST\par \par \uc2 Birmingham says PM\u8217\'20\'19s disability insult a \u8216\'20\'18serious mistake\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Simon Birmingham\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 who was once the hope of the \u8220\'20\'1Cmoderate Liberals\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 was also asked about Anthony Albanese\u8217\'20\'19s Tourette\u8217\'20\'19s comment in question time yesterday, which he later apologised for.\par \par \uc2 Birmingham :\par \par \uc2 The prime minister recognised it as a serious mistake. The point I make, is imagine the sanctimonious outage if this had been \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2  or a member of the Coalition.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.29pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.23pm BST\par \par \uc2 Birmingham blames Labor for bipartisan breakdown on October 7 motion\par \par \uc2 Liberal senator \uc2 Simon Birmingham\uc2  has appeared on ABC Breakfast TV where he was asked if the opposition regretted not supporting the October 7 motion that the government put to the parliament yesterday.\par \par \uc2 The Coalition rejected it as it included calls for a de-escalation and a ceasefire and acknowledged the civilian deaths in Palestine and Lebanon. The Coalition was also against the motion including hopes for a two-state solution.\par \par \uc2 Birmingham said it was the government\u8217\'20\'19s fault the Coalition couldn\u8217\'20\'19t agree, because the Coalition held the same view it has held since the attacks on October 7 last year and it is the government which has changed position.\par \par \uc2 (The government continues to recognise Israel\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cright to defend itself\u8221\'20\'1D as well as condemn the attacks on October 7 and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  more broadly. Its motion also acknowledged what has happened since October 7, which the Coalition objected to.)\par \par \uc2 Birmingham:\par \par \uc2 I regret that we weren\u8217\'20\'19t able to maintain the bipartisanship that existed 12 months ago, but that is largely because the Albanese government dramatically shifted part of its policy position in those 12 months, changed the words in the resolution carried 12 months ago, changed a decades-long policy on a two-state solution and how it is achieved.\par \par \uc2 It is not the Coalition who has changed position here, it is the Labor party.\par \par \uc2 The change to the two-state solution Birmingham is speaking about is that the Albanese government says that a two-state solution should be part of the peace process, not just something that comes at the end of the peace process.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.29pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.14pm BST\par \par \uc2 Ley comments on PM using disability as an insult in parliament\par \par \uc2 Liberal deputy leader \uc2 Sussan Ley\uc2  has been asked about Anthony Albanese\u8217\'20\'19s Tourette remark in the parliament yesterday and what it says about parliamentary debate. The prime minister returned to the chamber and apologised and said he should never have said it.\par \par \uc2 Ley:\par \par \uc2 What I saw yesterday was a prime minister under pressure, lashing out with a remark that he never should have made. Using disabilities as an insult in the house against another parliamentarian? Yes, definitely, the prime minister is under pressure, and he did come in and apologise, but it was at the end of the day, and it should have happened earlier, and it should have happened with more grace.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.16pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.09pm BST\par \par \uc2 Bandt defends Greens against charges of antisemitism\par \par \uc2 Q: Well, \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2  says that the Greens are racist and antisemitic, and some people agree with that, because Jewish organisations right now want the Greens to be preferenced last by the major parties. Do you accept that this charge of antisemitism has been a problem for you and your party?\par \par \uc2 Adam Bandt\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 I think \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2  makes all sorts of claims, and he\u8217\'20\'19s made a career out of fomenting division, and I\u8217\'20\'19ve seen that on display for decades now, and from the beginning, including the first time this came to parliament a year ago, the Greens have made it crystal clear that there is no place for antisemitism in Australia, and we condemn it.\par \par \uc2 There is no place for Islamophobia [either] but we think those two things can sit side by side with opposing the invasion as well \u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Q: There have been instances of antisemitic behavior, from some Greens MPs, right?\par \par \uc2 Bandt: No.\par \par \uc2 Q: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/video/2024/feb/12/labors-josh-burns-in-shock-at-greens-silence-over-jenny-leongs-jewish-lobby-comments-video"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Jenny Leong \u8230\'20\'26 that was antisemitic}}}\uc2 , wasn\u8217\'20\'19t it...?\par \par \uc2 Bandt:\par \par \uc2 So federally, no, with respect to that, she apologised and those comments were inappropriate, and she admitted that that and that they were insensitive, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/sep/20/jenny-leong-greens-mp-sydney-jewish-museum--tour-ntwnfb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 she apologised and gave a very strong and full and unconditional apology for those words}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 With respect to the approach that we\u8217\'20\'19ve taken federally, we\u8217\'20\'19ve been crystal clear. We\u8217\'20\'19ve been crystal clear that we oppose antisemitism and oppose Islamophobia.\par \par \uc2 But I think you can do that and still say that you have an extremist Netanyahu government that now international courts have said is committing the crime of apartheid with the occupation that is taking place in Palestine and are also now subject to court orders to prevent a genocide and criticise that as well.\par \par \uc2 And I know that there are many people, including in the Jewish community, who are critical of the approach taken by that extremist government.\par \par \uc2 Jenny Leong, the Greens member for Newtown, apologised for referring to the \u8220\'20\'1Ctentacles\u8221\'20\'1D of the \u8220\'20\'1CJewish lobby\u8221\'20\'1D and its influence across Australia {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/feb/07/chris-minns-jenny-leong-antisemitic-trope-octupus-greens-mp"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in February}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.15pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.00pm BST\par \par \uc2 Greens say Labor\u8217\'20\'19s October 7 motion did not recognise actions of \u8216\'20\'18extremist Netanyahu government\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 The Green\u8217\'20\'19s leader, \uc2 Adam Bandt\uc2 , is asked on the same program why his party didn\u8217\'20\'19t support the motion (it abstained) and said:\par \par \uc2 The Greens put forward our motion that not only mourned those who lost their lives in the attacks on October 7, which we condemned at the time and continue to condemn, but we have to mourn those who have died as part of this cycle of violence before and afterwards and since as well.\par \par \uc2 And from our view, we couldn\u8217\'20\'19t support a motion that talked about the things that have happened, not only on October 7, but since then, without also recognizing that the extremist Netanyahu government has also been found to be committing war crimes, and the there is an unfolding genocide and there are things that Australia can and should do, including starting to put pressure on this extremist Netanyahu government.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.07pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.58pm BST\par \par \uc2 Gallagher says friction over October 7 motion from left and right reflects Labor\u8217\'20\'19s position as \u8216\'20\'18mature, responsible government\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 The Greens and independent senators \uc2 Lidia Thorpe\uc2  and Fatima Payman also criticised the motion, but for the opposite reason to the Coalition \u8211\'20\'13 they did not believe the motion adequately addressed what had happened since October 7 in Palestine.\par \par \uc2 Katy Gallagher said:\par \par \uc2 I think the Senate yesterday, I mean, in a sense, spoke to the fact that the government\u8217\'20\'19s position is really seeking \u8230\'20\'26 to try and unite the country and bring the country together and not have this social division play out domestically and on the one side, we had the opposition who have a particular view.\par \par \uc2 And the on the other we have the Greens, which have the opposite view. And in the middle, you have the government that\u8217\'20\'19s \u8230\'20\'26 absolutely condemning \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and the role [of] the October 7 atrocities that happened, calling for the release of hostages, but also recognizing that there has been a significant loss of life from Palestine and calling for a ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 I think our position reflects a mature, responsible government, and we\u8217\'20\'19ve got \u8230\'20\'26 friction on either side of the political spectrum.\par \par \uc2 And you know, it\u8217\'20\'19s our job to continue to make decisions in the interests of our communities as broad as they are and as diverse as they are, and not be pulled into the politics of division that are being [played] on either side of us.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.03pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.51pm BST\par \par \uc2 Gallagher says usual for some \u8216\'20\'18give and take\u8217\'20\'19 that wasn\u8217\'20\'19t done over October 7 motion\par \par \uc2 Katy Gallagher is then asked by the host whether it would have been a better idea to \u8220\'20\'1Creach agreement\u8221\'20\'1D and commemorate October 7 and says:\par \par \uc2 I think that was the aim. I mean, that genuinely was the aim, and there was extensive dialogue between the opposition and the government about how to ensure that there was a bipartisan motion that could be supported.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s what happened in the days after the October 7 last year, when the parliament was able to provide that bipartisanship.\par \par \uc2 And in these kind of discussions where you are trying to negotiate a motion, it isn\u8217\'20\'19t unusual for there to be a bit of give and take on both sides to accept a motion that overall stood for the parliament that wasn\u8217\'20\'19t able to be done yesterday. And that\u8217\'20\'19s regrettable, but I think it speaks to some of the politics of division that are being played here locally, about, you know, awful conflict that\u8217\'20\'19s happening internationally.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.55pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.50pm BST\par \par \uc2 Gallagher defends support for ceasefire as part of October 7 motion\par \par \uc2 Asked why the government included the ceasefire element in the motion, given the Coalition didn\u8217\'20\'19t support it, Katy Gallagher said:\par \par \uc2 This is the position we have taken for many months now. It\u8217\'20\'19s in recognition of the loss of a large amount of civilian lives in that region.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s the position of a number of governments around the world, including the US and the UK, you know, key allies of ours.\par \par \uc2 And I find it unusual that you\u8217\'20\'19d have an opposition that wants to see the conflict continue and the continued loss of civilian lives.\par \par \uc2 So I think a statement about a ceasefire and calling for a de escalation has been part of our policy and our position for months, and I think it probably reflects the majority of the Australian people who want to see an end to the loss of civilian life in that region.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.53pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.48pm BST\par \par \uc2 Katy Gallagher says lack of opposition support for October 7 motion a question for \uc2 Peter Dutton\par \par \uc2 The finance minister, Katy Gallagher, is speaking to ABC radio RN Breakfast, where she is asked about what happened yesterday with the parliamentary motion to commemorate the October 7 attacks.\par \par \uc2 The opposition did not support the motion as it also included acknowledgment of civilian deaths in Palestine and Lebanon and called for a de-escalation and ceasefire and a two-party solution.\par \par \uc2 The opposition said it could not support those elements, as it believed the motion should only commemorate Israel.\par \par \uc2 Gallagher is asked why the government couldn\u8217\'20\'19t come to a bipartisan position on the motion and says:\par \par \uc2 I think that question has to be asked of Mr Dutton, of \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2 , because I know the length that were gone to try and reach agreement with the opposition to have a bipartisan motion and they were extensive, and unfortunately, you know, agreement couldn\u8217\'20\'19t be reached in the end.\par \par \uc2 But our preferred, the prime minister\u8217\'20\'19s preferred approach, was to reach agreement with the opposition and he certainly sought to do that.\par \par \uc2 At the end of the day, Mr Dutton made a decision not to support that. And I think that\u8217\'20\'19s unfortunate in terms of you know how the parliament could have projected and made a statement in respect of the atrocities that happened on October 7 last year.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.51pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.37pm BST\par \par \uc2 Opposition grows to social media age restrictions\par \par \uc2 Opposition to the federal and state proposals to ban younger teens from social media is growing, with more than 120 experts, professors and academics, as well as dozens of youth, mental health and legal organisations signing on to an open letter to the prime minister and premiers of NSW and South Australia arguing against the ban.\par \par \uc2 The letter has been released ahead of a two-day summit in NSW and SA on social media and includes signatories from universities across Australia and the world working in health, media, communications and education.\par \par \uc2 Organisations including the Project Rockit, \uc2 Save the Children\uc2 , Reach Out, Bravehearts, the \uc2 Alannah and Madeline Foundation\uc2  and the Australian Youth Affairs Coalition are among the signatories to the letter that says while the risks that social media has for children and young people are understood, they require \u8220\'20\'1Ca careful and evidence-based response\u8221\'20\'1D:\par \par \uc2 The online world is a place where children and young people access information, build social and technical skills, connect with family and friends, learn about the world around them and relax and play.\par \par \uc2 Any restrictions in the digital world must therefore be designed with care and we are concerned that a \u8216\'20\'18ban\u8217\'20\'19 is too blunt an instrument to address risks effectively.\par \par \uc2 The letter says the ban affects the rights of children to access and participate online and noted that implementing a ban remains a challenge.\par \par \uc2 There are not yet effective techniques for age assurance nor to verify parental consent, and privacy concerns remain.\par \par \uc2 They said a ban would disincentivise platforms from making their services safer, meaning it is a less safe environment for teens who slip through the ban.\par \par \uc2 A tender for the federal government trial of age assurance technology closed this week.\par \par \uc2 Some related reading below:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2024/oct/09/australian-teens-approaching-age-limit-could-be-spared-from-social-media-ban-act-leader-says"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Australian teens approaching age limit could be spared from social media ban, ACT leader says}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.29pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.31pm BST\par \par \uc2 Ask Amy: what\u8217\'20\'19s the parliamentary gameplan for the day?\par \par \uc2 As Martin and the team have laid out, the government is very keen to have people refocus on its agenda. So there is going to be a raft of new legislation introduced today, as well as a continued focus on the housing bills \u8211\'20\'13 which are not going anywhere, unless the Greens blink.\par \par \uc2 Keeping the NBN in public hands, cybersecurity and addressing Sydney airport congestion are all on the list.\par \par \uc2 This is one of the last gasps of legislation you\u8217\'20\'19ll see this year. There are just a dozen sitting days left in 2024 and only nine of those are joint sitting days, with estimates being held in the first week of November. So there is not a lot of time left this year to pass legislation this year.\par \par \uc2 But having a flurry of legislation is one way to try to get the focus back on the domestic agenda.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.35pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.17pm BST\par \par \uc2 Good morning\par \par \uc2 Hello and welcome to the midpoint of this short sitting week \u8211\'20\'13 and a very big thank you to Martin for taking us through the morning news.\par \par \uc2 You have Amy Remeikis with you for the parliamentary day.\par \par \uc2 Ready? It\u8217\'20\'19s going to be at least a four-coffee day. We\u8217\'20\'19ve got question time in just under three hours today and that\u8217\'20\'19s a jolt none of us needed. So maybe five coffees. And a donut.\par \par \uc2 Let\u8217\'20\'19s get into it.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.24pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.14pm BST\par \par \uc2 Report says 90% of those using paid family violence scheme say it has helped\par \par \uc2 The government is heralding the results of its new paid family and domestic violence leave program, with an independent statutory report showing 90% of those using the scheme said it had helped them.\par \par \uc2 The review will be tabled in parliament today. We understand it will recommend a focus on increasing awareness and understanding of the entitlement and making sure employers and employees know about the scheme.\par \par \uc2 The review also concedes there is an ongoing stigma among some workers around family violence, which can be a barrier to some people seeking to access the payment.\par \par \uc2 The program allows 10 days paid leave for people experiencing family violence. People surveyed for the review said they had used the payments to help them make arrangements for their safety or the safety of their children, or to access medical, legal and police services.\par \par \uc2 The payment, the review found, allowed people to take time off work to access services they could not access outside work hours, or let them access that help during work hours without the perpetrator of their abuse knowing about it.\par \par \uc2 Stakeholders \u8211\'20\'13 including peak union groups and employer groups \u8211\'20\'13 have also backed the change, saying it has improved workplace culture and helped support employees facing violence.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.13pm BST\par \par \uc2 Greens aim to force gambling debate\par \par \uc2 The Greens will try to force a debate on gambling reform today by introducing a bill modelled on the recommendations of the inquiry led by late Labor MP \uc2 Peta Murphy\uc2 , which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2023/jun/28/ads-for-online-gambling-should-be-banned-in-australia-within-three-years-inquiry-recommends"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 called for a full ban on gambling advertising}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe public are sick of the excuses and the delays,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Sarah Hanson-Young\uc2 , the Greens\u8217\'20\'19 communications spokesperson.\par \par \uc2 Tim Costello, the chief advocate for the Alliance for Gambling Reform, was also concerned at the delay in enacting change.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m completely mystified this has taken 16 months,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.01pm BST\par \par \uc2 Labor to seek legal power to compel responses to hacks\par \par \uc2 The federal government is moving to gain greater legal powers to respond to serious cyber incidents, including compelling the private sector to provide information and issuing directions to businesses on how they must respond in the event of a serious attack.\par \par \uc2 The new measures, contained in a legislative package to be introduced to parliament today, will also introduce minimum cybersecurity standards for smartphones and force businesses to upgrade their risk management programs and report payments they make in response to ransomware attacks.\par \par \uc2 The home affairs minister, Tony Burke, will today introduce a bill to create a standalone cybersecurity act and implement measures flagged in the cybersecurity strategy published last year.\par \par \uc2 The package of measures will include restrictions on how the Australian Signals Directorate and National Cyber Security Coordinator can use the information that businesses share about their systems and will clarify the protective obligations applying to systems that hold business-critical data.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis legislation ensures we keep pace with emerging threats, positioning individuals and businesses better to respond to, and bounce back from cybersecurity threats,\u8221\'20\'1D Burke said in remarks issued ahead of introducing the legislation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTo achieve Australia\u8217\'20\'19s vision of being a world leader in cybersecurity by 2030, we need the unified effort of government, industry and the community.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Burke said creating a stand-alone cybersecurity act was \u8220\'20\'1Ca long overdue step for our country\u8221\'20\'1D and reflected \u8220\'20\'1Cthe government\u8217\'20\'19s deep concern and focus on these threats\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Among the measures are changes to the existing Security of Critical Infrastructure Act, which will give government the power to step in as a last resort during a significant incident to manage the consequences.\par \par \uc2 They will also establish a cyber review board and shift responsibility for telecommunications security into the infrastructure legislation.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.54pm BST\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18They are not just numbers, they are precious lives\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Metres from yesterday\u8217\'20\'19s bitter debate in parliament about the motion remembering the 7 October \uc2 Hamas\uc2  attack, those with family and friends in Gaza pleaded for them to be seen as more than just headlines and numbers.\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s Daniel Hurst listened as Dr Bushra Othman, a Melbourne surgeon who recently returned from a volunteering stint at al-Aqsa hospital in Gaza, told a media conference in Parliament House: \u8220\'20\'1CThe people of Gaza are not just headlines. They are not just numbers. They are precious lives.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Read his whole report here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/08/as-politicians-fought-over-words-palestinian-australians-told-of-worlds-shattered-by-bombs-and-blockade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 As politicians fought over words, Palestinian Australians told of worlds shattered by bombs and blockade}}}\par \par \uc2 And Josh Taylor has written about a pro-Palestine professor from America whse visa is being reviewed after controversial comments at a rally in Sydney.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/08/australia-reviews-pro-palestine-activists-visa-after-he-called-7-october-a-day-of-considerable-celebration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Australia reviews pro-Palestine activist\u8217\'20\'19s visa after he called 7 October a day of \u8216\'20\'18considerable celebration\u8217\'20\'19}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.49pm BST\par \par \uc2 As mentioned at the top, the Middle East conflict is deeply and personally felt here in Australia, by so many who grieve for the lives lost. And it has also redrawn domestic political faultlines.\par \par \uc2 Political editor Karen Middleton tells Reged Ahmad how differing views of the Israel-Gaza war have percolated up into our political debate.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/audio/2024/oct/08/could-the-crisis-in-the-middle-east-reshape-australian-politics-full-story-podcast"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Could the crisis in the Middle East reshape Australian politics? \u8211\'20\'13 podcast }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.41pm BST\par \par \uc2 Prime minister issues second apology after Tourette syndrome remark\par \par \uc2 The prime minister has apologised for a second time for referring to Tourette syndrome during an exchange with the shadow treasurer, \uc2 Angus Taylor\uc2 , during question time yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Taylor had {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aph.gov.au/Parliamentary_Business/Hansard/Hansard_Display?bid=chamber/hansardr/28034/&sid=0000"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 asked a question on tax policy}}}\uc2 . After Taylor\u8217\'20\'19s repeated interjections during Albanese\u8217\'20\'19s answer, the prime minister remarked:\par \par \uc2 Have you got Tourette\u8217\'20\'19s or something? You sit there, \u8216\'20\'18babble, babble, babble\u8217\'20\'19.\par \par \uc2 He was called to order and replied: \u8220\'20\'1CI withdraw, Mr Speaker. I withdraw and apologise.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Later on Tuesday evening, Albanese {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aph.gov.au/Parliamentary_Business/Hansard/Hansard_Display?bid=chamber/hansardr/28034/&sid=0000"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 returned to the chamber}}}\uc2  to deliver a fuller apology, saying:\par \par \uc2 Today in question time I made comments that were unkind and hurtful. I knew it was wrong as soon as I made the comment. I apologised and I withdrew as soon as I said it, but it shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have happened. I also want to apologise to all Australians who suffer from this disability. I regret saying it. It was wrong. It was insensitive and I apologise.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.40pm BST\par \par \uc2 Labor to introduce law to keep NBN publicly owned\par \par \uc2 The Labor government will introduce a new bill to parliament on Wednesday to keep the $51bn network in public hands, \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports. The move will put pressure on the opposition to declare whether it plans to privatise the network if it wins power at next year\u8217\'20\'19s federal election.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CKeeping the NBN in public hands means high-speed broadband remains affordable for Australian families and businesses around the country,\u8221\'20\'1D Anthony Albanese said.\par \par \uc2 The communications minister, \uc2 Michelle Rowland\uc2 , said more than 8.5 million customers would be worse off if the network was privatised.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAustralians don\u8217\'20\'19t trust the coalition not to flog off the NBN just like they did with \uc2 Telstra\uc2 , resulting in higher prices and poorer services, especially in the regions,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 The finance minister, Katy Gallagher, said the NBN was a critical piece of national infrastructure that helped to fuel economic growth. The \uc2 Communication Workers Union\uc2 , which represents NBN staff, hailed the move.\par \par \uc2 NBN workers will breathe a sigh of relief, the national president, Shane Murphy, said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis bill will ensure that Australians will not be threatened with massive price hikes and service quality deterioration that would inevitably occur should this vital piece of our nation\u8217\'20\'19s public infrastructure be sold to the highest bidder,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.08pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.31pm BST\par \par \uc2 Welcome\par \par \uc2 Good morning and welcome to our live news blog. I\u8217\'20\'19m Martin Farrer with the top overnight stories and then it\u8217\'20\'19ll be Amy Remeikis taking the controls.\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19ve been reviews, investigations and a lot of talk but the Labor government will today {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/oct/08/controversial-sydney-airport-slot-system-faces-government-crackdown"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announce a }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/oct/08/controversial-sydney-airport-slot-system-faces-government-crackdown"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 crackdown}}}\uc2  on the controversial way \uc2 Qantas\uc2  and \uc2 Virgin\uc2  manage runway slots as the fight against anticompetitive business behaviours gathers pace. Airlines that strategically cancel flights to block competition at Sydney airport could face civil penalties.\par \par \uc2 On another front on the war against the cost of living, Anthony Albanese will say today that new laws to keep the NBN publicly owned will ensure high-speed broadband remains affordable for all Australians. His government will introduce a new bill to parliament today to keep the $51bn network in public hands. More coming up.\par \par \uc2 The impact of the Middle East crisis continues to reverberate through Australian politics \u8211\'20\'13 in Canberra, the parties {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/08/shows-how-small-he-is-duttons-refusal-to-support-7-october-motion-condemned-by-labor-and-crossbench"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 clashed again yesterday}}}\uc2  over how the country should be responding to the conflict. With more tough exchanges expected on the issue today, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/oct/08/as-politicians-fought-over-words-palestinian-australians-told-of-worlds-shattered-by-bombs-and-blockade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 we hear from Palestinian Australians}}}\uc2  who have pleaded for the appalling humanitarian cost of the war to be recognised properly. And we also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/audio/2024/oct/08/could-the-crisis-in-the-middle-east-reshape-australian-politics-full-story-podcast"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have a podcast}}}\uc2  on how it could change our politics.\par \par \uc2 And last night in Canberra, the prime minister returned to the chamber unexpectedly to apologise for an earlier comment, made in the heat of the question time debate, referring to Tourette syndrome. More on that in a few minutes.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241008eka8002uw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
